2 CONT Ceredig
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ceredig ap Cunedda, (424 - 453 AD), king of Ceredigion,[1] may have been born c. 420 AD in the Brythonic kingdom of Manaw Gododdin (modern Lothian in Scotland), centred on the Firth of Forth in the area known as Yr Hen Ogledd. Lit
2 CONC tle is known of him. One of the sons of Cunedda, grandfather of Saint David,[2] according to tradition, he arrived in what is now modern Wales from Gododdin with his father's family when they were invited to help ward off Irish in
2 CONC vaders. As a reward for his bravery, his father gave him the southernmost part of the territories in north-west Wales reconquered from the Irish. The realm came to be called Ceredigion after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ "Lives of the Cambro British saints",p. 396, 1853, Rev. William Jenkins Rees
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ The Cambrian, A Bi-Monthly Published in the interest of the Welsh people and their descendants in the United States, 1881, Vol. 1, 1881
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT Lives of the Cambro British saints,William Jenkins Rees, Thomas Wakeman, 1835
2 CONT
2 CONT A history of Wales from the earliest times, John Edward Lloyd, 1911
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cambrian, A Bi-Monthly Published in the interest of the Welsh people and their descendantsin the United States, 1881, Vol. 1, 1881
2 CONT
2 CONT Ceredig Ceredigion,
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Ceredigion
2 CONT
2 CONT (Born c.AD 420)
2 CONT
2 CONT (Latin: Caratacus; English: Caractacus)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ceredig was born in his father Cunedda Wledig's original homeland of Manau Gododdin around the Upper Firth of Forth. When called upon to expel Irish invaders from North Wales in the early 5th century, he travelled south, with the 
2 CONC rest of his family. He fought bravely against the advancing Gaels and his father rewarded him with the most southern area of his new kingdom, bordering on Dyfed, possibly including part of the Cantref Gwaelod. Here, he became a gr
2 CONC eat patron of his brother-in-law, St. Curig. The region became so associated with Ceredig that the people named it Ceredigion after him. In his old age, the kingdom was attacked once more by the Irish and he was persuaded by his c
2 CONC ounsellors to abdicate in favour of a younger man. However, his heir, his eldest grandson, Carannog, declined the offer of the Crown in order to become a hermit. Instead the people of Ceredigion had to look to Ceredig's second son
2 CONC , Usai.
