2 CONT
2 CONT   His (poss.) Great Grandchildren:        Ygerna (Igraine) verch AMLAWDD   ;   Brychan (Brachan) (II; Prince) of MANAU   ;   Thewer (Therew) verch BRYDW   ;   Braigad ap PASGEN   ;   Cadell (I) Ddyrnllwg ap CADEYRN   ;   Madrun (S
2 CONC aint) verch GWERTHEFYR 
2 CONT
2 CONT DESCENT FROM KINGS IN BRITAIN.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Although the family is recorded very much as Welsh, it started off in ‘Britain’, which was an amalgamation of the two islands speaking a common  Celtic language also used in ‘Europe’. It would seem that the 'kingships' held we
2 CONC re mainly in the Midlands area and it is thought that perhaps the ancient town of Ludlow could derive from Lludd Llaw Ereint and perhaps Gloucester from Gloyw Gwallthir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Before and during the Roman occupation they were Kings of Britain who, if not defeated by Rome, had to pay homage and rule under the Romans.
2 CONT
2 CONT     When the Romans left these shores they left a ‘High King’ or ‘Over King’ known as Vortigern, whose real name was thought to be Gwrtheyrn but, as was the custom in those days, was known by a description and became Vortigern Vor
2 CONC teneu meaning ‘High King – The Thin’ in Latin and Gwrtheyrn Gwrtheneu in Celtic Welsh.  Unfortunately this was also the man who invited the Saxons to help defend the country, leading to much internal war and strife before intermix
2 CONC ing to make the population Anglo-Saxon.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Because many people had the same or similar names the ‘descriptive’ name becomes very important in following an ancestral lineage; many having confused Brochfael c500 with Brochwel, who fought and died at the battle of Derva (
2 CONC Chester) in 613.
2 CONT
2 CONT     So the family tree starts as follows :-
2 CONT
2 CONT   1   Beli Mawr (The Great), King of Britain c110 BC
2 CONT
2 CONT       + Don ferch Matonwy.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2   Lludd Llaw Ereint (Silver Handed), King of Britain, c80 BC
2 CONT
2 CONT 3        Afallach ap Lludd, King, c45 BC
2 CONT
2 CONT     4    Euddolen ap Afallach, King, c12 BC
2 CONT
2 CONT   5    Eudos ap Euddolen, c35 AD
2 CONT
2 CONT 6        Eifydd ap Eudos, c80
2 CONT
2 CONT  7    Eudeyrn ap Eifydd, c125
2 CONT
2 CONT   8    Eeuddigan ap Eudeyrn, c170.
2 CONT
2 CONT    9    Rhodri ap Euddigan, c210
2 CONT
2 CONT     10   Gloyw Gwallthir (Long Hair), c250
2 CONT
2 CONT       11   Gwidolin ap Gloyw, c290
2 CONT
2 CONT         12   Gwidol ap Gwidolin, c330
2 CONT
2 CONT 13      Vortigern (Gwrtheyrn Gwrtheneu) (The Thin), High King of Britain, c 370 – 459
2 CONT
2 CONT + Severa ferch Macsen, c370
2 CONT
2 CONT                   14   Gwerthefyr ferch Gwrtheyrn, c400
2 CONT
2 CONT   14   Vortimer Fendigaid (The Blessed), King of Gwerthefyriwg, c402 – 460
2 CONT
2 CONT   14   Cadeyern Fendigaid (The Blessed), King of Powys, c404 – 447
2 CONT
2 CONT          This is our ancester and starts the next Family Tree
2 CONT
2 CONT   14   Pasgen ap Gwrtheyrn, King of Buellt and Gwerthrynion, c406
2 CONT
2 CONT   14   Brydw ap Gwrtheyrn, c408
2 CONT
2 CONT   14   St. Edeyrn ap Gwrtheyrn, c410
2 CONT
2 CONT        + Rowena of Kent, c405
2 CONT
2 CONT   14   Daughter (ferch) Gwrtheyrn / Rowena, c400
2 CONT
2 CONT     15   St. Madrun ferch Gwerthefyr, c440
2 CONT
2 CONT            + Ynyr Gwent, King of Gwent, c430
2 CONT
2 CONT     Starting B.C. it must be realised that before Christianity people worshipped gods such as the Sun. A king was the king of everything that was known, so was also king of the sun and should also be worshipped. When Christianity 
2 CONC was introduced these practices gradually died out, but took decades.
2 CONT
2 CONT BELI MAWR.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beli (or Belenos) had the descriptive title added of Mawr (the Great). He was said to be a King Of Britain who ruled in ‘Middle Britain’, but was also said to be the God of the Sun, so much so that bonfires were lit on May 1st. to
2 CONC  herald the coming of the the ‘sun season’ or summer. Beli’s wife was Anu.
2 CONT
2 CONT LLUDD LLAW EREINT.
2 CONT
2 CONT This son of Beli, Lludd Llaw Ereint (Lludd the Silver Handed) was known as the God of Healing and was known in Ireland as Nuadu. His symbol was a dog, whose lick was supposed to cure. A shrine was built to him at Llud’s Island (Ly
2 CONC dney in Gloucestershire) where models of diseased limbs were offered. He lost a hand himself in battle and Gofannon, a smith, made a new one for him out of silver, so giving him his ‘title’. Loss of the hand forced him to hand ove
2 CONC r to his nephew Lleu Llaw Gyffes (the Skillful Handed).
2 CONT
2 CONT AFALLACH.
2 CONT
2 CONT He ruled the Celtic heaven of Avalon and lived with his daughter Modron. Avalon was supposed to be an island where apples grew and after which it was named.
2 CONT
2 CONT VORTIGERN.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Romans left Britain leading up to 450 they left Vortigern as ‘High King’ of Britain. He is said to have had regional rulers that, being a weak man, he was afraid would supplant him, so set about murdering them and their f
2 CONC amilies, all except two. The two were small babies, Aurelius Ambrosius and Uther Pendragon, who were too small to threaten him. They were whisked away and brought up in Brittany. He married Severa, the daughter of Magnus Maximus, 
2 CONC after whom the Severn river was later named.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nervous that the Romans may return and being troubled by Jutes and Saxons, led by Hengist and Horsa, he came to an agreement with these two to defend the country in exchange for the city of Caer Correi (Caistor, Lincolnshire). Hen
2 CONC gist and Horsa later tricked Vortigern out of Ceint (Kent) in exchange for Hengist’s daughter Rowena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually Vortigern fought but was driven west into Wales where he met Merlin, of King Arthur fame, who told him such a story of fighting dragons that Vortigern fled. Ending up at a wooden castle at the hillfort of Caer Guorthigi
2 CONC rn (Little Doward). It was later struck by lightening and Vortigern burnt to death.
2 CONT
2 CONT By this time Ambrosius Aurelianus (Emrys Wledig) had risen to power to fight the Saxons. 
