1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_kings_of_D%C3%A1l_Riata
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://www.djhooker.com/48/22541.htm 
2 CONT ===Gabhran mac Domangart King of Dál Riada===
2 CONT * Born: Abt 490
2 CONT * Marriage: Ingenach
2 CONT * Died: Cir 559 about age 69 1468
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabhran (Scottish Gaelic: Gabhran mac Domangart) was king of Dál Riada, in modern Ireland and Scotland, from about 538 until 558. He is the eponymous founder of the Cenél nGabraín. The Cenél nGabraín dominated Dál Riada until the 
2 CONC late 7th century, and his son Aedan was later king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabhran in Arthurian Legend Gabhran was a wise and excellent Prince. He first instituted the office of the King's Advocate. He persuaded Loth, a Pictish King, to league with Uther Pendragon, King of the Britons against the Anglo-S
2 CONC axons in 502. Loth was also persuaded to marry Ann-Morgause, daughter to Gorlois and Igraine, step-daughter to the Pendragon and elder half-sister to King Arthur of the Round Table. When the famous King Arthur mounted the British 
2 CONC throne, Gabhran aided him against the Saxons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabhran was at last cut off, for one Toncetus, the Supreme Judge of Capital Crimes, a cruel and covetous man, expecting easy pardon of the King; the enraged people therefore cut him off. But finding they could not obtain the King'
2 CONC s mercy, they next slew the King himself in 535 at the instigation of one Donald of Athol after a reign of thirty-four years.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Queen and children fled to Ireland. Others say that he died after a tedious sickness and was buried at Icolmkill, the royal burying place.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his nephew, Eugenius III, who instead of revenging his uncle's death, received Donald into favor, which made him suspected of having a hand in the conspiracy; and he was succeeded in 558 by his brother Conall, 
2 CONC who introduced heraldry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conall recalled the children of Gabhran but died before their return in 568 after a reign of ten years. Conall was succeeded by his younger brother Kintellus, who courteously entertained Aedan of Dalriada, son of Gabhran and on hi
2 CONC s deathbed resigned to him. 1458
2 CONT
2 CONT bullet  Life Events:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: King of Dál Riada, 538-558. 1458
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabhran married Ingenach, daughter of Brychan of Manau Prince and Daughter of Domangall Hen Princess. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabhran_Goranus Gabhran Goranus (538-560?) was king of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Domgardus (Domangart), son of Fergus Mor MacEarc, and is reckoned as the forty-fifth king of Scotland according to the fictitious chronology of Fordun and Buchanan, but, according to the rectified chronology of T
2 CONC homas Innes and William Forbes Skene, was fourth king of the kingdom of Dalriada, founded by his grandfather Fergus in 503. He succeeded his brother Congallus I in 538 (Tigernach), and is called, as his father and brother also are
2 CONC , Ri Albain, which may imply, as Skene suggests, that during their reigns the Dalriad kingdom had extended beyond its original bounds in Argyle and the isles. Buchanan gives, following Fordoun, a full but unreliable account of the
2 CONC  events of the reign of Goranus, whom he makes the ally of Loth, king of the Picts, the eponymus of Lothian and the contemporary of Arthur. But almost all we really know of it is the brief notice of Tigernach in the year 560, when
2 CONC  he records the death of Gabhran, king of Alban, and the flight of the men of Alban before Brude MacMailchon, king of the Cruithnigh (Picts).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded in Dalriada by Conall son of Congallus, his brother, who reigned till 574, when Aidan, Gabhran's younger son, was inaugurated king at Iona by St. Columba, in preference to his elder brother Eoganan, and through th
2 CONC e influence of Columba obtained the recognition at the Council of Drumceat (515) of the independence of Scottish Dalriada from tribute formerly exacted by Irish Dalriada, although the Scots were to continue to assist the parent st
2 CONC ock in war. From this king the Cinal (or tribe) Gabhran, one of the three powerfuls, i.e. powerful tribes, of Dalriada who occupied Kintyre, Cowall, and several islands on the coast of Argyle, derived its name. The other two were 
2 CONC the Cinal Loarn in Lorn, and the Cinal Angus in Isla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Gabran Of Argyll King Of Dalriada 
2 CONT
2 CONT Weis' "Ancestral Roots. . ." (170:4). 
2 CONT
2 CONT the following is taken from an Internet posting of Michael R. Davidson of Edinburgh. Scotland, on 23 Oct 1995: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabran mac Domangart 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabran succeeded to the kingship upon his brother's death in 538. Welsh sources claim that Gabran married a certain Luan, daughter of Brychan, the founder of the Welsh kingdom of Brycheiniog. There are problems, however with accep
2 CONC ting this as fact, as it appears that the traditions 
2 CONT
2 CONT of two Brycheiniogs, one in Wales and the other in northern Britain, were merged by medieval writers. Gabran's forces were defeated by the Picts in 558. He died in the same year, and was succeeded by Comgall's son Conall. Gabran g
2 CONC ave his name to the Cenel nGabrain, one of the four 
2 CONT
2 CONT divisions of the Dal Riata. Most of the kings of Dal Riata, and later Scotland, were drawn from the Cenel nGabrain. According to the _Senchus_, Gabran was the father of five sons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. *Aedan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Eoganan, d. 595. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Cuildach. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Domnall. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Domangart. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.southing.net/BondFam019%20Web%20Cards/PS02/PS02_131.HTM
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabhran was king of Dalriada. He reigned from about 538- 558.
2 CONT
2 CONT c.525 - Prince Gabhran mac Domangart of Dalriada leads a Scottish party east to settle Gowrie, where he marries a British Princess, Lleian, daughter of King Brychan of Manau Gododdin and probably niece of King Cedic of Strathclyde
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 538, in the Battle of Dalriada, he took the regions of knapdale and Kintyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on merged profiles,
2 CONT
2 CONT Born circa 495, 499 or 500
2 CONT
2 CONT Scots of Argyll
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Dál Riata in the middle of the 6th century
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabrán_mac_Domangairt -SPF
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  In addition to Aidan(Aedan), Gabran had four other sons: Eoganan, Cuildach, Domnall, and Domangart.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gabrán mac Domangairt was king of Dál Riata in the middle of the 6th century. He is the eponymous ancestor of the Cenél nGabraín.
2 CONT
2 CONT The historical evidence for Gabrán is limited to the notice of his death in the Irish annals. It is possible that his death should be linked to a migration or flight from Bridei mac Maelchon, but this may be no more than coinciden
2 CONC ce.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adomnán of Iona, Life of Saint Columba, tr. & ed. Richard Sharpe. Penguin, London, 1995. ISBN 0-14-044462-9
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Bannerman, John, Studies in the History of Dalriada. Scottish Academic Press, Edinburgh, 1974. ISBN 0-7011-2040-1
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Lane, Alan & Campbell, Ewan, Dunadd: An early Dalriadic capital, Oxbow Books, Oxford, 2000. ISBN 1-84217-024-4
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sharpe, Richard, "The thriving of Dalriada" in Simon Taylor (ed.), Kings, clerics and chronicles in Scotland 500–1297. Fourt Courts, Dublin, 2000. ISBN 1-85182-516-9
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps05/ps05_460.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gabran is remembered as the first of the conquering kings. He is termed `king of the Forth', and in the central territories of the southern Picts, in Strathmore, north of Perth, Gowrie preserves his name, while beyond it Brechin 
2 CONC is named for another Irishman...perhaps the ally or subordinate of Gabran. The names suggest that in the middle of the sixth century Gabran's Irish armies overran the southern Picts and planted lasting colonies on their territory.
2 CONC " The Picts pulled themselves together, importing a leader, Bridei, brother of the most powerful British ruler - and he defeated and killed Gabran. {-"The Age of Arthur," John Morris (NY: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1973), pp.180-01.
2 CONC } "Ancestral Roots..." (Balt., 1992) 170-4: "Gabran: He and his son are both called, in Welsh sources, 'the Treacherous'. Welsh pedigrees make him a son of Dyfnwal Hen, allegedly of the line of Ceretic Guletic, regarded by later W
2 CONC elsh writers as an important ruler in northern Britain. According to Welsh sources, his wife was Lleian, dau. of Brychan, the ruler who gave his name to Brecknock."
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of Dál Riata, ca. 537-ca. 559.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king lists show Gabrán as the successor of his brother Comgall (d. ca. 537), and as the predecessor of Comgall's son Conall. The Duan Albanach gives him only a two year reign [Duan Albanach, 131], while the Latin Lists give 22
2 CONC  years [Poppleton MS, KKES, 253; Lists "D", "F", pp. 264, 270; probably correct] or 34 years [List "I", ibid., 281, probably an error for the 34 assigned to his son Áedán; Gabrán is accidently omitted by List "K", ibid., 286].
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gabran "the Treacherous", King of Dalriada, d. 559, He became King of Dalriada, ca. 538 in Dungad, Scotland 
2 CONT
