1 NOTE King Gabhran macDomangairt of Argyll
1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Gabran's forces were defeated by the Picts in 558. He died in the sameyear, and was succeeded by Comgall's son Conall. Gabran gave his name tothe Cenel nGabrain, one of the four divisions of the Dal Riata. Most ofthe kings of D
2 CONC al Riata, and later Scotland, were drawn from the CenelnGabrain.
1 NOTE Possible History
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabrán mac Domangairt was king of Dál Riata in the middle of the 6th century. He is the eponymous ancestor of the Cenél nGabraín.
2 CONT
2 CONT The historical evidence for Gabrán is limited to the notice of his death in the Irish annals. It is possible that his death should be linked to a migration or flight from Bridei mac Maelchon, but this may be no more than coinciden
2 CONC ce.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabrán's chief importance is as the presumed ancestor of the Cenél nGabraín,[2] a kingroup which dominated the kingship of Dál Riata until the late 7th century and continued to provide kings thereafter. Kings of Alba and of Scotla
2 CONC nd traced their descent through Gabrán to his grandfather Fergus Mór, who was seen as the ultimate founder of the royal house as late as the 16th and 17th centuries, long after the Gaelic origins of the kingdom had ceased to have 
2 CONC any real meaning.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike the Cenél Loairn, the Senchus Fer n-Alban does not list any kindreds within the Cenél nGabraín. However, probable descendants of Gabrán, such as Dúnchad mac Conaing and his many kinsmen, would appear to have disputed the su
2 CONC ccession with the descendants of Eochaid Buide grandson of Gabrán, so that this absence of explicit segments in the kindred may be misleading.[3] A genealogy of David I of Scotland in the Book of Ballymote notes the following divi
2 CONC sions:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * After Áedán mac Gabráin, between the main line, called "the sons of Eochaid Buide" and "the children of Cináed mac Ailpín", and the "sons of Conaing"
2 CONT     * After Eochaid Buide, between the main line and the "children of Fergus Goll" and the "children of Connad Cerr ... or the men of Fife", although modern studies make Connad Cerr a member of the Cenél Comgaill
2 CONT     * After Eochaid mac Domangairt, between the main line and the Cenél Comgaill
2 CONT
2 CONT The domain of the Cenél nGabraín appears to have been centred in Kintyre and Knapdale and may have included Arran, Jura and Gigha. The title king of Kintyre is used of a number of presumed kings of the Cenél nGabrain. Two probable
2 CONC  royal sites are known, Dunadd, which lies at the northern edge of their presumed lands, and Aberte (or Dún Aberte), which is very likely the later Dunaverty on the headland beside Southend, Kintyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilmartin may have been an important early Christian site by reason of its proximity to Dunadd and its dedication to Saint Martin of Tours, as may Kilmichael Glassary. However, there appears to be no religious site of the importan
2 CONC ce of Lismore in the lands of the rival Cenél Loairn.
1 NOTE King of Scots of Argyll.
2 CONT Killed by his nephew c 559
1 NOTE He had Kintyre and Knapdale with his chief stronhold on the rock of  Dunadd in the midst of Crinan Moss. He ruled from 538 to 560.
1 NOTE He had Kintyre and Knapdale with his chief stronhold on the rock of  Dunadd in the midst of Crinan Moss. He ruled from 538 to 560.
1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry, chart pg 45.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 43:
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.    Gabran (Boranus), fl.538-c.560.  He and his son Aedan are both called, in Welsh sources, "the Treacherous."  Gabran reigned about 22 years.  In the last year of his reign the Pictish King Brude inflicted on the Scots a crush
2 CONC ing defeat from which they were still staggering when St. Columba, came to their deliverance.  Using the Isle of Iona as a base, he proceeded with the conversion of the Picts as well as the rehabilitation of Scottish Dalriada.  Ga
2 CONC bran perhaps mar. Lieian, dau. of Brachan, a ruler who gave his name to Brecknock in Wales.  Gabran prob. d. in battle against the Picts.  He was succeeded by a nephew, Conall, then by a son, Aedan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 144 line (170-4):
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  Gabran.  He and his son are both called, in Welsh sources, "the treacherous".  Welsh pedigrees make him a son of Dyfnwal hen, allegedly of the line of Ceretic Guletic, regarded by later Welsh writers as an important ruler in n
2 CONC ortern Britain.  According to Welsh Sources, his wife was Lleian, dau. of Brychan, the ruler who gave his name to Brecknock.
