2 CONT
2 CONT    Acceded about 574. Consecrated by his cousin St. Columba. In 575 Aedanattended the Convention of Druim Cett in Ireland, which apparentlyconvened to decide the political relationship between Dal Riata and thekings of the Norther
2 CONC n Ui Neill in Ireland, whose power was growing. In581 he led an expedition to the Orkney islands, and he won a victory atthe Isle of Man in the following year. In 590, he won a battle againstthe Maetae, his British neighbours, but
2 CONC  lost two of his sons in thebattle. In 596, in the first battle between Scots and English, two moreof his sons were slain. In 600, he lead an army against the English ofNorthumbria, but was decisively defeated at Degsastan. He was
2 CONC  victoriousin a battle against the Picts sometime between 596 and 606.
1 NOTE KING OF DAL RIADA 570-604
1 NOTE King of Scots of Argyll
2 CONT Consecrated by his Cousin St Columba c 574
2 CONT Killed c 608
1 NOTE 40th great grandfather
1 NOTE He ruled a kingdom in Scotland from 574 to 606. In 575, he and St. Columba attended the Conventions of Druim Cett in Northern Ireland.
1 NOTE He ruled a kingdom in Scotland from 574 to 606. In 575, he and St. Columba attended the Conventions of Druim Cett in Northern Ireland.
1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry.  Chart: page 45.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 43:
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.    Aedan (Aidinus), c.533-c.608; succeeded Conall; crowned c.574 by his kinsman St. Columba on Iona.  In 576 at a synod at Drumceatt, County Londonderry, he succeeded in breaking the bond of allegiance which had made the two Da
2 CONC lriadas interdependent.  He was thus the first independent ruler of the Caledonian Scots.  He was defeated 603 by Ethelfryth, the neighboring King of Bernicia.  He may have retired to a monastery before his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amie, Adam and FAL.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 144 line (170-5):
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  Aedan.  Details of his life and those of his children and grandchildren are well attested in the near-contemporary life of St. Columba, by Adamnan.  Died about 608 A.D., after ruling Dalriada in Scotland for about 37 years.
