1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Saint Cowair. 'CatholicSaints'.Info===
2 CONT '''Memorial''': 11 July
2 CONT '''Patronage''': Llangower, Wales
2 CONT '''Canonized''': Pre-Congregation
2 CONT '''Additional Information:'''
2 CONT 1. ''Book of Saints'', by the Monks of Ramsgate [https://archive.org/details/bookofsaintsdict00stau/page/70/mode/1up?q=cywair&view=theater]
2 CONT 2. ''Our Sunday Visitor’s Encyclopedia of Saints'' [https://archive.org/details/oursundayvisitor0000buns_m1p1]
2 CONT ===Catholic Daily Readings===
2 CONT "St Cowair was also known as Cywair. He is the patron saint of Llangower, Wales"  [https://catholicreadings.org/saint-of-the-day-for-july-11/]
2 CONT ===Llangywer: St Cywair's Chrurch===
2 CONT The parish church is consecrated to a local female saint by the name of Cywair. Very little is known about her, but it is believed that an image of her appears in the stained-glass east window of the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a record of this church in the ''Taxatio Ecclesiastica'' of 1291. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxatio_Ecclesiastica]
2 CONT
2 CONT A nearby holy well - Ffynnon Gywair - is concealed by a stone called Llech Cywair. According to a version of the folk legend about a drowned kingdom where Bala Lake is today, the reason for the drowning of the kingdom by the lake 
2 CONC was neglecting to replace this stone. (T. D. Breverton, The Book of Welsh Saints (Glyndŵr publications, 2001), p. 184.)  [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llangywer]
2 CONT ===Lives of the British Saints===
2 CONT '''S. Cywair, Virgin''' page 278  [https://archive.org/details/livesbritishsai02fishgoog/page/278/mode/2up?view=theater&q=Cywair]
