2 CONT Father: Ceneu ap COEL b: 374 in Pennines, Britain
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Spouse Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sawyl "Benisel" ap PABO b: ABT 453 in , South Pennines, Britain
2 CONT
2 CONT  Cerwyd ap PABO b: ABT 455 in , Pennines, Britain
2 CONT
2 CONT  Dunod "Fawr" ap PABO b: ABT 459 in , North York, Britain
2 CONT
2 CONT  Arddun "Benasgell" ferch PABO b: ABT 467 in , Pennines, Britain
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Abbrev: Pedigree Resource File CD 6
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Pedigree Resource File CD 6 (Salt Lake City, UT: Intellectual Reserve, Inc., 1999)serve, Inc., 1999)serve, Inc., 1999).
2 CONT
2 CONT Repository:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ystradwel’s grandson, Pabo Post Pydein, Pabo ‘Pillar of Britain’, the son, according to the earliest genealogical sources, of Ceneu ap Coel Hen joined in the struggle to secure the vulnerable coastal lands. He was the cousin of Ei
2 CONC nion Yrth and it appears likely that prompted by the premature death of his kinsman he came to the assistance of Einion Yrth’s sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pabo’s base in north Wales was strategically situated on a raised ledge of land on a hillside overlooking the broad sweep of the Conwy estuary. The site has a clear, uninterrupted view of the eastern approaches to the Menai Strait
2 CONC , but is itself virtually invisible to anyone on the valley bottom.
2 CONT
2 CONT The enclosure has retained the name Pabo and the narrow track winding down to the estuary and what was once a Roman port is Lôn Pabo, Pabo’s Lane.
2 CONT
2 CONT ‘There was an ancient Tradition in the parish of LanBabo in Anglesey that Pabo with his Son and Daughter were buried in the Churchyard opposite to certain faces that were carv’d in the Wall, and to be seen to this Day. In King Cha
2 CONC rles ye 2nd’s Time or there about (as I was Inform’d) the Sexton happened to dig a Grave agst one of these carved Faces, at about six or seven feet deep, found a flat Grave stone, one corner of wch he pick’d, and demolished a few 
2 CONC letters before he knew what it was. The stone was then remov’d into the Quire, where it hath remained ever since….’
2 CONT
2 CONT                                     Letter from Lewis Morris to Mr. Carte, written c. 1745
2 CONT
2 CONT The late fourteenth century memorial slab erected in honour of the memory of Pabo, Post Pridd, the fifth century founder of the church, is now set upright against the north wall of the Nave. The intricate carving depicts Pabo as a
2 CONC  king of the Middle Ages with crown and sceptre, his head resting upon a cushion. He has a neatly trimmed beard and moustache and his hair hangs down in curls to below the ears.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sandstone slab was defaced and broken probably at the time of the Reformation and the pieces later buried in the graveyard for safekeeping.
2 CONT
2 CONT Llanbabo is situated in the north of Môn close to Llyn Alaw, four miles from the ‘Copper Mountain’, Mynydd Trysglwyn. It is one of the earliest Christian churches to have been founded on the island and the local traditions is that
2 CONC  it was built on or near the site of one of the battles that freed the land from the Irish invaders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pabo’s grandson Deiniol ap Dunod Fwr founded the cathedral at Bangor on Menai.
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother Gwrwst Ledlwm ‘Gwrwst the Ragged’ ap Ceneu is mentioned in Culhwch ac Olwen as having being taken prisoner by Gwyn ap Nudd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunod Fwr ap Pabo was remembered as one of the ‘Three Pillars of Battle’ of Ynys Prydein.  The Annales Cambriae record his death as being in 595, but this seems to be unlikely, unless he lived to a great age, as his son Deiniol di
2 CONC ed in 584. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONC --------------------------------------------------------------------------------St. Pabo Post Prydein, King of the Pennines (c.474-530) (Welsh: Pabo; Latin: Pabius; English: Pabio) Pabo Post Prydein, is most popularly called the s
2 CONC on of King Arthuis, though earlier sources suggest that he was this man's brother. His epithet means the Pillar of Britain, said to derive from his keeping Pictish invaders at bay. Though his Kingdom covered the Pennines of centra
2 CONC l Britain, in later life, Pabo turned to Christianity, abdicated the throne in favour of his sons, Dunaut Bwr and Sawyl Penuchel, and retired to Gwynedd where he founded the church of Llanabo on Ynys Mon (Anglesey). He died there 
2 CONC on 9th November 530 and a beautiful medieval stone slab carved with his image can still be seen covering his grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/226386896/pabo_post-prydain
2 CONT
2 CONT Pabo Post Prydain (supp. fl. before 500) was a king from the Hen Ogledd or Old North of sub-Roman Britain.[1] According to tradition Pabo "the Pillar of Britain" was driven out of the North in 460 and settled in Anglesey.[2] He is
2 CONC  said to have been buried in the area.[1] From the 14th century at least, when a stone cross was erected in the ruler's memory in the abbey's churchyard, Pabo has been identified as its founder, having retired, as many Welsh kings
2 CONC  are said to have done, to a heremitic retreat.
2 CONT The Old Welsh genealogies of British Library, Harleian MS 3859, calls him a son of Cenau son of Coel Hen. Later Welsh genealogies insert two generations by making him son of Arthwys son of Mar son of Cenau son of Coel, though this
2 CONC  presents greater chronological problems.[1] The genealogies give him a royal line of descendants as the father of Dunod Fawr, Sawyl Penuchel and Ardun Benasgell, and a saintly one as the grandfather of Deiniol, Asaph and Tysilio.
2 CONC [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT As to his period, Elis Gruffydd's Chronicle says that his daughter married Maelgwn Gwynedd[3] while an Irish genealogy says that his son "Samuel Chendisel" (the Irish equivalent of Pen-isel) married Deichter, daughter of Muiredach
2 CONC  Muinderg, the king of Ulster. Their son Sanctan founded Kilnasantan in County Dublin after travelling to Ireland with his brother Matóc Ailithir. The Irish Liber Hymnorum confirms that Sanctan and Matóc came to Ireland from Brita
2 CONC in.[4] Pabo must have been roughly the same age as Muiredach, whose death-date (after a reign of 24 years according to other sources) is given in the Annals of Tigernach as 489.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Llanbabo
2 CONT A tradition identifies Pabo as the founder of St Pabo's Church, Llanbabo (at Llanbabo, Anglesey). The first author to record it is antiquarian Henry Rowlands (d. 1723), who writes that "Pabo, frequently called Post Prydain, i.e. t
2 CONC he Support of Britain, for his great valour against the Picts and Scots, retired here [in Anglesey], and built his church at Llan Babo."[1][6] A stone cross was erected in the ruler's memory in the abbey's churchyard at the same t
2 CONC ime Bangor Cathedral was being built. Welsh poet Lewis Morris reports that the memorial cross was discovered there around 1650. The monument, dated to the 14th Century, bears the carved image of a king and an accompanying inscript
2 CONC ion. The inscription is in part illegible but the following reading has been suggested;
2 CONT
2 CONT Hic iacet Pa[bo] Post Priid Co[nf Gr] … [t]el [i]ma[ginem obtulit]
2 CONT "Here lies Pabo the Upholder of Britain, Confessor, Gruffudd ab Ithel offered (this) image"[1]
2 CONT Some scholars argue, in the absence of early evidence, that the tradition is probably spurious[1] though the identity of the historical Pabo who did give his name to the church remains otherwise unknown.
2 CONT
