1 NAME Fragan /ap Selyf/ Prince of Dumnonia
2 GIVN Fragan
2 SURN ap Selyf
2 NSFX Prince of Dumnonia
2 _MARNM ap Selyf
2 NICK Fracan
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 430
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT AI Overview
2 CONT Fragan (or Fracan) ap Selyf was a 5th-century prince of Dumnonia (modern-day Devon and Cornwall) and a Christian saint who migrated to Brittany to escape a plague. He is best known as the father of Saint Winwaloe (Gwenole), the fo
2 CONC under of Landévennec Abbey. 
2 CONT Background and Family
2 CONT Parentage: Some traditions identify him as the son of Selyf of Cornwall and potentially the nephew of King Cadwy of Dumnonia.
2 CONT Wife: He was married to Gwen Teirbron ("Gwen the Three-Breasted"), a noblewoman considered a saint.
2 CONT Children: Fragan and Gwen had three sons—Winwaloe (Gwenole), Wethenoc (Winock), and Jacut—and a daughter, Creirwy.
2 CONT Migration: To escape a plague raging in Dumnonia (often cited around 507 AD), Fragan moved his family to Armorica (Brittany). 
2 CONT Role and Legend
2 CONT Settlement in Brittany: Upon arriving in Brittany, Fragan settled in the region of Léon and founded the village of Ploufragan (near Saint-Brieuc).
2 CONT Military Action: Legend records that Fragan assembled an army to repel pagan pirates at the battle of Lochrist.
2 CONT Legacy: Fragan is venerated as a saint, with a feast day on 3 October in Brittany and 5 July in the Roman calendar. 
2 CONT Disputed Origins
2 CONT While many accounts place him in Dumnonia, other sources suggest Fragan may have been a "Prince of Albany" in Scotland who left to evangelize Armorica. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fragan
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392863938 (February 8, 2023; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Family of Emyr Llydaw; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id305.html (Steven Ferry, October 7, 2022.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kernow (Dumnonia/Cornwall)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Fracan, King of Cerniw (Born c.AD 482) (Welsh: Fragan; Latin: Bracanus; English: Bracan)
2 CONT
2 CONT The name is equivalent to the Welsh Brychan. Certainly a cousin of King Cado of Dumnonia, it seems most likely that Fracan was a younger son of King Salom of Cerniw (Cornwall). He probably inherited his father's kingdom shortly be
2 CONC fore he left Britain, with his wife, Gwen Teirbron (the Triple-Breasted) and their children. A pestilence was spreading through their kingdom around AD 507 and they were forced to flee. They landed on the Gouet Estuary in Brittany
2 CONC  and Fracan established a large estate now named after him, at Ploufragan. He went on to found a church there and at Saint-Frégan (near Lesneven). Hence his saintly reputation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fracan once had a stormy argument with King Riwal Mawr Marchou of Domnonée concerning the speed of their two horses. Fracan organised a race, but there was an accident and his jockey was injured. Luckily however, his son, St. Winw
2 CONC aloe, was on hand to heal the poor lad.
