2 CONT
2 CONT Cadwallon ap Einion (c. 460-534; reigned from c. 500), usually known as Cadwallon Lawhir ('Long Hand') and also called Cadwallon I by some historians, was a king of Gwynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition, Cadwallon ruled during, or shortly after, the Battle of Mons Badonicus, and King Arthur's victory over the Saxons (in either the the early 490s or the mid 510s). Cadwallon's name is not connected with the l
2 CONC egendary battle, but he may have benefitted from the period of relative peace and prosperity throughout Britain that it procured. The most momentous military achievement of Cadwallon's reign was the final expulsion of Irish settle
2 CONC rs on Anglesey, and the re-absorption of that island, which would later become the cultural and political base of the kingdom, into Gwynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadwallon's epithet, Lawhir, may possibly refer to him having longer than usual arms or might also be a metaphor, referring to the extent of his authority. The late medieval poet Iolo Goch claims that he could "reach a stone from 
2 CONC the ground to kill a raven, without bending his back, because his arm was as long as his side to the ground."[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Gildas, Cadwallon's son, Maelgwn Gwynedd, murdered his uncle to ascend to the throne, which suggests that someone other than Maelgwn himself inherited the kingdom upon Cadwallon's death. No clear evidence exists as to
2 CONC  who this "lost king" might be (assuming, of course, that Gildas's account is reliable), but some have suggested the name of Owain Ddantgwyn as the unfortunate heir/victim.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Cadwallon Lawhir ap Einion
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadwallon_ap_Cadfan
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadwallon ap Einion (c. 460-534; reigned from c. 500), also known as Cadwallon Lawhir ('Long Hand') and Cadwallon I, was a king of Gwynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition, Cadwallon ruled during, or shortly after, the Battle of Mons Badonicus, and King Arthur's victory over the Saxons (depending on which date you believe, said battle accorded either sometime between the early
2 CONC  490s and the mid 510s). Although it is unlikely that Cadwallon himself was present at the event, he would likely have benefitted from the period of relative peace and prosperity throughout Britain that it procured. The most momen
2 CONC tous military achievement of Cadwallon's reign was the final expulsion of Irish settlers on Ynys Mon (Anglesey), and the absorption of that island, which would later become the cultural and political base of the kingdom, into Gwyn
2 CONC edd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadwallon's second name, Lawhir, referred to an actual physical characteristic of the man: he apparently had unusually long arms. Iolo Goch claims that he could "reach a stone from the ground to kill a raven, without bending his b
2 CONC ack, because his arm was as long as his side to the ground."
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Gildas, Cadwallon's son, Maelgwn, murdered his uncle to ascend to the throne, which suggests that someone other than Maelgwn himself inherited the kingdom upon Cadwallon's death. No clear evidence exists as to who thi
2 CONC s "lost king" might be (assuming, of course, that Gildas's account is reliable), but some have suggested the name of Owain White Tooth as the unfortunate heir/victim.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Name: Cadwallon Lawhir (Long Hand) KING OF GWYNEDD Given Name: Cadwallon Lawhir (Long Hand) Surname: King of Gwynedd Sex: M Change Date: 13 MAY 2009 Note: !#4568> Welsh Genealogies Ad 300-1400,-v1-p3* (FHL #6025561); !ARCH REC> Wu
2 CONC rts Magna Charta; Plantagenent Ancestry; Ancestral Lines; Royal Ancestors of Magna Charta Barons; (birth 445, death 517); !MISC> said to have expelled the Irish from Anglesey-circa 500; @AFN #QL4TG8; 1 2 Birth: 450 in Gwynedd, Wal
2 CONC es, United Kingdom Death: 517 Ancestral File #: QL4T-G8 Reference Number: > 73 WEL
2 CONT
