1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ..."It has been suggested that Gartnait son of Áedán could be the same person as Gartnait son of Domelch, king of the Picts, whose death is reported in about 601, but this rests on the idea of Pictish matriliny, wh
2 CONC ich has been criticised. Even less certainly, it has been argued that Gartnait's successor in the Pictish king-lists, Nechtan, was his grandson and thus Áedán's great-grandson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Áedán's daughters, only one can be plausibly named. This is Maithgemm, or Gemma, who married a prince named Cairell of the Dál Fiatach. The names of Áedán's wives are not known, but one was said to be British and another may ha
2 CONC ve been a Pictish princess named Domelch, if indeed the Gartnait son of Domelch and Gartnait son of Áedán are one and the same."...
2 CONT
2 CONT https://dlab.epfl.ch/wikispeedia/wpcd/wp/%25/%25C3%2581ed%25C3%25A1n_mac_Gabr%25C3%25A1in.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolcott pokes holes in the assignment of Domlech...not Domelch...as a daughter of Maelgwn Gwynedd, and indeed, the idea that Domlech was even a woman, and more possibly a male figure:
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Bridei and Domlech, "Children" of Maelgwn Gwynedd; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id281.html. (Steven Ferry, July 24, 2021.)
