2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan ap Iago
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan ap Iago (c. 580–625; reigned from c. 615) (Latin: Catamanus; English: Gideon) was a King of Gwynedd. The son of King Iago, he assumed the crown of Gwynedd probably around 615, shortly after the Battle of Caerllion (today's 
2 CONC Chester), during which the forces of Powys were defeated by Æthelfrith of Bernicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan was generally considered to have been a wise and just ruler, noted for his ability to maintain the rule of law and peace during an increasingly hostile period in British history. His memorial stone at Llangadwaladr Church i
2 CONC n Anglesey refers to him as "Catamanus rex sapientisimus opinatisimus omnium regum" (King Cadfan the wisest and most renowned of all kings). He was succeeded by his son Cadwallon.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is one of the last of the legendary kings of Britain as accounted by Geoffrey of Monmouth, who calls him Cadvan and makes him king of the North Welsh, later king of all the Britons."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Cannon, John; Ralph Griffiths (1997). The Oxford Illustrated History of the British Monarchy. Oxford University Press, 8. ISBN 0-19-822786-8. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Dunn, Charles W.; revised translation of Sebastian Evans (1958). History of the Kings of Britain by Geoffrey of Monmouth. New York: E.P. Dutton, Book XI, 13 and XII, 1. ISBN 0-525-47014-X. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Considered a wise & just ruler, noted for his ability to maintain the rule of law & peace during an increasingly hostile period in British history. Buried: Community of Brecon in the co. of Powys. Gravestone states: 'Catamanus rex
2 CONC  sapientisimus opinatisimus omnium regum'. Translated: 'Cadfan, the wisest & most renowned of all kings.' Remarkable memorial, incised with a small cross, was discovered near the church many years ago, & is now built into the chur
2 CONC ch wall.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, 'Kings & Queens of Great Britain'
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadfan_ap_Iago
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Cadfan ap Iago (Latin: Catamanus; English: Gideon) was a King of Gwynedd,. He assumed the crown of Gwynedd probably around 615, shortly after the Battle of Caerllion (today's Chester), during which the forces of Powys (eastern Wal
2 CONC es) were defeated by King Æthelfrith of Bernicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan was generally considered to have been a wise and just ruler, noted for his ability to maintain the rule of law and peace during an increasingly hostile period in British history. His memorial stone at Llangadwaladr Church i
2 CONC n Anglesey refers to him as "Catamanus rex sapientisimus opinatisimus omnium regum" (King Cadfan the wisest and most renowned of all kings). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is one of the last of the legendary Kings of Britain as accounted by Geoffrey of Monmouth, who calls him Cadvan and makes him king of the North Welsh, later king of all the Britons."
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadfan_ap_Iago for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Cadfan ap Iago (c. 580–625; reigned from c. 615) (Latin: Catamanus; English: Gideon) was a King of Gwynedd. The son of King Iago, he assumed the crown of Gwynedd probably around 615, shortly after the Battle of Caerllion (today's 
2 CONC Chester), during which the forces of Powys were defeated by Æthelfrith of Bernicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan was generally considered to have been a wise and just ruler, noted for his ability to maintain the rule of law and peace during an increasingly hostile period in British history. His memorial stone at Llangadwaladr Church i
2 CONC n Anglesey refers to him as "Catamanus rex sapientisimus opinatisimus omnium regum" (King Cadfan the wisest and most renowned of all kings). He was succeeded by his son Cadwallon.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is one of the last of the legendary kings of Britain as recounted by Geoffrey of Monmouth, who calls him Cadvan and makes him king of the North Welsh, later king of all the Britons."[2]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan ap Iago
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan ap Iago (c. 580 – c. 625) was King of Gwynedd (reigned c. 616 – c. 625). Little is known of the history of Gwynedd from this period, and information about Cadfal and his reign is minimal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The historical person is known only from his appearance in royal genealogies, from his grant to Saint Beuno for the monastery at Clynnog Fawr, and from his inscribed gravestone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan was the son and successor of King Iago ap Beli, and is listed in the royal genealogies of the Harleian genealogies and in Jesus College MS. 20.[1][2] Cadfan came to the throne near the time of the Battle of Chester (Welsh: 
2 CONC Gwaith Caerlleon) in 616, in which the Northumbrians under Æthelfrith decisively defeated the neighboring Welsh Kingdom of Powys and then massacred the monks of Bangor Is Coed. However, there is no evidence that Gwynedd had any pa
2 CONC rt in the battle,[3] so Cadfan's accession at that time appears to be no more than coincidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan's gravestone is at Llangadwaladr (English: Cadwaladr's Church) on Anglesey, a short distance from the ancient llys (English: royal court) of the kings of Gwynedd, and reputed to be their royal burial ground. The inscription
2 CONC  refers to him as sapientisimus (English: most wise), and as this term is historically used for ecclesiastics, it suggests that at some point, Cadfan had resigned as king to live out his remaining years as an ecclesiast.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadfan was succeeded as king by his son, Cadwallon ap Cadfal.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Saint Beuno
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Beuno and the monastery at Clynnog Fawr are often cited in conjunction with Cadfan. An 1828 article by P. B. Williams in the The Cymmrodorion cites a manuscript stating that a local prince named 'Gwytheint' gave Clynnog Fawr
2 CONC  to God and Saint Beuno, who was then Abbot at the monastery at Clynnog, and that the donation was free from taxes and obligations forever. It goes on to say that Beuno founded a Convent at Clynnog in 616, and that Cadfan was Beun
2 CONC o's great patron, promising him extensive lands. The promise was carried out by Cadfan's son, King Cadwallon, and that Cadwallon was given a golden sceptre worth 60 cows as a token of acknowledgment.[6] A consistent version is giv
2 CONC en in W. J. Rees' 1853 Lives of the Cambro-British Saints[7] (Rees' was the editor of the 1828 Cymmrodorion that published P. B. Williams' account).
2 CONT
2 CONT There are minor variations of these accounts, sometimes with the details rearranged, such as in Rice Rees' 1836 Essay on the Welsh Saints, where he says that Cadfan (rather than his son Cadwallon) was given the golden sceptre by B
2 CONC euno.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Geoffrey of Monmouth
2 CONT
2 CONT The largely fictional stories of ancient Britain written by Geoffrey of Monmouth use the names of many historical personages as characters, and the use of these names is a literary convenience made in order to advance the plot of 
2 CONC Geoffrey's stories. One of these stories uses the names of Cadfan and other contemporary people, telling of how a certain Edwin spent his exiled youth at the court of King Cadfan, growing up alongside Cadfan's son, the future King
2 CONC  Cadwallon. There is no historical basis for this story, as is readily acknowledged in the preface of works on the subject.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, a "traditional" story arose blending Geoffrey's fiction with known history, implying that the future King Edwin of Northumbria had actually spent his youth at the court of King Cadfan, growing up alongside Cadfan's s
2 CONC on, the future King Cadwallon. In point of fact, Cadwallon and Edwin were enemies with no known youthful connections: King Edwin invaded Gwynedd and drove King Cadwallon into exile, and it would be Cadwallon, in alliance with Pend
2 CONC a of Mercia, who would ultimately defeat and at kill Edwin in 633 at the Battle of Hatfield Chase (Welsh: Gwaith Meigen). The story that they had spent an idyllic youth together may have had a romantic appeal.
2 CONT
2 CONT What is known from history is that in 588 King Ælla of Deira died, and Æthelfrith of Bernicia took the opportunity to invade and conquer Deira, driving Ælla 's 3-year old infant son, the future Edwin of Northumbria, into exile. Ed
2 CONC win would eventually ally himself with Rædwald of East Anglia in 616, defeating and killing Æthelfrith and becoming one of Northumbria's most successful kings. Edwin's life in exile is unknown, and there is no historical basis for
2 CONC  placing him at the court of King Cadfan.[10]
2 CONT
