2 TITL Beli Mawr  and Llyr Llediath in Welsh Pedigrees
1 NOTE OR "TACIT""TEGID (WELSH)"; POSSIBLY SON OF CEIN/CEIONIUS (A WELL-KNOWN ROMAN
2 CONT FAMILY NAME BORNE BY A CONSUL IN 240), SON OF DOLI/DOCIL (i.e. DOCILIS
2 CONT ['TEACHABLE']), SON OF DUMN/DOMN (i.e. DOMINUS), SON OF AMGUERIT (SON OF AN
2 CONT AMBIVARITAN MOTHER), SON OF DUBUN (SON OF A PRINCESS OF THE DOBUNI, A TRIBE
2 CONT SETTLED ABOUT THE HEAD OF THE SEVERN ESTUARY, IN GLOUCESTERSHIRE), SON OF
2 CONT BRITHGUEIN/PRYDEIN (SON OF A PRINCESS OF AN EAST MIDLAND TATOOED TRIBE,
2 CONT CRUITHNI OR CORITANI, OF WHICH THE KYMRIC FORM IS PRYDEIN), SON OF
2 CONT EUGEIN/AVIGENIOS ('OF NOBLE BIRTH'), SON OF ABALLAC/AMALECH ([CALLED BY WILLIAM
2 CONT OF MALMESBURY AVALLOC], BOTH ARCHAIC FORMS OF AFALLACH, WHICH MEANS 'RICH IN
2 CONT APPLES' OR 'APPLE-LANDER', WHO TOOK POSSESSION OF ICTIS), SON OF BELI
2 CONT ('MAGNUS', THE CHIEFTAN OF THE INVADING BELGAE, WHO DESTROYED THE LAKE VILLAGE)
1 NOTE [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Kraentzler 1751; History of Morgan Family; K & Q of Britain.   Kings and Queens of Britain Chart 16 from DeBrett's Peerage (Welsh ancestry of King Henry VII) goes down to King Edward IV as well as Henry VII. K&Q calls him
2 CONC  Tacitus.   History: Tegid or Tacitus was a third century Scottish chieftain who lived near Manaw Gododin on the Firth of Forth. Padarn was one of his sons.
2 CONT    K: Tegidd.   Line down from Tacitus to Gwladus and husband Ralph Mortimer is the same as pedigree printed in Kings and Queens of Britain, which is taken from Debrett's Peerage.   John Duran, a self-appointed ancient genealogies
2 CONC  expert, continues this line back with Tacitus Emperor, RIN 7170. He cites no sources. Have used Kraentzler's Iago as father of Tacitus.   A pedigree chart in Magna Charta, Part V, by John S. Wurts, says the father was Iago. This 
2 CONC chart (a line of descent down from Aedd Mawr, BC 1300, through Beli Mawr and Cunnedda the Great as preserved by the bards of early Welsh records) lists 69 generations, 53 of them above Tegid/Tacitus 15 below him, down to "Ankaret,
2 CONC  who married Rhodri Mawr, King of all Wales, from whom is descended Llewellyn the Great, ancestor of many noble families."
1 NOTE [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Kraentzler 1751; History of Morgan Family; K & Q of Britain.   Kings and Queens of Britain Chart 16 from DeBrett's Peerage (Welsh ancestry of King Henry VII) goes down to King Edward IV as well as Henry VII. K&Q calls him
2 CONC  Tacitus.   History: Tegid or Tacitus was a third century Scottish chieftain who lived near Manaw Gododin on the Firth of Forth. Padarn was one of his sons.
2 CONT    K: Tegidd.   Line down from Tacitus to Gwladus and husband Ralph Mortimer is the same as pedigree printed in Kings and Queens of Britain, which is taken from Debrett's Peerage.   John Duran, a self-appointed ancient genealogies
2 CONC  expert, continues this line back with Tacitus Emperor, RIN 7170. He cites no sources. Have used Kraentzler's Iago as father of Tacitus.   A pedigree chart in Magna Charta, Part V, by John S. Wurts, says the father was Iago. This 
2 CONC chart (a line of descent down from Aedd Mawr, BC 1300, through Beli Mawr and Cunnedda the Great as preserved by the bards of early Welsh records) lists 69 generations, 53 of them above Tegid/Tacitus 15 below him, down to "Ankaret,
2 CONC  who married Rhodri Mawr, King of all Wales, from whom is descended Llewellyn the Great, ancestor of many noble families."
1 NOTE [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Kraentzler 1751; History of Morgan Family; K & Q of Britain.   Kings and Queens of Britain Chart 16 from DeBrett's Peerage (Welsh ancestry of King Henry VII) goes down to King Edward IV as well as Henry VII. K&Q calls him
2 CONC  Tacitus.   History: Tegid or Tacitus was a third century Scottish chieftain who lived near Manaw Gododin on the Firth of Forth. Padarn was one of his sons.
2 CONT    K: Tegidd.   Line down from Tacitus to Gwladus and husband Ralph Mortimer is the same as pedigree printed in Kings and Queens of Britain, which is taken from Debrett's Peerage.   John Duran, a self-appointed ancient genealogies
2 CONC  expert, continues this line back with Tacitus Emperor, RIN 7170. He cites no sources. Have used Kraentzler's Iago as father of Tacitus.   A pedigree chart in Magna Charta, Part V, by John S. Wurts, says the father was Iago. This 
2 CONC chart (a line of descent down from Aedd Mawr, BC 1300, through Beli Mawr and Cunnedda the Great as preserved by the bards of early Welsh records) lists 69 generations, 53 of them above Tegid/Tacitus 15 below him, down to "Ankaret,
