2 CONT 660-673: King of Dal Riata [Ref: Tapsell Dynasties p180] King of Scots of Argyll [Ref: Moncreiffe RoyalAnc p20]
2 CONT
2 CONT did not reign [Ref: Weis AR7 #170]
2 CONT
2 CONT succeeded to kingship in 660, when the joint kingship of his uncle Conall Crandomna and Dunchad son of Duban ended with Conall's death [Ref: Michael Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT nothing about Domongart's reign is mentioned in the sources until he was killed in 673, and succeeded by his cousin Maelduin [Ref: Michael Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT Domangart mac Domnaill (died 673) was a king in Dál Riata (modern western Scotland) and the son of Domnall Brecc. It is not clear whether he was over-king of Dál Riata or king of the Cenél nGabráin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domangart is not listed by the Duan Albanach but is included in other sources, such as genealogies of William the Lion, and that of Causantín mac Cuilén found with the Senchus fer n-Alban. In these genealogies he is noted as the f
2 CONC ather of Eochaid mac Domangairt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annals of Ulster for 673 report: "The killing of Domangart, son of Domnall Brecc, the king of Dál Riata." Some king-lists state that in his time the Cenél Comgaill separated from the Cenél nGabráin.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not clear who succeeded Domangart as king of Dál Riata, if he was such, or as king of the Cenél nGabráin. Known kings after Domangart include Máel Dúin mac Conaill and Domnall Donn of the Cenél nGabráin and Ferchar Fota of t
2 CONC he Cenél Loairn is assigned a long reign of 21 years by the Duan Albanach and other king-lists, and this would place the beginning of his rule close to the death of Domangart.
2 CONT
