2 CONT
2 CONT ************************ ''The following information is copied from Wikipedia; it shows up in other places on the web.  No citations.  Please do not change the dates for Gwrgan to match this stub, as chronologically it does not wo
2 CONC rk.  See http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id24.html; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id24.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Gwrgan Fawr, King of Ergyng (Died c.AD 645) (Latin: Gurgantius; English: Fergus)
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Gwrgan probably fled Ergyng when his inheritance was seized by Gwrfoddw Hen around AD 619. Perhaps he was out of favour with the latter's nephew, High-King Arthur; although the chronology doesn't quite fit. He appears in fo
2 CONC ur charters in the Book of Llandaff during the episcopate of Bishops Euddogwy and Inabwy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little else is known of this monarch, despite his epithet, the Great. Overlordship of Glywysing & Gwent may, however, be indicated if he is identified with Gwrgan Frych (the Freckled) who features in the Life of St. Cadog. This po
2 CONC werful lord granted the saint half the fishing rights on the Rivers Usk and Neath in return for the great sword of King Rhun Hir of Gwynedd and a fine new horse with all the trappings. Of course, St. Cadog died some years before G
2 CONC wrgan's reign, but the story may relate to a later agreement with the saint's successors at Llancarfan Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwrgan ruled until about AD 645 when his sons, Caradog and Morgan, should have inherited the throne of Ergyng. It appears, however, that instead, his son-in-law, King Meurig of Glywysing & Gwent managed to seize power in the name 
2 CONC of his own son, Athrwys.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.earlybritishkingdoms.com/bios/gwrgafeg.html
