2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Royal Family of Gwynedd - Ancestry of Cynan Dindaethwy; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id16.html. (Steven Ferry, November 29, 2019.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Medieval "Redating" of Braint Hir; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id64.html. (Steven Ferry, July 5, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Bradwen of Llys Bradwen, in Meirionydd; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id195.html. (Steven Ferry, August 31, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Harleian Ms 3859; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id129.html. (Steven Ferry, March 8, 2021.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Interim Kings of Gwynedd's 1st Dynasty; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id306.html (Steven Ferry, October 19, 2022.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Cynan Dindaethwy - Furter Notes; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id307.html (Steven Ferry, October 21, 2022.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Iago ap Beli (c. 560-613; reigned from c. 599) (Latin: Iacobus; English: James) was King of Gwynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most notable feature of his reign (of which little information exists) is the growing strength of the nearby kingdoms of Mercia, under the rule of Pybba and his son Penda, and of Bernicia, under Æthelfrith. Some evidence exist
2 CONC s to suggest that Iago may have entered into some sort of alliance with Pybba, trading the latter's promise not to attack Gwynedd in exchange for Gwynedd's support against Northumbria, should that support be needed. In 604, after 
2 CONC being driven from his throne by Æthelfrith, Edwin, king of Deira, may have sought refuge at the Iago's court. Perhaps as a result, Æthelfrith turned his wrath against Gwynedd, killing the monks at a monastery at Bangor, and finall
2 CONC y facing the forces of Gwynedd and Powys at the Battle of Chester in 613.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known whether or not Iago took part in this battle: he may have done so, perishing amidst the slaughter, or he may have already abdicated his throne in favor of his son, Cadfan, and died later that year in a monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, 'Kings & Queens of Great Britain'
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Iago ap Beli
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Iago ap Beli (c. 560 –[citation needed] c. 616) was King of Gwynedd (reigned c. 599 – c. 616). Little is known of him or his kingdom from this early era, with only a few anecdotal mentions of him in historical documents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iago ap Beli (English: Jacob son of Beli) was the son and successor of King Beli ap Rhun, and is listed in the royal genealogies of the Harleian genealogies and in Jesus College MS. 20.[1][2] The only other record of him is the no
2 CONC te of his death, which occurred in the same year as the Battle of Chester (Welsh: Gwaith Caer Lleon), with no connection between Iago's death and the famous battle,[3] and with no evidence that Gwynedd had any part in the battle.[
2 CONC 4] He would be succeeded as king by his son, Cadfan ap Iago.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1766 publication of Henry Rowlands's Mona Antiqua Restaurata says that the archives of the cathedral at Bangor mention Iago as having founded a deanery there (' Iago ap Beli Rex Decanatu Ecclesiam ditavit ').[5] However, corre
2 CONC ctness of the archive's assertion is challenged in Haddan and Stubbs' authoritative Councils and Ecclesiastical Documents Relating to Great Britain and Ireland, where it is noted that "the earliest historical testimony to a Dean a
2 CONC t Bangor is 1162".[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the medieval Welsh Triads, the death of King Iago ap Beli is described as the result of an axe-blow by one of his own men, a certain Cadafael Wyllt (English: Cadafael the Wild).[7] In his Celtic Britain, John Rhys notes that th
2 CONC e Annals of Tigernach mention Iago's death and use the word dormitat (or dormitato, meaning sleep in the sense of a euphemism for death), contradicting the notion of a violent death. Further, as the word dormitato was generally us
2 CONC ed in reference to clerics, it is possible that Iago resigned his kingship and thereafter led a clerical life.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Geoffrey of Monmouth
2 CONT
2 CONT The largely fictional stories of ancient Britain written by Geoffrey of Monmouth use the names of many historical personages as characters, and the use of these names is a literary convenience made in order to advance the plot of 
2 CONC Geoffrey's stories. One of these stories uses the names of Iago's son Cadfan and other contemporary people, telling of how a certain Edwin spent his exiled youth in Gwynedd, growing up alongside Iago's grandson, the future King Ca
2 CONC dwallon. There is no historical basis for this story, as is readily acknowledged in the preface of works on the subject.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, a "traditional" story arose blending Geoffrey's fiction with known history, implying that the future King Edwin of Northumbria had actually spent his youth in Gwynedd, growing up alongside Iago's grandson, the future
2 CONC  King Cadwallon. In point of fact, Cadwallon and Edwin were enemies with no known youthful connections: King Edwin invaded Gwynedd and drove King Cadwallon into exile, and it would be Cadwallon, in alliance with Penda of Mercia, w
2 CONC ho would ultimately defeat and at kill Edwin in 633 at the Battle of Hatfield Chase (Welsh: Gwaith Meigen). The story that they had spent an idyllic youth together may have had a romantic appeal.
2 CONT
2 CONT What is known from history is that in 588 King Ælla of Deira died, and Æthelfrith of Bernicia took the opportunity to invade and conquer Deira, driving Ælla 's 3-year old infant son, the future Edwin of Northumbria, into exile. Ed
2 CONC win would eventually ally himself with Rædwald of East Anglia in 616, defeating and killing Æthelfrith and becoming one of Northumbria's most successful kings. Edwin's life in exile is unknown, and there is no historical basis for
2 CONC  placing him in Gwynedd.[10]
2 CONT
