1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peada_of_Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peada (?) (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #173785, d. April 656
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=27 Nov 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT      Peada (?) was the son of Penda, King of Mercia and Cynewise (?). (1), (2) He married Alhflæd (?), daughter of Oswiu, King of Northumbria, circa 653. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in April 656, murdered. (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT      In 653 he converted to Christianity, and the Middle Angles with him. (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p17379.htm#i173785
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peada_of_Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peada of Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Peada (died 656), a son of Penda, was briefly King of southern Mercia after his father's death in November 655[1] until his own death in the spring of the next year.
2 CONT
2 CONT In about the year 653, Peada was made king of the Middle Angles by his father. Bede, describing Peada as "an excellent youth, and most worthy of the title and person of a king", wrote that he sought to marry Alchflaed, the daughte
2 CONC r of King Oswiu of Northumbria; Oswiu, however, made this conditional upon Peada's baptism and conversion to Christianity, along with the Middle Angles (Peada was, at this time, still a pagan, like his father). Bede says that Pead
2 CONC a eagerly accepted conversion:
2 CONT
2 CONT When he heard the preaching of truth, the promise of the heavenly kingdom, and the hope of resurrection and future immortality, he declared that he would willingly become a Christian, even though he should be refused the virgin; b
2 CONC eing chiefly prevailed on to receive the faith by King Oswy's son Aifrid, who was his relation and friend, and had married his sister Cyneburga, the daughter of King Penda.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peada was subsequently baptized by Finan of Lindisfarne, and this was followed by a campaign to convert Peada's people:
2 CONT
2 CONT Accordingly he was baptized by Bishop Finan, with all his earls and soldiers, and their servants, that came along with him, at a noted village belonging to the king, called At the Wall. And having received four priests, who for th
2 CONC eir erudition and good life were deemed proper to instruct and baptize his nation, he returned home with much joy. These priests were Cedd and Adda, and Betti and Diuma; the last of whom was by nation a Scot, the others English. A
2 CONC dda was brother to Utta, ... a renowned priest, and abbot of the monastery of Gateshead. The aforesaid priests, arriving in the province with the prince, preached the word, and were willingly listened to; and many, as well of the 
2 CONC nobility as the common sort, renouncing the abominations of idolatry, were baptized daily.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 15, 655,[1] Oswiu defeated and killed Penda at the Battle of the Winwaed, and consequently he came to exercise power in Mercia. According to Bede, Oswiu allowed Peada to rule the southern part of Mercia; southern Merci
2 CONC a consisted of 5,000 families, Bede reports, while northern Mercia was populated by 7,000 families, and the two were divided by the River Trent.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Peada helped found the monastery at Peterborough:
2 CONT
2 CONT In his time they came together, [Peada] and Oswy, brother of King Oswald, and declared that they wished to establish a minster in praise of Christ and in honor of St Peter. And they did so, and gave it the name Medeshamstede, beca
2 CONC use there is a spring there called Medeswael. And then they began the foundations and built upon them, and then entrusted it to a monk who was called Seaxwulf. He was a great friend of God, and all people loved him, and he was ver
2 CONC y nobly born in the world and powerful. He is now much more powerful with Christ.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the Chronicle continues, "Peada ruled no length of time, because he was betrayed by his own queen at Eastertide";[4] Bede also reports that Peada was "very wickedly killed" through his wife's treachery "during the very ti
2 CONC me of celebrating Easter" in 656.[3]
