1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Œthelwald of Deira
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia  "Œthelwald was a King of Deira (651–c. 655). He was the son of King Oswald of Northumbria, who was killed at the Battle of Maserfield in 642.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Oswine of Deira was killed by Oswiu of Bernicia in 651, Œthelwald became king; it is uncertain whether Oswiu (who was Œthelwald's uncle) installed him as king or whether Œthelwald took the kingship in opposition to Oswiu.[1]
2 CONC  He subsequently allied himself with Oswiu's enemy, Penda of Mercia, and assisted Penda during his invasion of Northumbria in 655. However, when the armies of Oswiu and Penda met on 15 November at the Battle of the Winwaed, Œthelw
2 CONC ald withdrew his forces. Penda was defeated and killed,[2] perhaps in part because of this desertion, and afterward Œthelwald seems to have lost Deira to Alchfrith, who was installed there by the victorious Oswiu.
2 CONT Œthelwald's fate is unknown, as nothing is formally recorded of him after the battle. Local tradition, however, held that he became a hermit in Kirkdale, North Yorkshire.' A clarification of this tradition comes from  http://www.o
2 CONC rmerod.uk.net/Places/Kirkdale/placekirkdale.htm a description of Kirkdale's history   "One of these (churches), upon which is a fine cross, was said, a century ago, to bear the inscription in runic characters, 'Cyning Æthilwald,' 
2 CONC but no writing is now decipherable. This inscription was one of the foundations of the theory that Kirkdale was the true site of the monastery founded by S. Cedd in the 7th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT S. Cedd, an Angle of Northumbria, was one of a family of missionary priests all educated at Lindisfarne under S. Aidan, and Cedd preached first to the Middle Angles and then to the East Saxons. 
2 CONT The Venerable Bede writes of him:
2 CONT 'The same man of God, whilst he was bishop among the East Saxons, was also wont several times to visit his own country, Northumberland, to make exhortations. Ethelwald, the son of King Oswald, who reigned among the Deiri, finding 
2 CONC him a holy, wise and good man, desired him to accept some land to build a monastery, to which the king himself might frequently resort, to offer his prayers and hear the word, and be buried in it when he died; for he believed that
2 CONC  he should receive much benefit by the prayers of those who were to serve God in that place.'  That prelate, therefore, complying with the king's desires, chose himself a place to build a monastery among craggy and distant mountai
2 CONC ns, which looked more like lurking places for robbers and retreats for wild beasts, than habitations for men."  (This puts doubt in the accepted date of death for  Œthelwald). Although he allied himself with the pagan Penda, Œthel
2 CONC wald was a pious Christian and was remembered for his generosity towards St. Cedd, to whom he granted land for a monastery." See Eadwulf II king of Northumbria 796-810 whom many see as the descendant of the Eoppan kings of Northum
2 CONC bria. In 807-8 Eadwulf spent some time in exile and has now been found to have dwelt temporarily in those "craggy...more like lurking places for robbers and wild beasts."  Those places had been set up by St. Cedd for the benefit o
2 CONC f the king and Ethelwald was connected to those holy places as was King Eadwulf II, as is believed by the English archeologists. See article at https://www.cnn.com/travel/article/anglo-saxon-cave-house-scli-intl-gbr/index.html fro
2 CONC m CNN.
2 CONT Œthelwald is the Son of Saint Oswald, King of Northumbria and Cyneburg. St. Oswald goes back to Kings Ida and Eoppa of Northumbria. His wife was a daughter of the lines descending from the House of Wessex. St. Oswald attended the 
2 CONC baptism of King of Wessex Cynegils, as his godfather in 635 because St. Oswald was considered the primary influence upon the House of Wessex to become Christians. Then Cynegils gave his daughter Cyneburg to marry St. Oswald upon t
2 CONC he occasion of his becoming a Christian (Cynegils). See: 
2 CONT https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PL_pHinx0Ms   for information on the marriage of St. Oswald and Cyneburg as well as the son of the marriage, Œthelwald.(at just before minute 7). The same Youtube url also states that Œthelwald in 1
2 CONC 651 was crowned King of Deira and then had a short reign until 655 although he was said to be too young to rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The primary sources are: ‘’Anglo-Saxon Chronicle’’ (A) : Cottonian Fragment (British Museum, Cotton MS. Otho B xi, 2); ‘’Anglo-Saxon Chronicle’’ (E) : The Laud (or "Peterborough") Chronicle (Bodleian, MS. Laud 636).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Canterbury manuscript gives the ancestry of Ida, king of Mercia as Ida, son of Eoppa, son of Esa, son of Ingui, son of Angenwit, son of Aloc, son of Benoc, son of Brand, son of Beldeg, son of Woden (‘’Anglo-Saxon Chronicle’’ (
2 CONC F) : The Canterbury Bi-Lingual Epitome, British Library MS Cotton Domitian Aviii, ff.30-70). This chart and source has been compared with another major source for the ancient British royal family named: "The Royal Line of Successi
2 CONC on" dated 1972 from author Patrick W. Montague-Smith, Editor, Debrett's Peerage. It gives the accepted British Royal Line since Brand or Brond, father of Frithogar and Bernic (or Bennoc). Geni accepts the two lines as far back as 
2 CONC Brand. 
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#_Toc389126250
