2 CONC ests that Rhodri successfully invaded and occupied Dumnonia for a time, before being expelled by the Saxons. This story may indeed be apocryphal, and others have suggested that Rhodri instead focused on strengthening the island of
2 CONC  Anglesey, which, by this time, had become the stronghold of the kingdom. This argument is based on the understanding that Æthelbald, King of Mercia and self-styled Bretwalda, was continuing to press the kingdoms of Wales, and tha
2 CONC t Rhodri would have been far more likely to have been forced to adopt a defensive, rather than offensive, policy in dealing with incursions from Mercia and other Anglo-Saxon principalities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri married Margaret ferch Duplory, an Irish princess, who bore him a son by the name of Cynan Dindaethwy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of the limited reliable information surrounding this period in Welsh history, it is not entirely clear who succeeded Rhodri upon his death, although the most likely candidate seems to be Caradog ap Meirion, a distant cousi
2 CONC n, and not his son, who would ascend to the throne only after the death of Caradog.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodri_Molwynog_ap_Idwal for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodri_Molwynog_ap_Idwal
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rhodri Molwynog ap Idwal
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri ap Idwal (c. 690-c. 754; reigned from c. 720) (Latin: Rodericus; English: Roderick), also known as Rhodri Molwynog ('the Bald and Gray'), and by some contemporary historians as Rhodri I, was a king of Gwynedd, referred to a
2 CONC s a king of the Britons by the Annals of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Idwal Iwrch and his wife, Princess Angharad of Brittany, comparatively little is known of Rhodri's life or accomplishments. There is even some debate as to when he actually assumed the throne of Gwynedd, with the years 
2 CONC 712, 720, 722 or 730 being the most probable candidates. Legend suggests that Rhodri successfully invaded and occupied Dumnonia for a time, before being expelled by the Saxons. This story may indeed be apocryphal, and others have 
2 CONC suggested that Rhodri instead focused on strengthening the island of Anglesey, which, by this time, had become the stronghold of the kingdom. This argument is based on the understanding that Æthelbald, king of Mercia and self-styl
2 CONC ed Bretwalda, was continuing to press the kingdoms of Wales, and that Rhodri would have been far more likely to have been forced to adopt a defensive, rather than offensive, policy in dealing with incursions from Mercia and other 
2 CONC Anglo-Saxon principalities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri married Margaret ferch Duplory, an Irish princess, who bore him a son by the name of Cynan Dindaethwy. Because of the limited reliable information surrounding this period in Welsh history, it is not entirely clear who succe
2 CONC eded Rhodri upon his death, although the most likely candidate seems to be Caradog ap Meirion, a distant cousin, and not his son, who would ascend to the throne only after the death of Caradog.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri Molwynog ab Idwal, Brenin Gwynedd1,2,3
2 CONT
2 CONT b. circa 690, d. circa 754
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Idwal Iwrch ap Cadwaladr, Brenin Gwynedd4,2 b. circa 660, d. 712
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Afadda verch Alain de Bretagne1,2 b. circa 660
2 CONT
2 CONT      Rhodri Molwynog ab Idwal, Brenin Gwynedd also went by the name of Rhodri "the Bald and Gray". He was born circa 690.1 He was the son of Idwal Iwrch ap Cadwaladr, Brenin Gwynedd and Afadda verch Alain de Bretagne.4,2,1 Rhodri 
2 CONC Molwynog ab Idwal, Brenin Gwynedd was the successor of Idwal Iwrch ap Cadwaladr, Brenin Gwynedd; King of Gwynedd.5 King of Gwynedd at Wales between 712 and 754. Rhodri Molwynog ab Idwal, Brenin Gwynedd married Margaret of Ireland 
2 CONC before 720.1 Rhodri Molwynog ab Idwal, Brenin Gwynedd died circa 754.1,6,2,7,8 Chronicle of Ystrad Fflur 754: "In this year Rhodri, king of the Britons, died."9
2 CONT
