2 CONC ic was Frithuwald's son. 4.Jump up ^ Blair, "Frithuwold's kingdom", p. 106. 5.Jump up ^ Blair, "Frithuwold's kingdom", pp. 106–107; Yorke, p. 110. See also Blair, "Chertsey resting-place list", pp. 231–233, fig. 17.1 and table 1. 
2 CONC 6.Jump up ^ "S 1165". Retrieved 2007-10-06. 7.Jump up ^ "S 1181". Retrieved 2007-10-06. 8.Jump up ^ "S 69". Retrieved 2007-10-06. 9.Jump up ^ "S 27". Retrieved 2007-10-06. 10.Jump up ^ "S 752". Retrieved 2007-10-06. 11.Jump up ^ F
2 CONC or further discussion of Frithuwald's charters and their context, see Blair, "Frithuwold's kingdom", pp. 97–105. 12.Jump up ^ Kirby, p. 115; Yorke, pp. 108 & 120. Yorke notes the possible link between Wigheard and the later Mercia
2 CONC n "W-group" family represented by Wiglaf and Wigstan. One of these three is thought to be the ruler of the Sonningas—around Sonning in south-east Berkshire—see Kirby, p.116. 13.Jump up ^ Blair, "Frithuwold's kingdom", passim, espe
2 CONC cially pp. 105–107; Kirby, pp. 115–116; but see also Briggs, "Finding the Fullingadic". 14.Jump up ^ Patricia Reynolds, 'Frithuwold and the Farmer', Mallorn (the journal of the Tolkien Society), issue 28, 1991, p.7-11
2 CONT
