1 NOTE ÆTHELHERE [Æthelric] (-killed in battle Winwæd, near Leeds Autumn 654).  Bede names Æthelhere as brother and successor of King Anna[75].  Nennius names "Edric" as son of Eni, presumably indicating the same person called Æthelhere 
2 CONC by Bede as he also says that he was the father of Aldwulf[76].  According to the genealogy in the Anglian collection, the name of Hereswith's husband was Æthelric[77].  He succeeded his brother in 654 as ÆTHELHERE King of the East
2 CONC  Angles.  He supported Penda King of Mercia in the latter's invasion of Northumbria, and was killed in battle "near the river Vinwed" with Penda[78].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle names "Æthelhere brother of Anna king of East Anglia"
2 CONC  as one of the thirty princes slain with Penda by Oswy at "Winwidfeld" in 654[79].  m (repudiated 647) HERESWITH, daughter of HERERIC of Deira [Northumbria] & his wife Bregesuit.  Bede names "her sister [of Hilda, daughter of Here
2 CONC ric] Heresuid mother to Aldwulf king of the East Angles", specifying that she lived for a year in the same monastery as her sister "with the design of going abroad"[80].  According to the genealogy in the Anglian collection, the n
2 CONC ame of Hereswith's husband was Æthelric[81].  After being repudiated by her husband, she became a nun at the convent of Chelles near Paris in [647].
1 NOTE KING OF EAST ANGLIA 654
