1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT King of East Anglia 570-578; First East Anglian king, a powerful Saxon noble
2 CONT who landed in East Anglia and began the kingdom there. His father was Wehha, a
2 CONT Saxon noble most definitely decended from Woden.
2 CONT He was succeeded on his death in 578 by his son Tytil.
1 NOTE KING OF EAST ANGLIA ca. 571-ca. 578
1 NOTE He came to East Anglia in 512.
1 NOTE He came to East Anglia in 512.
1 NOTE Bede refers to the family of the kings of the East Angles as Wuffingas, but gives no information about Wuffa, their first reputed king, or Tytilus his son apart from their names [Bede, Book II, Chapter XV.].  These first two alleg
2 CONC ed kings are not mentioned in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, in which references to the kings of East Anglia are limited to brief mentions of Rædwald, Eorpwald, Anna, Æthelberht and (St) Edmund.   it is possible that Wuffa and his imm
2 CONC ediate successors were semi-historical figures.  The early 7th century King Rædwald is the first king of the East Angles about whom the sources give any information other than his name.  The only surviving regnal lists are those i
2 CONC n 12th century sources such as William of Malmesbury and Florence of Worcester.  The genealogy of Ælfwald King of the East Angles provided by the latter is inconsistent with the version which has survived in the Anglian collection
2 CONC .  The early 9th century Historia Brittonum of Nennius sets out a mythical descent of Wuffa from Woden: "Woden/Casser/Titinon/Trigil/Rodmunt/Rippa/Guillem Guercha/Uffa"[Nennius, 59, p. 26.].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle gives no anc
2 CONC estry of the kings of East Anglia.  There are many gaps in our knowledge of the history of the kingdom of East Anglia.  For example, nothing is known about any East Anglian kings after the reign of King Aldwulf, alleged to have su
2 CONC cceeded in 664, until King Selræd in 747.  The succession of the kings who succeeded Selræd and the relationships if any between them are uncertain.  East Anglia fell under Mercian domination in the mid-8th century.  Although the 
2 CONC kingdom of East Anglia survived until 869, when King Edmund was killed by the Danes, no pre-Viking charters have survived presumably because the invaders destroyed the religious houses which acted as repositories for such document
2 CONC ation[Yorke, p. 20.]    The information from which the genealogy of the kings of East Anglia is reconstructed is therefore sparse and unreliable.  Edward "the Elder" King of Wessex conquered East Anglia in [918/21] and expelled th
2 CONC e Danes[Malmesbury I, 97, p. 79.].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WUFFA, son of [WEHHA] (-578).  Bede names Wuffa as first king of the East Angles but gives no other details of his life[25].  A mythical descent of Wuffa from Woden is set out in the early 9th century Historia Brittonum of Nennius
2 CONC , as shown above[26].  According to 13th century chronicle of Roger of Wendover, Wuffa's accession is dated to 571 and his death to 578[27].  He is not named in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle and there must be considerable doubt about 
2 CONC his historical existence at all.
