1 NOTE {geni:about_me}   Eadwulf I was king of Northumbria from death of Aldfrith in December 704 until February or March of 705, when Aldfrith's son Osred was restored to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Osred was a child when his father died, and it is assumed that Eadwulf usurped the throne. Eadwulf's relationship, if any, to the ruling dynasty descendants of Ida is not known, but it is quite possible that he was indeed of royal
2 CONC  descent as two or more other branches of the Eoppingas are found as kings of Northumbria after the extinction of the main line which was  composed of Oswald, King of Northumbria 634-642 who married Cyneburh, daughter of Cynegils 
2 CONC who became a Christian and ruled Wessex 611-643. King Aldfrid was one branch of the Eoppings descending from Ida, King of Bernicia bc515. He was also associated by marriage to the Eoppings of Wessex through his marriage to the aun
2 CONC t of Eoppa of Wessex bc680, Cuthburh, Foundress of Wimborne Abbey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eadwulf I's son, Earnwine, was killed on the orders of his cousin,Eadberht of Northumbria in 740. Eadberht was the son of Eata, the son of Leodwald, and they each were descendants of Eoppa and Ida.  Eadwulf's great-grandson Eardwu
2 CONC lf II and Eardwulf's son Eadwulf III were later kings of Northumbria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Initially Eadwulf appears to have had the support of ealdorman Berhtfrith son of Berhtred, presumed to be the lord of the north-east frontier of Bernicia, in Lothian and along the Forth. However, a crisis soon arose. Bishop Wilfri
2 CONC d, exiled by Aldfrith, wished to return to Northumbria. Eadwulf aimed to keep the bishop an exile, but Berhtfirth appears to have supported Wilfrid's return. A short civil war, ending with a siege of Bamburgh, was won by Berhtfrit
2 CONC h, Wilfrid and the supporters of Osred, and Osred was restored as child-king of Northumbria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eadwulf appears to have been exiled to either Dál Riata or Pictland as his death is reported by the Annals of Ulster in 717.  Dál Riata was a Gaelic kingdom that encompassed the western seaboard of Scotland and the north-eastern c
2 CONC orner of Ireland, on each side of the Irish Sea. At Dal Riata's height in the 6th and 7th centuries, it covered what is now Argyll in Scotland and part of County Antrim in Northern Ireland. This resulted from the later invasion of
2 CONC   Celtic peoples into the Limerick, Thomond area pushing the earlier Gaelic peoples to the northeast into Ulster and then Scotland along with Northumbria. These Norse-Gaelics-Celtics then eventually formed the foundation for the p
2 CONC eople of Scotland and some of Northumbria as well as Cumbria."The royal house of Scotland was founded by Fergus MacErc, a Christian Irish chieftain, who left Dal Riata in the north of Antrim for Argyll about 503. The Kings gradual
2 CONC ly increased their territory, and Kenneth MacAlpin united the Picts and Scots under his rule in 843." (This historical section is quoted from "The Royal Line of Succession" Editor Patrick W. Montague-Smith, Debrett's Peerage page 
2 CONC 21.) How the Saxons came to be mixed in with the Celtics and Gaelics is still left to be explained. Maybe a prominent Saxon daughter would marry a Celtic chieftain and then change their names to Saxon names because they were in No
2 CONC rthumberland. Some of these names existed then in Wales as well since it was so close. The Germanic Dealbhna people of Ireland had some family branches which moved over to Wales and used the names Eadwine, Edwin, and Earnwine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much research has looked into the possible descent of Eadwulf from the Eoppan kings of Northumberland who ruled during the era of the son of Esa named Eoppa bc487 and Eoppa's son Ida who died 559ad. Ida had 5 sons that we know of:
2 CONC  Aethelric b537, Ocg b539, Adda of Bernicia d569, Theodoric b546 and Alric bc550. Aethelric's great grandson, Aldfrid ruled Northumbria 685-705 and married Cuthburh, an aunt of Eoppa bc665 who was a descendant of Cerdic rather tha
2 CONC n Esa. Aldfrid's descendant's line died out within a few years and his son was a very young boy when Aldfrid died. So Eadwulf, a relative of Aldfrid, usurped the throne of Northumberland for a short while. This Eadwulf line had 5 
2 CONC Eadwulfs succeeding to rule in Northumbria over a 247 year period. Disregarding this Eadwulf I's descent from any one of the sons of King Ida, Eadwulf's line by c860 for sure descended from two of the sons of Ida, Ocg and Alric. T
2 CONC his is because Eadwulf first Earl of Bamburgh married Alchfleda. Her father, Alchred, was the son of Eahlwine, the son of Byrnhorn who was a descendant of Ida's son, Alric. Then Alchfleda's mother named Osgyfu bc790 was a daughter
2 CONC  of King Oswulf, whose father was King Eadberht b710, the son of Eata who was the son of Leodwald. The latter's lineage went back to King Ida via his son, Ocg b539. 
2 CONT Source:  http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#_Toc389126250
2 CONT
2 CONT Leodwald's grandson through Eadwulf I, Earnwine, was killed on the orders of Eadberht of Northumbria in 740. Eadberht was the son of Eata, the son of Leodwald, and they each were descendants of Eoppa and Ida through Ocg. Since Ead
2 CONC wulf I is also a descendant of Ocg and Ida, the killing of Eadwulf's son, Earnwine, could have been caused by family rivalry, something like what happened in the reigns of Edward IV and Richard III of England. Eadwulf's great-gran
2 CONC dson Eardwulf II and Eardwulf's son Eadwulf III were later kings of Northumbria.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Back to the the possible descent of Eadwulf from the Eoppan kings of Northumberland who ruled during the era of the son of Esa named Eoppa bc487 and Eoppa's son Ida who died 559ad. Ida had 5 sons that we know of: Aethelric b537, O
2 CONC cg b539, Adda of Bernicia d569, Theodoric b546 and Alric bc550. Aethelric's great grandson, Aldfrid ruled Northumbria 685-705 and married Cuthburh, an aunt of Eoppa bc665 who was a descendant of Cerdic rather than Esa. Aldfrid's d
2 CONC escendant's line died out within a few years and his son was a very young boy when Aldfrid died. So Eadwulf, a relative of Aldfrid, usurped the throne of Northumberland for a short while.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidence for the ancestry of Eadwulf I coming from Ida and Ocg follows by refering to Eadwulf's 3x great grandson, Eadwulf Earl of Bamburgh's known family history.   This Eadwulf joined with King Alfred the Great of Wessex and was
2 CONC  made the first Earl of Bamburgh. Eadwulf the Earl of Bamburgh has now been seen to be married to a daughter, Alchfleda, from the Alric lineage, son of King Ida. Plus it is reasoned by researchers that Eadwulf the Earl himself was
2 CONC  descended from the line of Ocg who was the brother of Alric. 
2 CONT However, Earl
2 CONT
2 CONT Eadwulf's father is mistakenly believed by some to be King Osbert of Northumbria who died in the great Battle of York against the Viking Ragnarssons. Osbert is also descended from Ida certainly and particularly from his son Ocg, b
2 CONC ut Saxon Chronicle history clarifies that Earl Eadwulf was a cousin of Osbert and therefore so was Eadwulf the first also a cousin. That means that Eadwulf I was also descended from the son of Ida named Ocg. His line then comes th
2 CONC rough Aldheim b570, his son Ecgwulf bc600, and his son Leodwald b635, the MRCA for both Eadwulf I b670 and King Osbert d867. Ecgwulf, the grandfather of Eadwulf, was the first of the family with that name and it was distinctive to
2 CONC  that line of Kings of Northumbria down to the first Earl of Bamburgh, Eadwulf bc 830-38..This Eadwulf has now been seen to be married to a daughter, Alchfleda, from the Alric lineage, son of King Ida. Plus it is postulated by res
2 CONC earchers that Eadwulf  himself was descended from the line of Ocg who was the brother of Alric. However, Eadwulf 's father is mistakenly believed by some to be King Osbert of Northumbria who died in the great Battle of York agains
2 CONC t the Viking Ragnarssons. Osbert is also descended from Ida certainly and particularly from his son Ocg, but Saxon Chronicle history clarifies that Eadwulf was a cousin of Osbert and therefore so was Eadwulf I also a cousin. That 
2 CONC means that Eadwulf I was also descended from the son of Ida named Ocg. His line then comes through Aldheim b570, his son Ecgwulf bc600, and his son Leodwald b635, the MRCA for both Eadwulf I b670 and King Osbert d867. Ecgwulf, the
2 CONC  grandfather of Eadwulf, was the first of the family with that name and it was distinctive to that line of Kings of Northumbria.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  These ancient clans seemed to use a progression of sounds for their names and their spelling. In the 500s Cer-, Cea-, Ceo-, Cyn- dominated the names. Then in the 600s Ced-, Cent- with -wine, and later Eadwulf's son being named Ea
2 CONC rnwine came to being used. Then in the 600s to 700s Eop-, Eab-, Ead-, Eal, and Ethel- came into use leading to Ethelred being the English king in 866 and in 978. The first Edward being King was in 901. King Ethelwulf had sons Ethe
2 CONC lbald, Ethelbert, Ethelred, Ethelswith, and Alfred the Great (or is that Aelfred?) Four of them served time as kings of England. Finally next were Edgiva, Edwin, Edwine, Edhilda, Edwy, Edgar, Eadgyth, Aelfgifu, Edred, and Ealdred.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading[edit]
