1 NAME Æðelræd
2 GIVN Æðelræd
1 NAME Æðelræd I, king of Northumbria
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 APR 796
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Æðelræd I, King of Northumbria===
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born c.762 in Britain.
2 CONT He was the son of Æðelweald Moll the King of Northumbria and Æthelthryth.
2 CONT He succeeded to the title of ''Æðelræd I King of Northumbria'' in 774.
2 CONT He was deposed as King of Northumbria in 779.
2 CONT He succeeded again to the title of ''Æðelræd I King of Northumbria'' in 790.
2 CONT He married on 29 September 792 to Ælfflæd the daughter of Offa King of Mercia and Cynethryth.
2 CONT He was murdered on 18 April 796 by two of his ealdormen, Ealred and Wada, and Northumbria was plunged into chaos. The same year, his father-in-law King Offa makes a trade treaty with Holy Roman Emperor Charlemagne but Offa then su
2 CONC ddenly died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p15033.htm#i150322
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86thelred_I_of_Northumbria 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Expanded details
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelred (/ˈæθəlrɛd/; c. 762  – 18 April 796), was the king of Northumbria from 774 to 779 and again from 790 until he was murdered in 796. He was the son of Æthelwald Moll and Æthelthryth and possibly became king while still a ch
2 CONC ild after Alhred was deposed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of Æthelred's family isn't recorded, but his father Æthelwald, who was also called Moll, seems to have come from a noble background. Æthelwald first appears in the historical records in a letter written by Pope Paul I t
2 CONC o king Eadberht, ordering him to return lands taken from an Abbot Fothred, which were given to his brother Moll. After the abdication of king Eadberht in 758, his son Oswulf took his place but despite his father's long reign and h
2 CONC is powerful uncle Ecgbert, he was murdered just a year later in 759 at Market Weighton by his own bodyguards. The murder was possibly ordered by Æthelwald as he became king soon after. In 761 Oswulf's brother Oswine met Æthelwald 
2 CONC in battle but Oswine was killed in the fighting at Eildon Hill on 6 August. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After his victory, Æthelwald married Æthelthryth at Catterick on 1 November 762. Æthelwald was deposed as king on 30 October 765, by a council of noblemen and prelates, and replaced by Alhred, the brother-in-law of Oswulf and Oswi
2 CONC ne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After ruling for nearly ten years, the Northumbrians drove out King Alhred from York in 774. They then chose Æthelred as their king and he was "crowned with such great honour". In the year after his accession Æthelred, who may hav
2 CONC e been influenced by his father Æthelwald, ordered the killing of an Ealdorman, Eadwulf..
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelred was deposed as king and the throne passed on to Ælfwald,[1] a grandson of Eadberht Eating. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelred lived in exile during the reign of Ælfwald and his successor Osred II. However, in 788 or 789, Osred was deposed, forcibly tonsured and exiled and Æthelred was restored to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 790, during Æthelred's second reign, the ealdorman Eardwulf was ordered to be killed by Æthelred but survived and later became king. Ælfwald's sons Ælf and Ælfwine were killed, probably on Æthelred's orders, in 791. The next ye
2 CONC ar Osred attempted to regain the throne, but was defeated, captured and killed on 14 September 792. A year later, Lindisfarne was sacked by the Vikings with Alcuin's letters to Æthelred blaming this event on the sins of Æthelred a
2 CONC nd his nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 September 792 Æthelred married Ælfflæd the daughter of Offa of Mercia at Catterick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT While Æthelred was in Corbridge a group of conspiring nobles murdered him on 18 April 796. As a result, Osbald, an ealdorman and a friend of Alcuin, Æthelred's former adviser, became king, but within 27 days he abdicated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Swanton, Michael (1996). ''The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle''. London: Phoenix. pp. 52–53.
2 CONT
2 CONT Higham, N.J.,'' The Kingdom of Northumbria AD 350–1100''. Stroud: Sutton, 1993. ISBN 0-86299-730-5
2 CONT
2 CONT Kirby, D.P., ''The Earliest English Kings''. London: Unwin, 1991. ISBN 0-04-445692-1
2 CONT
2 CONT Yorke, Barbara, ''Kings and Kingdoms of early Anglo-Saxon England''. London: Seaby, 1990. ISBN 1-85264-027-8
