2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Idwal Foel ap Anarawd (Idwal the Bald) (died 942) was a King of Gwynedd, referred to as King of the Britons by William of Malmesbury, in whose Gesta Regum Anglorum his name is spelled Judwalum in the original Latin (anglicized Jot
2 CONC hwel). In the Annales Cambriae, his name is spelled Iudgual.
2 CONT
2 CONT Idwal inherited the throne of Gwynedd on the death of his father, Anarawd ap Rhodri in 916. He was obliged to acknowledge Athelstan of England as overlord. Following the death of Athelstan, Idwal and his brother Elisedd took to ar
2 CONC ms against the English, but both were killed in battle in 942. The rule of Gwynedd should now have passed to his sons, Iago ab Idwal and Idwal, usually called Ieuaf ab Idwal. However Hywel Dda, already ruler of most of south Wales
2 CONC , invaded Gwynedd and forced them into exile, adding Gwynedd to his realm. After Hywel's death in 950, Idwal's sons were able to claim the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ID: I105040 Name: Idwal I Foel The Bald Ap Anarawd Prefix: Prince Of Gwynedd Sex: M Birth: 883 CE in Aberffraw, Anglesey, Wales Death: 942 of Slain in battle with his brother fighting against the Anglo-Danes. 1 Event: Prince Of Gw
2 CONC ynedd Investiture 916 Event: Submitted To Edward The Elder Of England Politics Bet 918 and 937 CE Change Date: 13 Jan 2009 at 01:52 Note: Genealogists disagree as to which spouse was the mother of his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Idwal Foel ap Anarawd (Idwal the Bald) (died 942) was a King of Gwynedd, referred to as King of the Britons by William of Malmesbury, in whose Gesta Regum Anglorum his name is spelled Judwalum in the original Latin (anglicized Jot
2 CONC hwel). In the Annales Cambriae, his name is spelled Iudgual. Idwal inherited the throne of Gwynedd on the death of his father, Anarawd ap Rhodri in 916. He was obliged to acknowledge Athelstan of England as overlord. Following the
2 CONC  death of Athelstan, Idwal and his brother Elisedd took to arms against the English, but both were killed in battle in 942. The rule of Gwynedd should now have passed to his sons, Iago ab Idwal and Idwal, usually called Ieuaf ab I
2 CONC dwal. However Hywel Dda, already ruler of most of south Wales, invaded Gwynedd and forced them into exile, adding Gwynedd to his realm. After Hywel's death in 950, Idwal's sons were able to claim the kingdom.
