1 NOTE Kenneth MacAlpin
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT (Redirected from Kenneth I of Scotland)
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT Kenneth MacAlpin
2 CONT (Cináed mac Ailpín)
2 CONT King of the Picts
2 CONT Image:History-kenneth.jpg
2 CONT Reign 	843–858
2 CONT Died 	13 February 858
2 CONT Place of death 	Cinnbelachoir
2 CONT Buried 	Iona
2 CONT Predecessor 	See text
2 CONT Successor 	Donald I (Domnall mac Ailpín)
2 CONT Offspring 	Constantine I (Causantín mac Cináeda)
2 CONT Áed
2 CONT Máel Muire ingen Cináeda
2 CONT perhaps others
2 CONT Royal House 	Alpin
2 CONT Father 	Alpín mac Echdach
2 CONT
2 CONT Cináed mac Ailpín (Modern Gaelic: Coinneach mac Ailpein)[1], commonly Anglicised as Kenneth MacAlpin and known in most modern regnal lists as Kenneth I (died 13 February 858) was king of the Picts and, according to national myth, 
2 CONC first king of Scots, earning him the posthumous nickname of An Ferbasach, "The Conqueror".[2] Kenneth's undisputed legacy was to produce a dynasty of rulers who claimed descent from him. Even though he cannot be regarded as the fa
2 CONC ther of Scotland, he was the founder of the dynasty which ruled that country for much of the medieval period.
1 NOTE Kenneth I of Scotland
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=1be74788-3fb8-4ed4-ba2a-05c9d7706f47&tid=7053141&pid=-1146462007
1 NOTE 1  UID 0BA30D0F1821AA4CA0B3E7C41FAB19CBA52E
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Founder of the Kingdom of Scotland
1 NOTE King Kenneth I
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=d0a3ba25-81a3-4821-be38-e2c76250d3d6&tid=7053141&pid=-1145799933
1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry.  Chart: page 45.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 44:
2 CONT
2 CONT 14.    Kenneth I MacAlpin (Cinaed the Hardy), d. 858; came to the throne of Dalriada of the Scots 843 when the land was still made up of small kingdoms whose people were Scots, Picts, Angles and Britons.  He united Dalriada and Pi
2 CONC ctland 844 in a partnership never broken thereafter.  He himself was of Scottish-Pictish descent.  The new union was well timed, as the Norse incursions were becoming a serious threat.  Kenneth invaded Lothian of the Angles six ti
2 CONC mes, burning Dunbar and taking possessions of Melrose.  He is said to have called a Parliament.  About 850 he brought relics of St. Columba from Iona to Dunkeld, which was made the head church of his kingdom.  He d. of a fistula a
2 CONC t Forteviot, near Perth.  His brother ruled as Domnall I until 862.
2 CONT
2 CONT <I<<Dunkeld, Pertshire, is one of the most historic places in Scotland.  At an early date it emerged as an important center of the Celtic Church.  About 815, Constantine, King of the Picts, rebuilt the abbey for the Celtic monks o
2 CONC f Iona who had been displaced by the Norsemen.  Although its dignity as headquarters of the Scottish Church passed to St. Andrews, Dunkeld continued to be a major religious center.>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amie, Adam and FAL
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 145 line (170-13):
2 CONT
2 CONT 13.    Cinaed.  This is the famous Kenneth Mac Alpin, King of the Picts and Scots, 843-d. 858.  (For more details on generations 1-13, see also H. Pirie-Gordon "Succession of the Kingdom of Strathclyde" The Armorial vol. I pp. 35-
2 CONC 40, 79-87, 143-148, 192-196; vol. II 9-14,92-102, with cited authorities.  This reference also provides the descent to Kenneth MacAlpin of the lines of the Kings of Strathclyde and of the Picts.)
1 NOTE From Genealogical Library book "House of Adam".
1 NOTE Sucedeu ao pai em 841. Em 843 adquiriu ou conquistou a Pictavia. Em 843 os Pictos tinham unido suas terras com as de Dal Riata e aceitaram em 846 como seu rei KENNETH MAC ALPIN, rei dos escoceses. Uma das razões seria que Kenneth 
2 CONC mac Alpin descendia da linha real picta por sua mãe. Como os pictos eram uma sociedade matriarcal, mac Alpin tinha provavelmente direito de unir os dois reinos Mas lamentavelmente, poucas gerações mais tarde, a lingua e cultura si
2 CONC ngular dos Pictos se perdeu.
1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT See Europäisch Stammtafeln Bund II tafel 67.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Burke's Peerage 99th Ed (GS #942 D22bup prefix p. 285-86)
2 CONT     2. Dict of Nat'l Biog (GS #920.042 D561n vol 30 p. 437-38)
2 CONT     3. A Vindication of Macbeth (GS #929.2706 Ma88c p. 14)
2 CONT     4. Also searched without positive results: Scots Peerage, The Complete Peerage, Royal Dau of Engl, and Queens of Sctl and English Princes
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Burke's Peerage 99th Ed (GS #942 D22bup prefix p. 285-86)
2 CONT     2. Dict of Nat'l Biog (GS #920.042 D561n vol 30 p. 437-38)
2 CONT     3. A Vindication of Macbeth (GS #929.2706 Ma88c p. 14)
2 CONT     4. Also searched without positive results: Scots Peerage, The Complete Peerage, Royal Dau of Engl, and Queens of Sctl and English Princes
1 NOTE Known as Kinneth MacAlpin.
1 NOTE From Genealogical Library book "House of Adam".
1 NOTE King of Picts and Alba, King of Galloway. Acceded to the throne839.[FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Reign: 839-860. Burke calls him Kenneth II. Kings of Picts and Alba.King of Galloway. See Europäisch Stammtafeln Bund II tafel 67.
2 CONT    Within a year after the death of his cousins in 839, it appears thatKenneth seized the kingship of Dal Riata. There is little historicalrecord for the next eight years, but it appears that Kenneth followed inthe footsteps of hi
2 CONC s cousins, and made a bid for the kingship of thePicts. He was resisted, ineffectualy, by a short-lived dynasty bearingPictish names. Later legends suggest that Kenneth achieved his successthrough treachery; slaying his Pictish gu
2 CONC ests at a feast. Whatever hismeans, Kenneth defeated his last Pictish rival by 848, and in thefollowing year, he celebrated his victory by building a church dedicatedto St. Columba in his new Pictish lands. It is recorded that Ken
2 CONC nethraided England no fewer than six times. He was succeeded by his brotherDonald I.
1 NOTE Kenneth I mac Alpin (født ca. 810, død 858) var konge av Dalriada fra 834 og konge av hele Skottland fra 846. Han regnes som den første konge av et samlet Skottland, etter at han underla seg pikterne, og han var første konge fra h
2 CONC uset Alpin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den piktiske tronen hadde en særegen arverekkefølge, da den gikk i kvinnelige ledd. Kenneth hadde gjennom sitt mor mulighet til å kreve tronen, og da pikterne led et kraftig nederlag for vikingene i 839 ble de sterkt svekket. Nor
2 CONC dmenn tok kontroll over Shetland, de Ytre Hebridene og fastlandet ned til Firth of Clyde. Caithness, Sutherland og Dalriada ble angrepet av vikinger, og for Kenneth var det ikke bare ønskelig å ta kontroll over pikternes område,
2 CONC  men også nødvendig for å styrke motstanden mot nordmennene. Mesteparten av den piktiske adelen hadde falt i 839, inkludert kong Uven Mac Angus II og hans bror Bran.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kenneths krav på tronen i Dalriada kom gjennom hans far, Alpin II av Dalriada. Han var der en av flere som hadde rettmessig krav på tronen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kildene til hvordan han samlet Skottland er mangelfulle og usikre. I 841 skal han ha angrepet restene av den piktiske arméen og slått dem, noe som gav ham tilnavnet ?Ravnemateren?. Han skal så ha invitert den nye piktiske konge
2 CONC n, Drust, til Scone, antagelig under påskudd av å ville diskutere sitt krav på tronen. Alle de piktiske tronkreverne kom til møtet, hvor skottene skal ha møtt med skjulte våpen og drept alle. Hendelsen har gått inn i legenden
2 CONC e som ?MacAlpins forræderi?.
2 CONT
2 CONT Selv om hele den piktiske adelen nå var utslettet av vikinger og skotter nektet pikterne å underkaste seg, og de kjempet videre. Først i 846 sto de siste trefningene, ifølge Henry av Huntingdon, syv trefninger på samme dag.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kenneth la sin hovedstad til Scone, som dengang også kaltes Forteviot. Han flyttet også nasjonens religiøse sentrum til Dunkeld i Perthshire, og fikk blant annet overført relikviene til St. Columba dit fra Iona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han skal ha dødd i Forteviot av i 858, og ble gravlagt på Iona. Han ble etterfulgt av sin bror Donald I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans datter Maolmuire skal ha vært gift med den irske overkongen Áed Finliath. En av hans døtre var gift med kong Olav Kvite i Dublin.
2 CONT
2 CONT MacAlpins forræderi er en middelaldersk myte som forsøker å forklare hvorfor det piktiske språk og kultur ble erstattet av skotsk-gælisk språk og kultur på 800-tallet og 900-tallet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Myten forteller om det påståtte mordet på Oictavias (Piktland) adel i oldtidens nordlige Britannia (det som er dagens Skottland). Kenneth MacAlphins mor stammet sannsynligvis fra det kongelige huset av Fortriu og hans onkel til
2 CONC  hans stamfar, Alpin II av pikterne, hadde regjert som pikternes konge inntil han ble avsatt av Óengus I av pikterne i år 728 e.Kr. Kenneth MacAlpin var derfor en av flere adelige som hadde et krav til tronen til pikterne og sko
2 CONC ttene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kildene til de fakta om hvordan Kenneth MacAlpin ble konge over pikterne og skottene er svært få og tvilsomme. To slike kilder, Bercháns profeti, og De Instructione Principus, slår fast at i 841 angrep MacAlpin restene av den p
2 CONC iktiske hæren og beseiret den. MacAlpin inviterte deretter den piktiske kongen, Drest, og de øvrige piktiske adelskapet til Scone for å avgjøre spørsmålet om Dál Riatas frihet eller MacAlphins krav til Dál Riatas krone. I møte
2 CONC  med nylige seirerike MacAlpin i sør og den ødelagte hærstyrken i nord møtte Drest og alle de andre som hadde krav på den piktiske tronen fra de syv kongelige husene til møtet i Scone. Legenden vil ha til at skotten kom med våpe
2 CONC ne skjult til Scone og deretter slaktet ned og drepte Drest og de andre adelige.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det er Giraldus Cambrensis (eller Gerald av Wales) i De Instructione Principus som har skrevet ned om hvordan en stor festmiddag ble holdt i Scone, og hvordan den piktiske kongen og hans adelige ble gitt sterke drikker og de ble g
2 CONC anske fulle. Da pikterne var fulle nok skal skottene ha dratt vekk bolter fra benkene slik at pikterne ble fanget i skjulte gravhull under benkene, og i tillegg var det skarpe spyd stikkende opp av gravene slik at de fallende pikt
2 CONC erne ble spiddet til døde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bercháns profeti forteller at
2 CONT
2 CONT ?MacAlpin stupte dem jordgraven, dekket med dødelige knivblader. Fanget og uten muligheter til å forsvare seg selv ble de overlevende pikterne deretter myrdet ovenifra og deres kropper, klær og utsmykninger ble plyndret.? 
2 CONT Etter denne hendelsen, sies det, ble Kenneth MacAlpin konge over begge kongeriker, kunngjorde at han på morssiden hadde krav på tronen til Pictavia og arvet Dál Riada fra sin far. Han smeltet sammen de to rikene til ett rike un
2 CONC der navnet Alba.
1 NOTE Kenneth I, called MacAlpin (flourished 832-60), traditionally, the founder 
2 CONT and first king of Scotland. About 834 he succeeded his father, Alpin 
2 CONT (reigned about 832-34), as king of the Gaelic Scots in Galloway. In a 
2 CONT series of battles (841-46) he conquered the Pictish Kingdom and, uniting 
2 CONT it with his own, called his expanded domains Scotland. The kingdom is 
2 CONT sometimes called Scone, after Kenneth's capital. In later years, the king 
2 CONT led six invasions of Lothian, southern Scotland, then part of Saxon 
2 CONT Northumbria.
1 NOTE OR "CINAED""MACALPIN"; FOUNDER OF KINGDOM OF SCOTLAND (FIRST TO RULE BOTH SCOTS
2 CONT AND PICTS); RULED 839-859
1 NOTE 36th great grandfather
1 NOTE First king of the united Scots of Dalriada and the Picts and so of Scotland north of a line between the Forth and Clyde rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his father, Alpin, little is known, though tradition credits him with a signal victory over the Picts by whom he was killed three months later (c. 834). Kenneth succeeded him in Dalriada and ruled in Pictavia also, ruling for 1
2 CONC 6 years. The period is obscure. The gradual union of the two kingdoms from 843 doubtless owes much to intermarriage. By the Pictish marriage custom, inheritance passed through the female. Nevertheless, Kenneth probably made some c
2 CONC onquests among the eastern Picts and possibly invaded Lothian and burned Dunbar and Melrose. After attacks on Iona by Vikings he removed relics of St. Columba, probably in 849 or 850, to Dunkeld, which became the headquarters of t
2 CONC he Scottish Columban church. He died at Forteviot, not far from Scone in Pictish territory, and was buried on the island of Iona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another name for Kenneth was Kenneth Macalpin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Acceded: King of Scotland, 842.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Burke's Peerage 99th Ed (GS #942 D22bup prefix p. 285-86)
2 CONT     2. Dict of Nat'l Biog (GS #920.042 D561n vol 30 p. 437-38)
2 CONT     3. A Vindication of Macbeth (GS #929.2706 Ma88c p. 14)
2 CONT     4. Also searched without positive results: Scots Peerage, The Complete Peerage, Royal Dau of Engl, and Queens of Sctl and English Princes
1 NOTE First King of Scotland
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=819e6d60-69c3-4a85-8873-7317e4b38869&tid=10145763&pid=-466718599
1 NOTE First King of Scotland
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=819e6d60-69c3-4a85-8873-7317e4b38869&tid=10145763&pid=-466718599
1 NOTE 74th Ard Righ Albann. He succeeded his Father and brought together the Crowns of Dalriada and Caledonia in 844 to become the First King of Scots. He was succeeded by his brother Donald. {Burke�s Peerage} [GADD.GED]
2 CONT
2 CONT Also have death as 860. [The Timetables of History]
2 CONT King of the Picts & Scots and all Alba. United both nations with his Pictish Royal blood
1 NOTE     * REMARKS:
2 CONT       1st King of Scots (or King of Alba)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT       Kenneth was born about 800, the son of Alpin, king of Kintyre. Kenneth was king of the Picts and, according to national myth, first king of Scots, earning him the posthumous epithet 'An Ferbasach' ('The Conqueror'). His undi
2 CONC sputed legacy was to produce a dynasty of rulers who claimed descent from him. Even though he cannot be regarded as the father of Scotland, he was the founder of the dynasty which ruled that country for much of the medieval period
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth's origins are uncertain, as are his ties, if any, to previous kings of the Picts of Dál Riata (the Gaelic over-kingdom on the western seaboard of Scotland with some territory on the northern coasts of Ireland). Medie
2 CONC val genealogies are unreliable sources, but some historians accept Kenneth's descent from the Cenél nGabrain of Dál Riata.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Although later traditions provided details of his reign and death, Kenneth's father Alpin is not listed as among the kings in the _Duan Albanach_ (Song of the Scots), the Middle Gaelic poem found with the _Lebor Bretnach,_ a
2 CONC  Gaelic version of the _Historia Brittonum_ attributed to Nennius. The idea that Kenneth was a Gael is not entirely rejected, but modern historiography distinguishes between Kenneth as a Gael by culture, and perhaps in ancestry, a
2 CONC nd Kenneth as a king of Gaelic Dál Riata. Kenneth could well have been the first sort of Gael. Kings of the Picts before him, from Bridei, son of Der-Ilei, his brother Nechtan as well as Oengus I (Angus I), son of Fergus and his p
2 CONC resumed descendants, were all at least partly Gaelicised. The idea that the Gaelic names of Pictish kings in Irish annals represented translations of Pictish ones was challenged by the discovery of the inscription _Custantin filiu
2 CONC s Fircus(sa),_ the latinised name of the Pictish king Caustantin, son of Fergus, on the Dupplin Cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth's rise to power can be placed in the context of the recent end of the previous dynasty, which had dominated Fortriu (an ancient Pictish kingdom, often used synonymously with Pictland in general) for two or four gener
2 CONC ations. This followed the death of King Uen, son of Oengus of Fortriu, his brother Bran, Aed mac Boanta and many others in battle against the Vikings in 839. The resulting succession crisis seems, if the Pictish Chronicle's king-l
2 CONC ists have any validity, to have resulted in at least four would-be kings warring for supreme power.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth's reign is dated from 843, but it was probably not until 848 that he defeated the last of his rivals for power. The _Pictish Chronicle_ claims that he was king in Dál Riata for two years before becoming Pictish king 
2 CONC in 843, but this is not generally accepted. In 849, Kenneth had relics of St. Columba, which may have included the Monymusk Reliquary, transferred from Iona to Dunkeld. Other than these are bare facts, the _Chronicle of the Kings 
2 CONC of Alba_ reports that he invaded Saxonia six times, captured Melrose and burnt Dunbar, and also that Vikings laid waste to Pictland, reaching far into the interior.
2 CONT
2 CONT       The reign of Kenneth also saw an increased degree of Norse settlement in the outlying areas of modern Scotland. The Shetlands, the Orkneys, Caithness, Sutherland, the Western Isles and the Isle of Man, and part of Ross were 
2 CONC settled; the links between Kenneth's kingdom and Ireland were weakened, those with southern England and the continent almost broken. In the face of this, Kenneth and his successors were forced to consolidate their position in thei
2 CONC r kingdom, and the union between the Picts and the Gaels, already progressing for several centuries, began to strengthen. By the time of Donald II, the kings would be called neither of the Gaels nor of the Scots, but of Alba.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth died from a tumour on 13 February 858 at the palace of Cinnbelachoir, perhaps near Scone. The annals report the death as that of the 'king of the Picts', not the 'king of Alba'. The title 'king of Alba' is not used u
2 CONC ntil the time of Kenneth's grandsons Donald II (Domnall mac Causatin) and Constantine II (Constantin mac Aeda). The Fragmentary Annals of Ireland quote a verse lamenting Kenneth's death: Because Cináerd with many troops lives no l
2 CONC onger there is weeping in every house; there is no king of his worth under heaven as far as the borders of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth left at least two sons, Constantine and Aed, who were later kings, and at least two daughters. One daughter married Run, king of Strathclyde, and Eochaid mac Run, king of the Picts, resulted from this marriage. Kenne
2 CONC th's daughter Máerl Muire married two important Irish kings. Her first husband was Aed Finliath of the Cenél nEógain. Niall Glúndub, ancestor of the O'Neill, was the son of this marriage. Her second husband was Flann Sinna of Clan
2 CONC n Cholmáin.
1 NOTE Most lists of kings of Scots begin with him. Indeed, it is from him that the modern British monarchy traces its descent.  He reigned from 842 to 858, although his final victory over Pictish opposition came as late as 847. He built
2 CONC  a cathedral in Dunkeld honoring St. Columba and had her relics moved there. His reign was a time of almost constant warfare, including devastating Viking raids.  He was succeeded by his younger brother, Domnall.
1 NOTE Most lists of kings of Scots begin with him. Indeed, it is from him that the modern British monarchy traces its descent.  He reigned from 842 to 858, although his final victory over Pictish opposition came as late as 847. He built
2 CONC  a cathedral in Dunkeld honoring St. Columba and had her relics moved there. His reign was a time of almost constant warfare, including devastating Viking raids.  He was succeeded by his younger brother, Domnall.
1 NOTE Coinneach en gaélique (erse) moderne. Rìgh nan Gaidheal, Rìgh nan Cruithneach&nbsp;: Roi des Scots, Roi des Pictes. 
2 CONT Chronique Picte : 
2 CONT Kinadius igitur filius Alpini, primus Scottorum, rexit feliciter istam annis xvi. Pictaviam. Pictavia autem a Pictis est nominata; quos, ut diximus, Cinadius delevit. Deus enim eos pro merito suae malitiae alienos ac otiosos hered
2 CONC itate dignitatus est facere: quia illi non solum Domini missam ac preceptum spreverunt; sed et in jure aequitatis aliis aequi parari (n)voluerunt. Iste vero, biennio antequam veniret Pictaviam, Dalrietae regnum suscepit. Septimo a
2 CONC nno regni sui, reliquias Sancti Columbae transportavit ad ecclesiam quam construxit, et invasit sexies Saxoniam; et concremavit Dunbarre atque Marlos usurpata. Britanni autem concremaverunt Dubblain, atque Danari vastaverunt Picta
2 CONC viam, ad Cluanan et Duncalden. Mortuus est tandem tumore ante diem ? idus Februarii feria tertia in palacio Fothuirtabaicht.
