1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ceolwulf II, King of Mercia (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #150370, d. before 883
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=27 Nov 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.39%
2 CONT
2 CONT      Ceolwulf II, King of Mercia was the son of Æflæd (?) and Wigmund (?). (2) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died before 883, killed. (3)
2 CONT
2 CONT      Ceolwulf II, King of Mercia succeeded to the title of King Ceolwulf II of Mercia in 874. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p15037.htm#i150370
2 CONT
2 CONT '''_________________________'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''"King Alfred and King Ceolwulf II"'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Until recently, our view of Ceolwulf, King of Mercia, has been heavily influenced by the references in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, which described him as an 'unwise thegn' who was put in place by the Viking great army and who promi
2 CONC sed to hold the kingdom for them until they required it:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Her for se here of Lindesse to Hreope dune, ⁊ þær winter setl nam, ⁊ þone cining Burgred ofer sæ adrefdon ymb .xxii. wintra þæs he rice hæfde ... ⁊ þy ilcan geare hie sealdon Ceolwulfe anum unwisum cyniges þegne Myrcena rice to 
2 CONC healdenne, ⁊ he him aðas swor ⁊ gislas sealde, þet hit him georo wære swa hwilce dæge swa hi hit habban woldon, ⁊ he geare wære mid him sylfum, ⁊ mid eallum þam þe him ge læstan wolden to þæs heres þærfe.''
2 CONT
2 CONT In this year the army travelled from Lindsey to Repton, and there took up their winter quarters, drove the king, Burhred, over sea, when he had reigned about two and twenty winters, and subdued all that land ... And the same year 
2 CONC they gave Ceolwulf, an unwise king's thane, the Mercian kingdom to hold; and he swore oaths to them, and gave hostages, that it should be ready for them on whatever day they would have it; and he would be ready with himself, and w
2 CONC ith all those that would remain with him, at the service of the army.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anno 874 (actually 873), Laud Ms
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle also describes Ceolwulf being given lands in Mercia when the Viking army returned in 877:
2 CONT
2 CONT ... ''⁊ þa on herfeste ge for se here on Myrcena land, ⁊ hit gedældon sum, and sum Ceolwulfe sealdon.''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the harvest the army entered Mercia; some of which they divided among them, and some they gave to Ceolwulf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anno 877, Laud Ms
2 CONT
2 CONT As the Chronicle was compiled by order of King Alfred the Great, who was brother-in-law to the deposed King Burgred, it was always seen as a politically biased account, but the discovery of a Viking hoard in the parish of Watlingt
2 CONC on in South Oxfordshire in October 2015 has revealed just how biased that account is.
2 CONT
2 CONT The hoard, which was discovered by a metal detector user, consists of 186 silver coins, 1 item of gold jewellery, 6 items of silver jewellery and 15 silver ingots, and was probably deposited in the late 870's.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the coins show two emperor-like figures, representing Alfred and Ceolwulf.  (see here)  [''See the profile portrait image.'']
2 CONT
2 CONT Hitherto, only one example of this type of coin was known, and many numismatists thought that it might be from a "one-off" mint.
2 CONT
2 CONT The examples from the hoard, however, are produced in both Ceolwulf and Alfred's names and from a number of different mints.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that these coins are now known to have been produced extensively in both kingdoms indicates that Ceolwulf had a powerful alliance with Alfred, a very different situation from the one portrayed in the Chronicle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gareth Williams, of the British Museum, said that Ceolwulf had been "airbrushed out of history" by the increasingly powerful Alfred "because he's no longer convenient".
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anglo-Saxon Archaeology''' ... "''King Alfred and King Ceolwulf II''" ... http://anglosaxon.archeurope.info/index.php?page=king-alfred-and-king-ceolwulf-ii
2 CONT
2 CONT ? https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ceolwulf_II_of_Mercia
