2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Saint Amaethlu ap Caradoc''' was born circa 520, to Caradoc Friechfras ap Ynyr and Enynny ap Ynyr (born ferch Cynfarch). Caradoc was born circa 500, in Wales. Enynny was born circa 476, in England. Amaethlu had one brother: Llu
2 CONC ddoca Caradoc Vreichvras. Saint married Unknown. They had one son: Waroch Broërec.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.myheritage.com/names/saint_ap%20ynyr
2 CONT
2 CONT Carneddor in Môn. Carneddor was formerly a township in Llanfaethlu in Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Caradoc
2 CONT
2 CONT Caradoc or Caradog was a Welsh hermit and harpist.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Caradoc was a Welsh nobleman, native of Brecknockshire, who, after he had received a liberal education, enjoyed the confidence of Rees, prince of South Wales, and held an honourable place in his court. The prince one day, on accou
2 CONC nt of two greyhounds which were lost, fell into such a fury against Caradoc as to threaten his life. Caradoc, from this disgrace learned the inconstancy of worldly honours, and repaired to Landaff, where he received from the bisho
2 CONC p the clerical tonsure, and for some time served God in the church of St. Theliau.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Desirous of finding solitude, he afterwards spent some years in a little hut, which he built himself, near the abandoned church of St. Cendydd Church[2] in Gower, later taking up residence on Barry Island at St. Issels. His reputa
2 CONC tion for sanctity filled the whole country, and the archbishop of Menevia, or St. David's, calling him to that town, promoted him to priestly orders. Caradoc then retired with certain devout companions, to the isle of Ary. Certain
2 CONC  pirates from Norway, who often infested these coasts, carried them off prisoners, but, fearing the judgments of God, safely set them on shore again the next day. However, the archbishop of Menevia assigned Caradoc another habitat
2 CONC ion in the monastery of St. Hismael, commonly called Ysam, in the country of Ross, or Pembrokeshire.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He served a local king in southern Wales before Caradoc was later forced into exile by Henry I's invasion of the region, Caradoc went to Haroldston, where he occupied the cell of St. Ismael.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT St Caradoc's Church in Lawrenny, Wales probably dates back to the twelfth century.
