1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The earliest mention of Alpin is in a 10th century pedigree constructed to give the kings of Scotland an ancient heritage.  
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.friesian.com/perifran.htm#england
2 CONT
2 CONT Alpín, mac Eochaid, King of Dal Riada, Argyll, Kintyre
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpin_II_of_Dalriada
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.burkes-peerage.net/articles/scotland/page31d.aspx
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#_Toc209085736
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT 1.         [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000212119218825 EOCHAID].  m ---.  The primary source which names the wife of Eochaid has not yet been identified.  Eochaid & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >a)        [https://www.geni.com/people/Alp%C3%ADn-R%C3%AD-na-D%C3%A1l-Riata/6000000001041459819 ALPIN] (-killed in battle against the Picts in Galloway [20 Jul/Aug] 834).  The Chronicle of John of Fordun records the accession of 
2 CONC "Alpin the son of Achay" in 831, that he ruled for three years, was defeated by the Picts "20 July" and beheaded[12].  The 12th century Cronica Regum Scottorum lists "Alpin filius Eochal venenosi iii, Kynedus filius Alpini primus 
2 CONC rex Scottorum xvi…" as kings, dated to the 9th century[13].  m ---.  The name of Alpin's wife is not known.  Alpin & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>i)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Kenneth-I-Mac-Alpine-king-of-the-Picts/6000000001041559796 KENNETH] [Cinaed] MacAlpin (-Forteviot, Perthshire 13 Feb [858], bur [Isle of Iona]).  His parentage is confirmed by the Annals o
2 CONC f Ulster which record the death in 858 of "Cinaed son of Ailpín king of the Picts"[14].  He succeeded as KENNETH I King of Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>ii)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Domnall-mac-Alp%C3%ADn-R%C3%AD-na-D%C3%A1l-Riata/6000000123896637852 DONALD] [Domnall] (-Kinn Belachoir palace or killed in battle Scone 13 Apr [863], bur [Isle of Iona]). The 10th centur
2 CONC y Pictish Chronicle Cronica de Origine Antiquorum Pictorum records that "Kinadius…filius Alpini, primus Scottorum…Dunevaldus frater eius" ruled for four years[15].  The 12th century Cronica Regum Scottorum lists "Alpin filius Eoch
2 CONC al venenosi iii, Kynedus filius Alpini primus rex Scottorum xvi, Dolfnal filius Alpini iiii…" as kings, dated to the 9th century[16].  The Chronicle of John of Fordun records that "Donald also a son of Alpin" succeeded his brother
2 CONC  in 854, reigned for four years, died "at Scone" and was buried "in Iona beside his brother"[17].  He succeeded his brother as DONALD I King of Scotland.  The 11th century Synchronisms of Flann Mainistreach name (in order) "Cinaet
2 CONC  mac Ailpin…Domnall mac Ailpin, Custantin mac Cinaeta, (Aedh mac Cinaedha), Girg mac Dungaile, Domnall Dasachtach (mac Custantin)" as Scottish kings, dated to the 9th and 10th centuries[18].  The Annals of Ulster record the death 
2 CONC in 862 of "Domnall son of Ailpín king of the Picts"[19].  The Chronicle of the Scots and Picts dated 1177 records that "Douenald mac Alpin" reigned for 4 years, died "in Rathinueramon" and was buried "in Iona insula"[20].  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The precise relationship between the following family group and the main family of Scottish kings has not been determined, but the names suggest a close relationship. 
2 CONT 1.         ALPIN .  m ---.  The name of Alpin’s wife is not known.  Alpin & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT >a)         EOCHAID (-[937/40]).  The 10th century Pictish Chronicle Cronica de Origine Antiquorum Pictorum records the death of "Dubucan filius Indrechtaig mormair Oengusa, Adalstan filius Advar rig Saxan, et Eochaid filius Alpin
2 CONC i"[32], dated to [937/40] if the second person named can be identified as Æthelstan King of Wessex. 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#_Toc209085736
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Alpín father of King Kenneth
2 CONT
2 CONT Irish annals such as the Annals of Ulster and the Annals of Innisfallen name Kenneth's father as one Alpín. This much is reasonably certain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba usually begins with Kenneth, but some variants include a reference to Kenneth's father: "[Alpín] was killed in Galloway, after he had entirely destroyed and devastated it. And then the kingdom of
2 CONC  the Scots was transferred to the kingdom [variant: land] of the Picts."
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Fordun (IV, ii) calls Kenneth's father "Alpin son of Achay" (Alpín son of Eochu) and has him killed in war with the Picts in 836; Andrew of Wyntoun's version mixes Fordun's war with the Picts with the Chronicle version whi
2 CONC ch has him killed in Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 839--Kings Aed mac Boanta of Dal Riada and Eoganan mac Oengus of Pictavia are killed in battle with the Vikings. Pictavia fragments into several competing kingdoms. In Dal Riada, Alpin mac Echdach (son of Eochaid mac Aed Find) ass
2 CONC umes the throne. Death of King Aethelstan of East Anglia. He is succeeded by Aethelweard. A large Viking fleet under the command of Thorgest arrives in Ireland, sails up the Shannon and Bann rivers to Armagh, which he captures. Th
2 CONC orgest settles down and forms the first Viking kingdom in Ireland, spanning Ulster, Connacht and Meath.
2 CONT
2 CONT 839--Kings Aed mac Boanta of Dal Riada and Eoganan mac Oengus of Pictavia are killed in battle with the Vikings. Pictavia fragments into several competing kingdoms. In Dal Riada, Alpin mac Echdach (son of Eochaid mac Aed Find) ass
2 CONC umes the throne. Death of King Aethelstan of East Anglia. He is succeeded by Aethelweard. A large Viking fleet under the command of Thorgest arrives in Ireland, sails up the Shannon and Bann rivers to Armagh, which he captures. Th
2 CONC orgest settles down and forms the first Viking kingdom in Ireland, spanning Ulster, Connacht and Meath.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.geocities.com/robertp6165/arthuriantimeline5.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Alpín mac Echdach
2 CONT
2 CONT Alpín mac Echdach may refer to two persons. The first person is a presumed king of Dál Riata in the late 730s. The second is the father of Kenneth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín). The name Alpín is taken to be a Pictish one, derived 
2 CONC from the Anglo-Saxon name Ælfwine; Alpín's patronymic means son of Eochaid or son of Eochu.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Alpín father of King Kenneth
2 CONT
2 CONT Irish annals such as the Annals of Ulster and the Annals of Innisfallen name Kenneth's father as one Alpín. This much is reasonably certain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba usually begins with Kenneth, but some variants include a reference to Kenneth's father: "[Alpín] was killed in Galloway, after he had entirely destroyed and devastated it. And then the kingdom of
2 CONC  the Scots was transferred to the kingdom [variant: land] of the Picts."
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Fordun (IV, ii) calls Kenneth's father "Alpin son of Achay" (Alpín son of Eochu) and has him killed in war with the Picts in 836; Andrew of Wyntoun's version mixes Fordun's war with the Picts with the Chronicle version whi
2 CONC ch has him killed in Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Alpín of Dál Riata
2 CONT
2 CONT The genealogies produced for Kings of Scots in the High Middle Ages traced their ancestry through Kenneth MacAlpin, through the Cenél nGabráin of Dál Riata to Fergus Mór, and then to legendary Irish kings such as Conaire Mór.
2 CONT
2 CONT These genealogies, perhaps oral in origin, were subjected to some regularisation by the scribes who copied them into sources such as the Chronicle of Melrose, the Poppleton Manuscript and the like. Either by accident, or by design
2 CONC , a number of kings were misplaced, being moved from the early 8th century to the late 8th and early 9th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT The original list is presumed to have resembled the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Eochaid mac Domangairt
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Ainbcellach mac Ferchair
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Eógan mac Ferchair
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Selbach mac Ferchair
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Eochaid mac Echdach
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Dúngal mac Selbaig
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Alpín
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Muiredach mac Ainbcellaig
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Eógan mac Muiredaig
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Áed Find
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. Fergus mac Echdach
2 CONT
2 CONT After modification to link this list of kings of Dál Riata to the family of Kenneth MacAlpin, the list is presumed to have been in this form:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Eochaid mac Domangairt
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Ainbcellach mac Ferchair
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Eógan mac Ferchair
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Muiredach mac Ainbcellaig
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Eogan mac Muiredaig
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Áed Find
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. Fergus mac Echdach
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Selbach mac Ferchair (called Selbach mac Eógain)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Eochaid mac Echdach (called Eochaid mac Áeda Find)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Dúngal mac Selbaig (name unchanged)
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Alpín (called Alpín mac Echdach)
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the existence of the original Alpín is less than certain. No king in Dál Riata of that name is recorded in the Irish annals in the early 730s. A Pictish king named Alpín, whose father's name is not given in any Irish sour
2 CONC ces, or even from the Pictish Chronicle king-lists, is known from the late 720s, when he was defeated by Óengus mac Fergusa and Nechtan mac Der-Ilei. For the year 742, the Annals of Ulster are read was referring to the capture of 
2 CONC "Elffin son of Crop" (the former reading had besieged rather than captured). Whether Álpin son of Crup is related to the Álpin of the 720s is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Alpín mac Echdach may refer to two persons. The first person is a presumed king of Dál Riata in the late 730s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The second is the father of Kenneth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín). The name Alpín is taken to be a Pictish one, derived from the Anglo-Saxon name Ælfwine; Alpín's patronymic means son of Eochaid or son of Eochu.
2 CONT
