1 OCCU Konge av Vestfold
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The demise of Gofraid, King of Lochlann and father of Amlaíb and Imhar (or Ímar) and Auisle is recorded in the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland in 873:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ég righ Lochlainne .i. Gothfraid do tedmaimm grána opond. Sic quod placuit Deo. (The death of the king of Lochlainn i.e. Gothfraid of a sudden and horrible fit. So it pleased God.)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ucc.ie/celt/Vikings%20in%20Scotland%20and%20Ireland.pdf (page 36-37)
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Some historians believe that this passage is actually a record of Ímar's death in 873 rather than his father's death.- Alex Moes
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The Fragmentary Annals of Ireland record that in 849:
2 CONT
2 CONT >the sixth year of the reign of Máel Sechlainn, Amlaib Conung, son of the king of Lochlann, came to Ireland, and he brought with him a proclamation of many tributes and taxes from his father, and he departed suddenly. Then his you
2 CONC nger brother Imar came after him to levy the same tribute.
2 CONT
2 CONT This source is then clear that Amlaib is the son of Gofraid, who is the king of Lochlann, although the location of "Lochlann" is the subject of some dispute. This word is often translated as "Norway" although Ó Corráin (1998) argu
2 CONC es that Lochlann "is Viking Scotland and probably includes Man" at this time and suggests an early date for an organised Kingdom of the Isles.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gofraid_of_Lochlann
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The arrival of the so-called "Dark Foreigners" in Ireland is first recorded in Irish annals in 849 when the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland state euphemistically that "Amlaib [Olaf] Conung, son of the king of Norway, came to Ireland
2 CONC …with a proclamation of many tributes and taxes from his father, and he departed suddenly"[1122].  Clare Downham discusses the various theories of the meaning of the terms "Dark Foreigners" and "Fair Foreigners" used in early prim
2 CONC ary sources to describe the Viking raiders[1123].  The identity of the "king of Norway" in question is uncertain.  A later passage in the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland names him "Gofraid/Gothfraid, son of Ragnall, son of Gothfraid
2 CONC  Conung, son of Gofraid"[1124], and the same source records the death in 873 of "the Norwegian king, i.e. Gothfraid…of a sudden hideous disease"[1125].  "Gofraid/Gothfraid" cannot be linked to any of the main contemporary Norwegia
2 CONC n rulers who are shown in the document NORWAY KINGS, although it should be borne in mind that the government of Norway was fragmented at the time with numerous local rulers in different parts of the country who probably all referr
2 CONC ed to themselves as kings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/IRELAND.htm#_Toc389126206
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gudrødr of Lochlainn (Historical) was a historical person and dynastic ancestor to Amlaíb and Ímar of Dublin, but his genealogical ascent (i.e. F §401—Iomhar mc. Gothfraidh mc. Ragnaill mc. Gothfraidh Conung mc Gofraidh) is a cons
2 CONC truct without historical value.1 He was born circa 790. He was the son of Konung av Sverige Ragnarr Lodbrók Sigurdsson (Mythical).2
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family
2 CONT ===Children	
2 CONT *Amlaíb Conung mac rígh Lochlainne (Historical)+ b. c 820, d. 8751
2 CONT *Ímar mac rígh Lochlainne, rex Nordmannorum totius Hibernie & Brittanie+ b. c 822, d. 8731
2 CONT
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p152.htm#i17237
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Hyfaidd ap Bleddri versus Sons of Rhodri Mawr; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id266.html. (Steven Ferry, May 5, 2021.)
