2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.ourfamtree.org/pedigree.cfm?pid=12247
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Edgar, the Aetheling, was the son of Prince Edward of the ancient and royal Saxon House of Wessex and his mother, Agatha, was the daughter of Henry II, emperor of Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgar, his mother Agatha, and his two sisters Margaret and Christina, came to England to the court of Edward the Confessor, bringing with them a large number of Hungarian attendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgar, the Aetheling, had in his own person the Anglo-Saxon claim to the crown of England, but before he could act, William, Duke of Normandy, landed an army in England, gained the throne, and was crowned in London before the end 
2 CONC of the year 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgar abandoned his claim to the throne and swore loyalty to William at Berkhamstead on December 25, 1066. In return, William gave Edgar an earldom with large possessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgar later became suspicious of William, and taking his two sisters, his mother Agatha, and their attendants, fled England and sailed for Scotland. High winds drove their ship into the Firth of Forth at St. Margaret's Hope, Queen
2 CONC ferry, Scotland. They were received by King Malcolm II, Canmore of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Malcolm II was the son of King Duncan of Scotland. King Duncan had been killed in a battle with MacBeth. Afterwards, Malcolm II defeated MacBeth and took the Scottish crown as Malcolm II, Canmore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, King Malcolm offered his hand to Princess Margaret, daughter of Agatha, and sister of Edgar. Margaret accepted and became the Queen of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ======================
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the title of King Kenneth of Alba. He succeeded to the title of King Kenneth II of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was possbily killed by Finvela, a noblewoman whose son was killed by the king. She is said to have lured Kenneth into her home promising to unmask traiters. In one room, a statue was connected to several hidden crossbows which 
2 CONC were set to fire bolts from every side when a golden apple on the statue was lifted. After a great feast, at which wine flowed freely, Finvela took her drunken guest into the fatal room and offered him the golden apple as a gestur
2 CONC e of peace. As he lifted the apple, he was struck by a hail of bolts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He has an extensive biographical entry in the Dictionary of National Biography
