2 TITL From Pictland to Alba - by Alex Woolf, Edinburgh University Press, 2007
1 NOTE
2 CONT Born at Wantage, Berkshire, in 849, Alfred was the fifth son of
2 CONT A ethelwulf, king of the West Saxons. At their father's behest and by
2 CONT mutual ag reement, Alfred's elder brothers succeeded to the kingship in
2 CONT turn, rather th an endanger the kingdom by passing it to under-age
2 CONT children at a time when th e country was threatened by worsening Viking
2 CONT raids from Denmark.
2 CONT Since the 7 90s, the Vikings had been using fast mobile armies, numbering
2 CONT thousands of me n embarked in shallow-draught longships, to raid the
2 CONT coasts and inland waters of England for plunder. Such raids were evolving
2 CONT into permanent Danish settl ements; in 867, the Vikings seized York and
2 CONT established their own kingdom in the southern part of Northumbria. The
2 CONT Vikings overcame two other major Anglo- Saxon kingdoms, East Anglia and
2 CONT Mercia, and their kings were either tortured to death or fled. Finally,
2 CONT in 870 the Danes attacked the only remaining indep endent Anglo-Saxon
2 CONT kingdom Wessex, whose forces were commanded by King Aeth elred and his
2 CONT younger brother Alfred. At the battle of Ashdown in 871, Alfred routed
2 CONT the Viking army in a fiercely fought phill assault. However, further
2 CONT defeats followed for Wessex and Alfred's brother died.
2 CONT As king of Wessex at the age of 21, Alfred (reigned 871-99) was a
2 CONT strongminded but highly strung b attle veteran at the head of remaining
2 CONT resistance to the Vikings in southern England. In early 878, the Danes
2 CONT led by King Guthrum seized Chippenham in Wil tshire in a lightning strike
2 CONT and used it as a secure base from which to devas tate Wessex. Local people
2 CONT either surrendered or escaped (Hampshire people fle d to the Isle of
2 CONT Wight), and the West Saxons were reduced to hit and run atta cks seizing
2 CONT provisions when they could. With only his royal bodyguard, a smal l army
2 CONT of thegns (the king's followers) and Aethelnoth earldorman of Somerset as
2 CONT his ally, Alfred withdrew to the Somerset tidal marshes in which he had
2 CONT probably hunted as a youth. (It was during this time that Alfred, in his
2 CONT pre occupation with the defence of his kingdom, allegedly burned some
2 CONT cakes which he had been asked to look after; the incident was a legend
2 CONT dating from early twelfth century chroniclers.)
2 CONT A resourceful fighter, Alfred reassessed his st rategy and adopted the
2 CONT Danes' tactics by building a fortified base at Athelne y in the Somerset
2 CONT marshes and summoning a mobile army of men from Wiltshire, Somerset and
2 CONT part of Hampshire to pursue guerrilla warfare against the Danes . In May
2 CONT 878, Alfred's army defeated the Danes at the battle of Edington.
2 CONT A ccording to his contemporary biographer Bishop Asser, 'Alfred attacked
2 CONT the wh ole pagan army fighting ferociously in dense order, and by divine
2 CONT will eventu ally won the victory, made great slaughter among them, and
2 CONT pursued them to th eir fortress (Chippenham) ... After fourteen days the
2 CONT pagans were brought to the extreme depths of despair by hunger, cold and
2 CONT fear, and they sought peace '. This unexpected victory proved to be the
2 CONT turning point in Wessex's battle for survival.
2 CONT Realising that he could not drive the Danes out of the rest of E ngland,
2 CONT Alfred concluded peace with them with the treaty of Wedmore. King Gut hrum
2 CONT was converted to Christianity with Alfred as godfather and many of the
2 CONT Danes returned to East Anglia where they settled as farmers. In 886,
2 CONT Alfred negotiated a partition treaty with the Danes, in which a frontier
2 CONT was demarca ted along the Roman Watling Street and northern and eastern
2 CONT England came unde r the jurisdiction of the Danes an area known as
2 CONT 'Danelaw'. Alfred therefor e gained control of areas of West Mercia and
2 CONT Kent which had been beyond the b oundaries of Wessex. To consolidate
2 CONT alliances against the Danes, Alfred marri ed one of his daughters
2 CONT Aethelflaed to the ealdorman of Mercia (Alfred himse lf had married
2 CONT Eahlswith, a Mercian noblewoman), and another daughter Aelfthr yth to the
2 CONT count of Flanders, a strong naval poAlfred the Great, King of Englandfrom 871 to his death in 899
2 CONT Born in 849 at Wantage, Berkshire
2 CONT Died on October 28, 899 and interred at Hyde Abbey, Winchester
2 CONT    Alfred prevented the Danish conquest of England, defeating them atEdington in 878 after a campaign of guerrila warfare. After his victoryhe allowed the Danes to keep their conquests in Mercia and East Angliaprovided that Guthru
2 CONC m, their king, was converted to Christianity. Alfredbuilt a navy of Warships to defend the south coast against further Danishinvasions (885 - 886; 892 - 896) and protected Wessex with a chain offortifications. He took London in 88
2 CONC 6, thereby gaining control of allEngland except the Danish areas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred married in 868 to Ealhswith, a Mercian noblewoman, daughter ofÆthelred Mucil "the Great", Ealdorman of the Gainas and his wife Eadburghof the Royal House of Mercia. This marriage revived the ties betweenSessex and Mercia; a
2 CONC nd provided Alfred with a powerful ally north of theThames.
2 CONT Alfred and Ealhswith had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelflæd (869 - 919) who married Æthelred II Ealdorman of Mercia.
2 CONT Edmund, born about 870, died young.
2 CONT Edward the Elder, King of England, born 869
2 CONT Æfthryth who married Baldwin II "the Bald", Count of Flanders. Click onBaldwin the Bald. Their greatX4 granddaughter was Mathilda of Flanderswho married William the Conqueror.
2 CONT Æthelgeofu, Abbess of Shaftesbury
2 CONT Æthelweard (880 - 922)
2 CONT
2 CONT Reigned 871-899. Prevented the Danish conquest of England, defeating themat Eddington (878) after a campaign of guerilla warfare. After thevictory he allowed the Danes to keep their conquests in East Anglia &Mercia provided that G
2 CONC uthrum, their king, was converted to Christianity.Alfred built a navy of warships to defend the south coast aginst furtherDanish invasions (885-886, 892-896) & protected Wessex with a chain offortifications. He took London (886) t
2 CONC hus gaining control of all Englandexcept the Danish areas. Quote from Winston Churchill: ("Alfred theGreat", chapter 7 of volume 1 of his "History of the English-SpeakingPeoples". An appreciation of "the greatest Englishman that e
2 CONC ver lived").Quote from Maurice Ashley in "Great Britain to 1688" about Alfred: "Hewas the greatest Englishman in early history" (p. 41). Banner was agolden dragon (the golden dragon was the banner of the kingdom of Wessex,Alfred's
2 CONC  personal banner was the White Horse). Since Alfred was the fifthson, it was never thought he would be King, and thus unlike his olderbrothers who have the royal mark of the ruling house of Wessex "AEthel"(or "Ethel") as a part of
2 CONC  their names, Alfred was instead named after hismother's folk).
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: The Newsletter of Anglo-Saxon Studies at The University of GeorgiaVol I, Number 3 (Spring, 1994): "ALFRED'S MILITARY ACHIEVEMENTS", AlexBruce
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred the Great is remembered for his two great victories--his success in
2 CONT re-establishing learning in his kingdom of Wessex (see Matheliende 1.2),
2 CONT and his success in defeating the seemingly unstoppable forces of theinvading Danes. No matter how much we venerate the revival of educationunder Alfred, the latter of these successes is, however, the greater;there would have been 
2 CONC no possibility of restoring the centers of learninghad Alfred not brought peace to his land. When Alfred became king of theWest Saxons in 871, he was already an experienced military leader, as hehad participated in several campaig
2 CONC ns against the invading Danes. TheDanes had been present in the British Isles since at least 789, but untilthe time of Alfred they had concentrated their efforts on subjugating theeastern lands of Britain. However, in 865 a great 
2 CONC army of Danes hungryfor land and wealth moved quickly through the kingdoms of East Anglia andNorthumbria. After these two kingdoms capitulated and paid tribute to theinvaders, the Danes turned to Mercia. There, in 868, they met bo
2 CONC thMercians and West Saxons; the two nations had formed an alliance that hadbeen strengthened that very year by the marriage of Alfred and Ealhswith,daughter of a Mercian ealdorman (see
2 CONT Asser ch. 29). Alfred and his elder brother King Aethelred personally led
2 CONT the Wessex contingent, yet not even the combined forces of the Merciansand
2 CONT the West Saxons could keep the Danes at bay. The Mercians, like the East
2 CONT Anglians and Northumbrians, had to "make peace"--that is, pay tribute.
2 CONT    In 871 Alfred's brother Aethelred died, making Alfred, last son of King
2 CONT Aethelwulf, the new king of the West Saxons. In that year as well theDanes
2 CONT turned their attention to Alfred's kingdom, and for the next four years,
2 CONT until 875, Alfred bought peace for his people by paying tribute to the
2 CONT Danes. At first the invaders seemed satisfied, but in 875 they began
2 CONT altering the terms of the peace.
2 CONT    That year, after collecting their tribute, the Danes did not leaveWessex
2 CONT as they had before, but lived there, peacefully but at the expense of the
2 CONT West Saxons, until 878. Then, in their desire to subjugate completely the
2 CONT people of Wessex, the Danes went on the offensive. Alfred fought back, yet
2 CONT in March of that year he and his followers were forced into hiding, andthe
2 CONT hope of the West Saxons was fading. But that May Alfred met the Danish
2 CONT force at Edington; "there he fought against the entire host, and put it to
2 CONT flight, and pursued it up to the fortification [probably Chippenham] and
2 CONT laid siege there a fortnight; and then the host gave him preliminary
2 CONT hostages and solemn oaths that they would leave his kingdom, and promised
2 CONT him in addition that their king would receive baptism; and they fulfilled
2 CONT this promise" (Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, year 878). Alfred had defeated the
2 CONT invading Danes, forcing them to submit to his terms. They subsequentlyleft
2 CONT Alfred and Wessex, turning to the continent for new lands to plunder. Yetthough this particular force left, Danes still inhabited Britain;Northumbria, East Anglia, and parts of Mercia were all still under theDanelaw. Alfred felt c
2 CONC onstantly threatened, and had to fight skirmisheswith the Danes for many years. To help preserve his hard-earned peaceAlfred developed stronger defenses for his land of Wessex. In thesouthern part of Britain he established several
2 CONC  new fortified cities,better than the smaller forts, where great groups of people could gatherfor protection. He reorganized his army so that at any one time half ofit was prepared for war. Finally, in 886, Alfred took the initiat
2 CONC ivehimself and attacked the Danish-held city of London in an attempt todiminish the lands ruled under the Danelaw. He succeeded, and for hisefforts all the "Angles and Saxons--those who had formerly been scatteredeverywhere and we
2 CONC re not in captivity with the Danes--turned willingly toKing Alfred and submitted themselves to his lordship" (Asser ch. 83). Atthis point Alfred seems to have come closest to rightly earning the title"King of England," though in
2 CONT reality he governed perhaps a quarter of the land now known as England.Once he had brought peace to his land Alfred began to implement hisreforms. He encouraged learning and the keeping of the Anglo-SaxonChronicle; he also establi
2 CONC shed a code of law, based on the Bible, which"shows how the king sought to maintain social order" (Introduction to"Extracts from the laws of King Alfred," Alfred the Great 163). Law codeswere certainly not new to the people of Wes
2 CONC sex; what was different aboutAlfred's code was that by basing his laws so clearly on Biblical law,"Alfred places his own activity as a law-giver in what he regards as itsproper context, effectively implying that the legislation wh
2 CONC ich followsstands in the same tradition and represents that of the new chosenpeople" (Introduction to Alfred the Great 39). In 892-3 Alfred's peacewas disturbed by the violent return of the Danes. These invaders, drivenoff the con
2 CONC tinent, seemed intent upon "the final conquest and settlementof England" (McElwee 32). During these campaigns Alfred won praise fromthe writer of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle (perhaps not very surprising,considering who the sponsor w
2 CONC as!) for his forethought and stratagems. Hisstanding army was able to fight off the invaders while the peopleremained safe in his fortified cities. Alfred also employed new tactics;he scouted out the enemy and destroyed them at se
2 CONC a using larger war-shipsof his own design. The Danes were thwarted at every turn, and were forcedto retreat, unfulfilled, from the island of Britain. The last years ofAlfred's life were more peaceful and devoted to scholarly pursu
2 CONC its. WhenAlfred died on October 26, 899, he left a culture which had perhapsalready seen its best days, but, thanks to Alfred's care and
2 CONT courage, would be remembered for centuries to come.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHY
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred the Great: Asser's Life of King Alfred and other Contemporary
2 CONT Sources. Trans. with an introduction and notes by Simon Keynes and Michael
2 CONT Lapidge. New York: Penguin Books, 1983.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle. Trans., ed., and introduced by G. N.Garmonsway. London: J. M. Dent & Sons Ltd., 1972.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asser. Life of King Alfred. In Alfred the Great: Asser's Life of King
2 CONT Alfred and other Contemporary Sources. Trans. with an introduction and
2 CONT notes by Simon Keynes and Michael Lapidge. New York: Penguin Books, 1983.
2 CONT
2 CONT McElwee, William. A Short History of England, from the Time of King Alfred
2 CONT to the Present Day. New York: Frederick A. Praeger, 1968.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Oxford History of Britain. Revised edition. Ed. Kenneth O. Morgan.
2 CONT Oxford: The Oxford University Press, 1988.
2 CONT
2 CONT Plummer, Charles. The Life and Times of Alfred the Great. New York: Haskel
2 CONT House Productions Ltd., 1902.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: The Newsletter of Anglo-Saxon Studies at The University of GeorgiaVol I, Number 2 (Winter, 1994) "ALFRED THE EDUCATOR" Kirk Appletoft:Alfred the Great, considered the first king of England, is known forsaving his land from de
2 CONC cimation by the invading Danes and thereby givinghis countrymen a sense of nationalism. Yet Alfred achieved more thanmilitary and political successes during his reign from 871-901; hisdedication to the teaching of the liberal arts
2 CONC  helped preserve theliterary tradition of the Anglo-Saxons.   Alfred's appreciation foreducation began very early in his life. In 853, at the age of four,Alfred was sent by his father, King Aethelwulf, to Pope Leo IV in Romefor in
2 CONC struction. This instruction, no doubt, focused on religion ratherthan on the liberal arts. But this trip to Rome, as well as the oneAlfred made two years later, certainly helped Alfred recognize the roleof the Church in education.
2 CONC  Alfred would fully acknowledge this role whenhe became king. Alfred's interest in the liberal arts was encouraged byhis stepmother Judith, who was the first to pique his interest inreading. Alfred's contemporary biographer, Asser
2 CONC , a bishop from Wales,records that Judith offered a book of Saxon poems to the first ofAethelwulf's four sons who could recite the book to her. To win the book,Alfred, who could not read, had an instructor read the book to him unt
2 CONC ilhe had memorized every word. According to Asser, this "desire for wisdom,more than anything else, together with the nobility of his birth, . . .characterized the nature of his noble mind" (Asser 75). Not until afterhe became kin
2 CONC g in 871 did Alfred learn to read and write. Asser, whomAlfred had called to serve in his court, seems to have been responsiblefor this feat. Asser would copy passages from the Bible for Alfred tostudy; Alfred would then eagerly t
2 CONC ranslate them into English. The dutiesof being the king, however, constantly interrupted Alfred's education.His entire reign was spent in a religious war with the Danes. He believedthat the invaders represented punishment from God
2 CONC  for the decay ofeducation, and the corresponding lack of understanding of Latinmanuscripts and psalms. So, for Alfred, his war became not a matter ofthe English fighting the Danes; it was the Christians fighting theheathens. This
2 CONT belief was one of the primary reasons for the large number of religious
2 CONT translations and the increasing interest in education during Alfred'sreign. As well, Alfred supported education because he recognized that "aking's raw material and instruments of rule are a well peopled land, andhe must have men 
2 CONC of prayer, men of war, and men of work . . . [for]without these tools he cannot perform any of the tasks entrusted to him"(Plummer 153). In order to have these tools at his disposal he broughtmany Latin scholars from the continent
2 CONC  to teach at his institutions. Healso, even when his war with the Danes was at its height, took time totranslate several Latin works on his own, including Pope Gregory theGreat's Pastoral Care, Boethius's Consolation of Philosophy
2 CONC  and St.Augustine's Soliloquies. So important to Alfred was the ability to readthat he began to de Mand that other nobles of the land be able to read.Asser recounts Alfred's admonishments to a group of judges who werepoorly educat
2 CONC ed; they were told "either to relinquish immediately [their]offices of worldly power . . . or else to apply [them]selves much moreattentively to the pursuit of wisdom." (Asser 110) Needless to say theychose the latter option.
2 CONT The effect of all of Alfred's educational reforms was that we, more than1000 years later, have a wealth of Anglo- Saxon prose and poetry to readand study. Without his dedication to learning we would all have beenpoorer.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHY
2 CONT
2 CONT Asser. Life of King Alfred. In Alfred the Great: Asser's Life of King
2 CONT Alfred and other Contemporary Sources. Trans. with an introduction and
2 CONT notes by Simon Keynes and Michael Lapidge. New York: Penguin Books, 1983.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bosworth, George F. Alfred the Great: His Life and Times. London:Macmillan
2 CONT and County, Ltd., 1914.
2 CONT
2 CONT Loyn, H. R. Alfred the Great. New York: Oxford University Press, 1967.
2 CONT
2 CONT Plummer, Charles. The Life and Times of Alfred the Great. New York: Haskel
2 CONT House Productions Ltd., 1902.
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: *The Chronicle of Fabius Ethelwerd, From the Beginning of the World
2 CONT to the Year of Our Lord 975*, translated by J A Giles in *Six Old English
2 CONT Chronicles*, London (Bohn), 1848, p 28, 37.: "Lastly, in the same year,king Alfred departed out of this world, that immoveable pillar of theWestern Saxons, that man full of justice, bold in arms, learned inspeech, and, above all o
2 CONC ther things, imbued with the divine instructions.For he had translated into his own language, out of Latin, unnumberedvolumes, of so varied a nature, and so excellently, that the sorrowfulbook of Boethius seemed, not only to the l
2 CONC earned, but even to those whoheard it read, as it were, brought to life again. The monarch died on theseventh day before the solemnity of All Saints, and his body rests inpeace in the city of Winston. Pray, O reader, to Christ our
2 CONC  Redeemer,that he will save his soul!"
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: Weis & Sheppard, *Ancestral Roots ... *, 7th Edition, 1992, p 2 :Alfred the Great, King of England, 871-899, b. Wantage, Berkshire, 849;d. 26 Oct. 899; m. 868, Ealhsith (Alswitha), d. ca. 905, dau. of EarlAethelred of Mercia 
2 CONC and Eadburgh. Alfred was one of the greatest men inhistory. He was crowned king at Winchester, 871; founded the BritishNavy, organized the militia, compiled a code of laws, built schools andmonasteries, and invited scholars to liv
2 CONC e at his court. He was a goodscholar and translated many books."
2 CONT
2 CONT SRCE:  Asser of Saint David, *Annals of the Reign of Alfred the Great,from
2 CONT A.D. 849 to A.D. 887* (or *Life of Alfred*), translated by J A Giles,1848, p 43-44. Giles says in the preface, p vi: In the year of our Lord'sincarnation 849, was born Alfred, king of the Anglo-Saxons, at the royalvillage of Wanat
2 CONC ing [Wantage], in Berkshire, which country has its namefrom the wood of Berrod, where the box-tree grows most abundantly. Hisgenealogy is traced in the following order. King Alfred was the son ofKing Ethelwild, who was the son of 
2 CONC Egbert, who was the son, of Elmund,who was son of Eafa, who was the son of Eoppa, who was the son of Ingild.Ingild, and Ina, the famous king of the West-Saxons, were two brothers.Ina went to Rome, and there ending this life honour
2 CONC ably, entered theheavenly kingdom, to reign there for ever with Christ. Ingild and Inawere the sons of Coenred, who was the son of Ceolwald, who was the son ofCudam, who was the son of Cuthwin, who was the son of Ceawlin, who wast
2 CONC he son of Cynric, who was the son of Creoda, who was the son of Cerdic,who was the son of Elesa, who was the son of Gewis, from whom the Britonsname all that nation Gewis [FOOTNOTE: The Gewissae, generally understoodto be the West
2 CONC -Saxons.], who was the son of Brond, who was the son ofBeldeg, who was the son of Woden, who was the son of Frithowald, who wasthe son of Frealaf, who was the son of Frithuwulf, who was the son ofFinn of Godwulf, who was the son o
2 CONC f Geat, which Geat the pagans longworshipped as a god. Sedulius makes mention of him in his metricalPaschal poem, as follows:--
2 CONT    When gentile poets with their fictions vain,
2 CONT    In tragic language and bombastic strain,
2 CONT    To their god Geat, comic deity,
2 CONT      Loud praises sing, &c.
2 CONT "Geat was the son of Taetwa, who was the son of Beaw, who was the son of
2 CONT Sceldi [= SCELDWA in trans. by Keynes & Lapidge], who was the son ofHeremod, who was the son of Itermon, who was the son of Hathra, who wasthe son of Gula [= HWALA in trans. by K & L], who was the son of Bedwig,who was the son of 
2 CONC Shem [= SETH in trans. by K & L, but this is probablyan error], who was the son of Noah, who was the son of Lamech, who wasthe son of Methusalem [=METHUSALAH], who was the son of Enoch, who wasthe son of Malaleel [= MAHALALEEL], w
2 CONC ho was the son of Cainian [= CAINANin trans. by K & L], who was the son of Enos, who was the son of Seth,who was the son of Adam."
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: William of Malmesbury, *Chronicle of the Kings of England*, c 1135,
2 CONT tr John Allen Giles, London (Henry G Bohn) 1847, p 113-122 passim: He was
2 CONT married to Ealhswith of the Gani in 868."Received the royal unction andcrown from pope Leo the fourth at Rome, acceded to the sovereignty andretained it with the greatest difficulty, but with equal valour,twenty-eight years and a 
2 CONC half. ..... For nine successive years battlingwith his enemies, sometimes deceived by false treaties, and sometimeswreaking his vengeance on the deceivers, he was at last reduced to suchextreme distress, that scarcely three counti
2 CONC es, that is to say,Hampshire, Wiltshire, and Somersetshire, stood fast by their allegiance,as he was compelled to retreat to a certain island called Athelney, whichfrom its marshy situation was hardly accessible. ..... [However, l
2 CONC ater]Alfred had reduced the whole island to his power, with the exception ofwhat the Danes possessed. ..... After England had rejoiced for 13 yearsin the tranquility of peace and in the fertility of her soil, thenorthern pest of b
2 CONC arbarians again returned. With them returned war andslaughter ..... The king himself was, with his usual activity, present inevery action, ever daunting the invaders, and at the same timeinspiriting his subjects, with the signal d
2 CONC isplay of his courage. Hewould oppose himself singly to the enemy; and by his own personalexertions rally his declining forces. The very places are yet pointed outby the inhabitants where he felt the vicissitudes of good and evilf
2 CONC ortune. ..... His children by Elswitha, the daughter of earl Athelred,were Ethelswitha, Edward who reigned after him; Ethel fled who wasmarried to Ethered earl of the Mercians; Ethelwerd, whom they celebrateas being extremely lear
2 CONC ned; Elfred and Ethelgiva, virgins. His [Alfred's]health was so bad that he was constantly disquited either by the piles orsome disorder of the intestines. It is said, however, that he entreatedthis from God, in his supplications,
2 CONC  in order that, by the admonition ofpain, he might be less anxious after earthly delights.
2 CONT   "Yet amid these circumstances the private life of the king is to beadmired and celebrated with the highest praise. For although, as some onehas said, "Laws must give way amid the strife of arms," yet he, amid thesound of trumpet
2 CONC s and the din of war, enacted statures by which hispeople might equally familiarise themselves to religious worship and tomilitary discipline. And since, from the example of the barbarians, thenatives themselves began to lust afte
2 CONC r rapine, insomuch that there was nosafe intercourse without a military guard, he appointed centuries, whichthey call "hundreds," and decennaries, that is to say, "tythings," sothat every Englishman, living according to law, must 
2 CONC be a member of both.If any one was accused of a crime, he was obliged immediately to producepersons from the hundred and tything to become his surety; and whosoeverwas unable to find such surety, must dread the severity of the law
2 CONC s. Ifany who was impleaded made his escape either before or after he had foundsurety, all persons of the hundred and tything paid a fine to the king.By this regulation he diffused such peace throughout the country that heordered g
2 CONC olden bracelets, which might mock the eager desires of thepassengers while no one durst take them away, to be hung up on the publiccauseways, where the roads crossed each other. ..... He erectedmonasteries wherever he deemed it fi
2 CONC tting ..... [Alfred sent forGrimmald] that by his activity he might awaken the study of literature inEngland, which was now slumbering and almost expiring. ..... Confiding inthese auxiliaries [Grimmald, Asser, Werefrith, Johannes 
2 CONC Scotus], the kinggave his whole soul to the cultivation of the liberal arts, insomuch thatno Englishman was quicker in comprehending, or more elegant intranslating. ..... He translated into English the greater part of theRoman aut
2 CONC hors ..... Moreover he infused a great regard for literatureinto his countrymen, stimulating them both with rewards and punishments,allowing no ignorant person to aspire to any dignity in the court. Hedied just as he had begun a t
2 CONC ranslation of the Psalms. ..... He had oneunusual and unheard of custom, which was, that he always carried in hisbosom a book in which the daily order of the Psalms was contained, forthe purpose of carefully perusing it, if at any
2 CONC  time he had leisure. Inthis way he passed his life, much respected by neighboring princes, andgave his daughter Ethelswitha in marriage to Baldwin earl of Flanders, bywhom he had Arnulf and Ethelwulf; the former received from his
2 CONC  father thecounty of Boulogne, from the other at this day are descended the earls ofFlanders. [Footnote by Giles: "Matilda, queen of William the First, wasdaughter of Baldwin earl of Flanders, the fifth in descent fromEthelswitha.
2 CONC "]
2 CONT    "Alfred, paying the debt of nature, was buried at Winchester, in themonastery which he had founded ..... They report that Alfred was first was
2 CONT first buried in the cathedral, because the monaster was unfinished, butthat
2 CONT afterwards, on account of the folly of the canons, who asserted that the
2 CONT royal spirit, resuming its carcass, wandered nightly through thebuildings,
2 CONT Edward, his son and successor, removed the remains of his father, and gave
2 CONT them a quiet resting-place in the new minster. [Footnote by Giles: "On its
2 CONT removal called Hyde Abbey."] These and similar superstitions, such as that
2 CONT the dead body of a wicked man runs about, after death, by the agency ofthe
2 CONT devil, the English hold with almost inbred credulity, borrowing them from
2 CONT the heathens, according to the expression of Virgil, "Forms such as flit,they say, when life is gone." [Virg. Aeneid, x. 641.]
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: British Monarchy Official Website: From the late 8th century, therewere attacks by Vikings from Scandinavia. The kingdoms of Northumbria andMercia were rapidly over-run and in 871 the Danish army attacked Wessex.After initial
2 CONC  setbacks, Alfred, King of Wessex (reigned 871-99) defeatedthe Danes at the Battle of Edington in 878. The Danes withdrew to an areanorth of a frontier running from London to Chester, which became known asthe 'Danelaw'. Alfred the
2 CONC n began a programme of reforms, includingestablishing a legal code, improving education and learning, andreforming the coinage. He also started
2 CONT a building programme of well-defended towns ('borough' comes from the OldEnglish burgh, a fortress) and a new navy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruled England 871-899; Born at Wantage 849; Buried at Winchester TheWitan, so it turned out, chose well, for Alfred, seasoned by manybattles, proved also to be a great ruler. Though he was not, so it issaid, a man of any great phy
2 CONC sical strength, yet he was a considerablewarrior. He defeated the Danish onslaught, led by Guthrum in 878, at thefamous battle of Ethandun. In 884 Alfred received the submission not onlyof the English, but also of many Welsh princ
2 CONC es, and, after 897, theDanes, too, had learnt not to give him further trouble. Maraudings weresuccessfully combated by enlarging the navy, in the defences of thecountry were improved by fortifying townships and by building forts. 
2 CONC Butgreat though Alfred's military achievements were, his real greatness laynot in war, which only destroys, but in the arts of peace. Much ofAlfred's work, like his culinary misfortune, is probably fabulous, but hedid three great 
2 CONC things which must be noted, for they alone are enough toraise him high above others of his age: he started, after the fashion ofCharles the Great, a court school to educate the nobility; he encouragedthe greatest scholars of the d
2 CONC ay to come to England; and he translatedand 'published' three medieval' best sellers', Bede's History, Boethius'sConsolation of Philosophy, and Gregory's Paaatoral Care.
1 NOTE Konge av Wessex fra 871-899.
1 NOTE Alfred, called The Great (849-99), king of the West Saxons (871-99), and one of the outstanding figures of English history. Born in Wantage in southern England, Alfred was the youngest of five sons of King Ethelwulf. On the death 
2 CONC of his brother Ethelred Alfred became king, coming to the throne during a Danish invasion. Although he succeeded in making peace with the Danes, they resumed their marauding expeditions five years later, and by early 878 they were
2 CONC  successful almost everywhere. About Easter of 878, however, Alfred established himself at Athelney and began assembling an army. In the middle of that year he defeated the Danes and captured their stronghold, probably at present-
2 CONC day Edington. During the following 14 years Alfred was able to devote himself to the internal affairs of his kingdom. By 886 he had captured the city of London, and soon afterward he was recognized as the king of all England.
2 CONT In 893 the Danes invaded England again, and the following four years were marked by warfare; eventually, the Danes were forced to withdraw from Alfred's domain. The only ruler to resist Danish invasions successfully, Alfred made h
2 CONC is kingdom the rallying point for all Saxons, thus laying the foundation for the unification of England.
2 CONT Alfred was a patron of learning and did much for the education of his people. He began a court school and invited British and foreign scholars, notably the Welsh monk Asser and the Irish-born philosopher and theologian John Scotus
2 CONC  Erigena, to come there. Alfred translated such works as The Consolation of Philosophy by the Roman statesman and philosopher Boethius, The History of the World by the Spanish priest Paulus Orosius, and Pastoral Care by Pope Grego
2 CONC ry I. Alfred's laws, the first promulgated in more than a century, were the first that made no distinction between the English and the Welsh peoples."Alfred," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1994 Microsoft Corporation. Copyri
2 CONC ght (c) 1994 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists, 7th Ed., Weis (Line 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Danes Invade England 
2 CONT
2 CONT The new invaders were Scandinavians from Norway and Denmark (see Vikings). The English called them Danes. Summer after summer these bold pirates rowed up the rivers in their longboats, plundered the rich monasteries, and went home
2 CONC  with the gold and gems. Soon after 850 a great force remained in England, bent on conquest. Then permanent settlers poured in. The Danes were farmers and traders as well as warriors. When they founded a town usually a port they f
2 CONC ortified it and opened a market. All of eastern England north of the Thames passed under the rule of the Danish jarls, or earls, and came to be known as the Danelaw, the part under Danish law. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Danes would probably have wiped out Christianity in England if it had not been for Alfred the Great, king of Wessex. Alfred defeated the Danes' great army at Chippenham in 878 and forced the Danish leader to sign a treaty agre
2 CONC eing to leave Wessex free. The Danes promised also to be baptized, and many did become Christians. Alfred began English prose literature by translating Latin books into Anglo-Saxon. He also built schools and ordered the 'Anglo-Sax
2 CONC on Chronicle', the first historical record of England, to be begun. (See also Alfred the Great.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT A century after Alfred's time the Danes started once more to raid England's shores. In 991 the incompetent Ethelred the Unready tried to buy them off by paying them yearly a large sum in silver, called the Danegeld, or Dane tax, w
2 CONC hich was raised by a heavy tax on the people. Nevertheless the Danes came again, and in 1016 Canute, the king of Norway and Denmark, made himself king of England also. He proved to be a wise and strong ruler, but after his death h
2 CONC is empire fell apart, and in 1042 the Danish dynasty in England ended. (See also Canute the Great.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The English line then returned to the throne with Edward, son of Ethelred. He had been reared by French monks and was called The Confessor.
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
