1 NOTE ALFRED THE GREAT: CONSIDERED ONE THE GREATEST MEN IN ENGLIS
2 CONT
2 CONT --Other Fields
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Number: 323
1 NOTE Alfred is the only English king [Canute was Danish] to have been desinated 'the Great' and also the first to have a contemporary biographer in the person of Asser, the Welsh-born Bishop of Sherborne. Alfred was born at the royal m
2 CONC anor of Wantage in Berkshire [now re-located in Oxfordshire] in 849, according to Asser, but elsewhere he is stated to have been twenty-three years old at his accession, implying a date of 847 or 848, which seems more likely in vi
2 CONC ew of his early prowess against the Danes.
2 CONT Alfred's great love of learning, which was to be manifested in later life, is said to have been inculcated by his mother who showed her sons a beautifully illuminated book of poetry and promised to give it to the first one to lear
2 CONC n to read it.  Alfred was the winner.  While still in his teens, Alfred became the right hand of his brother King Ethelred and proved himself an able commander in the struggle against the Vikings.  In 868 he married Ealhswith, the
2 CONC  daughter of Ethelred Mucil, Ealdorman of the Gaini [a Mercian tribal group], and Eadburh [or Eadburga, a descendant of the Royal House ofMercia.  She bore him a large family, of whom two sons and three daughters survived infancy.
2 CONT When Alfred succeeded his brother Ethelwulf as king in April 871, the country was largely overrun by Danish invaders.  A month after his accession he won a victory at Wilton and followed it up with anumber of other successful skir
2 CONC mishes in the south of England, but within two years the Danes had most of Mercia and Northumbria in their grasp.  Alfred's brother-in-law, King Burhed of Mercia, was driven out and went to Rome, where he died.  After winning a se
2 CONC a battle in 875, Alfred concluded an uneasy and short-lived peace treaty with the invaders in 876.  Further incursions into Devon soon followed and 'the host', as the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle terms the invaders, took refuge in Exeter
2 CONC  where  more peace negotiations took place.  They withdrew to Mercia in the autumn of 877.
2 CONT Alfred retired with his army into the island of Athelney, an inaccessible marshy area of Somerset.  To this period belongs the story of Alfred disguising himself as a wandering harper and entering the Danish camp to gain vital kno
2 CONC wledge of the enemy.  Also attributed to this time is the story of the King taking refuge in a swineherd's hut and being soundly berated by the swineherd's wife for burning the cakes she had set him to watch.
2 CONT Alfred left Athelney in May 878 and led his army into Wiltshire, being joined by large contingents from Somerset, Wiltshire and Hampshire.  He met the Danes at Ethandune [Edington] and gained a resounding victory, driving the enem
2 CONC y back into their stronghold at Chippenham.  The peace terms were settled a few weeks later, the Danes agreeing to withdraw from Wessex and return into East Anglia, Mercia, and Northumbria, which were ceded to them and became know
2 CONC n as the Danelaw. Their leader, Guthrum, accepted Christianity and Alfred stood sponsor at his baptism, giving him the name of Athelstan.
2 CONT Over the next few years Alfred consolidated his kingdom, strengthening the defences, encouraging learning and religion and codifying laws.  Alfred was a polymath and could turn his hand to most things, including the translation of
2 CONC  classical texts and the invention of a candle-clock.  For the better government of the realm he called together a great council of bishops, ealdormen and thanes to meettwice a year, the first embryo parliament.
2 CONT Asser tells us that Alfred was afflicted with a mysterious illness which attacked him periodically causing great pain.  The late Sir Iain Moncreiffe of that Ilk had suggested that this might be an early reference to the 'royal mal
2 CONC ady', porphyria.  Alfred died on 26 October 899 and was buried at Winchester.  His wife Ealhswith survived until 5 December 902.
2 CONT [Cope.FTW]
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://www3.dcs.hull.ac.uk/cgi-bin/gedlkup/n=royal?royal01964
2 CONT http://home.att.net/~a.junkins/anglo1.html#X128
1 NOTE Acceded 871-899.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred
2 CONT From the late 8th century, attacks by Vikings from Scandinavia increased.After a major invasion in 865, the kingdoms of Northumbria and Merciawere rapidly overrun, and in 871 the Danish army attacked Wessex. TheWessex forces under
2 CONC  the command of Alfred (reigned 871-99), then aged 21,defeated the Danes at the Battle of Edington in 878. The Danes withdrewto an area north of a frontier running from London to Chester and knownas 'Danelaw'.
2 CONT
2 CONT This victory did not finish the Danish threat, and Alfred reorganised theWessex defences by organising his army on a rota basis, so he could raisea 'rapid reaction force' to deal with raiders whilst still enabling histhegns and pe
2 CONC asants to tend their farms. Second, Alfred started abuilding programme of well-defended settlements across southern Englandas a defence in depth against Danish raiders. Alfred also ordered thebuilding of a navy of new fast ships t
2 CONC o patrol the coasts and meetinvaders before they penetrated inland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other reforms included establishing a legal code (assembled from the lawsof his predecessors and of the kingdoms of Mercia and Kent), andreforming the coinage. Illiterate in Latin until the age of 38, Alfredpromoted literacy, reli
2 CONC gion and education, and directed the translationof works of religious instruction, philosophy and history into thevernacular; this was partly so that people could read his orders andlegislation. The energetic royal authority demon
2 CONC strated in Alfred'spolicies presaged the Wessex kings' rule of all England during the nextcentury.
2 CONT [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Reigned 871-899.  Prevented the Danish conquest of England, defeatingthem at Eddington (878) after a campaign of guerilla warfare.  After thevictory he allowed the Danes to keep their conquests in East Anglia &Mercia provided that
2 CONC  Guthrum, their king, was converted to Christianity.Alfred built a navy of warships to defend the south coast aginst furtherDanish invasions (885-886, 892-896) & protected Wessex with a chain offortifications.  He took London (886
2 CONC ) thus gaining control of all Englandexcept the Danish areas. Quote from Winston Churchill:  ("Alfred theGreat", chapter 7 of volume 1 of his "History of the English-SpeakingPeoples".  An appreciation of "the greatest Englishman t
2 CONC hat everlived").  Quote from Maurice Ashley in "Great Britain to 1688" aboutAlfred:  "He was the greatest Englishman in early history" (p. 41).Banner was a golden dragon (the golden dragon was the banner of  thekingdom of Wessex, 
2 CONC Alfred's personal banner was the White Horse). SinceAlfred was the fifth son, it was never thought he would be King, and thusunlike his older brothers who have the royal mark of the ruling house ofWessex "AEthel" (or "Ethel") as a
2 CONC  part of their names, Alfred was insteadnamed after his mother's folk).
2 CONT
2 CONT REF: The Newsletter of Anglo-Saxon Studies at The University of GeorgiaVol I, Number 3 (Spring, 1994): "ALFRED'S MILITARY ACHIEVEMENTS", AlexBruce
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred the Great is remembered for his two great victories--his success in
2 CONT re-establishing learning in his kingdom of Wessex (see Matheliende 1.2),
2 CONT and his success in defeating the seemingly unstoppable forces of theinvading Danes. No matter how much we venerate the revival of educationunder Alfred, the latter of these successes is, however, the greater;there would have been 
2 CONC no possibility of restoring the centers of learninghad Alfred not brought peace to his land. When Alfred became king of theWest Saxons in 871, he was already an experienced military leader, as hehad participated in several campaig
2 CONC ns against the invading Danes. TheDanes had been present in the British Isles since at least 789, but untilthe time of Alfred they had concentrated their efforts on subjugating theeastern lands of Britain. However, in 865 a great 
2 CONC army of Danes hungryfor land and wealth moved quickly through the kingdoms of East Anglia andNorthumbria. After these two kingdoms capitulated and paid tribute to theinvaders, the Danes turned to Mercia. There, in 868, they met bo
2 CONC thMercians and West Saxons; the two nations had formed an alliance that hadbeen strengthened that very year by the marriage of Alfred and Ealhswith,daughter of a Mercian ealdorman (see
2 CONT Asser ch. 29). Alfred and his elder brother King Aethelred personally led
2 CONT the Wessex contingent, yet not even the combined forces of the Merciansand
2 CONT the West Saxons could keep the Danes at bay. The Mercians, like the East
2 CONT Anglians and Northumbrians, had to "make peace"--that is, pay tribute.
2 CONT      In 871 Alfred's brother Aethelred died, making Alfred, last son ofKing
2 CONT Aethelwulf, the new king of the West Saxons. In that year as well theDanes
2 CONT turned their attention to Alfred's kingdom, and for the next four years,
2 CONT until 875, Alfred bought peace for his people by paying tribute to the
2 CONT Danes. At first the invaders seemed satisfied, but in 875 they began
2 CONT altering the terms of the peace.
2 CONT      That year, after collecting their tribute, the Danes did not leaveWessex
2 CONT as they had before, but lived there, peacefully but at the expense of the
2 CONT West Saxons, until 878. Then, in their desire to subjugate completely the
2 CONT people of Wessex, the Danes went on the offensive. Alfred fought back, yet
2 CONT in March of that year he and his followers were forced into hiding, andthe
2 CONT hope of the West Saxons was fading. But that May Alfred met the Danish
2 CONT force at Edington; "there he fought against the entire host, and put it to
2 CONT flight, and pursued it up to the fortification [probably Chippenham] and
2 CONT laid siege there a fortnight; and then the host gave him preliminary
2 CONT hostages and solemn oaths that they would leave his kingdom, and promised
2 CONT him in addition that their king would receive baptism; and they fulfilled
2 CONT this promise" (Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, year 878). Alfred had defeated the
2 CONT invading Danes, forcing them to submit to his terms. They subsequentlyleft
2 CONT Alfred and Wessex, turning to the continent for new lands to plunder. Yetthough this particular force left, Danes still inhabited Britain;Northumbria, East Anglia, and parts of Mercia were all still under theDanelaw. Alfred felt c
2 CONC onstantly threatened, and had to fight skirmisheswith the Danes for many years. To help preserve his hard-earned peaceAlfred developed stronger defenses for his land of Wessex. In thesouthern part of Britain he established several
2 CONC  new fortified cities,better than the smaller forts, where great groups of people could gatherfor protection. He reorganized his army so that at any one time half ofit was prepared for war. Finally, in 886, Alfred took the initiat
2 CONC ivehimself and attacked the Danish-held city of London in an attempt todiminish the lands ruled under the Danelaw. He succeeded, and for hisefforts all the "Angles and Saxons--those who had formerly been scatteredeverywhere and we
2 CONC re not in captivity with the Danes--turned willingly toKing Alfred and submitted themselves to his lordship" (Asser ch. 83). Atthis point Alfred seems to have come closest to rightly earning the title"King of England," though in
2 CONT reality he governed perhaps a quarter of the land now known as England.Once he had brought peace to his land Alfred began to implement hisreforms. He encouraged learning and the keeping of the Anglo-SaxonChronicle; he also establi
2 CONC shed a code of law, based on the Bible, which"shows how the king sought to maintain social order" (Introduction to"Extracts from the laws of King Alfred," Alfred the Great 163). Law codeswere certainly not new to the people of Wes
2 CONC sex; what was different aboutAlfred's code was that by basing his laws so clearly on Biblical law,"Alfred places his own activity as a law-giver in what he regards as itsproper context, effectively implying that the legislation wh
2 CONC ich followsstands in the same tradition and represents that of the new chosenpeople" (Introduction to Alfred the Great 39). In 892-3 Alfred's peacewas disturbed by the violent return of the Danes. These invaders, drivenoff the con
2 CONC tinent, seemed intent upon "the final conquest and settlementof England" (McElwee 32). During these campaigns Alfred won praise fromthe writer of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle (perhaps not very surprising,considering who the sponsor w
2 CONC as!) for his forethought and stratagems. Hisstanding army was able to fight off the invaders while the peopleremained safe in his fortified cities. Alfred also employed new tactics;he scouted out the enemy and destroyed them at se
2 CONC a using larger war-shipsof his own design. The Danes were thwarted at every turn, and were forcedto retreat, unfulfilled, from the island of Britain. The last years ofAlfred's life were more peaceful and devoted to scholarly pursu
2 CONC its. WhenAlfred died on October 26, 899, he left a culture which had perhapsalready seen its best days, but, thanks to Alfred's care and
2 CONT courage, would be remembered for centuries to come.
2 CONT
