2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86thelfl%C3%A6d
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, (d. 12 June 918) was the eldest daughter of Alfred the Great, king of the Anglo-Saxon kingdom of Wessex, and his queen, Ealhswith. Æthelflæd was born at the height of the Viking invasions of Englan
2 CONC d. Her father married her to Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians. After his death in 911 she ruled Mercia until her own death in 918. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle referred to her as the Myrcna hlæfdige, "Lady of the Mercians".
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethelflaed was according to Asser the first born child of Alfred the Great and his Mercian queen Ealhswith[1] who was a descendant of king Coenwulf of Mercia through her own mother Eadburh. Ethelflaed's date of birth is not known 
2 CONC but it was probably sometime beetween the marriage of her parents in 868 and birth of her brother Edward the Elder, whose own date of birth is not known but presumably took place before 878.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred was the fifth son of king Ethelwulf and was never expected to become king. However by 865 three of his brothers had died and Ethelred, the only other one still alive, was King of Wessex. Soon afterwards in the same year a g
2 CONC reat Viking army arrived and wreaked havoc in much of England. It would be the start of a long period of war between the Vikings and the Anglo-Saxons which lasted many battles until after the Battle of Eddington in 878 in which Al
2 CONC fred was king and commander.
2 CONT _______________________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-44069889
2 CONT
2 CONT Aethelflaed: The warrior queen who broke the glass ceiling
2 CONT 11 June 2018
2 CONT
2 CONT How does a ruler defeat bloodthirsty invaders, secure a kingdom and lay the foundations for England - and then almost get written out of history? Be a woman, that's how. Exactly 1,100 years after her death Aethelflaed, Lady of the
2 CONC  Mercians, is emerging from the shadows.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr Downham says one striking fact is something Aethelflaed did not do.
2 CONT
2 CONT "She did not remarry. This is for two likely reasons - a new husband would become her lord with control over her territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT "But also the religious literature of the time said a celibate woman was more manly and therefore she would be taken more seriously.
2 CONT
2 CONT "It also seems her only child, a daughter, did not marry at all."
2 CONT
2 CONT (Interesting article, regarding why we do not hear as much about her compared to Edward.)
