2 CONT
2 CONT Edward the Elder
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Anglo-Saxons
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	26 October 899 – 17 July 924
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation 	8 June 900, Kingston upon Thames
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Alfred the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Athelstan of England and/or Ælfweard of Wessex
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Ecgwynn, Ælfflæd, and Eadgifu
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Alfred the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Ealhswith
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	c.874-77
2 CONT
2 CONT Wantage, Wessex, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	17 July 924
2 CONT
2 CONT Farndon-on-Dee, Cheshire England
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	New Minster, Winchester, later translated to Hyde Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward the Elder (Old English: Ēadweard se Ieldra) (c. 874-7[1] – 17 July 924) was an English king. He became king in 899 upon the death of his father, Alfred the Great. His court was at Winchester, previously the capital of Wesse
2 CONC x. He captured the eastern Midlands and East Anglia from the Danes in 917 and became ruler of Mercia in 918 upon the death of Æthelflæd, his sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT All but two of his charters give his title as "king of the Anglo-Saxons" (Anglorum Saxonum rex).[2] He was the second king of the Anglo-Saxons as this title was created by Alfred.[2] Edward's coinage reads "EADVVEARD REX."[3] The 
2 CONC chroniclers record that all England "accepted Edward as lord" in 920.[4] But the fact that York continued to produce its own coinage suggests that Edward's authority was not accepted in Northumbria.[5] Edward's eponym "the Elder" 
2 CONC was first used in Wulfstan's Life of St Æthelwold (tenth century) to distinguish him from the later King Edward the Martyr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
