2 CONT Edward the Elder, King of England from 899 to his death in 924
2 CONT Born in 869
2 CONT Died on July 17, 924 at Farndon-on-Dee and interred at WinchesterCathedral, England
2 CONT    Edward built upon the successes of his father Alfred and set aboutcreating a new Kingdom of England. He defeated the Danes in 918, takingEast Anglia, and also conquered Mercia in 918 and Northumbria in 920.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward married first to Ecgwyn (died circa 901) and they had thefollowing children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelstan, King of England 924 - 939; born in 894 and died on October 27,939
2 CONT Alfred who died young.
2 CONT St. Edith who married Sihtric Caoch (Sigtryggr Gale), King of Dublin &York. On widowhood became a nun at Polesworth Abbey and transferred toTamworth Abbey, Glocestershire where she was elected Abbess. She wascanonised and her feas
2 CONC t day is July 15th.
2 CONT Edward married second to Ælflæda (died 920), a daughter of Æthelhelm,Ealdorman of Wiltshire and a granddaughter of Æthelred I, King of England866 - 871. Thus, Edward and Ælflæda were first cousins once removed.
2 CONT Edward the Elder and Ælflæda had the following children:
2 CONT Ælfweard, King of England for a brief period in 924. He died on August 1,924.
2 CONT Edgifu (902 - 951) who married Charles III "the Simple", King of France
2 CONT Edhilda who married Hugh Capet "the Great" of Neustria, Count of Paris
2 CONT Eadgyth (Edith), died January 26, 946, who married Otto I "the Great",King of Germany
2 CONT Ælfgifu who is said to have married "a prince near the Alps", likelyBoleslaw II "the Pious", Duke of Bohemia or perhaps Conrad "the Pacific",King of Burgundy
2 CONT Edward and Ælflæda are also said to have had the following children:
2 CONT Edfæd, a nun at Wilton
2 CONT Edwin possibly a Sub King of Kent who drowned in 933.
2 CONT Ælfæda, a nun at Winchester who died circa 963.
2 CONT Æthelfæda, Abbess of Romsey
2 CONT Æthelhild, a recluse who died and was interred at Romsey Abbey, Hampshire
2 CONT Edward married third to Eadgifu (Edgiva) a daughter of Sigehelm,Ealdorman of Kent and they had the following children:
2 CONT Edgifu who married Louis II, King of Provence/Arles
2 CONT St. Edburga, a nun at Nunnaminster who died on June 15, 960
2 CONT Edmund I the Elder, King of England 939 - 946, born in 921
2 CONT Eadred, King of England 946 - 955, born circa 924 and died on November23, 955.
2 CONT Gregory, Abbot of Einsiedlen who may have been an illegitimate son ofKing Edward by another mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reigned 899-924. He defeated the Danes (918), taking East Anglia, & alsoconquered Mercia (918) and Northumbria (920).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruled England 899-924 Crowned at Kingston upon Thames 31 May 902 Buriedat Winchester Possibly Edward reigned for a time with his father, Alfred,or as a lesser king under him, for he signed charters as "rex" in 898.But in any case 
2 CONC he was elected king by the Witan when Alfred died. Hespent the early part of his reign in fighting the apparently interminablewars against the Danes which so vitiated this era of our history. Inthese wars he was helped by his sist
2 CONC er Ethelfleda, Lady of Mercia, onwhose death he annexed Mercia and was acknowledged "father and lord" bymost of England, parts of Wales and Scotland, and also by many of theNorse leaders. The last years of Edward's reign were peac
2 CONC eful, and aboutthem little is known, for history in those days was principally concernedwith war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward, byname EDWARD THE ELDER (d. July 17, 924, Farndon on Dee,England), Anglo-Saxon king in England, the son of Alfred the Great. Asruler of the West Saxons, or Wessex, from 899 to 924, Edward extended hisauthority over almost 
2 CONC all of England by conquering areas that previouslyhad been held by Danish invaders. Edward ascended the throne upon hisfather's death in October 899, and in a battle in 902 his forces killed arival claimant, Aethelwald, who had al
2 CONC lied with the Danes. Afterdefeating the Northumbrian Danes at Tettenhall, he set out in August 912to subdue the Danes of the eastern Midlands and East Anglia. From 910 to916 he constructed a series of fortified enclosures around h
2 CONC is Kingdom ofWessex. At the same time, his sister, the Mercian ruler Aethelflaed,constructed a complementary series of fortresses in the northwestMidlands. In 917 Edward and Aethelflaed launched a massive offensive,quickly overwhe
2 CONC lming the entire Danish army of East Anglia. UponAethelflaed's death in June 918, Edward assumed control of Mercia, and bythe end of the year the last Danish armies in the Midlands had submitted.By that time Edward's kingdom inclu
2 CONC ded all the land south of the Humberestuary; in 920 he pacified Northumbria. Complete political unificationof England was achieved during the reign of his son and successor,Athelstan (reigned 924-939). [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD
2 CONC  '97, EDWARD]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: According to the pedigree of Augustine H. Ayers as contained onCD-100, Automated Archives, Automated Family Pedigrees #1, Eadgyth wasthe daughter of Edward the Elder, King of England, and Elfleda/Aelflaedof Wiltshire. Regard
2 CONC ing Edward, Encyclopaedia Britannica (1961 edition)states, "he was thrice married: (1) to Ecgwyn, a lady of rank, by whom hehad a son Aethelstan, who succeeded him, and a daughter Eadgyth, whomarried Sihtric of Northumbria in 924.
2 CONC  This marriage was probably anirregular one. (2) To Aelflaed, by whom he had two sons----Aelfweard, whodied a fortnight after his father, and Eadwine, who was drowned in933----and six daughters, Aethelflaed and Aethelhild, nuns, a
2 CONC nd fourothers (see AETHELSTAN). (3) To Eadgifu, the mother of Kings Edmund andEdred, and of two daughters."
2 CONT
2 CONT The article on Aethelstan states "one of Aethelstan's first public actswas to hold a conference at Tamworth with Sihtric, the Scandinavian kingof Northumbria, and as a result, Sihtric received Aethelstan's sister inmarriage. In th
2 CONC e next year Sihtric died and Aethelstan took over theNorthumbrian kingdom...By the marriage of his half-sisters he was broughtinto connection with the chief royal and princely houses of France andGermany. His sister Eadgifu marrie
2 CONC d Charles the Simple, Eadhild becamethe wife of Hugh the Great, duke of France, Eadgyth was married to theemperor Otto the Great, and her sister, Aelfgifu, to a petty Germanprince."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Edward article, Aethelstan and Eadgyth (who married Sihtric) hadthe same mother and implies no other children of that relationship. Inthe Aethelstan article, the Eadgyth who married Otto had a sister,Aelfgifu. Therefore, th
2 CONC is Eadgyth cannot be Eadgyth the sister ofAethelstan since Aethelstan had only one sister. The article on Edwardmentions six daughters by Aelflaed (two of whom are named, the ones whobecame nuns,) leaving four who are unnamed (and
2 CONC  referring the reader tothe article on Aethelstan), and two daughters by Eadgifu, we have a totalof six unnamed daughters. From the information in the Aethelstan article,we are provided with the names of four, Eadgifu, Eadhild, Ea
2 CONC dgyth, andAelfgifu. By reference from the Edward article, does this mean all fournamed in the Aethelstan article are the remaining unnamed daughters byAelflaed? We are still left with two unnamed daughters, and are unsure ifthey a
2 CONC re the two daughters of Eadgifu, or possibly one or more ofAelflaed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Aethelstan and Edward articles were written by A. M., the author ofthe Otto article is not identified. Since the Aethelstan and Edwardarticles were authored by the same person, the implication is that he/sheis including the na
2 CONC mes of Aelflaed's remaining four daughters in theAethestan article.
2 CONT
2 CONT The article on Otto I, the Great, in the same set of Britannica states,"In 929 he married Edith, daughter of Edward the Elder, king of theEnglish, and sister of the reigning King Aethelstan." Note the referenceto 'sister' and not 
2 CONC 'half-sister.' Does this mean this is the sameEadgyth/Edith who married Sihtric? This does not seem so inasmuch as theAethelstan article clearly states Eadgyth who married Otto had a sister,Aelfgifu.
2 CONT
2 CONT Did Edward have two daughters named Eadgyth? From the information given,it is impossible to determine the mother of the Eadgyth who married Otto.
2 CONT
2 CONT Additionally, in The Saxon and Norman Kings, by Christopher Brooke,Barnes & Noble, Inc., 1963, is found, in reference to the continentalfriends of Athelstan, "Henry I of Germany, who asked for Athelstan'ssister as wife to his son 
2 CONC Otto; Hugh the Great, duke of the Franks, andConrad duke of Burgundy, who married two other sisters of the king." ThisConrad, duke of Burgundy, must be the 'petty German prince' to whomAelfgifu was married, for, at that time, Burg
2 CONC undy was not a part ofFrance, but was Germanic.
2 CONT
2 CONT REFN: 3435
2 CONT Well-trained by Alfred, his son Edward 'the Elder' (reigned 899-924 ) was
2 CONT a bold soldier who defeated the Danes in Northumbria at Tettenhall in 9 10
2 CONT and was acknowledged by the Viking kingdom of York. The kings of
2 CONT Strathc lyde and the Scots submitted to Edward in 921. By military success
2 CONT and patien t planning, Edward spread English influence and control. Much
2 CONT of this was due to his alliance with his formidable sister Aethelflaed,
2 CONT who was married to t he ruler of Mercia and seems to have governed that
2 CONT kingdom after her husband' s death.
2 CONT Edward was able to establish an administration for the kingdom of
2 CONT E ngland, whilst obtaining the allegiance of Danes, Scots and Britons.
2 CONT Edward d ied in 924, and he was buried in the New Minster which he had had
2 CONT completed a t Winchester. Edward was twice married, but it is possible
2 CONT that his eldest so n Athelstan was the son of a mistress.Ancestral FileNumber:<AFN> 9GB3-CL
2 CONT [mary Stewart1.FTW]
