2 CONC  Holstein. This is most likely as indicated by her great love and devotion to the Danish people in her efforts to better their conditions and to educate them. Based upon the one supposition mentioned above, however, she was errone
2 CONC ously sealed to the above couple on 28 Sep 1937.
1 NOTE Succeeded his Father about 901. He raised the supremacy of Wessex into something little short of imperial authority, holding sway over Mercia, East Anglia and Northumberland. His death may have been in 924. Succeeded by the son of
2 CONC  his 1st marriage. {Burke�s Peerage & Chamber�s Biographical Dictionary} [GADD.GED]
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Anglo-Saxons 899-924. Also has birth date of 875 and death date of 17 Jul 924. [ROWLEYHR.GED]
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional information: Britannia.com http://britannia.com/history/monarchs/mon7.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Also have birth as 875 in Wessex, England. [Betz Homepage http://info.lu.farmingdale.edu/~betzja/gene]
2 CONT
2 CONT Also have death as 925 in Farrington, Berkshire, England. [Betz Homepage http://info.lu.farmingdale.edu/~betzja/gene]
1 NOTE He succeeded as king of the Angles and Saxons in 899, despite arebellion led by his cousin Athelwald (died 902) with the support ofthe Danes of Northumbria and East Anglia. After a protracted strugglehe defeated the Danes, and in 
2 CONC 912, on the death of his brother-in-lawAthelred, ealderman of Mercia, he annexed the cities of London andOxford and their environs. The Danes submitted formally in 918, andsoon thereafter the sovereignty of Eadward was acknowledge
2 CONC d by theNorth Welsh, the Scots, the Northumbrians, and the Welsh ofStrathclyde. Eadward was succeeded by his son Athelstan.
