1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1.         ALHRED (-after 774).  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that "Alhred succeeded to the kingdom of Northumbria" in 765 and reigned eight years[1060].  King Alhred was allegedly descended from Ealric, son o
2 CONC f Ida King of Deira:  Florence of Worcester records that "Alhredus" succeeded after "Moll" was expelled from "regnum Northanhymbrorum", adding that he was "filius Eanwini…qui fuit Byrnhom, qui fuit Bofa, qui fuit Bleacman, qui fui
2 CONC t Ealric, qui fuit Idæ"[1061].  This is another suspect line of descent.  It is not included in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle.  Simeon of Durham records the succession of "Alcred, prosapia Idæ regis exortus, ut quidam dicunt", the wor
2 CONC ding suggesting that he did not believe it to be true[1062].  He succeeded in 765 as ALHRED King of Northumbria.  The Continuator of Bede records that "Aluchredus" became king in 765[1063].  He authorised St Willehad's mission to 
2 CONC Frisia which led to the foundation of the archbishopric of Bremen.  He was deposed and expelled from York at Easter 774[1064], and fled into the land of the Picts.  m (768) OSGIFU, daughter of OSWULF King of Northumbria.  "Alhredu
2 CONC s rex et Osgeofu regina" wrote to Lullus, dated 773[1065].  Simeon of Durham records the marriage in 768 of "King Alcred" and "Osgearn"[1066].  Her ancestry is deduced from the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle which records that "Osred, Alhr
2 CONC ed's son, succeeded to the kingdom because he was the nephew of Ælfwald"[1067].  King Alhred & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >a)         OSRED (-murdered 14 Sep 792, bur Tynemouth monastery[1068]).  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle names "Osred, Alhred's son" when recording his accession[1069].  Simeon of Durham records that "King Elfwald" was killed "IX Kal O
2 CONC ct at…Scythlescester near the Wall" in 788 and succeeded by "his nephew Osred son of Alcred" who reigned for one year[1070].  He succeeded in 789 as OSRED II King of Northumbria[1071].  He was "betrayed and driven from the Kingdom
2 CONC " in 790[1072].  Simeon of Durham records that "king Osred" was "taken prisoner" in 790 "assumed the tonsure in the city of York and afterwards, driven by necessity, went into exile"[1073].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that 
2 CONC he fled to the Isle of Man, returned from exile and was restored as king, but was "seized and slain" in 792[1074].  Simeon of Durham records that "Osred…came secretly from his exile in Eufania [Man]", was captured by King Æthelred
2 CONC  in 792 and killed "at…Aynsburg XVIII Kal Oct" and buried at "the mouth of the river Tyne…monastery"[1075]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alhred had [one possible illegitimate child]: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >b)         [ALHMUND (-murdered 800).  Simeon of Durham records that "Alchmund son of king Alcred as some say" was seized and murdered by the "guardians of king Eardwulf" in 800[1076], implying that he was involved in a conspiracy
2 CONC  to kill the king.  The phrase “as some say” suggests doubt about his parentage.  Florence of Worcester does not mention this doubt when he records that "Alhmundus filius Alhredi regis Northymbrorum" was killed, undated[1077].  m 
2 CONC [(after 784) as her [second] husband, ---, widow of EALHMUND of Wessex King of Kent, daughter of ---.   According to a manuscript which recounts the founding of Wilton Monastery, “Elburga, filia Alqmundi martyris, filii Alrudi reg
2 CONC is Northumbrorum” was “soror Egberti Regis, ex parte regis”, clarifying that he was Ecgberht King of Wessex (“quia Egbertus fuit filius Alqmundi, filii Offæ Regis, de prosapia Inæ”)[1078].  The reliability of this manuscript is no
2 CONC t known.  The document dates the founding of Wilton abbey by King Echberht to 773, which is clearly anachronistic, and shows that it cannot be relied upon entirely.  It is probably safer to treat the narrative with caution until s
2 CONC ome other corroboration is found in another source.]  Alhmund & his wife had [one possible child]
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#AlhredNorthumbriadiedafter774
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Alchred was a descendant of King Eoppa and his son King Ida born 515 through Ida's son Alric bc542. Alchred's line up to Alric was father, Eahlwine bc 750, his father Byrnhom bc730, his father Bofa bc700, his father Blaecman bc660
2 CONC , his father Bofa bc610, his father Blaecman bc 570, who was the son of Alric b542, the son of King Ida b515. Quote from Project Medlands: from Florence of Worcester that Alchred was "filius Eanwini…qui fuit Byrnhom, qui fuit Bofa
2 CONC , qui fuit Bleacman, qui fuit Ealric, qui fuit Idæ" (Missing two generations).https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#AlhredNorthumbriadiedafter774
