1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elfwina of Mercia married Edulph Earl of Mercia, son of Ordgar , Earl of Devon. Elfwina married a nobleman of Wessex, and was mother of Leofwine, Earl and Duke of Mercia. She was spirited away by her uncle Edward w
2 CONC hen he siezed Mercia in 918
2 CONT   ————————————————————
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86lfwynn
2 CONT
2 CONT Ælfwynn (c. 888–after 919) was the daughter of Æthelred, ruler of English Mercia, and Æthelflæd, daughter of King Alfred the Great and herself ruler of Mercia after her husband's death. Following the death of her mother on 12 June
2 CONC  918, Ælfwynn too was for a short time ruler of Mercia. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle states that she was "deprived of all control in Mercia, and was led into Wessex three weeks before Christmas".
2 CONT
2 CONT Ælfwynn's parents may have married as early as 882 and not later than 887. According to William of Malmesbury, Ælfwynn was the only child of Æthelflæd and Æthelred. The date of her birth is nowhere recorded, but it is presumed tha
2 CONC t she was born soon after her parent's marriage, perhaps around 888. William's account states that her birth was a difficult one, and that this led her mother to abstain from further sexual relations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ælfwynn's father spent much of the decade after her birth on campaign with his father-in-law King Alfred and brother-in-law Edward ætheling (later King Edward the Elder). By 902 his health was poor and Æthelflæd was from then onwa
2 CONC rds the effective ruler of Mercia. William of Malmesbury states that Alfred sent his eldest grandson, Æthelstan son of Edward, to be educated at the court of Æthelflæd. William's panegyric on Æthelstan claims that he received a fi
2 CONC rst-class education in Mercia, and it is thought likely that Ælfwynn will have been equally well educated.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first contemporary written evidence of Ælfwynn is dated to around 904, a charter (S 1280) recording the lease of land by Æthelred and Æthelflæd for the traditional three lives—those of Æthelred, Æthelflæd and Ælfwynn—in and ar
2 CONC ound Worcester from Bishop Waerferth and the monks and clerics of Worcester Cathedral. Ælfwynn did not witness this charter, but she may have witnessed charter S 225 of circa 915, concerning lands around Farnborough, and she is ve
2 CONC ry probably the Ælfwynn who witnesses S 367 of circa 903 relating to lands in Buckinghamshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ælfwynn's mother appears to have died suddenly in the summer of 918. Unlike her mother, Ælfwynn may have lacked broad support. No opposition to Edward's decision to remove her from power and send her to Wessex in December 918 is r
2 CONC ecorded by the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle or elsewhere. Ælfwynn is sometimes considered the last ruler of Mercia, but that kingdom was not entirely absorbed into the kingdom of the Anglo-Saxons, later the kingdom of England, until much
2 CONC  later. Her cousin Æthelstan was ruler of Mercia only before becoming king of the Anglo-Saxons, and so too was King Edgar ruler of the Mercians under his elder brother King Eadwig.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no certain record of Ælfwynn after her removal from power. It is unlikely that she married and probable that she entered holy orders. It has been suggested that the religious woman named Ælfwynn who is the beneficiary of 
2 CONC charter S 535, a charter dated to 948 in the reign of King Eadred, should be identified with this Ælfwynn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebecca Tingle's 2005 young adult novel Far Traveler has an imagined Ælfwynn as its protagonist.
