2 CONT https://everything.explained.today/Ecgwynn/
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/290917566/ecgwynn-of_england
2 CONT
2 CONT Ecgwynn (also spelled Ecgwynna; Old English: Eċġwynn, meaning "sword joy";fl. 890s) was the first known consort of Edward the Elder, who reigned as King of the English from 899 to 924. She was the mother of Æthelstan (r. 924–939),
2 CONC  the first monarch to rule a unified England, and is also believed to have had a daughter who married Sitric Cáech, Norse king of Dublin, Ireland and Northumbria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virtually nothing is known about Ecgwynn's origins or personal life and her name is absent from all contemporary records and does not appear in any surviving sources until after the Norman Conquest. The earliest known reference co
2 CONC mes from William of Malmesbury, who Latinized her name as Egwinna and who is in fact the principal source for her existence.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT According to William of Malmesbury, Æthelstan was thirty years old when he became king in 924, placing his birth around 894 and suggesting that Ecgwynn's union with Edward the Elder occurred circa 893.[2][3] By this time, Edward h
2 CONC ad reached majority, and one of his priorities would have been to secure the continuation of Alfred's line.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT No contemporary sources record what became of Ecgwynn after Æthelstan's birth, but two developments are particularly relevant. First, William notes that, at Alfred's direction, Æthelstan was sent to be raised at the Mercian court 
2 CONC under the care of his aunt Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians.[5] Second, by 901, Edward had married Ælfflæd, daughter of Ealdorman Æthelhelm.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The reasons behind this transition remain uncertain. It is possible that Ecgwynn had died by 899, prompting Edward to seek a new consort.[3] Alternatively, the first marriage may have lacked the political weight necessary to reinf
2 CONC orce Edward's position as king of the English.[7] Some scholars suggest that Alfred himself may have arranged Edward's initial marriage, and that his death in 899 allowed Edward and his counsellors room to follow a different cours
2 CONC e.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT An anonymous daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that King Æthelstan arranged the marriage of his sister to Sitric Cáech (d. 927), Norse king of Northumbria and Dublin. The wedding took place on 30 January 926 at Tamworth, a prominent Mercian ro
2 CONC yal center.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to William of Malmesbury, the bride was a daughter of Ecgwynn, though he was unable to discover her name from any of the sources available to him.[1] Only later medieval writers offer possible identifications, though the
2 CONC ir reliability remains uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger of Wendover (d. 1236) and Matthew Paris (d. 1259) proposed that she was St Edith (Eadgyth), who, according to the Old English saints' list Secgan, was buried at the nunnery of Polesworth in Warwickshire, not far from Tamwort
2 CONC h.[10]
2 CONT The Chronicle of John of Wallingford, compiled in the early 13th century from earlier material, names Sitric's wife as Orgiue, possibly a corruption of Eadgifu or Eadgyth,[11] and claims that their son was Olaf, king of Northumbri
2 CONC a identified with the Norse-Gaelic ruler Amlaíb Cuarán.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT These accounts have received mixed evaluations from modern historians. Some scholars accept the possibility that her name was Eadgyth, or at least support Roger's identification.[13] However, historian Barbara Yorke argues that th
2 CONC e name Eadgyth is unlikely to have belonged to two daughters of Edward the Elder, given that another daughter by Ælfflæd bore the same name. Yorke instead suggests that Edith of Polesworth may have been an earlier namesake, unrela
2 CONC ted to Ecgwynn.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family background
2 CONT
2 CONT Ecgwynn's own family background and social status cannot be identified with any certainty. What little evidence there is appears in the main to be coloured by a controversy which surrounded Æthelstan's succession, contested as it 
2 CONC probably was by supporters of Edward's sons by Ælfflæd.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT William of Malmesbury claims that Alfred had intended the throne to go to Æthelstan, and to give ceremonial expression to his grandson's status as successor, personally invested him with a cloak, belt and sword.[2] Moreover, Alfre
2 CONC d is said to have ensured his education at the Mercian court of his aunt Æthelflæd.[5] A Latin acrostic poem, possibly contemporary (c. 893/4 x 899), in which a young Æthelstan appears to be addressed as future ruler, would seem t
2 CONC o lend credence to the idea that Æthelstan's eligibility for kingship was already acknowledged in the 890s.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Edward may have entertained other plans when his second wife Ælfflaed had borne him sons. While his intentions are unknown, it appears to have been Ælfweard, Edward's eldest son by Ælfflæd, who on 17 July 924 succeeded hi
2 CONC s late father in Wessex, while the Mercians chose Æthelstan for their king. By some mishap, Ælfweard died within a month and Wessex was ceded to Æthelstan, who thereby obtained his father's entire kingdom. His accession in Wessex,
2 CONC  however, met with considerable resistance. One indication of this is that his coronation at Kingston upon Thames was delayed until 4 December the following year (925).[16] William notes explicitly that "a certain Ælfred" at Winch
2 CONC ester opposed the succession on grounds that Æthelstan was a concubine's son and hence an illegitimate son.[17] Such allegations seem to have served the interests of a royal contender, especially Edwin, Ælfflæd's eldest surviving 
2 CONC son.[16] In a royal charter for a thegn (minister) called Ælfred, Edwin subscribes as cliton "ætheling", witnessing after Æthelstan, which implies that he was recognised as his heir to the throne.[18] The circumstances of his deat
2 CONC h in 933 suggest that any peaceful understanding which may have existed between the half-brothers had come to an end. The Annals of St. Bertin compiled by Folcuin the Deacon note laconically that Edwin, "driven by some disturbance
2 CONC  in his kingdom," attempted to sail to the continent, but was caught in a storm and drowned.[19]
