2 CONT
2 CONT Also known as Edgiva of Kent or Eadgifu.
2 CONT
2 CONT 946-55 Politically Influential Dowager Queen Edgiva of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also known as Eadgifu, she was a dominant force during the reign of her son Edred (924-46-46), who came on the throne when his older brother, Edmund the Magnificent was murdered in 946. She was the third wife of King Edward of Wes
2 CONC sex (Ca. 871-88-924). 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was daughter of Sigehelm, Ealdrman of Kent, mother of 4 children, and lived (905-68). [http://www.guide2womenleaders.com/womeninpower/Womeninpowe-chronological1.htm].
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eadgifu_of_Kent
2 CONT
2 CONT Eadgifu of Kent (also Edgiva or Ediva) (died August 25, 968) was the third wife of Edward the Elder, King of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eadgifu was the daughter of Sigehelm, Ealdorman of Kent (died 903). She became the mother of two sons, Edmund I of England, later King Edmund I, and Eadred of England, later King Eadred, and a daughter, Saint Eadburh of Winchester
2 CONC . Eadgifu survived Edward by many years, dying in the reign of her grandson Edgar. As queen dowager, her position seem to have been higher than that of her daughter-in-law; In a Kentish charter datable between 942 and 944, her dau
2 CONC ghter-in-law Ælfgifu of Shaftesbury subscribes herself as the king's concubine (concubina regis), with a place assigned to her between the bishops and ealdormen. By comparison, Eadgifu subscribes higher up in the witness list as m
2 CONC ater regis, after her sons Edmund and Eadred but before the archbishops and bishops
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eadgifu_of_Kent
