1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Saint Eadburh or Edburga (died June 15, 960) was the daughter of King Edward the Elder of England and his third wife, Eadgifu of Kent. There is little contemporary information for her life, but in a Winchester char
2 CONC ter dated 939, she appears as the beneficiary of land in Hampshire granted by her brother King Athelstan.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edburga_of_Winchester
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#Eadgifudiedafter951
2 CONT
2 CONT EADBURGA (-15 Jun 960, bur Nunnaminster Abbey, transferred to Pershore Abbey, Worcestershire).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger of Hoveden names her as the daughter of King Edward by "regina Edgiva", although he also attributes the king's son Eadwin and three other daughters to the king's third marriage[1710].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Book of Hyde names "sanctam Edburgam Deo dictam...[et] Edgivam" as the two daughters of King Eadweard by his second wife "Edgiva", specifying that the former was buried "in monasterio monialium Wyntoniæ"[1711].  A nun at Nunna
2 CONC minster Abbey, Winchester.  She was canonised as St Edburga of Winchester, feast day 15 June[1712].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a nun at, and possibly abbess of, the Nunnaminster in Winchester where she was buried. Following her canonisation in 972, some of her remains were translated to Pershore Abbey in Worcestershire, which is dedicated to her. 
2 CONC Her feast is celebrated June 15.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the twelfth century, a Latin Life was written for her by Osbert de Clare, who became prior of Westminster in 1136 (and who also wrote a Life of King Edward the Confessor).[2] Her cult continued to flourish to judge by the Lives
2 CONC  written in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Sawyer no. 446
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ Ridyard, S.J. *ed.). In eadem, The Royal Saints of Anglo-Saxon England. 253 ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sawyer no. 446
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Osbert de Clare, Vita Edburgae, MS. Laud Misc. 114, f. 85–120 (Bodleian, Oxford), ed. S.J. Ridyard, The Royal Saints of Anglo-Saxon England. A Study of West Saxon and East Anglian Cults. Cambridge Studies in Medieval Life an
2 CONC d Thought 4. Cambridge, 2008. 253 ff (Appendix).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anonymous, De vita sanctae Edburgae virginis, preserved in the early fourteenth-century MS Lansdowne 436, f. 41v-43v (British Library, London), ed. Laurel Braswell, "Saint Edburga" (see below). 329-33.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Lectiones in Breviary of Hyde Abbey (late 13th century), Rawlinson liturg. E I and Gough liturg. 8 (Bodleian, Oxford)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Middle English Life (late 13th century), Egerton 1993, f. 160-1 (BL, London); Eng. Poet. A I f. 32-32v and Bodley 779, f. 282-293v (Bodleian, Oxford), ed. Laurel Braswell, "Saint Edburga" (see below). 329-33.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
