1 OCCU cr Kingston-upon-Thames 29.11.939; King of England from 939 until his death; Roi de Wessex, d'Angleterre, 940, murdered king, King of England, The Magnificant, King 939 - 946, Roi d'Angleterre, King of the English
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Edmund I, the Magnificent, King of England (20ggf)
2 CONT Name: King Edmund
2 CONT Born: c.922
2 CONT Parents: Edward the Elder and Edgiva
2 CONT Relation to Charles III: 31st great-grandfather
2 CONT House of: Wessex
2 CONT Ascended to the throne: October 27, 939
2 CONT Crowned: November 29, 939 at Kingston-upon-Thames, aged c.18
2 CONT Married: 1) Elgiva (2) Ethelfleda
2 CONT Children: 2 sons Edwy and Edgar
2 CONT Died: May 26, 946 at Pucklechurch near Bath (murdered)
2 CONT Buried at: Glastonbury
2 CONT Reigned for: 6 years, 6 months, 28 days
2 CONT Succeeded by: his brother Edred
2 CONT King of England 939–46. The son of Edward the Elder, he succeeded his half-brother, Athelstan, as king in 939. He succeeded in regaining control of Mercia, which on his accession had fallen to the Norse inhabitants of Northumbria,
2 CONC  and of the Five Boroughs, an independent confederation within the Danelaw. He then moved on to subdue the Norsemen in Cumbria and finally extended his rule as far as southern Scotland. As well as uniting England, he bolstered his
2 CONC  authority by allowing St Dunstan to reform the Benedictine order. He was killed in 946 at Pucklechurch, Gloucestershire, by an outlawed robber.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Timeline for King Edmund
2 CONT Year	Event
2 CONT 939	Edmund becomes King. Scandinavian forces from Northumbria overrun the East Midlands.
2 CONT 942	Edmund re-establishes control over Northumbria and rules a united England.
2 CONT 943	Edmund extends his rule into southern Scotland,
2 CONT 945	Dunstan becomes abbot of Glastonbury Abbey
2 CONT 945	Edmund conquers Strathclyde, but Cumbria is annexed by the Scots.
2 CONT 946	Edmund murdered at a party in Pucklechurch
2 CONT
