2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Was also called "The Great." 3rd Count of Flanders (918-964, 962-964)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Arnulf of Flanders''' (c. 890 – March 28, 965), called the Great, was the third Count of Flanders, who ruled the County of Flanders, an area that is now northwestern Belgium and southwestern Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf was the son of count Baldwin II of Flanders and Ælfthryth of Wessex, daughter of Alfred the Great.[1] Through his mother he was a descendant of the Anglo-Saxon kings of England, and through his father, a descendant of Charl
2 CONC emagne.[2] Presumably Arnulf was named after Saint Arnulf of Metz, a progenitor of the Carolingian dynasty.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT At the death of their father in 918, Arnulf became Count of Flanders while his brother Adeloft or Adelolf succeeded to the County of Boulogne.[1] However, in 933 Adeloft died, and Arnulf took the countship of Boulogne for himself,
2 CONC  but later conveyed it to his nephew, Arnulf II.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf I greatly expanded Flemish rule to the south, taking all or part of Artois, Ponthieu, Amiens, and Ostrevent. He exploited the conflicts between Charles the Simple and Robert I of France, and later those between Louis IV and
2 CONC  his barons.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his southern expansion Arnulf inevitably had conflict with the Normans, who were trying to secure their northern frontier. This led to the 942 murder of the Duke of Normandy, William Longsword, at the hands of Arnulf's men.[5] 
2 CONC The Viking threat was receding during the later years of Arnulf's life, and he turned his attentions to the reform of the Flemish government.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Arnulf's first wife is unknown but he had at least one daughter by her:[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Name unknown; married Isaac of Cambrai. Their son Arnulf succeeded his father as Count of Cambrai.[6] In 934 he married Adele of Vermandois, daughter of Herbert II of Vermandois.[1] Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Hildegarde, born c. 934, died 990; she married Dirk II, Count of Holland. It is uncertain whether she is his daughter by his first or second wife.[6]
2 CONT # Liutgard, born in 935, died in 962; married Wichmann IV, Count of Hamaland.[1]
2 CONT # Egbert, died 953.[1]
2 CONT # Baldwin III of Flanders (c. 940 – 962), married Mathilde of Saxony († 1008), daughter of Hermann Billung.[1]
2 CONT # Elftrude; married Siegfried, Count of Guînes.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Succession'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf made his eldest son and heir Baldwin III of Flanders co-ruler in 958, but Baldwin died untimely in 962, so Arnulf was succeeded by Baldwin's infant son, Arnulf II of Flanders.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1: a b c d e f g h Detlev Schwennicke, Europäische Stammtafeln: Stammtafeln zur Geschichte der Europäischen Staaten, Neue Folge, Band II (Verlag von J. A. Stargardt, Marburg, Germany, 1984), Tafel 5
2 CONT
2 CONT 2: The Annals of Flodoard of Reims, 919–966, ed. Steven Fanning & Bernard S. Bachrach (University of Toronto Press, CA, 2011), p. xx
2 CONT
2 CONT 3: Philip Grierson, 'The Relations between England and Flanders before the Norman Conquest', Transactions of the Royal Historical Society, Vol. 23 (1941), p. 86 n. 1
2 CONT
2 CONT 4: Renée Nip, 'The Political Relations between England and Flanders (1066–1128)', Anglo-Norman Studies 21: Proceedings of the Battle Conference 1998, ed. Christopher Harper-Bill (The Boydell Press, Woodbridge, UK, 1999), p. 150
2 CONT
2 CONT 5: David Nicholas, Medieval Flanders (Longman Group UK Limited, London, 1992), p. 40
2 CONT
2 CONT 6: a b c Heather J. Tanner, Families, Friends and Allies: Boulogne and Politics in Northern France and England, C.879–1160 (Brill, Leiden, Netherlands, 2004) p. 55 n. 143
2 CONT
