2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~havens5/p35255.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Adaloff sur Mer de Therouanne1,2
2 CONT
2 CONT b. circa 893, d. 13 November 933, #35255
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth* 	circa 893 	Adaloff sur was born circa 893 at St Pol sur Mer, Dunkerque, Artois, France.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage* 	circa 917 	Adaloff sur Mer de Therouanne married Mahaut de Crequy circa 917.2  
2 CONT
2 CONT Death* 	13 November 933 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Adaloff sur died on 13 November 933 at Therouanne, at Artois, at France.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 	  	Mahaut de Crequy b. circa 895, d. after 922
2 CONT
2 CONT Child  	1. 	Maude de St Pol sur Mer de Therouanne+ b. c 918, d. 9651
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Download, Larry Overmire e-mail address http://awt.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=glencoe&id=I19321 9 Nov 2003 Citing Jim Weber.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Download, Jim Weber e-mail address 14Nov2003 http://worldconnect.genealogy.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jweber&id=I26481 citing Newsgroup: soc.genealogy.medieval, at groups - google.com Page: Todd A. Farmerie, 13 M
2 CONC ar 2001 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelolf, Count of Boulogne (- 933) was a son of Baldwin II, Count of Flanders and of Ælfthryth. He succeeded his father in 918 as count of Saint-Pol, Boulogne and Thérouanne. He was also the leken-abbot of the Abbey of Saint Berti
2 CONC nus.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the father of:
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf II, Count of Boulogne (-971) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin (-973), guardian of Arnulf II, Count of Boulogne.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Adelolf, Count of Boulogne[1] (died 13 November 933) was a son of Baldwin II, Count of Flanders, and of Ælfthryth. He was probably named for his maternal great-grandfather, King Æthelwulf of Wessex.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin II's extensive lands and many offices in what is now the north of modern France and the west of Belgium were divided among his sons on his death in 918. The elder, Arnulf, became Count of Flanders. Adelolf succeeded his fa
2 CONC ther as count of Saint-Pol, Count of Boulogne and of Thérouanne. He was also the lay abbot of the Abbey of Saint Bertinus (Saint-Bertin) at Saint-Omer.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 926 Adelolf was sent as an ambassador to his maternal first cousin King Æthelstan of England by Count Hugh the Great, effective ruler of northern France under Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy, who had been elected king of France in 92
2 CONC 3. Adelolf was to seek the English king's agreement to a marriage between Hugh and another of Æthelstan's sisters. Among the lavish gifts sent to Æthelstan, an avid collector of relics, were said to be the sword of the Roman Emper
2 CONC or Constantine the Great and the Holy Lance. The embassy was a success and Hugh was married to Æthelstan's half-sister Eadhild.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelolf was the father of Arnulf II, Count of Boulogne (died 971), and of an illegitimate son named Baldwin (died 973) who was guardian of Arnulf II, Count of Flanders.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Folcwine's Gesta abbatum Sancti Bertini Sithiensium (Deeds of the abbots of Saint-Bertin), Adelolf died on 13 November 933. He was buried at Saint-Bertin.[5]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Adelolf, Count of Boulogne (- 933) was a son of Baldwin II, Count of Flanders and of Ælfthryth. He succeeded his father in 918 as count of Saint-Pol, Boulogne and Thérouanne. He was also the leken-abbot of the Abbey of Saint Berti
2 CONC nus. He was the father of: Arnulf II, Count of Boulogne (-971) Baldwin (-973), guardian of Arnulf II, Count of Boulogne. Adelolf, Count of Boulogne[1] (died 13 November 933) was a son of Baldwin II, Count of Flanders, and of Ælfth
2 CONC ryth. He was probably named for his maternal great-grandfather, King Æthelwulf of Wessex. Baldwin II's extensive lands and many offices in what is now the north of modern France and the west of Belgium were divided among his sons 
2 CONC on his death in 918. The elder, Arnulf, became Count of Flanders. Adelolf succeeded his father as count of Saint-Pol, Count of Boulogne and of Thérouanne. He was also the lay abbot of the Abbey of Saint Bertinus (Saint-Bertin) at 
2 CONC Saint-Omer. In 926 Adelolf was sent as an ambassador to his maternal first cousin King Æthelstan of England by Count Hugh the Great, effective ruler of northern France under Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy, who had been elected king of 
2 CONC France in 923. Adelolf was to seek the English king's agreement to a marriage between Hugh and another of Æthelstan's sisters. Among the lavish gifts sent to Æthelstan, an avid collector of relics, were said to be the sword of the
2 CONC  Roman Emperor Constantine the Great and the Holy Lance. The embassy was a success and Hugh was married to Æthelstan's half-sister Eadhild. Adelolf was the father of Arnulf II, Count of Boulogne (died 971), and of an illegitimate 
2 CONC son named Baldwin (died 973) who was guardian of Arnulf II, Count of Flanders. According to Deeds of the abbots of Saint-Bertin, Adelolf died on 13 November 933. He was buried at Saint-Bertin 
