2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that Gunnhild, widow of ex-King Eirik of Norway, arranged the marriage of their daughter Ragnhild to “Earl Thorfinn Skull-Splitter” before she and her sons left Orkney[210], which can probably be dated to [
2 CONC 955] or soon after. Snorre records the marriage of "King Eirik's daughter, Ragnhild" and "Arnfin, a son of Thorfin Hausakljufer" at the time the ex-king's sons left Orkney[211]. She married secondly her brother-in-law, Havard Jarl
2 CONC  of Orkney. Orkneyinga Saga records that “Ragnhild Eirik´s daughter plotted the death of her husband Arnfinn at Murkle in Caithness” before marrying “his brother Havard the Fecund who succeeded to the earldom”, but that she plotte
2 CONC d her second husband´s death with his “nephew…Einar Buttered-Bread” (whom she promised to marry), that she then plotted with “Einar Hardmouth, the son of another of Havard´s sisters” to kill Einar Buttered-Bread, but that she fina
2 CONC lly married “Ljot the brother of Arnfinn and Havard”[212]. She married thirdly her brother-in-law, Ljot Jarl of Orkney.]
2 CONT
2 CONT d) [HAVARD Torfinnsson "Season-prosperous" (-murdered, bur Stennis, Hrossey).
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…Ljot…Skuli” as the five sons of Thorfinn[213].
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Skule, the sons of Thorfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when Queen Gunhild and her sons returned to Orkney[214]. In a later saga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlod
2 CONC ver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[215]. He succeeded his brother as Jarl of Orkney. Orkneyinga Saga records that Havard was killed by his “nephew…Einar Buttered-Bread” at “Stenness on Mainland”[216].
2 CONT
2 CONT m as her second husband, RAGNHILD Eiriksdatter, widow of ARNFINN Torfinnsson Jarl of Orkney, daughter of ERIK I "Blodøks/Blood-axe" King of Norway & his wife Gunhild Gormsdatter. Orkneyinga Saga records that “Ragnhild Eirik´s daug
2 CONC hter plotted the death of her husband Arnfinn at Murkle in Caithness” before marrying “his brother Havard the Fecund who succeeded to the earldom”, but that she plotted her second husband´s death with his “nephew…Einar Buttered-Br
2 CONC ead” (whom she promised to marry), that she then plotted with “Einar Hardmouth, the son of another of Havard´s sisters” to kill Einar Buttered-Bread, but that she finally married “Ljot the brother of Arnfinn and Havard”[217]. She 
2 CONC married thirdly her brother-in-law, Ljot Jarl of Orkney.]
2 CONT
2 CONT e) [LJOT Torfinnsson (-killed in battle). Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…Ljot…Skuli” as the five sons of Thorfinn[218].
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Skule, the sons of Thorfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when Queen Gunhild and her sons returned to Orkney[219]. In a later saga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlod
2 CONC ver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[220]. He succeeded his brother as Jarl of Orkney. Orkneyinga Saga records that, after the death of “Skuli, Ljot´s brother” whose invasion of Orkney was repelled, “Earl Ljot to
2 CONC ok over in Caithness” and defeated “Earl Macbeth [who] came north from Scotland with a large army…at Skitten in Caithness” but died from his wounds after returning to Orkney[221]. It is assumed that “Earl Macbeth” was not the futu
2 CONC re Scottish King Macbeth, who was Mormaer of Moray in the early 1030s, but an earlier earl of the same name who has not yet been identified in other primary sources. Otherwise the chronology of Orkneyinga Saga must be even more sh
2 CONC aky than appears, and the family relationships of the Jarls of Orkney as described therein even less likely to be correct.
2 CONT
2 CONT m as her third husband, RAGNHILD Eiriksdatter, widow firstly of ARNFINN Torfinnsson Jarl of Orkney and secondly of HAVARD Torfinnsson Jarl of Orkney, daughter of ERIK I "Blodøks/Blood-axe" King of Norway & his wife Gunhild Gormsda
2 CONC tter. Orkneyinga Saga records that “Ragnhild Eirik´s daughter plotted the death of her husband Arnfinn at Murkle in Caithness” before marrying “his brother Havard the Fecund who succeeded to the earldom”, but that she plotted her 
2 CONC second husband´s death with his “nephew…Einar Buttered-Bread” (whom she promised to marry), that she then plotted with “Einar Hardmouth, the son of another of Havard´s sisters” to kill Einar Buttered-Bread, but that she finally ma
2 CONC rried “Ljot the brother of Arnfinn and Havard”[222].]
2 CONT
2 CONT f) [HLODVIR Torfinnsson ([945/50]-[988], bur Hofn, Caithness). Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…Ljot…Skuli” as the five sons of Thorfinn[223]. Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Sk
2 CONC ule, the sons of Thorfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when Queen Gunhild and her sons returned to Orkney[224]. In a later saga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufe
2 CONC r"[225].
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that “after Ljot´s death, Hlodvir took charge of the earldom”, presumably indicating Ljot´s brother but this is not explicitly stated in the text, recording that “he died in his bed” and was buried “in a bu
2 CONC rial mound at Ham in Caithness”[226].
2 CONT
2 CONT m EITHNE, daughter of KIARVAL King of Ireland & his wife ---. Orkneyinga Saga records that “Hlodvir” married “Eithne, the daughter of King Kjarval of Ireland”, adding in a later passage that “Sigurd´s mother was a sorceress”[227].
2 CONC  Hlodve´s supposed father-in-law has not been identified among known Irish kings. Hlodve & his wife had [three] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT i) [SIGURD "Digri/the Stout" ([965/70]-killed in battle Clontarf 23 Apr 1014). Orkneyinga Saga names “Sigurd the Stout” as the son of Hlodvir and his wife, adding that he “took over the earldom” after his father died[228].
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "Sigurd the Thick" as the son of Hlodver[229]. He succeeded his father [988] as Jarl of Orkney and Caithness.]
2 CONT
2 CONT ii) [SVANLAUG or Nereid. m (Orkney 990) GILLI Jarl of the Hebrides 988-1014.]
2 CONT
2 CONT iii) [daughter . m HAVARD (-winter 988/89). Steward of Caithness for Jarl Sigurd. Killed by Jarls Melsnati and Hundi, who were defeated when Jarl Sigurd attacked them in revenge.]
2 CONT
2 CONT g) [SKULI Torfinnsson (-killed in battle). Orkneyinga Saga names (in order) “Arnfinn…Havard the Fecund…Hlodvir…Ljot…Skuli” as the five sons of Thorfinn[230]. Snorre names "the Earls Hlodver, Arnfid, Ljot and Skule, the sons of Tho
2 CONC rfin Hausakljufer" as rulers in Orkney when Queen Gunhild and her sons returned to Orkney[231]. In a later saga, Snorre names (in order) "Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot and Skule" as sons of Thorfin "Hausakljufer"[232].
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that “Skuli, Ljot´s brother” was given “the title earl by the King of Scots”, before going north to Caithness from where he sailed to Orkney to claim the islands, but was defeated and fled “first over to Ca
2 CONC ithness and then south to Scotland” and later killed[233].
2 CONT
2 CONT The uncertain chronology of events recorded in Orkneyinga Saga make it difficult to be certain about the identity of the Scottish king in question, although Kenneth II (who ruled from 971 to 995) appears the most likely possibilit
2 CONC y. It is interesting to note that the text does not specify that Skuli was made “Earl of Caithness” just earl without a territorial epithet, reminiscent of the position in many other European countries at the time where the title 
2 CONC was not linked to the landholding.] 
