1 NOTE 221797454. Kong Ludvig IV den Oversjøiske KARLSON av Vestfranken (12663) was born about 921.(12664) He was a Konge in 936 in Vestfranken. (12665) He died on 10 Sep 954.(12666) Da hans far 923 blev fanget av oprørske stormenn, brag
2 CONC te moren ham i sikkerhet i England, hvor han siden opdroges ved sin onkel Kong Athelstans hoff. 936 kalte stormennene ham tilbake til frankrike og tok ham til konge. hans regjering var lite rolig. Under stadige kamper med Otto I. 
2 CONC av Tyskland, Normannerne, Magyarerne og oprørske stormenn med Hugo den Store i spissen måtte han hevde sin makt. kampen førtes med skiftende hellm men ha beholdt dog kongetitelen til sin død. He was married to Gerberge HENRIKSDTR
2 CONC  av Tyskland in 939.
1 NOTE Ancestral File Number:<AFN> GXQQ-JG
2 CONT [mary Stewart1.FTW]
2 CONT ?? Line 1850: (New Pennsylvania F RIN=5883)
2 CONT 1 NAME Louis IV "Transmarinus" King of /France/[FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Byname Louis D'Outremer (Louis from Overseas), king of France from 936 to954 who spent most of his reign struggling against his powerful vassalHugh the Great. "When Louis's father, Charles III the Simple, wasimprisoned in 923, his
2 CONC  mother, Eadgifu, daughter of the Anglo-Saxon kingEdward the Elder, took Louis to England. He was recalled to France in 936and crowned on June 19 at Laon by Artand, Bishop of Reims, who becameLouis's chief supporter against Hugh t
2 CONC he Great. Louis proved not to bethe puppet monarch that Hugh had anticipated; he even moved from Paris toLaon to avoid Hugh's influence. When Hugh and Herbnert of Vermandiosseized Reims and attacked Laon in 940, Louis
2 CONT valiantly defended his city; but because of Louis's earlier interferencein Lorraine the German king, Otto I, sent aid to the rebels. Louisappeared to be totally defeated in 941, but he made peace with Otto inNovember 942 at Vise o
2 CONC n the Meuse, and Hugh and he were reconciled afterHerbert, Hugh's chief
2 CONT supporter, died in 943. "In 945 while intervening in Norman politics,Louis was captured and handed
2 CONT over to Hugh, who imprisoned him for a year. On his release, Louisclosely allied himself with Otto to retake Reims in 946. In 949 Louisagain received control of Laon, and Hugh, Excommunicated by French andGerman synods and by the 
2 CONC Pope, made a peace in 951 that lasted untilLouis's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Encyclopedia Britannica
1 NOTE He ruled from 936 to 954.
1 NOTE He ruled from 936 to 954.
1 NOTE "from overseas"
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Die Nachkommen Karls des Grossen, Germ Pub BT, v. 11, p. 3, 10
2 CONT     2. Tab. Gen. Souv., France 22, Tab. IV
2 CONT     3. Ahnen zu Karl der Grossen, Germ FH 694, p. 95, 103, 155
2 CONT     4. Plantagenet Ancestry, Eng. 116, p. 171
2 CONT     5. Anderson's Royal Genealogies, Eng. 132, p. 451-52, 592, 616
2 CONT     6. Betham's Gen. Tab., Eng. 133, Tab. 253
2 CONT     7. George's Gen. Tab., Eng. 102, Tab. 1
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Louis was born about 920, the son of Charles III 'the Simple', king of France, and Eadgifu of Wessex, a daughter of Edward 'the Elder', king of England. Called 'd'Outremer' or 'Transmarinus' ('from overseas'), he reigned as king o
2 CONC f France from 936 to 954. He was member of the Carolingian dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was only two years old when his father was deposed by the nobles, who set up Robert I in his place. When Louis was only three, Robert died and was replaced by Raoul, duke of Burgundy. Raoul's ally Heribert II, comte de Vermando
2 CONC is, took Charles III captive by treachery and the young Louis' mother took the boy 'over the sea' to the safety of England, hence his epithet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles III died in 929, but Raoul ruled on until 936, when Louis was summoned back to France unanimously by the nobles, especially Hugues 'the Great', duke of The Franks and count of Paris, who had probably organised his return t
2 CONC o prevent Heribert II, or Raoul's brother Hugues 'the Black', taking the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned king at Laon by Artald, archbishop of Reims, on Sunday 19 June 936. Effectively, his sovereignty was limited to the town of Laon and to some places in the north of France, but Louis displayed a keenness beyond his y
2 CONC ears in obtaining the recognition of his authority by his feuding nobles. Nonetheless, his reign was filled with conflict, in particular with Hugues 'the Great'.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 939 Louis became involved in a struggle with the Emperor Otto I 'the Great' on the question of Lorraine, but then he married Otto's sister Gerberga von Sachsen, the widow of Giselbert II of Lorraine. Louis and Gerberga had eigh
2 CONC t children of whom three would have progeny. In November 942 he lost Lorraine and part of Burgundy. On 13 July 945 at Rouen he was captured by the Vikings who handed him over to Hugues 'the Great'. After his release he settled at 
2 CONC Compiègne on 1 July 946.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis was about thirty-four when he died, on 10 September 954 in Reims, after a fall from his horse. He is interred at the basilica of Saint Rémi in Reims. His widow became regent for their son. In 959 she became abbess of Notre D
2 CONC ame in Soissons. She died in Reims on 5 May 984.
1 NOTE Louis d'Outre-Mer: aka Louis from beyond the sea.  Carrie
2 CONT
