1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://www.geni.com/people/Hugues-Capet-roi-des-Francs/5411162384740027078 HUGUES], son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Hugh-of-Paris-count-of-Paris-duke-of-the-Franks/348989271720012699?through=54111623847400270
2 CONC 78 '''HUGUES “le Grand” Duc des Francs'''] & his third wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Hedwige-of-Saxony/6000000003726054418?through=5411162384740027078 Hedwig of Germany] ([940]-villa "Les Juifs", near Prasville, Eure-et-Loire 
2 CONC 24 Oct 996, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  The Historia Francorum Senonensis names (in order) "Hugo, Otto et Heinricus" as the three sons of "Hugo Magnus dux Francorum…ex filia Odonis regis"[142].  Flodoard names 
2 CONC "Hugonem et Oddonem clericum" as brothers of "Otto filius Hugonis", when he records that the rectores of Burgundy named them as his successors[143].  Rodulfus Glauber names "Hugoni, Parisiensis ducis filio…illius Magni Hugonis", s
2 CONC pecifying that his mother was "Ottone…sorore"[144].  His father named Richard Comte [de Normandie] as Hugues's guardian in 956, the arrangement being confirmed by Richard's betrothal to the sister of Hugues.  The Annales Nivernens
2 CONC es record in 958 that "rex et mater sua et Ugo filius Ugonis et mater sua" attended a hearing "apud Marziacum vicum iuxta Nevernis…contra Guillelmum comitem Aquitaniæ post missa sancti Martini"[145].  He was installed as Duc des F
2 CONC rancs/dux Francorum by Lothaire King of the West Franks in 960.  By 974, Hugues had become effective leader of France under King Lothaire and headed the army which retook the kingdom of Lotharingia from Otto II King of Germany in 
2 CONC 978[146].  He was elected HUGUES "Capet" King of France by an assembly of nobles at Senlis 29 May 987, after the death of Louis V King of France.  He was consecrated at Noyon 1 Jun 987.  Charles Duke of Lotharingia, the late king'
2 CONC s uncle who opposed the accession of King Hugues, captured Laon in [May] 988, and Reims in [Aug/Sep] 989, with the help of his nephew Arnoul Archbishop of Reims, but was finally captured at Laon in 991[147].  The Historia Francoru
2 CONC m Senonensis records the death in 998 of "Hugo rex" and his burial "in basilica beati Dyonisii martiris Parisius"[148].  The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "IX Kal Nov" of "Hugo rex"[149]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAPET.htm#_Toc151056321
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00020106&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.friesian.com/flanders.htm#burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT From the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_the_Great English Wikipedia page] on Hugh the Great (with references to additional material on the [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugues_le_Grand_%28Robertien%29 French Wikipedia page] :
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hugh the Great''' (898 – 16 June 956) was duke of the Franks and count of Paris, son of King Robert I of France (with Beatrice de Vermandois) and nephew of King Odo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Paris, Ile-de-France, France. His eldest son was Hugh Capet who became King of France in 987. His family is known as the Robertians.
2 CONT
2 CONT (French Wikipedia notes that he was the Count of Paris, Marquis of Neustria 923-956, Duke of the Franks 936-956, and Comte d'Auxerre 954-956, and was a forefather of the Capets. He was sometimes called Hugh the White because of hi
2 CONC s pale complexion - he was a powerful figure in the Kingdom of West Francia.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh's first wife was Eadhild, daughter of Edward the Elder, king of England, and sister of King Athelstan. At the death of his step-brother King Rudolph (Raoul), duke of Burgundy, in January 936, Hugh was in possession of nearly 
2 CONC all the region between the Loire and the Seine, corresponding to the ancient Neustria, with the exception of the territory ceded to the Normans in 911. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He took a very active part in bringing Louis IV (d'Outremer, who had followed his mother in exile to England) from the Kingdom of England in 936, but in the same year Hugh married Hedwige of Saxony (sister of d'Ogive, Louis' mothe
2 CONC r), a daughter of Henry the Fowler of Germany and Matilda of Ringelheim, and soon quarrelled with Louis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (He had sought an advantage over Herbert II de Vermandois in his struggle for hegemony in West Francia. Louis IV was crowned on Sunday, June 19, 936, by Artaud, Archbishop of Rheims, in the abbey church of St-Vincent de Laon, his 
2 CONC hometown and Carolingian stronghold. A month later, July 25, 936, Louis IV gave Hugh the title of Dux Francorum, or Duke of the Franks - a title that was explained in a document from December 26 that same year in which Louis IV st
2 CONC ated that Hugh was "in all our regina the second after us," equal to a viceroy position or a Merovingian Mayor of the Palace.)
2 CONT
2 CONT (In July 937, he became a lay abbot of St-Germain d'Auxerre and champion of Autun, two major powers in the principality of Burgundy. He also increased his power by challenging the legitimacy of Hugh the Black to proclaim himself P
2 CONC rince of Burgundy, a position he thought he inherited from his brother, King Raoul. However, the young King sought to free himself of the overbearing Hugh, and took up arms to fight, joining forces with Herbert de Vermandois and W
2 CONC illiam Longsword, Duke of Normandy. In 940, Louis IV was defeated near Rheims.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh even paid homage to the Emperor Otto the Great, and supported him in his struggle against Louis. When Louis fell into the hands of the Normans in 945, he was handed over to Hugh, who released him in 946 only on condition that
2 CONC  he should surrender the fortress of Laon (after consultation with Thibaud de Blois, and under considerable pressure from the Emperor). 
2 CONT
2 CONT (In 946, he settled the estate of Herbert de Vermandois, dividing it among his children - Hugh's nephews - and weakening the power of that dynasty.At the Synod of Ingelheim in 948, the third in a series after Verdun and Mouzon, Lo
2 CONC uis IV succeeded at getting the Archbishop of Rheims to excommunicate Hugh the Great.)
2 CONT
2 CONT At the council of Ingelheim (948) Hugh was condemned, under pain of excommunication, to make reparation to Louis. It was not, however, until 950 that the powerful vassal became reconciled with his suzerain and restored Laon. But n
2 CONC ew difficulties arose, and peace was not finally concluded until 953.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Louis IV, Hugh was one of the first to recognize Lothair as his successor, and, at the intervention of Queen Gerberga, was instrumental in having him crowned. In recognition of this service Hugh was invested by the
2 CONC  new king with the duchies of Burgundy (his suzerainty over which had already been nominally recognized by Louis IV) and Aquitaine. But his expedition in 955 to take possession of Aquitaine was unsuccessful. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, however, Giselbert, duke of Burgundy, acknowledged himself his vassal and betrothed his daughter to Hugh's son Otto. At Giselbert's death (8 April 956) Hugh became effective master of the duchy, but died soon aft
2 CONC erwards, on the 16 or 17 June 956, in Dourdan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Divine Comedy Dante meets the soul of Duke Hugh in Purgatory, lamenting the avarice of his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT French Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT From Raingarde, his concubine, he had Herbert or Heribert (d. 996), who became Bishop of Auxerre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a daughter of Roger du Maine in 914, and then to Eadhilda or Edith of Wessex, daughter of Edward the Elder, in 926, and finally to Hedwig of Saxony (922-965) daughter of Henry I l'Oiseleur in 937, the latter of which ga
2 CONC ve him:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Beatrice, who married Frederic I, Comte de Bar and Duc de Haute-Lotharingie
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Hugues Capet, King of the Franks in 987 (OUR ANCESTOR)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Emma, who married Richard I, Duc de Normandie
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Otton and Eudes-Henri, ducs of Bourgogne.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues le Grand[1], né vers 898, mort à Dourdan en 956, comte de Paris, marquis de Neustrie de 923 à 956, puis duc des Francs à partir de 936, comte d'Auxerre de 954 à sa mort.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on France Capetian Kings:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAPET.htm#_Toc154136998
2 CONT
2 CONT HUGUES “le Grand”, son of ROBERT I King of France & his second wife Béatrix de Vermandois [Carolingian] ([898]-Dourdan, Essonne 16 Jun 956, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum Senonensis names "Hugo Magnus" as son of "Robertus princeps [et] sororem Herberti"[78].  "Rodbertum fratrem Odonis regis, qui erat pater Hugonis postea Francorum ducus" is named in the Cartulaire de Saint-Be
2 CONC rtin[79].  Rodulfus Glaber names "Hugo filius Rotberti, Parisiorum comitis", commenting on the obscurity of his ancestry[80].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was recognised by the king as heir to his father’s lands in 914.  He declined the succession to the throne of France on the death of his father in 923, when his brother-in-law Raoul Duke of Burgundy was elected king.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Abbot of Saint-Martin de Tours: "l´abbé Hugues" granted "la ville de Mons…dans le pays de Melun" to "la reine Emma sa sœur, fille du roi Robert" by charter dated 926[81].  
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of King Raoul, Hugues once more declined the succession, instead negotiating the return from England of the Carolingian Prince Louis, son of Charles III “le Simple”, who was his wife's nephew and whom he installed as 
2 CONC King Louis IV.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugo rector Abbatiæ sancti Martini" names "genitoris nostri Rotberti quondam regis ac genitricis nostræ domnæ Beatricis" in a charter dated 26 Mar 931[82].  "Hugues abbé de Saint-Martin" donated "son alleu de Lachy…dans le comté d
2 CONC e Meaux", inherited from "comte Aledramnus", to Tours Saint-Martin by charter dated 14 Sep 937 which names "sa femme Havis"[83].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The position of power acquired by Hugues is confirmed by the title dux francorum/Duc des Francs used in charters dated 25 Jul 936 and 25 Dec 936[84], and the king's references to him as “notre second dans tous nos royaumes”.  Disp
2 CONC utes between Hugues and the king quickly followed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of King Louis IV in 954, Hugues was confirmed as Duc des Francs.  He was granted lordship over Burgundy and Aquitaine[85].  He only succeeded in subjugating the former, succeeding Duke Giselbert as Duke of Burgundy in
2 CONC  Apr 956.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum Senonensis records the death "XVI Kal Iul apud Drodingam villam" of "Hugo Magnus dux Francorum" and his burial "in basilica beati Dyonisii martiris Parisius"[86].  The necrology of Chartres cathedral records
2 CONC  the death "XVI Kal Jul" of "Hugo dux Francorum"[87].  The Obituaire de Notre-Dame de Paris records the death "XV Kal Jul" of "Hugo dux Francorum"[88].  The necrology of Auxerre cathedral records the death 16 Jun of "Hugo comes"[8
2 CONC 9].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ([914]) [JUDITH] du Maine, daughter of ROGER Comte du Maine & his wife Rothilde [Carolingian] (before 900-925).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage of Hugues Comte de Paris with the daughter of Roger Comte du Maine is deduced from Flodoard naming "Rothildis, amitæ suæ [regis Karoli], socrus autem Hugonis" when recording that the king deprived her of "abbatiam…Gol
2 CONC am" [Chelles] in favour of his favourite Hagano, the context dictating that "Hugonis" was "Hugo filius Rotberti"[90].   The source which names her father has not yet been identified, but it appears reasonably certain from the sour
2 CONC ces quoted in the document MAINE that Rothilde's husband was Roger.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She is named Judith in Europäische Stammtafeln[91] but the primary source on which this is based has not been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Settipani her name is not known[92].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([926]) EADHILD, daughter of EDWARD I "the Elder" King of Wessex & his second wife Ælfleda (-937).  At the time of the couple's betrothal, her future husband sent sumptuous gifts to King Athelstan, including spices, jew
2 CONC els, richly caparisoned horses, three holy relics and a gold crown[95].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m thirdly ([9 May/14 Sep] 937) HEDWIG of Germany, daughter of HEINRICH I King of Germany & his second wife Mathilde [Immedinger] ([922]-9 Jan [958 or after 965]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): RAINGARDA [Ringare], daughter of ---.  The Historia Episcoporum Autissiodorensium names "Heribertus Francigena filius Hugonis Ducis cognomento Magni ex concubina Raingarda"[101].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Hugues & his [second/third] wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Beatrix (d. 23 September 1003, married Comte Frederic, Duke of Upper Lotharingia)
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Hugues & his third wife had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugues (b. c.940, d. 24 October 996 at Villa Les Juifs, near Prasville, Eure-et-Loire, buried Eglise de l'Abbaye Royale de St-Denis, First King of France, OUR ANCESTOR)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Emma (b. c.943, d. after 18 March 968, married as first wife Richard I Sans Peur Comte de Normandie)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Otton (Eudes, b. c.945, d. 23 February 965, buried St-Germain d'Auxerre, installed by Lothaire as Duke of Burgundy)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Eudes (Odo, b. c.948, d. 15 October 1002 at Chateau de Poilly-sur-Saone, buried Auxerre, adopted the name of Henri when becoming Duke of Burgundy, apparently different from previous brother)
2 CONT
2 CONT Duc Hugues "le Grand" had one illegitimate son by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Heribert (d. 23 August 996 or after at Chateau de Toucy, buried Church of Notre Dame d'Auxerre, Bishop of Auxerre 971-996)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Hugues of Neustria (Forrás / Source):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10519.htm#i105190
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues of Neustria, Comte de Paris (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #105190, b. circa 895, d. 9 June 956
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=19 Jun 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues of Neustria, Comte de Paris was born circa 895. He was the son of Robert I, Roi de France. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Eadhilda (?), daughter of Eadweard I, King of Wessex and Ælflæd (?), between 926 and 927. (2) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, thirdly, Hedwig von Sachsen, daughter of Heinrich I von Sachsen, Holy Roman Emperor and Mathilda von Ringelheim, before 14 September 938. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 9 June 956.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues of Neustria, Comte de Paris also went by the nick-name of Hugues 'the Great'. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the title of Comte de Paris. He gained the title of Duc de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Hugues of Neustria, Comte de Paris and Hedwig von Sachsen
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Hugues de Paris, Roi de France+ b. c 938, d. 24 Oct 996
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Emma de Paris b. a 942, d. 19 Mar 968
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Greve av Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugo gifte sig med Hedwige Rikissa av Sachsen, dotter till Kejsare av det Tysk-Romerska Riket Henril I "Fågelfängaren" av TYSKLAND och Den Heliga Matilda van Reingelheim, den 14 September 937. (Hedwige Rikissa Magnusdotter föddes 
2 CONC under 922 i Sachen, Tyskland och dog den 4 Oktober 965 i Aix-la-Chapelle, Aachen, Tyskland.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Dutch Wikipedia page on Hugo de Grote:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugo_de_Grote
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugo de Grote (Fontaines-en-Sologne, 897 - Dourdan, 16 juni 956) was in zijn tijd de machtigste edelman in Frankrijk. Hij weigerde tot driemaal toe om koning te worden maar gaf er de voorkeur aan om zwakkere koningen op de troon t
2 CONC e plaatsen en direct zijn eigen belangen te kunnen behartigen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugo was de zoon van Robert I van Frankrijk en Beatrix van Vermandois. Na het overlijden van zijn vader in de Slag bij Soissons in 923, werd hem de kroon aangeboden maar Hugo weigerde en zijn zwager Rudolf I van Frankrijk werd toe
2 CONC n tot koning gekozen. Hugo was toen markgraaf van Bretagne, graaf van Parijs, Troyes, Orléans, en lekenabt van Saint-Denis, Saint-Germain-des-Prés, Marmoutier, Saint-Martin te Tours, Carmery, Villeloin. In de volgende jaren werd h
2 CONC ij hertog van Neustrië en verwierf hij ook nog de graafschappen Autun, Auxerre, Nevers, Sens, Chalon en Mâcon. Na het kinderloos overlijden van Rudolf in 936 weigerde Hugo opnieuw de kroon maar vroeg Lodewijk IV van Frankrijk, die
2 CONC  als kind door zijn moeder in Engeland in veiligheid was gebracht, om koning te worden. Hugo bedong voor zichzelf natuurlijk een positie van uitzonderlijke macht en invloed, onder de nieuwe koning. In 938 werd hij benoemd tot mede
2 CONC -hertog van Bourgondië.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daarna kwam Hugo in conflict met Lodewijk, die probeerde een zelfstandige positie als koning te verwerven. Hugo sloot in 940 een bondgenootschap met Herbert II van Vermandois en met Willem I van Normandië, tegen Lodewijk. Ze beleg
2 CONC erden Reims en versloegen Lodewijk toen die probeerde om de stad te ontzetten. In plaats van Lodewijk erkenden ze Otto I de Grote als koning. Uiteindelijk werd er in 942 te Visé een vrede bemiddeld door Otto en zijn zuster Gerberg
2 CONC a van Saksen, die met Lodewijk was getrouwd. Toen de Normandiërs Lodewijk in 945 gevangennamen, droegen ze hem over aan Hugo. En die liet Lodewijk pas in 946 vrij toen die de stad Laon aan hem had afgestaan. In dat jaar gebruikte 
2 CONC Hugo de dood van Herbert II van Vermandois om diens erfenis te versnipperen over diens kinderen, zodat geen van hen meer zo machtig zou kunnen worden als hun vader. De Universele Synode van Ingelheim dreigde Hugo in 948 met excomm
2 CONC unicatie als hij Lodewijk niet zou compenseren. De excommunicatie is ook een korte tijd daadwerkelijk uitgesproken maar Lodewijk kreeg Laon terug en geleidlijk verzoenden Hugo en Lodewijk zich met elkaar. Na het overlijden van Lod
2 CONC ewijk in 954 weigerde Hugo opnieuw de kroon maar steunde het regentschap van Gerberga. In ruil daarvoor werd Hugo tot hertog van Bourgondië en Aquitanië benoemd. Een expeditie naar Aquitanië om zijn gezag als hertog te vestigen mi
2 CONC slukte, maar Bourgondië erkende hem wel als hertog.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugo is begraven in de abdij van Saint-Denis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Huwelijken en kinderen
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugo was in zijn eerste huwelijk getrouwd met Judith, dochter van Rogier van Maine. In zijn tweede huwelijk was hij getrouwd met Eadhild, een zuster van koning Athelstan van Engeland. Als derde vrouw trouwde hij 14 september 937 m
2 CONC et Hedwig van Saksen, dochter van de Duitse koning Hendrik de Vogelaar en zuster van Otto I van Duitsland. Zij kregen de volgende kinderen:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Beatrix, geboren rond 938, huwde met Frederik I van Lotharingen
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Hugo, geboren rond 940, die later onder de naam Hugo Capet Koning van Frankrijk zal worden.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Emma (ovl. na 968), getrouwd met Richard I van Normandië
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Otto, geboren in 945, wordt hertog van Bourgondië en graaf van Auxerre.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Odo, geboren in 948, noemt zich ook wel Hendrik
2 CONT
2 CONT Bij een minnares kreeg hij nog een zoon Herbert, die werd benoemd tot bisschop van Auxerre.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Capet var greve av Paris, Orléans, Vexin och Le Mans och hertig av Frankrike. Hugh kallas även Hugh Magnus och ska inte förväxlas med sin stora sonsons son som också är känd som Hugh "den Store" och Hugh Magnus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Capet hade tre äktenskap. De två första är det oklart i vilken ordning, var att en dotter till greven av Maine och till Edhilda, dotter till Edward the Elder, kung av England. Hugh gift tredje till Hedwig, dotter till Henry "
2 CONC av Sachsen", se nedan. Hugh hade en dotter, men det är osäkert i vilken hustru: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma av Paris (d. 962) som gifte sig i 960 till Richard (I) "The Fearless" 3: e hertig av Normandie. Obs: Weis sägs att: "Richard I" den Fearless "... M. (2) 960 Emma... DAU. Hugh Capet (se 53-20) "Hänvisningen (53-20) är för Hugh
2 CONC  Capet (941 till 996), den första Capatian kung Frankrike och en son Hugh "den Store". Naturligtvis kan Emma inte vara en dotter till Hugh Capet (941 till 996) när hon gifte sig med 960, Weis är felaktig och hänvisningen bör (53-1
2 CONC 9) som är Hugh "den Store". Se Frederick Lewis Weis, Ancestral Roots, Baltimore, 1999, 121E-20 och 53-20. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Capet gift tredje circa 938 till Hedwig, dotter till Henry "av Sachsen", hertig av Sachsen, kung av Tyskland och Lorraine och hans fru St Matilda (eller Mechtilde) av Ringleheim. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anmärkning: Den tredje äktenskap visas i 1985 fotografisk reproduktion av George Andrews Moriarty's Den Plantagenet Ancestry Kung Edward III och Drottning Phillipa sidorna 24 och 25 och i Frederick Lewis Weis, Ancestral Roots, Bal
2 CONC timore, 1999, 53-19. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh och Hedwig hade följande barn: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Capet, Kung Frankrike 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice, född cirka 939 som gifte sig i 954 till Frederick, Duke of Upper Lorraine. 
2 CONT
