1 NOTE Edgar the Peaceful, King of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 944
2 CONT Acceded: 11 MAY 973, Bath Abbey
2 CONT Died: 8 JUL 975, Winchester, England
2 CONT Interred: Glastonbury Abbey, Somerset
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT Reigned 959-975. The first King of a united England. He allowed his Danish
2 CONT subjects to retain Danish laws. Edgar promoted a monastic revival and
2 CONT encouraged trade by reforming the currency. He improved defence byorganising
2 CONT coastal naval patrols and a system for manning warships.
2 CONT Although he suceeded on 1st October 959, he was not crowned until 973because
2 CONT St. Dunstan, the Archbishop of Canterbury, disaproved of his way of life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: , Edmund I the Elder, King of England, b. 921
2 CONT Mother: , AElfgifu (St.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Married CIR 961 to , AEthelflaeda the Fair
2 CONT Child 1: , Edward (St.) the Martyr, King of England, b. 963
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Associated with , Wulfryth (St), Abbess of Wilton
2 CONT Child 2: , Eadgyth (St.), Abbess of Barking, b. ABT 962
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 964 to , Aelfthryth (Elfrida)
2 CONT Child 3: , Edmund, b. CIR 965
2 CONT Child 4: , AEthelred II the Unready, King of England, b. ABT 968
2 CONT [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    king of the Mercians and Northumbrians from 957 who became king of theWest Saxons, or Wessex, in 959 and is reckoned as king of all Englandfrom that year. He was efficient and tolerant of
2 CONT local customs, and his reign was peaceful. He was most important as apatron of the English monastic revival.
2 CONT    The younger son of Edmund I, king of the English, Edgar was made kingof the Mercians and Northumbrians in place of Eadwig, his brother, whowas deposed. On Eadwig's death (Oct. 1, 959),
2 CONT Edgar succeeded to the West Saxon throne. His ecclesiastical policy wasalso that of the archbishop of Canterbury, St. Dunstan, who insisted onstrict observance of the Benedictine Rule. The king supported ArchbishopOswald of York a
2 CONC nd Bishop Aethelwold of Winchester in founding abbeys.Edgar's laws were the first in England to prescribe penalties fornonpayment of tithes and Peter's pence, the annual contribution made byRoman Catholics for support of the Holy 
2 CONC See.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Edgar" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?eu=32534&tocid=0&query=edgar%20the%20peaceful>
2 CONT Edgar the Peaceful, King of England from 959 to his death in 975
2 CONT Born in 944
2 CONT Died on July 8, 975 at Winchester, England and interred at GlastonburyAbbey, Somerset
2 CONT    Edgar was the first King of a united England. He allowed his Danishsubjects to retain Danish laws, he promoted a monastic revival and heencouraged trade by reforming the currency. Edgar improved defence byorganising coastal nav
2 CONC al patrols and a system for manning warships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgar married first circa 961 to Æthelfæda "the Fair", a daughter ofOrdmaer, an Ealdorman and they had the following son:
2 CONT St. Edward "the Martyr", King of England 975-978 was born in 963. He wasmurdered on March 18, 978 at Corfe Castle in Dorset, reputedly as aresult of a plot hatched by his step-mother Ælfthryth so her son ÆthelredII "the Unrede" co
2 CONC uld become king. St. Edward was canonised in 1001, andhis feast day is 20th November.
2 CONT Edgar was associated with St. Wulfryth, Abbess of Wilton (circa 945 -1000) and they had a daugher:
2 CONT St. Eadgyth, Abbess of Barking, 962 - 984
2 CONT Edgar married second in 964 to Ælfthryth (Elfrida), daughter of Ordgar,Ealdorman of Devonshire and his wife Wilfrith. Ælfthryth was the widow ofEthelwald, Ealdorman of East Anglia.
2 CONT Edgar and Ælfthryth had the following sons:
2 CONT Edmund (965 - 970)
2 CONT Æthelred II "the Unrede", King of England, 878 - 1016
2 CONT
2 CONT Reigned 959-975. First KIng of a united England. He allowed his Danishsubjects to retain Danish laws. Edgar promoted a monastic revival &encourgaed trade by reforming the currency. He improved defense byorganizing coastal naval pa
2 CONC trols & a system for manning warships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruled England 959-975 Born 943 Crowned at Bath 11 May 973 Buried atGlastonbury, Somerset When his brother Edwy died Edgar became king of allEngland. He is called the Peaceful because his long reign was in factfree from war. When e
2 CONC ight British princes rowed the small but, it wouldseem, ostentatious monarch on the River Dee, no one questioned hissupremacy. An interesting fact about King Edgar's reign is that he wasnot crowned until 973, two years before he d
2 CONC ied. The reason for this maybe that while he led the wicked life which he apparently did, hisminister, the holy Dunstan, would not consent to his crowning, so greatwas the veneration in which Dunstan held this rite. The delay may,
2 CONC however, have been due to the fact that the king wanted his power to beabsolute before his crowning. It is just possible that he was crowned in958, during his reign as King of Mercia and the Dane law (957-59), andthat the solemnit
2 CONC ies at Bath were a second and more significantcoronation, when he was crowned king of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgar (b. 943/944--d. July 8, 975), king of the Mercians andNorthumbrians from 957 who became king of the West Saxons, or Wessex, in959 and is reckoned as king of all England from that year. He wasefficient and tolerant of local c
2 CONC ustoms, and his reign was peaceful. Hewas most important as a patron of the English monastic revival.
2 CONT
2 CONT The younger son of Edmund I, king of the English, Edgar was made king ofthe Mercians and Northumbrians in place of Eadwig, his brother, who wasdeposed. On Eadwig's death (Oct. 1, 959), Edgar succeeded to the WestSaxon throne. His 
2 CONC ecclesiastical policy was also that of the archbishopof Canterbury, St. Dunstan, who insisted on strict observance of theBenedictine Rule. The king supported Archbishop Oswald of York and BishopAethelwold of Winchester in founding
2 CONC  abbeys. Edgar's laws were the firstin England to prescribe penalties for nonpayment of tithes and Peter'spence, the annual contribution made by Roman Catholics for support of theHoly See. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, EDGAR]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward, byname SAINT EDWARD THE MARTYR (b. c. 963--d. March 18, 978,Corfe, England; feast day March 18), king of England from 975 to 978. Hisreign was marked by a reaction against the promonastic policies of hisfather and predeces
2 CONC sor, King Edgar (reigned 959-975). Upon Edgar's deatha faction sought to win the throne for his younger son, Ethelred, butEdward was quickly elected king. He evidently played little part in theantimonastic reaction, which was led 
2 CONC by Aelfhere, ealdorman of Mercia.Edward was assassinated while visiting Ethelred in Corfe. AlthoughEthelred succeeded to the throne, it is not known whether he had a handin the murder of his brother. Edward was widely mourned; his
2 CONC  remains weresaid to produce miracles, and he was declared a martyr in 1001.[Britannica CD, 1997, EDWARD]
