1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''She is a Direct Descendant of Charlemagne.'''
2 CONT This is her lineage:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlemagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Pépin, king of Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard, King of Lombardy
2 CONT
2 CONT Pépin II, lord of Péronne
2 CONT
2 CONT Héribert I de Vermandois, comte de Vermandois, Senlis, Péronne
2 CONT
2 CONT Héribert II, count of Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT Adele of Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT Elstrude, Countess of Flanders
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Elstrude Of Flanders
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marriage: Siegfred Count Of Guines
2 CONT
2 CONT See:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants, Compiled by J. Orton Buck, and Timothy Beard, 1978, published by the Order of the Crown of Charlemagne, page 292
2 CONT
2 CONT picture
2 CONT
2 CONT Elstrude married Siegfred Count Of Guines. (Siegfred Count Of Guines died in 965.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Flanders:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FLANDERS,%20HAINAUT.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ELSTRUDE de Flandre (-966[159] or after).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Comitum Ghisnensium names "comes Balduinus sororem…Elstrudem" as wife of "Sifridus"[160].  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Chronica Monasterii Sancti Bertini, the couple were never married[161].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([960/65]) SIEGFRIED Comte de Guines, son of --- (-[965]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT From the French Wikipedia page on Siegfried I de Guines:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siegfried_Ier_de_Guines
2 CONT
2 CONT Siegfried LeVieux, premier comte de Guines dit le Danois (° v.940 - † ap.965).
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographie 
2 CONT
2 CONT Les terres de Guînes appartenaient originairement à l'abbaye Saint-Sithiu (devenue Saint-Bertin par la suite) de Saint-Omer. En 877, Charles le Chauve, leur en confirmait la possession.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ces terres tombèrent ensuite dans le comté de Flandre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il existe deux hypothèses quant à la prise de possession du comté de Guînes par Sigfrid le Danois
2 CONT
2 CONT Contre-offensive envers la Flandre 
2 CONT
2 CONT En 965, Guillaume Ier, comte de Ponthieu, enleva le Boulonnais, Guînes et Saint-Pol à Arnoul II, comte de Flandre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnoul II appela les Danois qui vinrent à sa rescousse, sous la conduite de Knut Gormsson, frère de Harald Ier de Danemark, roi du Danemark, et Siegfried, son cousin. L'expédition fut victorieuse, et Arnoul II confia le fief de Gu
2 CONC înes à Siegfried ainsi que sa sœur Elstrude de Gand.
2 CONT
2 CONT La légende dit que Siegfried aurait devancé le mariage, et que de leur union coupable serait né Ardolf (ou Adolphe, Ardolph). Siegfried se serait alors pendu pour échapper au ressentiment d'Arnoul II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Invasion viking 
2 CONT
2 CONT Suivant d'autres historiens, Siegfried le Danois et ses Vikings serait venu s'emparer, en 928, de l'endroit où s'éleva ensuite la ville de Guînes. Arnoul Ier renonça à la contre-attaque et livra sa fille Elstrude de Gand en mariag
2 CONC e au pirate normand, qui fut investit comte de Guînes, vassal du comte de Flandre
2 CONT
2 CONT Mariage et enfants 
2 CONT
2 CONT Il épousa en 964 Elstrude de Gand, née vers 932, et eut Ardolf comme fils et successeur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT L'art de vérifier les dates des faits historiques, des chartes, des chroniques et autres anciens ... par Maur-François Dantine, Charles Clémencet, Saint-Allais (Nicolas Viton), Ursin Durand, François Clément
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Siegfried "The Elder" ("Le Vieux"), also called "The Dane", was the first Comte de Guines (c.940 - around 965)
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT The County of Guines originally belonged to the Abbey of St-Sithiu (later St-Bertin) in St-Omer. In 877, Charles the Bald confirmed it as a possession of the Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later these lands fell to the County of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are two hypotheses regarding how Siegried the Dane took possession of the County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Counter-Offensive against Flanders
2 CONT
2 CONT In 965, William I, Comte de Ponthieu, siezed Boulogne, Guiness, and St-Pol from Arnulf II, Comte de Flandres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf II called in the Danes, who came to his rescue - led by Knut Gormsson, brother of King Harald I of Denmark, and Siegfried, his cousin. The expedition was a success, and Arnulf II gave the fief of Guiness to Siegfried, and m
2 CONC arried off his daughter (sister?) Elstrude de Gand, to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The legend says that Siegfried would have foregone the marriage had he not already impregnated Elstrude with their son Ardolf (or Adolphe Ardolph). Siegfried would have been hanged by Arnulf II if he refused.
2 CONT
2 CONT Viking Invasion
2 CONT
2 CONT According to other historians, the Dane Siegfried and the Vikings came to take Guiness in 928. Arnulf I, Comte de Flandres, offered his daughter Elstrude de Gand in marriage to the Norman pirate, to stop the siege of his vassel Co
2 CONC mte de Guines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 964 Elstrude de Gand (born in 932), and they had Ardolf as a son, and Siegfried's successor.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Celtic Casimir online family tree:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.celtic-casimir.com/webtree/5/25563.htm
2 CONT
