2 CONT Ælfgâr was the son of Leofric, earl of Mercia, and Godgifu (Godiva). He married, perhaps in the late 1020s, Ælfgifu, probably a kinswoman of Cnut's first wife, Ælfgifu of Northampton. Her known lands lay in the east midlands and E
2 CONC ast Anglia, and in the 1040s Ælfgâr attested an East Anglian will at the head of the Essex witnesses (AS chart., S 1531). These connections perhaps explain why he was given the earldom of East Anglia, first during the exile of Har
2 CONC old Godwineson in 10512, and again after Harold succeeded to Wessex in 1053. In 1055 he was outlawed for treason; the E text of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle alleges his guilt but the other texts regard him as at most mildly culpable
2 CONC . Perhaps Ælfgar was alarmed at the appointment of Tostig Godwineson, rather than Waltheof, Earl Siward's heir, as earl of Northumbria, and feared that he too might be displaced when his father died. He raised troops in Ireland, a
2 CONC nd, in alliance with the Welsh king, Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, attacked Hereford. The local levies were overwhelmed and the city sacked, and Earl Harold had to collect a force from very nearly all England (ASC, s.a. 1055, text C) to
2 CONC  drive the invaders back into Wales. A peace was concluded on terms noticeably favourable to Ælfgâr, who was reinstated. In 1057 he succeeded to Mercia on his father's death; East Anglia passed to Harold's brother Gyrth. Ælfgâr's 
2 CONC second exile, in 1058, must be seen against the continuing rise of the Godwinesons, for by now Harold had received not only Wessex but Hereford too, after the death of Earl Ralph in 1057. Ælfgâr again allied with Gruffudd, now hus
2 CONC band of his daughter Ealdgyth and was reinstated once more. In 1062 he supported the election of Wulfstan as bishop of Worcester, but is not heard of thereafter. He was probably dead before Harold launched his successful campaign 
2 CONC against Gruffudd in 1063, for he would surely have tried to help his son-in-law had he been living.
2 CONT --------------------
