1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivar_of_Waterford '''Ivar of Waterford''']= 
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT . . . Ivar was very likely a grandson of Ragnall ua Ímair, although the precise line of descent is uncertain, and the middle generation, that of the "Imhar, Tanist of the foreigners" slain in a major battle against Ruaidrí ua Cana
2 CONC nnáin in 950, is only an educated guess made by Todd and followed by Valante.
2 CONT
2 CONT The names of Ivar's wife or wives are unknown but one was likely the daughter of Donnubán mac Cathail,[22] king of Uí Fidgenti, probably by a daughter or granddaughter of Ivar of Limerick. Another may have been a daughter of an Os
2 CONC raige dynast,[23] possibly Gilla Pátraic mac Donnchada, king of Osraige.
2 CONT
2 CONT His known descendants were:[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gilla Pátraic (died 983) - name suggests association with the Osraige as well as Christian identity [25]
2 CONT     Ragnall (died 995)
2 CONT     Donndubán (died 996) - an extremely rare name, thus probably a grandson of Donndubán mac Cathail. According to a tradition of uncertain provenance which made itself into the Encyclopædia Britannica for several decades, Donndub
2 CONC án mac Ímair, along with his maternal grandfather Donndubán mac Cathail, is the eponymous ancestor of some of the modern O'Donovan family.[26] Likely no pedigree survives to verify the claim.
2 CONT     Ragnall mac Ímair, king of Waterford (died 1018) - Ivar's successor
2 CONT          ? mac Ragnaill (died 1015)
2 CONT         Ragnall mac Ragnaill, king of Waterford (died 1035)
2 CONT     Sihtric mac Ímair, king of Waterford (died 1022)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of Dubllin
