2 CONT !17.   Edgar the Peaceful, 943-75, the younger son; King of England from 959.  The ceremony of his crowning at Bath Abbey 11 May 973 was a splendid affair devised by St Dunstan, Archbishop of Canterbury.  It was the first to be pr
2 CONC operly documented and it formed the basis of all subsequent coronation services.  Its millinary was celebrated in 1973 with a summer-long observance
2 CONT in Bath called "Monarchy 1000."  Edgar's reign was enhanced by the political ministry of St. Dunstan.  For a long time this king had a strong grip of England, but he d. at the height of his fame.  he had mar. (2) 965 Ealfthryth (E
2 CONC lfrida), 945-1000, dau. of Earl Ordgar of Devon.  Edgar's young son by his first marriage, Edward the Martyr, took the throne, which he held briefly, but a conspiracy on the part of some of the nobles, alleged aided by Elfrida, re
2 CONC sulted in the lad's assassination 978 by poison of stabbling in Corfe Castle, dorest, clearing the way for her son by Edgar -- the 10 year old Ethelred.  It
2 CONT was generally considered the worst crime committed among the English since the arrival of Cerdic, but nothing was done to bring the murderers to justice, and Queen Mother Elfrida's illfated little boy became King of England.
2 CONT
