1 NOTE Data From Lynn Jeffrey Bernhard, 2445 W 450 South #4, Springville UT  84663-4950 
2 CONT email - bernhardengineer@netscape.net
1 NOTE Lothair (French: Lothaire; 941-986), sometimes called Lothair IV,[1] was the Carolingian king of West Francia (10 September 954 - 1 March 986), son of Louis IV and Gerberga of Saxony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regency
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 954 at the age of thirteen, and was at first under the guardianship of Hugh the Great, Duke of the Franks and Count of Paris, who had been an adversary to his father but nonetheless was appointed guardia
2 CONC n of the king's estates, though not as regent for the young king who assumed his royal dignities at thirteen. This gave the young Lothair the opportunity to come to know his guardian's heir, the sixteen year old Hugh Capet, before
2 CONC  his father's death in 956; Capet later became king and founder of the Capetian Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT The beginning of his reign was occupied with wars against the vassals, particularly against the duke of Normandy, and it should be made clear that the monarch of Western Francia was more a ceremonial title, more of a first among e
2 CONC quals status, than that state which would represent the later centralized authority meant by monarchies of later historical epochs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 955, Lothair and Hugh together took Poitiers by siege. Hugh died soon after and Lothair mediated between his son, the aforementioned Hugh Capet and the younger Otto Henry. The king gave Capet Paris and the ducal title, but inve
2 CONC sted Otto with the Duchy of Burgundy in 960. With young Hugh the new count of Paris et al., Lothair, now only fifteen, came under the guardianship of his maternal uncle Bruno, archbishop of Cologne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military conflicts
2 CONT In 962, Baldwin III of Flanders, son, co-ruler, and heir of Arnulf I died and Arnulf bequeathed Flanders to Lothair. On Arnulf's death in 965, Lothair invaded Flanders and took many cities, but was eventually repulsed by the suppo
2 CONC rters of Arnulf II. He temporarily remained in control of Arras and Douai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lothair, when thirty-seven, seems to have conceived the design of recovering Lorraine, once held by his family. He attempted to precipitate matters by a sudden attack, and in the spring of 978 nearly captured the emperor Otto II a
2 CONC t Aachen. He took the imperial capital itself and even reversed the direction of the eagle sitting atop the palace.[2] Otto took his revenge in the autumn by invading France. He penetrated as far as Paris, devastating the country 
2 CONC through which he passed (Soissons, Reims, and Laon), but failed to take the town, and was eventually forced to retreat with heavy loss across the Aisne, as Hugh Capet and through him, other key nobles supported Lothair. Peace was 
2 CONC concluded in 980 at Margut-sur-Chiers, and in 983 Lothair was even chosen guardian to the young Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor when Otto II died on 7 December 983. Around 980, however, Lothair quarrelled with Hugh Capet, who, at the
2 CONC  instigation of Adalberon, archbishop of Reims, became reconciled with Otto III, despite the defeat of his father with Capet's help.
2 CONT
2 CONT The rest of Lothair's reign was dominated by internal troubles which distracted him from important peripheral affairs. In 985, when the caliph of Córdoba, Al-Mansur, sacked Barcelona, Lothair offered no assistance to the Count Bo
2 CONC rrel II upon receiving his envoys at Verdun. This caused the final rift between the Hispanic March and the French crown during the reign of his successors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of his reign, Lothair's power seemed markedly less than that of Hugh Capet. In a letter of March or April 985, Gerbert of Aurillac wrote to the Archbishop Adalberon that "Lothair is king of France in name alone; Hu
2 CONC gh is, however, not in name but in effect and deed."[3] Gerbert goes on to suggest that if Adalberon wishes to free his father, held captive by Lothair, he need only warm up to Hugh by arranging an advantageous marriage alliance w
2 CONC ith Otto III. Lothair died a year after this, on 1 March 986.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Twelfth-century bust of Lothair, from the Musée Saint Rémi at Reims
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT Lothair married Emma of Italy, daughter of Lothair II of Italy and Adelaide of Italy. Her maternal grandparents were Rudolph II of Burgundy and Bertha of Swabia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lothair and Emma had a son who succeeded his father as Louis V. Louis V held the office for only a single year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lothair also had a bastard son who became archbishop of Rheims, Arnulf.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ Riche, Pierre, The Carolingians (see index). 
2 CONT ^ Chronicles differ as to whether he made it face eastwards or westwards, either to symbolise his dominance over the eastern kingdom or the supremacy of his own kingdom. 
2 CONT ^ Lewis, 15. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
2 CONT Gwatkin, H. M., Whitney, J. P. (ed) et al. The Cambridge Medieval History: Volume III. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1926. 
2 CONT Lewis, Andrew W. (1981). Royal Succession in Capetian France: Studies on Familial Order and the State. Cambridge, Massachusetts: Harvard University Press. ISBN 0 674 77985 1. 
