2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT *Konrad "der Rote/Rufus", Herzog von Lotharingen (945-953, killed in the battle of Lechfeld 10 August 955, bur. Worms Cathedral)
2 CONT *Liutgard von Sachsen (c931 - 18 Nov. 953, bur. St. Alban near Mainz)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Judith (d. 991, bur. Worms Cathedral)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT *1. Heinrich (d. 28 September 989/1000, b. Worms Cathedral), Graf im Wormsgau, first husband of Adelheid von Metz (d. 19 May [1039/46], bur. Öhningen Stiftskirche, House of Malfride)
2 CONT *2. Bruno, later Pope Gregory V (3 May 996 - 18 February/12 March 999)
2 CONT *3. Konrad I, Duke of Carinthia (1004 - 12/15 December 1011, bur. Worms Cathedral), husband of Mathilde von Schwaben
2 CONT *4. Wilhelm, Bishop of Strasbourg (1029 - 7 November 1047)
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information and justifications:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: c948 (German Wikipedia estimate), could be Lotharingia (Lorraine), Nahegau, or Niddegau. No sources pin down the exact location.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: Unknown date and place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 4 November 1004, presumably in Carinthia (though no source ties him to this location).
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Bruchsal, Landkreis Karlsruhe,  Baden-Württemberg, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Graf im Nahegau (958); Herzog von Kärnten (978-985, 995-1002); Graf im Speiergau, Graf im Wormsgau, Graf in Elsenzgau, Graf in Kraichgau, Graf im Engpfinzgau, Graf im Ufgau (985-995)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate Names: Otto von Worms, Otto von Kärnten, Otton
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Medlands page on Franconia (covering his family before Carinthia):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANCONIA.htm#OttoWormsDukeCarinthiadied1004A
2 CONT
2 CONT KONRAD "der Rote/Rufus" (-killed in battle Lechfeld 10 Aug 955, bur Worms Cathedral[336]).  
2 CONT *Regino records the succession in 943 of "Chuonradus filius Werinheri" as duke after the death of "Otto dux"[337].  It is assumed that "Werinheri" was the same person as Werner who is named in the 906 charter cited above, but this
2 CONC  is not beyond doubt.  
2 CONT *He was installed as KONRAD Duke of Lotharingia in [945].  Widukind records that "Conrado" was installed as Duke of Lotharingia after the deaths of "Oddone, Lothariorum præside, ac regis nepote Heinrico"[338].  "Otto…rex" donated 
2 CONC property to Kloster St Petrus Gorze at the request of "Cuonradi Lodariensis ducis" by charter dated 13 Jul 945[339].  
2 CONT *Graf im Nahegau: "Otto…rex" donated property "in forasto…Lutara…in pago Nahgouue…in comitatu Cuonradi" to "fideli nostro Franco" at the request of "ducis…Cuonradi" by charter dated 17 Dec 945[340].  "Chuonradus dux Wernharii comi
2 CONC tis filius" donated property to the church of Speyer by charter dated 13 Mar 946[341].  
2 CONT *Graf im Niddagau: "Otto…rex" granted property "in pago Nitehgouue in comitatu Cuonradi ducis" to "nostro villico Vuetti" at the request of "Cuonardi nostri…comitis" by charter dated 14 Feb 947[342].  "Otto…rex" granted property t
2 CONC o the church of Cambrai at the request of "germani nostri Brunonis et Cuonradi ducis atque Herimanni ducis" by charter dated 30 Apr 948[343].  "Otto…rex" confirmed the possession of Abtei Süsteren by Kloster Prüm by charter dated 
2 CONC 1 Jun 949, signed by "Cuonradus dux, Herimannus dux, Hezzo comes, Godefridus comes, Rudolfus comes, Reginherus comes"[344].  "Otto…rex" granted property confiscated from "Hatto Aladramque fratres…in villis Bechi et Auici situm in 
2 CONC pago Ganipi in comitatu Arnulfi" to the church of St Florin, Koblenz at the request of "ducis nostri Cuonradi eius coniugis filiæ nostræ Liutgartæ" by charter dated 22 Nov 949[345].  
2 CONT *He rebelled against his father-in-law, together with his brother-in-law Liudolf Duke of Swabia, but was ultimately forgiven, although deposed in 953 as Duke of Lotharingia.  
2 CONT *He was killed fighting the Magyars near Augsburg[346].  The necrology of Fulda records the death "955 Id Aug" of "Cuonrat dux"[347].  The necrology of Lüneburg records the death "10 Aug" of "Conradus dux"[348].  The necrology of 
2 CONC St Gall records the death "IV Id Aug" of "Perchtoldi comitis et Chuonradi ducis et Uodalrici ac Thietpoldi comitum aliorumque multorem ab Ungaris occisorum"[349].  "Chuonradus…Romanorum imperator augustus" donated property to the 
2 CONC church of Worms naming "filii nostri Heinrici Regis, filie quoque nostre Beatricis" for the souls of "parentum nostrorum defunctorum atavi nostri ducis Chuonradi, avie nostre Iudithe, patris nostri Heinrici, patrui nostri ducis Ch
2 CONC uonradi eiusque coniugis Mathildis, sororis etiam nostre Iudithe", all buried at Worms Cathedral, by charter dated 30 Jan 1034[350].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([947]) LIUTGARD of Saxony, daughter of OTTO I "dem Großen" King of Germany & his first wife Eadgyth of Wessex ([931]-18 Nov 953, bur St Alban near Mainz).  
2 CONT *Regino records that in 947 "Chuonradus dux…Liutgardam filiam regis in matrimonium sumpsit"[351].  "Liudolfo et Liutgarde" are named as the two children of King Otto I and his wife Eadgyth[352].  
2 CONT *Widukind records that "Conrado" Duke of Lotharingia married "unicam filiam [regis]"[353].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Konrad & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)  OTTO (-4 Nov 1004, bur [Bruchsal]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Ottonem" as son of "duci Wormatie Conrado", specifying that he was father of "Cono dux et Henricus pater imperatoris Conradi"[354].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Graf im Nahegau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT (Married life)
2 CONT
2 CONT OTTO, son of KONRAD Duke [of Lotharingia] & his wife Liutgard of Saxony (-4 Nov 1004, bur [Bruchsal]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Ottonem" as son of "duci Wormatie Conrado", specifying that he was father of "Cono dux et Henricus pater imperatoris Conradi"[355].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Graf im Nahegau 956: "Otto…rex" granted property "in pago Nahgouue in forasto…Vuasago…in comitatu Ottonis filii Cuonradi ducis" to Worms St Peter by charter dated 8 Mar 956[356].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was installed as OTTO Duke of Carinthia by Otto II King of Germany in 978 after the trial of Heinrich Duke of Carinthia who had been implicated in the rebellion of Heinrich II "der Zänker" Duke of Bavaria[357].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Otto was deprived of Carinthia in 985 when it was returned to Duke Heinrich I [Liutpoldinger], but allowed to retain the ducal title and granted lands around Worms and Frankfurt[358].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was restored as Duke of Carinthia from 995 to 1002.  Presumably he is the "Duke Otto" who is listed first among those presiding over a court hearing in the presence of Emperor Otto III at Pavia 14 Oct 1001[359].  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Thietmar, he was proposed as candidate for the imperial throne in 1002 but declined the offer[360].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Thietmar names "Duke Otto of Carinthia and Verona" among those sent by Heinrich II King of Germany to Lombardy after his election to quell unrest[361].  Wipo names "Ottone duce Francorum" when recording the names of his sons[362].
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of Fulda records the death "1003 II Non Nov" of "Otto dux"[363]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m JUDITH, daughter of --- (-991, bur Worms Cathedral).  
2 CONT *"Chuonradus… Romanorum imperator augustus" donated property to the church of Worms naming "filii nostri Heinrici Regis, filie quoque nostre Beatricis" for the souls of "parentum nostrorum defunctorum atavi nostri ducis Chuonradi,
2 CONC  avie nostre Iudithe, patris nostri Heinrici, patrui nostri ducis Chuonradi eiusque coniugis Mathildis, sororis etiam nostre Iudithe", all buried at Worms Cathedral, by charter dated 30 Jan 1034[364].  According to Wegener[365], J
2 CONC udith was the daughter of Heinrich, son of Arnulf Duke of Bavaria [Liutpoldinger], but he cites no primary source to support the suggestion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Otto & his wife had [four] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  HEINRICH (-28 Sep [989/1000], bur Worms cathedral).  
2 CONT *Wipo names "Hezil et Chuono…Brunone et Willihelmo" as sons of "Ottone duce Francorum"[366].  
2 CONT *m as her first husband, ADELHEID, daughter of [RICHARD Graf von Metz or GERHARD Graf von Metz] [Matfride] & his wife --- (-19 May [1039/46], bur Öhningen Stiftskirche).  Wipo names "Adalheida ex nobilissima gente Litharingorum or
2 CONC iunda…soror…comitem Gerhardi et Adalberti" as mother of "maioris Chuononis"[367].  She married secondly ---, as suggested by the Vita Meinwerci which names "Gebehardus iuvenis, frater imperatoris" when recording his tonsure at the
2 CONC  synod in 1028[368], 
2 CONT *Gerhard not being named elsewhere as full brother of Emperor Konrad I.  "Heinricus…rex" donated property "Lohwilare in pago Bietgowe in comitatu Bezelini comitis…ex avia nostra domina Adelheit iure hereditario suscepimus" to the 
2 CONC cathedral of Speyer by charter dated 7 Sep 1046[369].  
2 CONT *Graf Heinrich & his wife had two children: Emperor Konrad I (b. c990, d. 4 June 1039 Utrecht, b. Speyer Cathedral), and Judith (d. c998, b. before 30 January 1034 in Worms Cathedral)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  BRUNO (-Rome 18 Feb or 12 Mar 999, bur Rome, St Peter's).  
2 CONT *Herimannus names "Counradus dux Carentani, filius Ottonis ducis fraterque Brunonis dudum papæ" when recording his death in 1012[372].  Wipo names "Hezil et Chuono…Brunone et Willihelmo" as sons of "Ottone duce Francorum", specify
2 CONC ing that Bruno was elected Pope as Gregory[373].  
2 CONT *Elected Pope GREGORY V 3 May 996.  
2 CONT *Thietmar records the death in Rome of Pope Gregory on 4 Feb[374]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  KONRAD (-12 or 15 Dec 1011, bur Worms Cathedral).  
2 CONT *Herimannus names "Counradus dux Carentani, filius Ottonis ducis fraterque Brunonis dudum papæ" when recording his death in 1012[375].  Wipo names "Hezil et Chuono…Brunone et Willihelmo" as sons of "Ottone duce Francorum"[376].  
2 CONT *Candidate for the Imperial throne 1002.  He was installed as KONRAD I Duke of Carinthia in 1004.  
2 CONT *The necrology of Fulda records the death "1011 II Id Dec" of "Cuonradus dux"[377].  
2 CONT *m ([1002]) as her first husband, MATHILDE of Swabia, daughter of HERMANN II Duke of Swabia & his wife Gerberga of Upper Burgundy ([988]-29 Jul [1031/32], bur Worms cathedral).  Wipo names "Mahthilda de filia Chuonradi regis Burgu
2 CONC ndiæ" as mother of "iunioris Chuononis"[378].  Thietmar refers to "Konrad" as son-in-law of Hermann Duke of Swabia, recording that they attacked Strasbourg together after the election of Heinrich II King of Germany[379].  "Chuonra
2 CONC dus…Romanorum imperator augustus" donated property to the church of Worms naming "filii nostri Heinrici Regis, filie quoque nostre Beatricis" for the souls of "parentum nostrorum defunctorum atavi nostri ducis Chuonradi, avie nost
2 CONC re Iudithe, patris nostri Heinrici, patrui nostri ducis Chuonradi eiusque coniugis Mathildis, sororis etiam nostre Iudithe", all buried at Worms Cathedral, by charter dated 30 Jan 1034[380].  She married secondly Frédéric II Duke 
2 CONC of Upper Lotharingia [Wigeriche].  The primary source which records her second marriage has not so far been identified.  However, the Chronicon Sancti Michælis, monasterii in pago Virdunensi names "duabus puellulis Sophia et Beatr
2 CONC ice" as daughters of the son of "duce Theodorico", specifying that the empress was their amita and that she adopted them after their father died[381].  The Alberti Milioli Notarii Regini Liber de Temporibus names "comitissam Beatr
2 CONC icam…de Gallia…filia comitis Frederic, mater…domina Matilda", but does not give the origin of Mathilde[382].  She married thirdly Esiko Graf im Schwabengau [Askanier-Ballenstedt].  The Annalista Saxo names "Machtildis" sister of G
2 CONC isela, wife of Emperor Konrad II, and her third husband[383].  She attended the Easter celebrations at Ingelheim in 1030[384].  The Liber Anniversariorum of Einsiedeln records the death in Jul of "Mechthild soror imperatricis Gisl
2 CONC æ"[385].  
2 CONT *Duke Konrad & his wife had [three] children: Konrad "der Jüngere" (b. c1003, d. 20 July 1039, b. Worms Cathedral), Bruno (b. c1004, d. 27 May 1045 Persenbeug, b. Wurzburg Cathedral) Bishop of Wurzburg, and the unknown wife of Haz
2 CONC zelin, Graf im Zulpichgau.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  WILHELM (-7 Nov 1047).  
2 CONT *Wipo names "Hezil et Chuono…Brunone et Willihelmo" as sons of "Ottone duce Francorum", specifying that Wilhelm was made "Argentinensis ecclesiæ episcopus"[396].  
2 CONT *Chaplain of Empress Gisela.  
2 CONT *He was elected Bishop of Strasbourg in 1029[397].  Jackman speculates that Wilhelm may have been illegitimate[398]. 
2 CONT
