2 CONT Ethelred 'the Unready'
2 CONT Edgar's sudden death at the age of 33 led to a succession dispute betweenrival factions supporting his sons Edward and Ethelred. The elder sonEdward (reigned 975-978) was murdered in 978 at Corfe Castle, Dorset, byhis seven year o
2 CONC ld half-brother's supporters. For the rest of Ethelred'sreign (reigned 978-1016), his brother became a posthumous rallying pointfor political unrest; a hostile Church transformed Edward into a royalmartyr. Known as the Un-raed or 
2 CONC 'Unready' (meaning no counsel, or that hewas unwise), Ethelred failed to win or retain the allegiance of many ofhis subjects. In 1002, he ordered the massacre of all Danes in England toeliminate potential treachery.
2 CONT Not being an able soldier, Ethelred defended the country againstincreasingly rapacious Viking raids from the 980s onwards by diplomaticalliance with the duke of Normandy in 991 (he later married the duke'sdaughter Emma) and by buy
2 CONC ing off renewed attacks by the Danes with moneylevied through a tax called the Danegeld. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle in1006 was dismissive: 'in spite of it all, the Danish army went about asit pleased'. By 1012, 48,000 pounds of sil
2 CONC ver was being paid in Danegeldto Danes camped in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, in 1013, Ethelred fled to Normandy when king Sweyn of Denmarkdispossessed him. Ethelred returned to rule after Sweyn's death in 1014.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethelred's son Edmund set himself up as an independent ruler in theDanelaw. After Ethelred's death in 1015, Edmund cleared southern Englandof Danish marauders in a series of fiercely fought and highly mobilefighting, but he lost t
2 CONC he battle of Ashingdon of 1016 (his Mercian alliesdeserted him) against Sweyn's son Canute, and died in the same year.Before his death, Edmund made an agreement with Canute giving Canuteterritorial concessions, including Wessex. E
2 CONC dmund was buried atGlastonbury.
2 CONT [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
