2 CONT
2 CONT ===Succession===
2 CONT Caradog left a son, Owain ap Caradog, who contented himself with the rule of Gwynllwg and was the founder of the line of the Lords of Caerleon in south east Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.oxforddnb.com/view/printable/48555
2 CONT
2 CONT Iorwerth ab Owain (d. 1175x84), prince in Wales, lord of Caerleon, succeeded the king, his elder brother Morgan ab Owain, on Morgan's violent death in 1158. There is no indication that Iorwerth ever used the royal style, derived f
2 CONC rom their grandfather, Caradog ap Gruffudd (d. 1081), one-time dominant king of Gwlad Morgan and client of William I.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://welshleigh.org/genealogy/prichardancestry/prichardhistorical4.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of CARADOG put an end to effective Welsh leadership in south east Wales, and the Normans took over much of the fertile lowlands, though CARADOG’s son OWAIN WAN (the weak) did retain some influence. OWAIN’s eldest son Mor
2 CONC gan was recognized as the Lord of Caerleon (west Monmouthshire) by king Henry II, and after his death in 1158 he was succeeded by his brother IORWERTH ab OWAIN. For some reason Henry dispossessed him in 1171, but two years later I
2 CONC ORWERTH and his son HYWEL seized Caerleon and other castles in Monmouthshire. Though they lost them soon afterwards, their friendship with the LORD RHYS induced the king to return Caerleon to them, and in 1184 HYWEL was the only W
2 CONC elshman among six men who held castles in Glamorgan and Monmouthshire in the king’s name when the Welsh attacked Glamorgan (DWB “Morgan ap Hywel”, Bradney, Vol III, p.187). He was complimented by Gerald of Wales for “observing a s
2 CONC trict neutrality between the Welsh and the English” (quoted by R.R.Davies, Conquest p.102). Lowland Monmouthshire had been under nominal Norman control for a century, but Davies noted: “It was indicative of continuing native influ
2 CONC ence in the area that when a Cistercian abbey was founded at Llantarnam near Caerleon in 1179, its mother house should be Strata Florida in the heart of native Wales, and its patron and protector was the local Welsh ruler in Monmo
2 CONC uthshire, HYWEL ab IORWERTH ab OWAIN” (p.273).
