2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE Gruffydd, the eldest living son of Prince Rhys, became Prince of South 
2 CONT Wales at the death of his 96 year old father.
1 NOTE By 1150, Gruffydd ap Rhys and Gwenllian verch Gruffydd's sons had succeeded in seizing almost the whole of Ystrad Tywi and much of Dyfed (page 124 of History of Wales)
1 NOTE http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gruffydd_ap_Rhys
1 NOTE He was murdered by his second wife. Name unknown.
1 NOTE He was crowned in 1112.
1 NOTE He was crowned in 1112.
1 NOTE Gruffydd ap Rhys (died 1137) ruled a portion of Deheubarth, Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT Following the death of Gruffydd's father Rhys ap Tewdwr in 1093, Deheubarth was taken over by the Normans, and Gruffydd spent much of his early years in exile in Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned about 1113, and after several years of wandering from place to place was able to raise enough men to attack a number of Norman castles and towns in 1116 with some success. However an attack on Aberystwyth was defeated 
2 CONC and Gruffydd's army dispersed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd came to terms with King Henry I of England and was allowed to rule a portion of his father's kingdom, the Cantref Mawr, although he was soon under pressure from the Normans again and was forced to flee to Ireland for a pe
2 CONC riod in 1127.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebellion
2 CONT In 1136 Gruffydd joined with Owain Gwynedd and Cadwaladr, the sons of Gruffydd ap Cynan of Gwynedd, in a rebellion against Norman rule. While Rhys was away from home, his wife Gwenllian ferch Gruffydd raised an army and attacked t
2 CONC he Norman castle of Kidwelly but was defeated and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd himself with Owain and Cadwaladr gained a crushing victory over the Normans at Crug Mawr near Cardigan the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and succession
2 CONT In 1137 Gruffydd gained further success in Dyfed, but died shortly afterwards in uncertain circumstances.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd had four sons by Gwenllian ferch Gruffydd, Maredudd, Rhys, Morgan and Maelgwn. He also had two older sons by a previous marriage, Anarawd and Cadell, and at least two daughters, Gwladus and Nest. He was followed by his el
2 CONC dest son, Anarawd. Of his other sons, Cadell, Maredydd and Rhys (later known as The Lord Rhys) all ruled Deheubarth in turn.
1 NOTE "AP RHYS AP TUDOR MAWR"; KING OF SOUTH WALES 1135-1137; HAD 3 SONS & 2
2 CONT DAUGHTERS BESIDES THOSE LISTED
1 NOTE [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Dictionary of National Biography; History of the Morgan Family; Dakota.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Dakota: Griffith ap Lord Rhys ap Tudor Mawr, P/W.
2 CONT    Dictionary says he was brought up in Ireland after his father's death in 1093. But about 1113 he returned to Dyved, waged war, picked up some territory and laid claims to more. After a successful battle in 1136 he regained many
2 CONC  of the lands of his ancient inheritance.   He remarried and was slain in 1137 through the treachery of his new wife. Dictionary doesn't give details. One and one-half pages on this man, Page 310.                         ******** 
2 CONC Gruffydd (Griffith) ap Rhys, King of Wales, 1135.
2 CONT "Returned from exile in Ireland to fight the Normans in 1113.  Married Gwenillian, daughter of Griffith ap Cynan, King of Gwynedd, in 1116.  She was killed leading her husband's army in an attack on the Norman castle at Kidwell in
2 CONC  1136.  The spot where she fell is named Maes Gwenillian in her honor. Griffith continued the rebellion against the Normans.  Their sons were Meredith, Morgan, and Rhys." [Source 1]
2 CONT He was brought up in Ireland after his father's death in 1093.  But about 1113, he returned to Dyfed, waged war, picked up some territory and laid claims to more.  After a successful battle in 1136, he regained many of the lands o
2 CONC f his ancient inheritance.  He remarried and was slain in 1137 through the treachery of his new wife.  This source does not give details.  It devotes one and a half pages to Gruffydd.  [Source 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT   1.  Morgan, Dennis.  _A History of the Morgan Family_.
2 CONT   2.  _Dictionary of National Biography_; page 310.
