2 CONT 1  NAME Brian /Boru/ 1  NAME Brian of the /Tributes/ 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001 1  BIRT 2  DATE 940 2  SOUR S033320 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001 1  DEAT 2  DATE 1014 2  SOUR S03332
2 CONC 0 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: Young; Kings and Queens of Britain and A. Roots, 175, 239; Kraentzler 1441.   Roots: Brian of the Tributes (Boru, Borama and Boroimhe), King of the Dalcassians, then King of Munster, 976-1002, and finally usurped the high
2 CONC  kingship of Ireland, 1002-1014. Killed at the Battle of Clontarf, 1014, while fighting a mixed force of Norse and Leinstermen. (Brian's amcestry table depends solely on tradition and is not accepted as proven. The ancestors liste
2 CONC d by Young are different from those in Roots. Have used Young's line.)    K&Q calls him Brian Boru, High King of Ireland from 1002-1014. According to K&Q he had another son, Teige (Terence) who died in 1023 and apparently was succ
2 CONC eeded by his brother, Donnchad, that year. One of Teige's descendants, some 30 generations down, was Lady Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon, who married King George the VI and is the mother of Queen Elizabeth II.     And, according to Roots, D
2 CONC ictionary of National Biography and several other sources, Brian and another wife, through a daughter Slani, were ancestors of a a distinguished Welsh line.    Young: Brian Boromhe, 940-1014, king of Munster, high king of Ireland,
2 CONC  most famous of all Irish kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clan O'Brien
2 CONT Note: Killed in the Battle of Clontarf.  First King to unite all ofIreland Reigned 39 years. Crowned at the Rock Of Cashel. Held Bunratty Castle, County Clare.
1 NOTE In 978 he became king of Cashel, capital of the ancient kingdom of 
2 CONT Munster. By 984 he controlled all of Munster, and in 1001 he was 
2 CONT acknowledged the chief king of Ireland. From his youth, Brian had been 
2 CONT fighting the Vikings, who had occupied part of the country. In the Battle 
2 CONT of Clontarf (April 23, 1014), his sons led an Irish army to a decisive 
2 CONT victory over the Vikings, thereby permanently destroying their power in 
2 CONT Ireland. Brian, too aged to fight, was awaiting news of the battle when he 
2 CONT was slain in his tent by a Viking intruder. Brian is one of the great 
2 CONT heroes of the Irish, but fact and legend have become so intertwined in the 
2 CONT accounts of his life that an accurate biography cannot be given.
1 NOTE 32nd great grandfather
