2 CONT An Anglo-Saxon gentlewoman, patron of the arts, equestrienne, and t ax
2 CONT protester, etc. All the historians say that she "flourished, circa 1040
2 CONT 1080 A.D." Leofric, earl of Mercia, and husband of Lady Godiva, was a man
2 CONT o f broad but obscure interests; a religious man thoroughly Christian, and
2 CONT an e ntrepreneur, raconteur, and general all around good sport. At the
2 CONT same time, he did have an avaricious streak in him, and it is for this
2 CONT reason that his n ame has survived through the ages, but more prominently
2 CONT for his interesting r esponse to the nagging of his bleeding-heart wife,
2 CONT who pestered him incessant ly over details of the daily lives of the
2 CONT peasants under his control, and sim ilar fussy matters.
2 CONT It's not that she was always being petulant about everythi ng he did, but
2 CONT certain of his actions caused her to be irritable, but that pa rt of the
2 CONT story comes later.
2 CONT Actually both Leofric and Godiva were quite rel igious, and upon their
2 CONT move to Coventry, Warwickshire, from Shrewsbury, Shrop shire (where
2 CONT Leofric had earned his fortune and title from amazing successes in the
2 CONT mutton trade), they were immediately impressed by the lack of proper
2 CONT facilities for training and housing men of the cloth in or around the
2 CONT raucou s little district of Coventry (pop. 6215). How, they thought, were
2 CONT the spirit ual needs of these simple souls to be cared forAt the same
2 CONT time, being some what "nouveau riche" and anxious to make their mark in
2 CONT what passed for gentee l society there (not having had the proper
2 CONT credentials to enter such circles back in Shropshire- which may have
2 CONT been a big piece of the motivation for t he move), they decided to apply
2 CONT some of their ready cash to a worthy public c ause. Near the physical
2 CONT center of Coventry, where the bombed-out ruin of migh ty Coventry
2 CONT Cathedral stands today, Leofric and Godiva (in 1043) founded and funded
2 CONT an abbey (some accounts say monastery), named in honor of Ste. Eunice of
2 CONT Saxmundham (an early martyr, slain by flaying at the hands of the
2 CONT Romans ). The abbey faced the rising sun, as was decreed it should by
2 CONT Leofric in a m oment of romantic inspiration. A simple affair, made of
2 CONT wattle and daub with a roof of thatched reeds in the manner of the day,
2 CONT it was nevertheless imposi ng by standards in the village otherwise; it
2 CONT was the biggest structure in the entire region.
2 CONT Before long this edifice was something of a social focus for t he town,
2 CONT functioning not only as a center for the education and housing of th ose
2 CONT who had received the calling, but also as a gathering point where more
2 CONT vulgar popular events and festivities could be celebrated. Leofric and
2 CONT Godiva came to be awarded the attention and respect they longed for from
2 CONT the start, and they had served the purposes of the church as well. They
2 CONT did not seem to mind that much of the activity circulating around the
2 CONT abbey was decidedly Dr uid in origin; at least the peasants were occupied
2 CONT and happy. Coventry grew a round the abbey.
2 CONT As a gentleman, and now established philanthropist of some lo cal repute,
2 CONT Leofric assumed a growing role in the governance of public affair s, and
2 CONT was given responsibility for certain financial matters (the town had
2 CONT grown enough to actually have financial matters), which he quickly
2 CONT understoo d as presenting especially interesting opportunities. One public
2 CONT work generat ed the idea of another, if only some money were at hand to
2 CONT facilitate such gr owth... Meanwhile, Godiva's proficiency as a
2 CONT horsewoman had become polished to a considerable degree, as she had
2 CONT acquired a taste for the hunt and the so cial pleasantries which accrue to
2 CONT the activity "apres." Plus, the people she met during these excursions
2 CONT which provided such complacency, were of a dispos ition and delicacy of
2 CONT interest to which she had aspired for some time. She co uld do worse than
2 CONT to engage in certain preoccupations of the intellect, and c onsiderations
2 CONT of the aesthetic, and so she imme
1 NOTE LADY GODIVA (Godgifu, in the spelling of her time.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT An Anglo-Saxon gentlewoman, patron of the arts, equestrienne, and taxprotester, etc. All the historians say that she "flourished, circa 1040 -1080 A.D."
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofric, earl of Mercia, and husband of Lady Godiva, was a man of broadbut obscure interests; a religious man thoroughly Christian, and anentrepreneur, raconteur, and general all around good sport. At the sametime, he did have an 
2 CONC avaricious streak in him, and it is for this reasonthat his name has survived through the ages, but more prominently for hisinteresting response to the nagging of his bleeding-heart wife, whopestered him incessantly over details o
2 CONC f the daily lives of the peasantsunder his control, and similar fussy matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT It's not that she was always being petulant about everything he did, butcertain of his actions caused her to be irritable, but that part of thestory comes later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Actually both Leofric and Godiva were quite religious, and upon theirmove to Coventry, Warwickshire, from Shrewsbury, Shropshire (whereLeofric had earned his fortune and title from amazing successes in themutton trade), they were 
2 CONC immediately impressed by the lack of properfacilities for training and housing men of the cloth in or around theraucous little district of Coventry (pop. 6215). How, they thought, werethe spiritual needs of these simple souls to b
2 CONC e cared for? At the sametime, being somewhat "nouveau riche" and anxious to make their mark inwhat passed for genteel society there (not having had the propercredentials to enter such circles back in Shropshire -- which may havebe
2 CONC en a big piece of the motivation for the move), they decided to applysome of their ready cash to a worthy public cause.
2 CONT
2 CONT Near the physical center of Coventry, where the bombed-out ruin of mightyCoventry Cathedral stands today, Leofric and Godiva (in 1043) founded andfunded an abbey (some accounts say monastery), named in honor of Ste.Eunice of Saxmu
2 CONC ndham (an early martyr, slain by flaying at the hands ofthe Romans). The abbey faced the rising sun, as was decreed it should byLeofric in a moment of romantic inspiration. A simple affair, made ofwattle and daub with a roof of th
2 CONC atched reeds in the manner of the day,it was nevertheless imposing by standards in the village otherwise; itwas the biggest structure in the entire region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before long this edifice was something of a social focus for the town,functioning not only as a center for the education and housing of thosewho had received the calling, but also as a gathering point where morevulgar popular even
2 CONC ts and festivities could be celebrated. Leofric andGodiva came to be awarded the attention and respect they longed for fromthe start, and they had served the purposes of the church as well. Theydid not seem to mind that much of th
2 CONC e activity circulating around theabbey was decidedly Druid in origin; at least the peasants were occupiedand happy. Coventry grew around the abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a gentleman, and now established philanthropist of some local repute,Leofric assumed a growing role in the governance of public affairs, andwas given responsibility for certain financial matters (the town hadgrown enough to act
2 CONC ually have financial matters), which he quicklyunderstood as presenting especially interesting opportunities. One publicwork generated the idea of another, if only some money were at hand tofacilitate such growth...
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Godiva's proficiency as a horsewoman had become polished to aconsiderable degree, as she had acquired a taste for the hunt and thesocial pleasantries which accrue to the activity "apres." Plus, thepeople she met during 
2 CONC these excursions which provided such complacency,were of a disposition and delicacy of interest to which she had aspiredfor some time. She could do worse than to engage in certainpreoccupations of the intellect, and considerations
2 CONC  of the aesthetic, andso she immersed herself in the arts and, therefore, society.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps, she earnestly thought, aid to those industrious in this field ofelevated concern (artists) would inspire the rude masses by means ofexample. Commissioning a proper portrait of herself would be a good placeto start; it wou
2 CONC ld be an inspiration for simpler souls, so the work wasbegun.
2 CONT
2 CONT It only slowly entered Godiva's consciousness that the lack of successshe was having in interesting the base masses in artistic concerns,beautiful pictures of herself spread around or not, was rooted in thefact that nearly all of 
2 CONC them spent 100% of their waking hours inpartially effective efforts to feed and clothe themselves, and to providesome form of shelter from the elements. Most were having a hard time ofit, in light of the fact that Leofric, in his 
2 CONC new-found megalomaniacalgrand-public-works mode, had been taxing everything he could think of,even including a levy on manure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Godiva would not have such noble aspirations -- such as art foreveryone -- placed on a back burner for the sake of boring considerationslike a municipal water supply. Men had such ignoble visions, alwaysfunctional and mechani
2 CONC cal, mostly never above waist level. This would notdo, the taxes must be reduced if this early medievalsubsistence-agriculture village was going to pull itself up into the 11thcentury and its more cultured concerns. She went to ha
2 CONC ve a pointed talkwith Leofric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beside himself with raucous laughter, Leofric injured his left wristslightly as he fell off his stool in the hall of the village burghers,and this sobered him up rather quickly. Reduce taxes in order to fosterthe peasants' appreci
2 CONC ation of silly pictures? Was she mad? No waterworks?There would be no tax reduction; as a matter of fact, Leofric added a newtax on pictures, which only had to be paid by his wife since she was theonly person who had any, except f
2 CONC or the church which was exempt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their argument became a classic war of wills, taking the equally classicform of nagging versus stone-walling. However, at very long last, sincehis wife would not give up and was driving him to distraction and worse,Leofric capitul
2 CONC ated, but, regarding it all as something of a sport,attached an interesting condition to his offer to allow some reduction intaxation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancient Greeks, he pointed out, and those coarser Romans as well,viewed the nude human body as one of the highest expressions of theperfection of Nature. Nudity was not seen as erotic in any sense, but aspurity, and a celebrat
2 CONC ion of the wonderful form of a sensuous beingdisplayed in all its marvelous glory for the betterment and appreciationof those enlightened enough to consider this aesthetic. To present a wellformed nude body as an object of great b
2 CONC eauty, even art, would be tooffer a lesson of inestimable value to the simple peasants of Coventry,whose experiences and perceptions had never been enlightened toappreciate such perfection.
2 CONT
2 CONT If Lady Godiva truly believed in the crusade she was promoting, then sheshould lead it herself, and offer to the citizens of Coventry an exampleof the glorious beauty to be understood by careful consideration of aperfect nude huma
2 CONC n body. There could be no shame in this, it would be themost gross error to consider it as such. Was she ashamed of the wondersof God's work? Besides, with all that horse-back riding, and similar, shehad lost some weight and looke
2 CONC d pretty good.
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore, Leofric proclaimed that if Lady Godiva would ride her horsethrough the crowded market-place of Coventry, in the full light ofmid-day, clothed in only that which God had given her, as an example ofthe perfection of God's
2 CONC  work and as an expression of the highest possibleaesthetic -- she had been spreading pictures of herself around anyway --then he would reduce taxes on the populace, lifting from them the burdenGodiva perceived, and erasing from h
2 CONC imself any further doubt he mightharbor of the sincerity of Godiva's convictions.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Leofric's absolute surprise, she agreed, once she had ensured that sheactually had his "permission" to do so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken aback by his wife's courage and certainty in her purposes Leofric,somewhat overwhelmed, then stated that he fully accepted the truth ofLady Godiva's belief in the merits of her cause, and so in response, oncompletion of her 
2 CONC ride he would not just reduce taxes, but would removeall of them -- save those tolls on horses which were already in placebefore he assumed his office, and which were necessary for basic needs ofthe city.
2 CONT
2 CONT A day was chosen for the event, and while no particular effort had beenexpended to publicize the ride, talk of it had spread in whispersthroughout the whole of Coventry. Not wishing to reveal that thisconcealed discussion had take
2 CONC n place, and since people were curious aboutall aspects of the affair and did not want to interrupt it, themarketplace's business proceeded as it might have done on any other lessinteresting Thursday in late August.
2 CONT
2 CONT As noon approached, so did Lady Godiva. She was not alone, but wasaccompanied by two female aides also on horseback, but normally clothed;one rode on each side and slightly to the rear. Three horses walking onthe cobbles in format
2 CONC ion at a measured gait did not have the sound of theusual traffic and bustle, and so -- since all were secretly and eagerlyanticipating the event -- her appearance was announced clearly toeveryone.
2 CONT
2 CONT She sat straight and properly in the saddle with a look of composure onher face; relaxed, confident, unashamed. Her hair was done in two largebraids which were curled snugly at the back of her head, one on eachside; she wore no je
2 CONC welry or other adornment. People looked at her andsaw that she was not merely naked, or nude; rather she was in a higherstate of presentation -- being a correct and elevated quality of hercomposure, and resulting also from the peo
2 CONC ple's appraisal, appreciation,and consideration beyond simple voyeurism.
2 CONT
2 CONT To all present this was an experience like no other in their lives. Theonly images of people unclothed they had ever seen were in the church:Adam and Eve, and the crucified Christ. This was a lady, simple andnormal with a body lik
2 CONC e that of every other woman present, a human, acreature of God's earth. Though he half meant it as a joke, Leofric'swords rang true: here was a celebration of being in its perfection.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps, as well, some believed with Zoroaster that sex is the bounty ofGod.
2 CONT
2 CONT So, all survived the event with peacefulness and dignity, and the taxeswere removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the CHRONICA, written by Roger of Wendover (who died in 1236), theaccount of the year 1057 tells the story of Godiva's ride in full detail,and is the earliest surviving written description.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even more complete versions are provided by the famous historian RanulfHigden (died in 1364) in his POLYCHRONICON, and by Henry Knighton (diedc. 1396) who followed him, which explain not only the details of the rideand its reasons
2 CONC , but also the specifics of the removed taxes, inparticular that all save those on horses were eliminated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much later, King Edward I, being an inquisitive man (he devised anearnest, but rather awkward system for the classification of songbirds inWales), wished to discover the truth of the Godiva story and, therefore,commissioned an inq
2 CONC uiry of ancient records which showed that in 1057 andthereabouts, there were indeed no taxes levied in Coventry except thoseon horses, which was a rather anomalous situation not seen elsewhere atthe time, thereby establishing the 
2 CONC merit and probable accuracy of thelegend.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tale of "Peeping Tom", who was struck blind (or dead) when he alonegazed upon Lady Godiva was not added until the 17th century. This is alsotrue of the detail of the story, often added, that Godiva was coveredtotally, except f
2 CONC or her legs, by an enormous and improbable quantity ofhair.
2 CONT
2 CONT Doubtless both of these embellishments were supplied later by prudishChristian churchmen who entirely missed the point and considered thatviewing the unclothed human body under any circumstance was a heinous actwhich would damn on
2 CONC e to eternal hell fire; they certainly thought thefemale body to be dirty and inferior to appreciation, and only worthy ofbeing hidden from view. On the face of it such a view would seem to be aperversion, and affront to the beaut
2 CONC y of God's work. The Greeks felt thatthe idealized human form was the only one worthy to represent the gods onearth. On the other hand, the Christian faith is unique in that it alonehas, throughout its history, suppressed any cele
2 CONC bration of the beauty ofthe human body.
2 CONT
2 CONT So, what was Lady Godiva? A visionary; a social climber; a patron of thearts; a dilettante of the worst order?
2 CONT
2 CONT In any estimation, she had the guts to follow her convictions, and mayhave brought a degree of enlightenment to a small corner of 11th centuryEngland. And, probably, no one went to Hell because of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT © Jerome C. Krause
1 NOTE 998? - Born of an English-Saxon nationality. She was the wife of the Danish Lord Leofric, the Earl of Mercia (who was given land by King Canute).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1057 - She and Leofric got in a dispute over the taxes he had levied on the growing city of Coventry, and he challenged her to ride naked through the marketplace, promising to ease the tax burden if she did.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Polychronicon, a fourteenth-century chronicle by Ranulf Higden, says that, as a result, Leofric did excuse the town of all taxes except those on horses. A later chronicle adds that Godiva requested the townspeople to remain in
2 CONC doors during her ride. In the seventeenth-century, Peeping Tom became part of the legend, being struck blind or dead when he looked out his window.
1 NOTE Lady Godiva:  aka Godgifu, wife of Leofric, Earl of Mercia
2 CONT
