1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Godiva, Lady (flourished about 1040-80), Anglo-Saxon noblewoman, wife ofLeofric, earl of Mercia (flourished 1005-57).  She is known to havepersuaded her husband to found monasteries at Coventry (1043) and Stow.According to legend,
2 CONC  she obtained a reduction in the excessive taxeslevied by her husband on the people of Coventry by consenting to ridenaked through the town on a white horse. Only one person disobeyed herorders to remain indoors behind closed shut
2 CONC ters; this man,  a tailorknown afterward as Peeping Tom, peered through a window and immediatelybecame blind. The oldest form of the legend is in the 13th-century FloresHistoriarum (Flowers of the Historians). A festival in her ho
2 CONC nor wasinstituted as part of Coventry Fair in 1678.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'The World Book Encyclopedia', 1968, p G235. 'Godiva, Lady,'Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1993
2 CONT Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 1993 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation
