1 NOTE The famous lady Godiva who rode nude on her horse through CoventryThis is the famous Lady Godiva.  She complained constantly to her husband that the taxes were too high on the townspeople of Coventry.  He finally said that he woul
2 CONC d reduce the taxes if she would ride nude through the marketplace on market day.  She arranged for all of the men to remain inside and covered her entire body, except her legs, with her hair. Supposedly one person named Tom did no
2 CONC t remain inside during her ride and became known as "Peeping Tom".   Leofric eliminated all taxes, except for one on horses.  During Edward I's reign, a check was made and the only tax in Coventry was one on horses.  Since 1678 th
2 CONC e town of Coventry still celebrates the ride during its annual fair.Concerning her dates of birth and death.  I originally had 980-1067, but this was not in agreement with Ancestral Roots line 176a-2, which has her birth at c1010 
2 CONC and her marriage at c1030.  Nor was it consistant with the Encyclopedia Britannica which had Godiva fl. 1040-1080.  However the 980 birth date was consistant with her gr grandaughter Nesta b. c1055/7, dau of Edith, dau of Aelfgar,
2 CONC  son of Godiva.  With some serious scrunching of dates I could conceive of:
2 CONT
2 CONT      Godiva b. 1010 (originally 980)
2 CONT      Aelfgar b. 1025 (originally 1002)
2 CONT      Edith    b. 1041 (originally 1030)
2 CONT      Nesta   b. 1057with two other daughters of Leofric born to a "first wife or mistress". It is physically possible for 3 generations in a row born at the 15/16th year of their parents life (however unlikely), so that is what I 
2 CONC have done.
1 NOTE This is the famous Lady Godiva. She complained constantly to her husband that the taxes were too high on the townspeople of Coventry. He finally said that he would reduce the taxes if she would ride nude through the marketplace on
2 CONC  market day. She arranged for all of the men to remain inside and covered her entire body, except her legs, with her hair. Supposedly one person named Tom did not remain inside during her ride and became known as "Peeping Tom". Le
2 CONC ofric eliminated all taxes, except for one on horses. During Edward I's reign, a check was made and the only tax in Coventry was one on horses. Since 1678 the town of Coventry still celebrates the ride during its annual fair.
