3 FORM pdf
3 TITL Keats-Rohan, "Antecessor Noster"
1 CHAN
2 DATE 02 JUL 2025
3 TIME 23:48:06
0 @I6000000000970342399@ INDI
1 NAME Leofric
2 GIVN Leofric
2 NICK Leofric
2 NICK Earl of Mercia
2 NICK Leofric III Earl of Mercia
2 NICK Leogric the Great
2 NICK Earl of Mercia and Lord of Coventry
1 NAME Leofric III, earl of Mercia
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 968
2 ADDR
3 CITY Mercia
3 STAE England
3 CTRY UK
1 BURI
2 DATE 31 AUG 1057
2 ADDR
3 CITY Coventry
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 OCT 1057
2 ADDR
3 CITY King's Bromley
3 STAE England
1 OCCU Founder of the church of Coventry, Earl of Mercia, founder of the Church of Coventry, Earl of Leicester, Earl of Chester, Lord of Coventry, King of Mercia, koning van Mercia
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Leofric (b 968, d 31 Aug or 30 Sep 1057) Earl of Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Leofwine & his wife Spouse: "Lady Godiva" Godgifu Child: Ælfgar of Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT LINKS http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leofric,_Earl_of_Mercia http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20nobility.htm#Leofricdied1057B
2 CONT
2 CONT MEDIEVAL LANDS
2 CONT
2 CONT LEOFRIC, son of LEOFWINE Ealdorman of the Hwicce in Mercia (-Bromley 30 Oct 1057, bur Coventry[222]).   The Genealogia Fundatoris of Coventry Monastery names “Leofricum postea comitem, et Edwinum occisum per Walenses, et Normannum
2 CONC  occisum cum Edrico duce Merciorum per Cnutonem regem” as sons of “Leofwinus comes Leicestriæ”[223].  The De Gestis Herwardi Saxonis names "Lefricus de Brunne, nepos comitis Radulfi cognominati Scalre", when recording that he was 
2 CONC father of "Herwardus"[224].  This "comitis Radulfi…Scalre" has not otherwise been identified nor any possible relationship with Leofric.  Simeon of Durham records that King Canute appointed "Leofric" as Earl of Mercia after his br
2 CONC other Northman was killed in 1017[225], although this was apparently during the lifetime of their father.  He and his wife founded the abbey of Coventry in 1043[226].  “Leofricus comes” founded the monastery of Coventry by undated
2 CONC  charter[227].  ”Leofricus comes…et conjux mea Godgyve” donated property to Evesham Monastery by undated charter which names “frater meus Normannus”[228]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m GODGIFU, sister of THOROLD de Bukenhale, Sheriff of Lincolnshire, daughter of ---.  She is named as wife of Earl Leofric by Florence of Worcester, who specifies that she and her husband founded monasteries at Leominster, Wenlock
2 CONC , Chester and Stowe[229].  The Annals of Peterborough record that “Thoroldus vicecomes et frater germanus Godivæ comitissæ Leycestriæ” founded Spalding Monastery in 1052[230].  Her family origin is also indicated by the undated ch
2 CONC arter under which “Thoroldus de Bukenhale…vicecomiti” donated Spalding monastery to Croyland abbey which names “domino meo Leofrico comite Leicestriæ et…comitissa sua domina Godiva sorore mea…et cognati mei comitis Algari primogen
2 CONC iti et hæredis eorum”[231].  The De Gestis Herwardi Saxonis names "Aediva trinepta Oslaci ducis" as wife of "Lefricus de Brunne, nepos comitis Radulfi cognominati Scalre", when recording that they were parents of "Herwardus"[232].
2 CONC   "Oslaci ducis" could be "Oslac" recorded in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle as "earl [of Northumbria]" in 966[233], but any precise relationship has not been identified.  ”Leofricus comes…et conjux mea Godgyve” donated property to Eve
2 CONC sham Monastery by undated charter which names “frater meus Normannus”[234].  Godgifu wife of Leofric granted property to St Mary's, Stow by charter dated [1054/57][235].  She was the Lady Godiva of legend. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofric & his wife had [two] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         ÆLFGAR (-[1062]).  The Genealogia Fundatoris of Coventry Monastery names “Algarus tertius” as son of “Leofricus tertius”[236].  Florence of Worcester records that he was created Earl of the East Angles in 1053, in succe
2 CONC ssion to Harold Godwinson who had succeeded his father as Earl of Wessex[237].  Florence of Worcester also records that Ælfgar was banished in 1055 by King Edward "without any just cause of offence"[238].  He went to Ireland, then
2 CONC  to Wales where he allied himself with Gruffydd ap Llywellyn King of Gwynedd and Powys, and invaded England, sacking Hereford in Oct 1055[239].  He was reinstated in 1056 when Gruffydd accepted Edward's overlordship.  Florence of 
2 CONC Worcester records that Ælfgar was appointed to succeed his father in 1057 as Earl of Mercia[240], the earldom of the East Angles passing to Gyrth Godwinsson.  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that in 1057 he was banished again[24
2 CONC 1], but Florence of Worcester states that he forced his restoration in 1058 with the help of Gruffydd and a Norwegian fleet[242].  His death removed from the scene the only potential challenger to Harold Godwinson Earl of Wessex. 
2 CONC  m firstly ÆLFGIFU, daughter of MORCAR & his wife Ealdgyth ---.  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  m secondly ([1058]) --- of Gwynedd, daughter of GRUFFYDD ap Llywellyn Prin
2 CONC ce of Gwynedd and Powys & his first wife ---.  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.  Earl Ælfgar & his first wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         EDWIN (-killed 1071).  The Genealogia Fundatoris of Coventry Monastery names “Edwinum et Morcar postea comites” as sons of “Algarus tertius”[243].  He succeeded his father in 1062 as Earl of Mercia.  With support from h
2 CONC is brother, he expelled Tostig Godwinsson from Lindsay in 1066.  John of Worcester records that they at first supported the claim of Edgar Atheling to succeed Harold II as King of England after the battle of Hastings, but soon wit
2 CONC hdrew their armies and swore allegiance to King William I at Berkhamsted[244].  Florence of Worcester records that "…comites Edwinum et Morkarum…" went with King William to Normandy 21 Feb [1067][245].  They rebelled against Willi
2 CONC am in 1068, leaving court for Yorkshire, but were soon brought to submission.  Orderic Vitalis says that the rebellion was triggered because King William broke his promise to give his daughter in marriage to Edwin[246], and in a l
2 CONC ater passage that Edwin was killed by his servants while on his way to relieve his brother in Ely[247].  Florence of Worcester records that "comites Edwinus et Morkarus" rebelled against King William in [1071], and that Edwin was 
2 CONC killed[248]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         MORCAR (-after 1087).  The Genealogia Fundatoris of Coventry Monastery names “Edwinum et Morcar postea comites” as sons of “Algarus tertius”[249].  Snorre names “Earl Morukare”, although stating that he was the son of “
2 CONC Earl Gudin Ulfnadson” and “Earl Ulf´s sister Gyda”[250].  He was chosen by the Northumbrians as Earl of Northumbria in 1065 to replace Tostig, son of Godwin Earl of Wessex.  With support from his brother, he expelled Tostig Godwin
2 CONC sson from Lindsay in 1066.  John of Worcester records that they at first supported the claim of Edgar Atheling to succeed Harold II as King of England after the battle of Hastings, but soon withdrew their armies and swore allegian
2 CONC ce to King William I at Berkhamsted[251].  Florence of Worcester records that "…comites Edwinum et Morkarum…" went with King William to Normandy 21 Feb [1067][252].  They rebelled against William in 1068, leaving court for Yorkshi
2 CONC re, but were soon brought to submission.  Orderic Vitalis states that Morcar joined the resistance at Ely in 1071[253], but surrendered to the king.  Florence of Worcester records that "comites Edwinus et Morkarus" rebelled agains
2 CONC t King William in [1071], and that "Morkarus…et Siwardus cognomento Barn" took refuge in Ely[254].  Florence of Worcester records that "comites Morkarum et Rogerum, Siwardum cognomento Barn, et Wlnothum regis Haroldi germanum" wer
2 CONC e released by King William on his deathbed in 1087[255].  He was taken to England by King William II but placed in confinement again in Winchester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         EALDGYTH.  Florence of Worcester´s genealogies name "regina Aldgitha, comitis Ælfgari filia" as mother of King Harold´s son "Haroldum"[256].  Orderic Vitalis records that "Edwinus…et Morcarus comites, filii Algari…Edgiv
2 CONC am sororem eorum" married firstly "Gritfridi…regis Guallorum" and secondly "Heraldo"[257].  Her parentage and marriage to King Harold are confirmed by Florence of Worcester who records that "earls Edwin and Morcar…sent off their s
2 CONC ister Queen Elgitha to Chester" after the battle of Hastings[258].   m firstly as his second wife, GRUFFYDD ap Llywellyn Prince of Gwynedd and Powys, son of LLYWELLYN ap Seisyll King of Gwynedd & his wife Angharad of Gwynedd (-kil
2 CONC led Snowdonia 5 Aug 1063).   m secondly ([1064/early 1066][259]) HAROLD Godwinson, son of GODWIN Earl of Wessex & his wife Gytha of Denmark ([1022/25]-killed in battle Hastings 14 Oct 1066, bur [Waltham Abbey]).  He succeeded in 1
2 CONC 066 as HAROLD II King of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         [HEREWARD .  The De Gestis Herwardi Saxonis names "Herwardus" as son of "Lefricus de Brunne, nepos comitis Radulfi cognominati Scalre" and his wife "Aediva trinepta Oslaci ducis", being the "Hereward the Wake" of semi-l
2 CONC egend[260].  m firstly TURFRIDA, daughter of ---.  The De Gestis Herwardi Saxonis records that "Herwardus" married "Turfrida", adding in a later passage that she became a nun "in Cruland" after she was repudiated[261].   m secondl
2 CONC y as her second husband, ---, widow of DOLFIN, daughter of ---.  The De Gestis Herwardi Saxonis records that "Herwardus" married secondly "uxor Dolfini comitis"[262].] 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT WIKIPEDIA Leofric (born 968, died 31 August or 30 September 1057) was the Earl of Mercia and founded monasteries at Coventry and Much Wenlock. Leofric is best remembered as the husband of Lady Godiva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life and political influence Leofric was the son of Ealdorman Leofwine of the Hwicce, who died c. 1023. Leofric's elder brother Northman was killed in 1017, in the losing battles against Cnut.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The victorious Cnut divided England into four great provinces: Wessex, East Anglia, Mercia and Northumbria each of which he eventually placed under the control of an earl (a title new to the English, replacing the Anglo-Saxon "eal
2 CONC dorman"). Mercia he initially left in the hands of Eadric Streona, who had been Ealdorman of Mercia since 1007, but Eadric was killed later in the same year of 1017.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mercia may have been given to Leofric immediately after that [1]. He had certainly become Earl of Mercia by the 1030s. This made him one of the most powerful men in the land, second only to Earl Godwin of Wessex among the mighty e
2 CONC arls. He may have had some connection by marriage with Ælfgifu of Northampton, the first wife of Cnut. That might help to explain why he supported her son Harold Harefoot against Harthacanute, Cnut's son by Emma, when Cnut died in
2 CONC  1035.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT However Harold died in 1040 and was succeeded by Harthacanute, who made himself unpopular with heavy taxation in his short reign. Two of his tax-collectors were killed at Worcester by angry locals. The king was so enraged by this 
2 CONC that in 1041 he ordered Leofric and his other earls to plunder and burn the city, and lay waste the whole area.[3] This command must have sorely tested Leofric. Worcester was the cathedral city of the Hwicce, his people.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Harthacanute died suddenly in 1042, he was succeeded by his half-brother Edward the Confessor. Leofric loyally supported Edward when he came under threat at Gloucester from Earl Godwin in 1051. Leofric and Earl Siward of Nort
2 CONC humbria gathered a great army to meet that of Godwin. Wise heads counseled that battle would be folly, with the flower of England on both sides. Their loss would leave England open to its enemies. So the issue was resolved by less
2 CONC  bloody means. Earl Godwin and his family were outlawed for a time.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Leofric's power was then at its height. But in 1055 his son Ælfgar was outlawed, "without any fault", says the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle. He raised an army in Ireland and Wales and brought it to Hereford, where he clashed with th
2 CONC e army of Earl Ralph of Herefordshire and severely damaged the town. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle wryly comments "And then when they had done most harm, it was decided to reinstate Earl Ælfgar".
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofric died "at a good old age" in 1057 at his estate at Kings Bromley in Staffordshire. According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, he died on 30 September, but the chronicler of Worcester gives the date as 31 August. Both agree tha
2 CONC t he was buried at Coventry.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofric used a double-headed eagle as his personal device, and this has been adopted by various units of the British Army as a symbol for Mercia [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Religious works Earl Leofric and Godiva were noted for great generosity to religious houses. In 1043 he founded and endowed a Benedictine monastery at Coventry.[3] John of Worcester tells us that "He and his wife, the noble Counte
2 CONC ss Godgifu, a worshipper of God and devout lover of St Mary ever-virgin, built the monastery there from the foundations out of their own patrimony, and endowed it adequately with lands and made it so rich in various ornaments that
2 CONC  in no monastery in England might be found the abundance of gold, silver, gems and precious stones that was at that time in its possession."[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1050s Leofric and Godiva appear jointly in the grant of land to the monastery of St Mary, Worcester,[4], and the endowment of the minster at Stow St Mary, Lincolnshire.[5] She and her husband are commemorated as benefactors
2 CONC  of other monasteries at Leominster, Chester, Much Wenlock, and Evesham.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Apart from Northman, killed in 1017, Leofric had at least two other brothers. Edwin was killed in battle by Gruffydd ap Llywelyn in 1039.[1] Godwine died some time before 1057.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofric may have married more than once. His famous wife Godiva survived him and may have been a second or later wife. Since there is some question about the date of marriage for Leofric and Godgifu, it is not clear that she was t
2 CONC he mother of Ælfgar, Leofric's only known child. If Godiva was married to Earl Leofric only in 1040, she could not have been the mother of Ælfgar (whose own children were born in that decade or earlier). If she was married earlier
2 CONC  (as early as 1017, as some sources claim), she could have been Ælfgar's mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ælfgar succeeded Leofric as Earl of Mercia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT    1. ^ a b c d The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle
2 CONT    2. ^ M. Lapidge, The Blackwell Encyclopedia of Anglo-Saxon England (1999), p.282; The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle 1036 E.
2 CONT    3. ^ The Chronicle of John of Worcester ed. and trans. R.R. Darlington, P. McGurk and J. Bray (Clarendon Press: Oxford 1995), pp.533.
2 CONT    4. ^ a b c The Chronicle of John of Worcester ed. and trans. R.R. Darlington, P. McGurk and J. Bray (Clarendon Press: Oxford 1995), pp.582-3.
2 CONT    5. ^ The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle ed. M. Swanton (1996), p. 294.
2 CONT
