1 OCCU Vicomte, de Porhoët, de Châteautro-en-Penhoët, de Rennes, Vicomte de Chateaudro en Porhoët-Comte de Poher, He became Vicomte, and in about 1008 he moved to La Trinite, in Porhoet, Morbihan, Brittany. There he built Castle Josselin
2 CONC , 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Stuart Baldwin Website, http://sbaldw.home.mindspring.com/hproject/prov/conan000.htm writes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Juthael, count of Porhoët (existence uncertain)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Breton genealogist du Paz is the evident source (followed by numerous secondary sources) for the statement that Conan had a son Juthael, count of Porhoët, the claimed father of viscount Guethenoc, who witnessed a charter of du
2 CONC ke Geoffroy I (d. 1008) [Cart. Redon, 247]. In a discussion of the origin of the counts of Porhoët [Morice (1750): 1: 975-9 (Note XLV)], Morice cites "Hist. Mss. des Comtes de Porhoet" by du Paz as the source. However, there does 
2 CONC not appear to be any confirmation of even the existence of Juthael, count of Porhoët, and '''the well documented genealogy of the viscounts and counts of Porhoët begins with Guethenoc.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT =----------------------------=
2 CONT From Charles Cawley on the Medieval Lands database:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''GUETHENOC, son of [unknown]''' (-1046). Vicomte du château de Thro-en-Porhoët. 1008. A charter dated 1021 records that the bishop of Vannes restored rights to Redon abbey, witnessed by "…Alanus Cornugallensis comes et Guethenoc
2 CONC us vicecomes et Gozolinus eius filius…"[281]. "Guethenocus vicecomes de Castello Thro" granted the abbey of Redon the right to build a chapel at his castle, by charter dated [1040][282]. m '''ALLARUM, daughter of BENEDICT Comte de
2 CONC  Cornouaïlle & his wife Guinodeon''' ---. The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified. Guethenoc & his wife had one [known] child:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) '''JOSCELIN''' (-1074). A charter dated 1021 records that the bishop of Vannes restored rights to Redon abbey, witnessed by "…Alanus Cornugallensis comes et Guethenocus vicecomes et Gozolinus eius filius…"[283]. Vicomte de Bret
2 CONC agne et de Rennes. "Eudo comes et uxor eius, Joscelinus vicecomes…Alfredus frater Alani vicecomitis…Hodierna soror Hugonis vicecomitis…" witnessed a charter dated to 1050 which recorded an agreement between "Gualterio eius preposi
2 CONC to" and the abbess of Saint-Georges de Rennes[284]. "Defuncto…Guethenoco…filius eius Goscelinus" donated property to the abbey of Redon, by charter dated [1066/82], witnessed by "filius eius Maenguis episcopus, et Rogerius et Eudo
2 CONC  et alii filii eius"[285]. m ---. The name of Joscelin's wife is not known. Joscelin & his wife had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT >i) MAINGUY [Méen] (-after 1082). "Defuncto…Guethenoco…filius eius Goscelinus" donated property to the abbey of Redon, by charter dated [1066/82], witnessed by "filius eius Maenguis episcopus, et Rogerius et Eudo et alii filii eiu
2 CONC s"[286]. Bishop of Vannes 1066. Tresvaux records that he was elected as Bishop of Vannes in 1066 but does not cite the primary source on which this is based[287].
2 CONT
2 CONT >ii) ROGER . "Defuncto…Guethenoco…filius eius Goscelinus" donated property to the abbey of Redon, by charter dated [1066/82], witnessed by "filius eius Maenguis episcopus, et Rogerius et Eudo et alii filii eius"[288].
2 CONT
2 CONT >iii) EUDES [I] (-after 1092). "Defuncto…Guethenoco…filius eius Goscelinus" donated property to the abbey of Redon, by charter dated [1066/82], witnessed by "filius eius Maenguis episcopus, et Rogerius et Eudo et alii filii eius"[
2 CONC 289]. Vicomte de Porhoët.
2 CONT
2 CONT - VICOMTES de PORHOËT.
2 CONT
2 CONT >iv) son . "Defuncto…Guethenoco…filius eius Goscelinus" donated property to the abbey of Redon, by charter dated [1066/82], witnessed by "filius eius Maenguis episcopus, et Rogerius et Eudo et alii filii eius"[290]. Monk at Saint-
2 CONC Georges.
2 CONT
2 CONT =--------------------------------------=
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porhoët 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The noble Breton family line of Porhoët is represented in modern times by the Franco-Breton House of Rohan.
2 CONT Josselin Castle and the River Oust
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The first recognised vicomte de Porhoet was Guithenoc''' (abt. 990-1040CE), was born in Guilliers. He married Allurum (994-?) of Guilliers. He became Vicomte, and in about 1008 he moved to La Trinite, in Porhoet. Both Guilliers
2 CONC  and Porhoet are located in the modern day French Departement of Morbihan in the province of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Porhoët Guithenoc built Josselin Castle, which he named for his son, Josselin (1020–1074). It is still owned by the descendants of Porhoet and is the longest continuously held private estate in the world.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vicomte Josselin de Porhoet had three sons with the surname Rohan: Mainguy De Rohan, Jostho de Rohan, and Roger de Rohan. He had a fourth son, the third vicomte de Porhoet, Eudo I de Porhoet (1049-?).
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudo (or Eudes) I married Anne de Leon (1065-?). They had two children, Vicomte Geoffrey de Porhoet (1092–1141) and Alain I de Rohan
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey is said to have married Hawisa Fergant of Brittany (abt. 1105-?). They had two sons, one of which was as Odo II or Odo, Viscount of Porhoet (1122-?).
2 CONT
2 CONT Franco-Breton House of Rohan
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain le Noir was the 1st Viscount de Rohan. He was the third son of Eudes I de Porhoet and his wife Emma de Léon (the daughter of Guyomarch II, Viscount de Léon). The name Rohan comes from the name of the castle he built, and pas
2 CONC sed on to his descendants, on the shores of the Oust.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Eudes, married Bertha, the daughter of Conan III and Empress Matilda. They had no descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Viscount of Porhoet as Duke of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudes II of Porhoet, known also as Odo II is a notable figure in the history of the Duchy of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Odo II married into the ruling duchy of Brittany through his first marriage to Bertha (b.1114-?). This was Bertha's second marriage. She was the daughter of Conan III, Duke of Brittany. Upon Bertha's death, Odo II served as Regent
2 CONC  to Bertha's son Conan IV of Penthièvre. Bertha and Odo II had three children permitting the Porhoet line to continue. Bertha's sister Constance (1118-?) was next in line to the duchy (after Bertha); she married Alan, younger brot
2 CONC her to Odo II, thus cementing Porhoet claims to Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Bertha died, Odo II tried to deny Conan IV his inheritance and usurp the rule of Brittany. He formed an alliance with Hoel, Count of Nantes, Conan III's disinherited son.[a] In order to counter Odo II, Henry II of England inv
2 CONC aded Brittany. [b] In 1516 Odo was deposed by his step son and imprisoned by Conan IV's ally Raoul de Fougeres. Henry II raised Josselin Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Odo II possibly had a second marriage with Jeanne/Eléonore de Léon, daughter of Guiomar III, Vicomte de Léon. They had two, and possibly three, children.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Vicomtes and Counts of Porhoët
2 CONT
2 CONT * Guithenoc (990 - 1040) - becomes Vicomte;
2 CONT * Josselin - Vicomte de Porhoet - son of Vicomte Guithenoc
2 CONT * Odo[c] I, Vicomte de Porhoët- son of Vicomte Josselin
2 CONT * Geoffrey, Victome de Porhoët - son of Odo I, married Hawise Fergant of Brittany
2 CONT * Odo II - husband of Bertha and so son-in-law of Conan III, Duke of Brittany; contested the Ducal Crown of Brittany
2 CONT * Odo III, Comte de Porhoët - son of Odo III
2 CONT * Yolande de Dreux (1218-1272), Comtesse de Porhoët [d]
2 CONT * Alan, [e] - son of Odo III; flees to England
2 CONT * Alan de Porhoët - younger brother of Odo II, husband to Constance of Brittany, the younger sister of Bertha
2 CONT * Alan - Vicomte de Rohan; son of Odo I and founder of the House of Rohan
2 CONT
2 CONT 	 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT # ^ In order to consolidate his power, King Henry II of England (5 March 1133- 6 July 1189) had planned to replace Duke Conan III of Brittany with someone from his immediate family. He had promised his younger brother Geoffrey, of
2 CONC  the house of Plantegenet, this duchy if he succeeded to the throne of England. To this end, he had Conan III disinherit his son Hoel III, claiming him as illegitimate. (Hoel III's mother, the Duchess, Maude, was a daughter and il
2 CONC legitimate heir to Henry I of England). This meant that Bertha, who was the next in line to the duchy of Brittany, became the legitimate heir to the estate of Conan III.
2 CONT # ^ Henry II faced anarchy on many fronts; he was in the midst of the "Nineteen Year Winter." His younger brother Geoffrey, to whom he had promised the duchy, also contested the throne. As a result of the invasion of Brittany, the
2 CONC  Porhoet family eventually recognised Henry II as King.
2 CONT # ^ Odo, in French, is Eudes.
2 CONT # ^ Countess of Porhoët suo jure; her father was Pierre Mauclerc
2 CONT # ^ In an earlier edition of this article he is listed as the 1st Baron Zouche. This claim is under review.
2 CONT
