2 CONT Wikipedia:  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duncan_I_of_Scotland
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Duncan was the ancestor of the entire Irving Clan. Sometime before 1034, Duncan was named Prince of Cumberland by his Grandfather, Malcolm II, King of Scotland. Prince Duncan took several of the old Clans to the south border to de
2 CONC fend Scotland from England, and Prince Duncan's uncle brought his clan, the Erivine's, with him. They built the Towers of Bonshaw along the banks of the Kirtle and many manor houses in what became the ancient home of the Irving Cl
2 CONC an.
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm II had no male heir when he was assassinated in 1034. The throne was occupied by the grandson of Malcolm and son of Erinus, Duncan Erivine I. During his reign, Duncan was defeated in his campaign against the Norsemen and l
2 CONC ed the remnants of his army home in 1040. While returning, he was attacked and killed by his first cousin, MacBeth the Usurper; who assumed the throne and ruled for 17 years. It is around Duncan's murder that Shakespeare's play Ma
2 CONC cBeth is based. Erinus was killed by MacBeth's forces in 1045 while seeking revenge for the murder of his son. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Acceded to title (King)	Nov 1034 
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan I King of Scotland Fanciful artists impression commisioned many hundred years after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 	 1 - Duncan was a haemophiliac who bled to death after fighting with MacBeth. In 1034 Malcolm's grandson Duncan I succeeded him, but in 1040 he was killed by Macbeth, who contrary to Shakespearian legend ruled well and wise
2 CONC ly tor seventeen years, extending Scotland's connections with England and the Continent, until defeated and killed by Duncan's son, Malcolm III, known as Malcolm Canmore.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - Duncan's reign was short and unsuccessful. He was killed, probably in battle at Pitgaveny near Elgin, by a rival claimant and cousin MacBeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 - On Malcolm's death in 1034, Duncan became king, the second to rule all Scotland. He was a weaker man than his grandfather, Malcolm II, with less understanding of the vulnerability of his position. The northern Scots, ever on t
2 CONC he watch to snatch the throne, took advantage of this weakness. Macbeth, chief of this powerful race, harboured a claim to the throne through his mother. He fought tenaciously, and finally managed to kill Duncan in 1040 becoming K
2 CONC ing of Scotland in his place. [ An Illustrated History of Scotland by Elisabeth Fraser pub. 1997 ] [5]
2 CONT
