0 @N6000000003949663840@ NOTE St. Brychan Brycheiniog was the son of King Anlach of Garthmadrun by Marchel, heiress of that kingdom. Perhaps he was a freckled baby as his name implies. Brychan was born in Ireland but, soon afterward, his parents moved Wales, t
1 CONC o Y Fenni-Fach, then Marchel's homeland of Garthmadrun. At the age of four, Brychan was sent to be tutored by a holy-man named Drichan beside the River Ysgir. Seven years, Brychan was schooled in the ways of the World, before the 
1 CONC poor blind Drichan finally called Brychan to bring him his trusty spear for the last time. With it, he pointed to a nearby boar and a stag who came from the forest to stand with a fish in the river, by a beech-tree dripping with h
1 CONC oney; and Drichan predicted a happy and abundant future for the young Brychan.
1 CONT A few years later, war broke out between Anlach and Banadl, the usurping Irish King of Powys. The fight did not go well for Anlach, and he was forced to send Brychan to Powys as a hostage in order to protect his lands. Brychan was
1 CONC  treated well at the Irishman's court, but he fell madly in love with his host's daughter, Banhadlwedd. The match was frowned upon and, overcome with lust, Brychan took the poor girl by force. Before Brychan was sent back to Gathm
1 CONC adrun at the end of the War, the Irish Princess bore him a son named Cynog. Brychan gave his child a golden armilla as a sign of his paternal recognition.
1 CONT Back in Garthmadrun, Anlach eventually died and the nobles raised Brychan to the Kingship. From Talgarth, His reign was triumphant, as Drichan had predicted, and the people decided to rename the Kingdom Brycheiniog in his honour. 
1 CONC He was a saintly King dedicated to the Christian Church and its teachings. He married three times and had so many saintly children, they are almost impossible to count. The most popular figure is twenty-four sons and twenty-four d
1 CONC aughters. Together they are known as one of the "Holy Families of Britain".
1 CONT Depsite his piety, Brychan was not above defending his lands or his family when the need arose. One of his eldest daughters, Gwladys, was once abducted by King Gwynllyw of Gwynllwg. Brychan and his armies pursued them for many day
1 CONC s and many nights before a horrendous battle was fought at which many men fell. Luckily, the High-King Arthur intervened and the two Welsh Monarchs were soon reconciled. On another occasion, the King of Dyfed (or Gwynedd) raided B
1 CONC rychan's Kingdom in order to dispel a boast by one of his countrymen, that no spoil could ever be taken from Brychan's land. When the King of Brycheiniog discovered this treachery, he led his armies to a great battle victory, afte
1 CONC r which the dismembered limbs of the enemy were collected as trophies!
1 CONT In old age he is believed to have abdicated the throne of Brycheniog in order to become a hermit. Professor Thomas suggests that his life at this period should be identified with that of his so-called son, St. Nectan. He died at a
1 CONC  great age in the mid-5th century and was buried on Ynys Brychan (Lundy Island).
0 @N6000000000437623456@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Saint
0 @N6000000003222440492@ NOTE Founded church of St. Nevyn in Cawnarvonshire, Wales
0 @N6000000000437376205@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> High King Of Britain
0 @N6000000003051322722@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Kingdom of Powys", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT The Kingdom of Powys was a Welsh successor state that emerged during the Dark Ages following the Roman withdrawal from Britain. Based on the Romano-British tribal lands of the Cornovii, its boundaries originally extended from the 
1 CONC Cambrian Mountains in the west to include the modern West Midlands region in the east. The fertile river valleys of the Severn and Tern are found here, and this region is referred to in later Welsh literature as "the Paradise of P
1 CONC owys". The name is thought to derive from the Latin "pagus" meaning the country-side, also a cognate of 'pagan'. During the Roman Empire this region was organised into a Roman province, with the capital at Viroconium Cornoviorum (
1 CONC modern Wroxeter), the fourth largest Roman city in Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Throughout the Early Middle Ages, Powys was ruled by the Gwerthernion dynasty, a family claiming descent jointly from the marriage of Vortigern and Princess Sevira, the daughter of Magnus Maximus. Archaeological evidence has shown
1 CONC  that, unusually for the post-Roman period, Viroconium Cornoviorum survived as an urban centre well into the 6th century and thus could have been the Powys capital. Nennius, writing in the 8th century in his "History of the Briton
1 CONC s", recorded the town as Caer Guricon, one of his "28 British Towns" of Roman Britain. In the following centuries, the Powys eastern border was encroached upon by English settlers from the emerging Anglian territory of Mercia. Thi
1 CONC s was a gradual process, and English control in the West Midlands was uncertain until the late 8th century.
0 @N6000000003949917700@ NOTE The most hated man in Britain, as he later became known, was Vortigern Vorteneu. The Welsh form of this is Gwrtheyrn Gwrtheneu (the Thin). Despite the name being synonymous with the man, Vortigern has long been accepted as only a 
1 CONC title, with Pictish, or perhaps Northern British overtones. It means "Over-King", and there are some indications that, like his semi-legendary forebears, his real name was Vitalis (Gwidol in Welsh) or Vitalinus (Gwidolin), though 
1 CONC his origins are obscure. His name would almost certainly have been Roman in influence, although he was probably aware of its Celtic version. The north and west of Britannia was only ever under Roman military rule, so Celtic tradit
1 CONC ions were still very strong there. The other possibility, equally strong, put forward by Charles Thomas, is that Bede knew of Vortigern as Vertigernus or Uuertigernus, representing a British name Wortigernos (see the introduction 
1 CONC on Gildas for a fuller explanation).
1 CONT His power-base was always the area along the Welsh border, and while he was High King, his eldest son Vortimer set up a thriving kingdom in Gwent. Vortigern himself appears to have spent more of his time, and exercised more direct
1 CONC  control in Caer Gloui (Gloucester) in his early years. His great grandfather is said to have been Gloyw Gwallthir (Long-Hair), one of the city's supposed founders (Gloyw = Glou-, and Latin castrum (fort) = British caer or Saxon c
1 CONC ester). This name is still linked to the place itself in its nickname of "Gloucester Long-Wall", but the appendage to Gloyw's name indicates he was a long-haired Pict. This is also born out to an extent in the name Vortigern itsel
1 CONC f. "gern" was a Pictish (or pre-Pict) word for leader.
1 CONT Despite this Pictish link, the "Life of St Cadog" gives Vortigern an alternative and very typically Celtic ancestry descending from the Celtic gods, Beli Mawr, Lludd Llaw Ereint & Afallach. Pictish descent was always measured thro
1 CONC ugh the female side, so perhaps this explains the apparent conflict. Vortigern could have possessed a Pictish female in his ancestry, a possible wife of Gloyw's, not unlikely if she was from the Southern Picts around Manau Goutodd
1 CONC in or the Clyde.
1 CONT The St Cadog ancestry shows too few generations to be complete, but it is replicated in the King List for Vortigern's Powys, as similar ancestries are for other kings, such as Gwent, Dyfed, and Gwynedd. The ancestry of Celtic king
1 CONC s was very important to their prestige and their clan name, so even minor kings (who were usually descended from greater royal houses anyway) would find an ancestry that linked them to their royal title.
1 CONT Vortigern became High King of Britain in around 425, after years of building up his power and position. Its entirely possible that, given the fact that the Romano-Britons at this time were still very much following standard Roman 
1 CONC policies on defence and their way of life, that he adjusted the title to become Emperor of Britain. The precedence would have been his own father in law, Magnus Maximus, and the more recent claimant to Rome, Constantine III, who l
1 CONC eft Britain in 407. There was no possibility of following these two overseas to claim a now much reduced Rome, even if the manpower was available, which it was not. The title of emperor would bear much more meaning to the Romanise
1 CONC d Britons who were in command of Britannia, before the mid-century Celtic resurgence took hold.
1 CONT Between AD 380 - 400 Vortigern married Severa ferch Macsen (daughter of Magnus Maximus, the Roman general proclaimed emperor in Britain in 383, and responsible for large scale changes in the way Britain defended itself before he l
1 CONC eft to pursue his claim to the purple). Later, he married again.
