0 @N6000000002187699625@ NOTE Magnus Maximus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnus Maximus.Magnus Maximus (Welsh: Macsen Wledig), also Maximianus, (c. 335–July 28, 388) was a usurper of the Western Roman Empire from 383 until his death in 388.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Legendary accounts 
1 CONT 3 External links 
1 CONT 3.1 Historical Magnus 
1 CONT 3.2 Legendary Magnus 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT A Spanish Celt, Maximus was proclaimed emperor by his troops in 383, while serving with the army in Britain. Later legend mapoop to Gaul to pursue his imperial ambitions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following his destruction of Gaul, Maximus went out to meet his main opponent, Gratianus, who he defeated near Paris. Gratianus, after fleeing, was killed at Lyons on August 25, 383. Soon after, he managed to force Valentinian II 
1 CONC out of Rome after which he fled to Theodosius I, the Eastern Roman Emperor. Maximus made his capital at Augusta Treverorum (Treves, Trier) in Gaul. He became a popular emperor, although also a stern persecutor of heretics.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximus was defeated in the battle of the Save (388) and was captured and executed by Theodosius, along with his son. Valentinian was restored to the emperorship of Rome while Dionotus, the brother of Caradocus, became king of the
1 CONC  Britons.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Legendary accounts
1 CONT According to Geoffrey of Monmouth, basis for many English and Welsh legends, Magnus was king of the Britons following the death of Octavius and a nephew of King Coel through his brother Ioelinus. According to the Mabinogion tale T
1 CONC he Dream of Macsen Wledig, Magnus Maximus took as his wife Helen or Elen, daughter of a chieftain based at Segontium (Caernarfon). This agrees with the story Geoffrey tells that Octavius, the king of the Britons, wanted to wed his
1 CONC  daughter to a powerful half-Roman, half-Briton and give the kingship of Britain as a dowry to that husband. Although the story of their meeting is undoubtedly fictional, there is some evidence for the basic claims. Caradocus, the
1 CONC  Duke of Cornwall, supported the marriage between Octavius's daughter and Maximus. Maximus accepted and left Rome for Britain. Geoffrey claims further that Maximus gathered an army as he sacked Frankish towns along the way. He inv
1 CONC aded Clausentum (modern Southampton) unintentionally and nearly fought the army of the Britons before a truce was made. Following further negotiations, Maximus was given the kingship of Britain and Octavius retired. Welsh legend a
1 CONC lso claims that he was responsible for the withdrawal of Roman troops from Wales, twenty years before the rest of Britain was left to govern itself. Five years into his kingship, Magnus Maximus assembled a vast fleet and invaded G
1 CONC aul leaving Britain in the control of Caradocus. Upon reaching the kingdom of Armorica, he defeated the king and killed thousands of inhabitants. Before departing, he summoned Conanus (Welsh: Kynan Meriadec, French: Conan Meriadoc
1 CONC ), the rebellious nephew of Octavius, and asked him to rule as king of the land, which was renamed Brittany, or 'Little Britain'.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Wikimedia Commons has media related to: 
1 CONT Magnus Maximus[edit]
1 CONT Historical Magnus
1 CONT De Imperatoribus Romanis account 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Legendary Magnus
1 CONT Family fact sheet of Magnus Maximus, based on the Welsh legends about h
1 CONT Translated text of "The Dream of Macsen Wledig", included in the Mabinogion 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Octavius Legendary British Kings Succeeded by:
1 CONT Caradocus 
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Magnus Maximus was born to a poor Spanish family, probably in the province of Callaecia in north western Spain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT After a military career, Maximus came to serve under Theodosius the Elder in Britain in AD 369, and in Africa from AD 373 to 375.
1 CONT His efforts were rewarded by being granted overall military command in Britain, where he successfully campaigned against the Picts and Scots. 
1 CONT
1 CONT But the army was very disillusioned and dissatisfied with its emperor Gratian. In particular jealousies toward privileges awarded to barbarian units fighting for the empire ignited much ill-feeling with the soldiery.
1 CONT Finally, in AD 383 it all boiled over and the garrison in Britain revolted and proclaimed Maximus as the new Augustus of the west.
1 CONT At once the new emperor crossed the Channel with his troops, taking Gratian by complete surprise.
1 CONT As Gratian marched his troops west to meet the usurper at Lutetia in battle, his troops simply deserted him and changed allegiance to Maximus.
1 CONT Gratian fled, but was caught up with by Maximus' 'Master of Horse' Andragathius who assassinated him. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximus now established his capital at Treviri and began negotiations with Theodosius, emperor of the east, who reluctantly recognized him as emperor, if only to save young Valentinian II who still remained in charge of Italy, Pan
1 CONC noniae and Africa.
1 CONT With his recognition as emperor, Maximus adopted the name Flavius, in order to portray himself as an adopted member of the imperial family. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximus was a orthodox Catholic who, like Theodosius in the east, vehemently pursued and punished heretics and pagans. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In AD 387 Maximus elevated his infant son Flavius Victor to be co-Augustus.
1 CONT Then, in the summer of AD 387, Maximus invaded Italy in a successful attempt to oust Valentinian II. The young emperor Valentinian II fled with his mother to Theodosius in Constantinople. 
1 CONT
1 CONT But Maximus attempt on increasing his realm of influence backfired. Theodosius was no longer tied up in trouble in the east anymore and stood ready to launch an attack on the usurper. 
1 CONT In AD 388, with Maximus staying behind in Aquileia, Andragathius led his army against Theodosius, only to be defeated at Siscia.
1 CONT Thereafter his brother Marcellinus led the re-grouped army back into battle, but he too was defeated at Poetovio. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximus was captured and pleaded for the victor to show mercy. Though Theodosius showed none and had him executed (AD 388).
1 CONT His son, Flavius Victor, was captured by Arbogast, Theodosius 'Master of Soldiers', and was also put to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: http://www.roman-empire.net/collapse/maximus.html
