1 CONC hat the political state of sub-Roman Britain would probably have made it impossible to exercise such centralised control by the fifth century. As Maximus himself was dead by the end of 388, and Constantine III departed from Britai
1 CONC n with the last of Rome's military forces in 407, less than a generation later, it is doubtful that Rome had much direct influence over the military actions of the Votadini, either through Maximus or any other emissary, for any si
1 CONC gnificant length of time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximus (or his successors) may have handed over control of the British frontiers to local chieftains at an earlier date; with the evacuation of the fort at Chester (which Mike Ashley, incidentally, argues is most likely where Cun
1 CONC edda established his initial base in the region, some years later) in the 370s, he may have had little option. Given that the archaeological record demonstrates Irish settlement on the Llyn peninsula however and possible raids as 
1 CONC far west as Wroxeter by the late 4th century, it is difficult to conceive of either Roman or allied British forces having presented an effective defence in Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Academics such as Sheppard Frere have argued that it may have been Vortigern who, adopting elements of Roman statecraft, moved the Votadini south, just as he invited Saxon settlers to protect other parts of the island. According t
1 CONC o this version of events, Vortigern would have instructed Cunedda and his Votadini subjects to move to Wales in response to the aforementioned Irish incursions no later than the year 442, when Vortigern's former Saxon allies rebel
1 CONC led against his rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cunedda's supposed grandson Maelgwn Gwynedd was a contemporary of Gildas, and according to the Annales Cambriae died in 547. The reliability of early Welsh genealogies is not uncontested however, and many of the claims regarding t
1 CONC he number and identity of Cunedda's heirs did not surface until as late as the 10th century. Nonetheless, if we accept this information as valid, calculating back from this date suggests the mid-5th century interpretation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Cunedda personally even less is known. Probably celebrated for his strength, courage, and ability to rally the beleaguered Romano-British forces of the region, he eventually secured a politically advantageous marriage to Gwawl,
1 CONC  daughter of Coel Hen, the Romano-British ruler of Eboracum (modern York), and is claimed to have had nine sons. The early kingdoms of Ceredigion and Meirionnydd were supposedly named after his two sons Ceredig and Meirion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Other notes
1 CONT
1 CONT Name pronunciation, maybe: /k??ne?ða?/ or /k??ne?ða/.  (Based off of the "Predicting vowel length" portion of the Welsh alphabet Wikipedia article: <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Welsh_alphabet&oldid=219806442>)
0 @N6000000003949916950@ NOTE One of the founding fathers of Welsh royalty.  The name Cunedda (Kenneth), means "good lord" or "born of fire".  He is considered to be the grandson of Padarn Beisrudd which translates to "Paternus of the Scarlet Cloak".  Paternus
1 CONC  was a Roman official of high rank in charge of troops in the area that later became known as Lothian & Bernicia.  This command probably continued thru his son Eternus(Edern) and thence to Cunedda.  Cunedda became leader of the tr
1 CONC ibes of the Votadini in about 430.  Because of famine (caused by the plague) he moved south to Wales.  A battle poem celebrates his success in battle calling him Cunedda the Lion. 
1 CONT Cunedda, like many of his contemporaries, claimed descent from the Celtic gods, Beli and Afallach. Yet, his immediate ancestors had extremely Roman names: Aeternus, Paternus & Tacitus. They were probably leaders of the Votadini tr
1 CONC ibe. They were on excellent terms with the Roman administration south of Hadrian's Wall and may have formed part of a pro-Roman borderland buffer zone after the irruptions of AD 367/9. Paternus' Red-Robe may even suggest the offic
1 CONC ial purple garb of the Roman Administration.    Who came from Manaw Gododin on the Firth of Forth with his eight sons and one grandson to drive the Irish out of Gwynedd circa 440.   Ruled abt 450-460
1 CONT It seems clear therefore that Cunedda and his warbands rode clean across Britain to assist their Western cousins. Soon only Llyn, Arfon, Arllechwedd and the majority of Anglesey remained in Irish hands. Cunedda also took raiding p
1 CONC arties further south and expelled the Ui Liathain from Gwyr, Cedweli and parts of Dyfed. He finally settled down in Rhos which was the original heartland of Gwynedd. His family founded a number of Royal dynasties in the regions no
1 CONC w named after his many sons: Meirionydd, Ysfeilion, Rhufoniog, Dunoding, Ceredigion, Afflogion, Dogfeiling and Edeyrnion.
0 @N6000000002188329312@ NOTE Cunedda
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cunedda ap Edern (c.AD 386-c.460; reigned from the 440s or 450s) (Latin: Cunetacius; English: Kenneth), also known as as Cunedda Wledig ('the Imperator'), was an important early Welsh leader, and the progenitor of the royal dynast
1 CONC y of Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name 'Cunedda' derives from the Brythonic word counodagos, meaning 'good lord'. His genealogy is traced back to Padarn Beisrudd, which literally translated as Paternus of the Scarlet Robe. One traditional interpretation identi
1 CONC fies Padarn as a Roman (or Romano-British) official of reasonably high rank who had been placed in command of Votadini troops stationed in the Clackmannanshire region of Scotland in the 380s or earlier by the Emperor Magnus Maximu
1 CONC s. Alternatively, he may have been a frontier chieftain who was granted Roman military rank, a practice attested elsewhere along the empire's borders at the time. In all likelihood, Padarn's command in Scotland was assumed after h
1 CONC is death by his son, Edern (in Latin, Aeturnus), and then passed to Edern's son, Cunedda.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cunedda and his forebears led the Votadini against Pictish and Irish incursions south of Hadrian's Wall. Sometime after this, the Votadini troops under Cunedda relocated to North Wales in order to defend the region from Irish inva
1 CONC sion. Cunedda established himself in Wales, in the territory of the Venedotians, which would become the centre of the kingdom of Gwynedd. Two explanations for these actions have been suggested: either Cunedda was acting under the 
1 CONC orders of Maximus (or Maximus's successors) or Vortigern, the high king of the British in the immediate post-Roman era. The range of dates (suggested by PC Bartrum) runs from the late 370s, which would favor Maximus, to the late 4
1 CONC 40s, which would favor Vortigern.
1 CONT
1 CONT The suggestion that Cunedda was operating under instructions from Rome has been challenged by several historians. David Dumville dismisses the whole concept of transplanting foederati from Scotland to Wales in this manner, especia
1 CONC lly given the political state of sub-Roman Britain which may not have been able to exhibit such centralised control by the fifth century. As Maximus himself was dead by the end of 388, and Constantine III departed from Britain wit
1 CONC h the last of Rome's military forces in 407, less than a generation later, it is doubtful that Rome had much direct influence over the military actions of the Votadini, either through Maximus or any other emissary, for any signifi
1 CONC cant length of time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maximus (or his successors) may have handed over control of the British frontiers to local chieftains at an earlier date; with the evacuation of the fort at Chester (which Mike Ashley, incidentally, argues is most likely where Cun
1 CONC edda established his initial base in the region, some years later) in the 370s, he may have little option. Given that the archaeological record demonstrates Irish settlement on the Lleyn peninsula however and possible raids as far
1 CONC  west as Wroxeter by the late 4th century, it is difficult to conceive of either Roman or allied British forces having presented an effective defence in Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Academics such as Sheppard Frere have argued that it may have been Vortigern who, adopting elements of Roman statecraft, moved the Votadini south, just as he invited Saxon settlers to protect other parts of the island. According t
1 CONC o this version of events, Vortigern would have instructed Cunedda and his Votadini subjects to move to Wales in response to the aforementioned Irish incursions no later than the year 442, when Vortigern's former Saxon allies rebel
1 CONC led against his rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cunedda's supposed grandson Maelgwn Hir ap Cadwallon was a contemporary of Gildas, and according to the Annales Cambriae died in 547. The reliability of early Welsh genealogies is not uncontested however, and many of the claims re
1 CONC garding the number and identity of Cunedda's heirs did not surface until as late as the 10th century. Nonetheless, if we accept this information as valid, calculating back from this date suggests the mid-5th century interpretation
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Cunedda personally even less is known. Probably celebrated for his strength, courage, and ability to rally the beleaguered Romano-British forces of the region, he eventually secured a politically advantageous marriage to Gwawl,
1 CONC  daughter of Coel Hen, the Romano-British ruler of Eboracum (modern York), and is claimed to have had nine sons. Cardigan (Welsh: Ceredigion) and Merioneth (Welsh: Meirionydd) were supposedly named after his two sons Ceredig and M
1 CONC eirion.
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Cunedda Weledig (Cunedda the Great), was the first in the dynasty of Cunnedda, the line of Gwynedd. According to Davies tradition states Cunneda and eight sons and one grandson came down from the north and drove the Irish from Gwy
1 CONC nedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT notes or source:
1 CONT ancestry.com & HBJ
1 CONT "A History of Wales" by John Davies
1 CONT
1 CONT A group of Votadini Picts (nominal Britons from the Pictish border areas) under Cunedda Wledig were transferred by Magnus Maximus to secure Western Britain from Irish raiders, moving from the Manau Goutodin kingdom. In Wales, Cune
1 CONC dda governed most of the north (hence "King of North Wales"). His father and grandfather bore Roman names and in true Celtic fashion, Cunedda could trace his lineage back to Beli Mawr.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following that Celtic tradition, upon Cunedda's death the territory under his control was divided between his sons. Most of these were "regained" by the main Gywneddian kingdom within a generation or two. Ceredigion, along the upp
1 CONC er west coast of Wales, remained independent for much longer. The name of [Sub-Kingdoms of Gwynedd] Gwynedd either derives from the Latin Venedotia, or more probably from Cunedda (=Weneda =Gwynedd).
1 CONT
1 CONT Cunedda (or Cunedag) was a northern British chieftain, a sub-King of Gododdin who ruled Manau Gododdin on the Firth of Forth around Clackmannan. He was requested by the northern Welsh to help them expell the invading Irish from th
1 CONC eir lands, and he eagerly obliged. With his many sons, Cunedda settled down in the area and founded a number of Royal dynasties: Gwynedd Rhos Ceredigion
1 CONT Meirionydd
1 CONT Dunoding
1 CONT Dogfeiling
1 CONT Rhufoniog: founded by Rhufon around Denbigh
1 CONT Edeyrnion: founded by Edeyrn around Bala
1 CONT Afflogion: founded by Afloyg on the Lleyn Peninsula Osmaeliog: founded by Osfael probably on Anglesey
1 CONT
1 CONT Cunedda Wledig (or Cunedag) hailed from Manau Gododdin, a sub-division of the greater Kingdom of Gododdin (Lothian) in modern Scotland. His capital may have been in the Clackmannan region. His father, grandfather & great grandfath
1 CONC er bore Roman names and were probably confederate allies of the Roman administration living just north of Hadrian's Wall. The appendage to Paternus' name is particularly telling. Like many prominent men of his era, Cunedda claimed
1 CONC  descent from Beli Mawr, the Celtic Sun-God, through his son, Lludd Llaw Ereint, God of Healing & grandson, Afallach, God of the Underworld.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marwnad Cunedda (Elegy for Cunned - in Welsh)
1 CONT
