0 @N6000000003658819681@ NOTE TEILION, eldest son of the Over-King Cunedda, was King of Cardigan, in succession to his brother Keredig.
1 CONT
1 CONT My notes
1 CONT
1 CONT Apparently, Teilion is listed as Cunedda's son in John R. Boots' book/webpage.  However, in the book A History of Wales..., it is said that Cunedda had eight sons, none of which is named Teilion, and possibly a ninth son, whose na
1 CONC me started with T but otherwise did not sound like Teilion at all (it was like Typaud or something).  Furthermore, only one of the son's names listed in A History of Wales remotely matched one of the several sons of Cunedda that I
1 CONC 'd gotten from Boots.  From Boots, I had "Keredig", and from A History of Wales, I had Ceredic, which is very similar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Now, "Typaud", the ninth son, supposedly died before Cunedda and his sons did their little battle thing, and in his place went Typaud's son Meriaun.  From Boots, I had learned that Telion had a son name Merion, which is practicall
1 CONC y homophonous, at least to English ears.  So, I just assumed that Telion was the ninth son.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, from Boots, I gathered the above info, that Telion was the eldest son and ended up as King, succeeding his brother Keredig/Ceredig.  The fact that Ceredig was king first and succeeded by one of his brothers is supported e
1 CONC lsewhere.  However, the brother he was succeeded by was named Einion, which doesn't bear *much* of a resemblance to Teilion -- I thought that it might have still been the same person, except that it's mentioned that Einion, the Ki
1 CONC ng, had a *nephew* named *Meirion*.  Meirion had a county named after him, so it seems like he is the one who went in his father's place, since only the sons should have gotten counties named after them.
1 CONT
1 CONT So I don't know what to do.  I guess that whoever said Teilion was king must have gotten him confused with someone else... regardless, I'm descended from Meirion (probably, assuming everything else is correct) so the worst thing w
1 CONC ould be that I was just not descended from the third king (after Ceredig, who was after Cunedda).  So meh.
0 @N6000000002188329857@ NOTE Rhufon, King of Rhufoniog
1 CONT (Born c.AD 426)
1 CONT (Latin: Rumanus; English: Roman)
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhufon (alias Brochwel) was the third son of Cunedda Wledig (the Imperator). When the Irish became dominant in North Wales, he travelled, with his family, from their home in Manau Gododdin (on the Forth) to expel them. Rhufon was 
1 CONC then given the area around Denbigh as a reward and here he set up the kingdom named after him. He probably ruled as a sub-king under his brother, King Einion Yrth (the Impetuous) of Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is unclear for how long his descendants continued to use the title of "King", but they appear to have enjoyed a certain amount of power in the area possibly into the late fifth century. Rhufon was initially succeeded by his son
1 CONC , Mor.
