0 @N6000000002795191100@ NOTE http://www.my.familytree.dsl.pipex.com/king_arthur_fact_or_fiction.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT King Arthur – Fact or Fiction
1 CONT Introduction.
1 CONT Much has been written and theories put forward about the legendary King Arthur. Did he, in fact, ever live?
1 CONT Put forward below is my own interpretation of the stories and historical documents that I have heard and read, not of his battles but of the man himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact or Fiction
1 CONT On other pages of my ‘family history’ has been put forward the idea that ‘King Arthur’ was an Anglo Saxon miss-understanding of the Welsh pronunciation of the name Cunedda. As the letter ‘u’ in Welsh is pronounced ‘i’ as in sing, 
1 CONC an ‘e’ more like an ‘a’ as in farm, the ‘dd’ in Welsh sounding like ‘th’ and an ‘a’ sounding more like ‘ah’ it is my belief that Cunedda sounds ‘kinathah’ to a non Welsh ear. With the Welsh language having 28 letters (7 vowels – A
1 CONC , E, I, O, U, W and Y) with no J, K, Q, V, X or Z, but with Ch, Dd, Ff, Ng, Ll, Ph, Rh and Th added, and, worse yet, the pronunciation of some being completely different to English (w = oo), a person brought up to speak English ca
1 CONC nnot convert. At least all letters of Welsh are pronounced, unlike English with its changing sounds, such as a silent p (as in bath), etc.
1 CONT After finishing my ‘history’ and publishing it on the web, I went back to reading up ‘King Arthur’ and have added two pages on whether he was from the Scottish borders or was indeed a Welshman. I still had doubts, however and deci
1 CONC ded to investigate the pronunciation of Cunedda with Arthur further.
1 CONT The original Cunedda was ruled out as it is known that he, as a young man, fought and made himself king of Gododin (meaning south of the Firth) from about 385 onward. 
1 CONT Cunedda had between 8 and 12 sons and ruled over most of present day Yorkshire, Lancashire, Cumbria and north into Scotland including Ayreshire until the year 400 when he made two of his sons rulers in his place and with the other
1 CONC s and a grandson moved into north Wales to drive the Irish out. One son, Ceretic, he left to rule along the northern wall to defend from the sea wolves of the Forth. Another, Coel, (named after an old war god) he left to rule the 
1 CONC Goidels of Duvnovia, now Ayeshire. Coel later took over all the northern lands in England and later became known as Coel Hen or ‘Old King Cole’. The Romans started their withdrawal from all over Britain about 410 onward because of
1 CONC  the threat to their empire nearer home.
1 CONT After pushing out the Irish, Cunedda settled his sons in north Wales as rulers and named many of the areas they ruled after them. He was a devout Catholic and two or three of his sons became ecclesiastics, organising the church in
1 CONC  the kingdoms. Six of the sons were known to be Ceredig (Ceredigion), Edern (Edeirnion), Meirionydd (Merioneth), Dunoding, Rhufoniog and Dogfeiling (along the north Wales coast). These are all coastal kingships, which lends streng
1 CONC th to his driving the Irish out. An engraved stone to Cunorix has been found at Wroxeter near Shrewsbury, which was a Roman fort and at which he may have been buried.
1 CONT Cunedda was also called Cunorix and Gwledig (the fire burner), which again falls in with the battle system of Arthur, when he sometimes burned crops, etc. to weaken his opponents. Cunorix was the Latin name by which he was known a
1 CONC nd Gwledig the Irish.
1 CONT Cunedda was originally discounted because of the date, living in the early 400s, whereas Arthur lived much later into the 500s. This might be true of Cunedda I, but Coel Hen had a son Cunedda II and a grandson Cunedda III. Not onl
1 CONC y that but Yvor ap Cunedda had a son Arthwys.
1 CONT Coel Hens eldest son was Garmonion and was Coels successor, Cunedda II was the second son of Coel so was free to roam and make his own fortune, indeed he was made (or seized) the office of Dux Britanniarum, an office often credite
1 CONC d to Arthur. 
1 CONT Looking closer at this Cunedda (Arthur) we find that his headquarters were close to Carlisle, not that far from Arthurs early battles, and that he had a retinue of 900 horse with a banner of a gold and red dragon on a green backgr
1 CONC ound (a silver dragon was the emblem of the Count of Britain in the South). Indeed, the dragon had a red head and gold body, so could this be the origination of ‘Pen Dragon’, pen being the early Welsh/English word for head (head o
1 CONC f the dragon). This could even be the reason that Arthur was referred to as ‘son of pen-dragon’.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT History mentions Cunedda I frequently because of his march south from Gododin to expel the Irish from north Wales, but very little, except a childrens poem, is said about Coel Hen (old King Cole) and his descendants, especially th
1 CONC e two further Cuneddas. Therefore it is thought relevant to list a family tree from Cunedda I, bearing in mind that not all his sons and none of his daughters are known. This is listed in generations. The Welsh ap and ab are used 
1 CONC to denote ‘son of’ – ap follows a consonant and ab follows a vowel. A daughter is denoted by verch.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Family Tree of Cunedda I ( The Great ).
1 CONT
1 CONT 1 Cunedda I (also known as Cunorix and Gwledig) c375-430
1 CONT 2 Ceretic ap Cunedda I c421
1 CONT Coel ap Cunedda I (later known as Coel Hen (Coel the Old) or Old King Cole) c425
1 CONT Ceredig ap Cunedda I (King of Ceredigion – now Cardigan Bay) c427
1 CONT Edern ap Cunedda I (King of Edierion) c429
1 CONT Meirionydd ap Cunedda I (King of Merioneth) c431
1 CONT Dunoding ap Cunedda I c434
1 CONT Rhufoniog ap Cunedda I c435
1 CONT Dogfeiling ap Cunedda I (King of an area from about Rhyl to Chester) c437
1 CONT 3 Garmonion ap Coel c471
1 CONT Cunedda Menrudd ap Coel (Cunedda II) c474
1 CONT 4 Dunwal Moelmut ap Garmonion c499
1 CONT Cunedda ap Cunedda Menrudd (Cunedda III) c500
1 CONT Gwrwst Ledlwm ap Cunedda Menrudd c 502
1 CONT 5 Cyngar (Yugr) ap Dunwal Moelmut c530
1 CONT Yvor ap Cunedda c530
1 CONT 6 Morgan Bule ap Cyngar (led some sons of Coel in battle) c559
1 CONT Arthwys ab Yvor c561
1 CONT
1 CONT The above gives six generations from Cunedda I, who was known to live within a year or two of the dates given. The other dates are very approximate but in these times, although women started breeding from fourteen upwards, the men
1 CONC  of high status usually proved themselves on the battlefield from about the age of seventeen until mid thirties, fathering many illegitimate children, before settling down and producing heirs. A generation then was about 38 years 
1 CONC compared with today’s 22.
1 CONT Lifespan in these days was only about fifty to fifty-five years, so that if Coel lived into his mid or late sixties he would have earned the addition of ‘hen’, making him Coel ‘the old’.
1 CONT
1 CONT Because the above family tree extends to Henry VIII and beyond, although not relevant to this argument, it is given below as a matter of interest. Generation numbers still apply but only known dates are shown. The + sign is used t
1 CONC o denote husband or wife. 
1 CONT Family Tree Extension.
1 CONT 7 Meuric ap Morgan Bule
1 CONT Cynvelyn ab Arthwys
1 CONT 8 Adras (Isris) ap Meuric
1 CONT Cynwyd Cynwydion ap Cynvelyn
1 CONT 9 Morgan Mwynvawr ab Adras
1 CONT Cadrod Calchvynydd ap Cynwyd Cynwydion
1 CONT 10 Ithel ap Morgan Mwynvawr
1 CONT Ysbwch ap Cadrod Calchvynydd
1 CONT 11 Rhys ab Ithel
1 CONT Mwyntwrch ab Ysbwch (Carcludals)
1 CONT 12 Brochwel ap Rhys
1 CONT Yabwys ap Mwyntwrch
1 CONT 13 Uriat ap Brochwel
1 CONT Mynan ab Yabwys
1 CONT 14 Arthwal ab Uriat
1 CONT Yvor ap Mynan
1 CONT 15 Rhys ab Arthwal
1 CONT Elffin ab Yvor
1 CONT 16 Howel ap Rhys
1 CONT Cynan ab Elffin
1 CONT 17 Owen ap Howel
1 CONT Marchudd ap Cynan
1 CONT 18 Gwrgan ab Owen
1 CONT Carwedd ap Marchudd
1 CONT 19 Iestyn ap Gwrgan
1 CONT Iasseth ap Carwedd
1 CONT 20 Rrhydderch ab Iestyn
1 CONT Inathan ab Iasseth
1 CONT 21 Gruffydd ap Rhydderch 1033-
1 CONT Edryd ab Inathan
1 CONT 22 Caradoc ap Gruffydd 1055-
1 CONT Idnerth ab Edryd
1 CONT 23 Owen ap Caradoc
1 CONT Gwgon ab Idnerth
1 CONT 24 Iorworth Caerlleon ab Owen
1 CONT Iorworth ap Gwgon
1 CONT 25 Howel ab Iorworth Caerlleon
1 CONT Owen ab Iorworth Caerlleon
1 CONT Cynric ab Iorworth
1 CONT + Yngharad verch Hwva
1 CONT 26 Morgan ap Howel
1 CONT Ednyved ap Cynric (Minister to Llywelyn Vawr)
1 CONT + Gwenllian verch Rhys
1 CONT 27 Gweryl verch Morgan
1 CONT + Gruffydd ab Owain
1 CONT Grono Vychan ab Ednyved 1268-
1 CONT 28 Meredyd ap Gruffydd
1 CONT Ednyfed Vychan ap Grono Vychan 1282-
1 CONT Tudur ap Grono Vychan 
1 CONT 29 Morgan ap Meredyd (Led the 1294 rising in Morgannoe)
1 CONT Gruffydd ab Ednyed Vychan
1 CONT Goronwy ap Tudur 1331-
1 CONT 30 Tudur ap Goronwy
1 CONT + Margaret verch Thomas
1 CONT Rhys ap Gruffydd
1 CONT 31 Maredudd ap Tudur (House of Tudor)
1 CONT Gruffydd Llwyd (Sir) ap Rhys 1322-
1 CONT 32 Owain ap Maredudd 1461-
1 CONT + Catherine (House of Valois)
1 CONT Evan ap Gruffydd Llwyd
1 CONT 33 Edmund ab Owain (Tudor)
1 CONT + Margaret verch Henry VI (House of Lancaster)
1 CONT 34 Henry VII ab Edmund (Field of Bosworth)
1 CONT + Elizabeth verch Edward IV
1 CONT 35 Henry VIII ap Henry VII 1491-1547
1 CONT + Catherine of Aragon 1485-1536 (divorced 1532)
1 CONT + Anne Boleyn c1507-1536 (married 1533, beheaded 1536)
1 CONT + Jane Seymour c1509-1537 (died 12 days after giving birth)
1 CONT + Anne of Cleeves 1515-1557 (married 1540, divorced 1540)
1 CONT + Catherine Howard 1520-1542 (married 1540, beheaded 1542)
1 CONT + Catherine Parr 1512-1548 (married 1543)
1 CONT Margaret verch Henry VII
1 CONT + James IV ap James III
1 CONT 36 Mary I verch Henry VIII (Catherine of Aragon) 1516-1558
1 CONT + Philip II of Spain (Bloody Mary, Queen from 1553)
1 CONT Elizabeth I verch Henry VIII (Anne Boleyn) 1533-1603 (Queen from 1558)
1 CONT Edward VI ap Henry VIII (Jane Seymour) 1537-1553
1 CONT (King from 1547)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Conclusion
1 CONT According to the historical facts available above coupled to reasonable thinking and deduction I am of the opinion that the legend of King Arthur is a gross exaggeration of a simple truth. It is thought that Arthur did live in the
1 CONC  late 5th and early 6th centuries, albeit not under the specific name of Arthur, but rather under the Celtic pronunciation of the name Cunedda.
1 CONT It is strongly argued that Arthur was indeed of royal blood, albeit not a full king until later in life. It is further thought that the ancestral line before and after him in his existence as Cunedda, speaks for itself.
1 CONT This document will certainly not quell the arguments that rage about the origins of King Arthur but is believed to be a lot nearer the truth than most of the theories put forward.
1 CONT
1 CONT ===
