0 @N6000000003051221586@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Cynan Garwyn", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT Cynan Garwyn has little more recorded of him than his name and that he was the ruler of lands in the Kingdom of Powys, Wales in the 7th century. Even those records are in poetry or manuscripts written more than two hundred years a
1 CONC fter he is thought to have lived.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is thought to be the eldest son of Brochwel Ysgithrog and father of Selyf Sarffgadau, and usually considered as a Prince of Powys who held authority for a period between those two. Some genealogies record that he married Gwenwy
1 CONC nwyn 'of the Scots'.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is sometimes argued that he died with his son at the Battle of Chester in circa AD 613 but any precise description would be based more on the desire to create a myth of the foundation of a dynasty or legend of Powsyian glory th
1 CONC an on available evidence.
