0 @N6000000003949916929@ NOTE According to tradition, Cadwallon ruled during, or shortly after, the Battle of Mons Badonicus, and King Arthur's victory over the Saxons (depending on which date you believe, said battle accorded either sometime between the early
1 CONC  490s and the mid 510s). Although it is unlikely that Cadwallon himself was present at the event, he would likely have benefitted from the period of relative peace and prosperity throughout Britain that it augured. The most moment
1 CONC ous military achievement of Cadwallon's reign was the final expulsion of Irish settlers on Ynys Mon (Anglesey), and the absorption of that island, which would later become the cultural and political base of the kingdom, into Gwyne
1 CONC dd.
1 CONT Cadwallon's second name, Lawhir, referred to an actual physical characteristic of the man: he apparently had unusually long arms! Iolo Goch claims that he could "reach a stone from the ground to kill a raven, without bending his b
1 CONC ack, because his arm was as long as his side to the ground."
1 CONT According to Gildas, Cadwallon's son, Maelgwn, murdered his uncle to ascend to the throne, which suggests that someone other than Maelgwn himself inherited the kingdom upon Cadwallon's death. No clear evidence exists as to who thi
1 CONC s "lost king" might be (assuming, of course, that Gildas's account is reliable), but some have suggested the name of Owain Danwyn ('White-Tooth') as the unfortunate heir/victim.
0 @N6000000009304904058@ NOTE #Générale##Générale#<David Nash Ford>
1 CONT
1 CONT note couple : #Générale#<David Nash Ford>
0 @N6000000002187905641@ NOTE Owain Ddantgwyn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain Ddantgwyn is the popularly recognised form of the name of a prince of North Wales, probably a King of Rhôs in the late 5th century. Next to nothing is known of him, yet he has become widely accepted in some quarters as a pos
1 CONC sible candidate for a so-called 'real' King Arthur.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Extant Records
1 CONT The correct modern spelling of the man's name is Owain Danwyn (Owen White-Teeth). He appears in various ancient Welsh genealogies as the son of Einion Yrth and the father of Cynlas Goch. One of these is given the title, 'Pedigree 
1 CONC of (the Kings of) Rhôs'. According to the Bonedd y Saint, he was also the father of SS. Einion Frenin (the King), Seiriol, Meirion and possibly others. Other than these genealogies, no documentary evidence exists concerning his li
1 CONC fe.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Arthurian identification
1 CONT Some investigative historians have conjectured that Owain could be the original of a ‘real’ King Arthur:
1 CONT
1 CONT This theory has been chiefly proposed by Graham Phillips and Martin Keatman in their book, King Arthur: The True Story (1992). They suggest that 'Arthur' was a title and identify its recipient as Owain from a passage in De Excidio
1 CONC  Britanniae. Its contemporary author, Gildas, refers (in Latin) to Owain’s son, Cynlas, literally as "guider of the chariot which is the receptacle of the bear". 'Bear' in Brythonic is 'Arth', so Phillips and Keatman take this to 
1 CONC infer that 'the Arthur' was Cynlas’ predecessor, known from the genealogies to be Owain. They go on to claim that Owain ruled in Powys.
1 CONT
1 CONT The idea is expanded by Mick Baker on Kessler-Web. He adds that Owain was probably the uncle who was, according to Gildas, murdered by Maelgwn Gwynedd (whom he compares to Mordred); he lived in North Wales where there are three si
1 CONC tes called 'Camlan' (being the site of Arthur's terminal battle with Mordred); and his father was called Yrth (compare Uther).
