1 CONT c.110 BC Heli (Beli Mawr - the Great) m. Don ferch Mathonwy. Daughter m. Llyr, ancestor of Gwent. 
1 CONT  Beli Mawr is claimed as the founder of the Deisi, later rulers of the kingdom of Dyfed. His eldest son, Aballac, is claimed as the ancestor of Coel Hen, of Ebruac. His second child, daughter Lweriadd, marries Llyr Lleddiarth, who
1 CONC  is claimed as the founder of Gwent.  
1 CONT c.80 BC Lludd Llaw Ereint (the Silver-Handed) Third child. Began the line of Cunedda Wledig of Gwynedd.  
1 CONT ar.60 - 48 BC Cassivellaunos King of the Catuvellauni. Fought Julius Caesar. 
1 CONT c.20 BC Bran Fendigaid (the Blessed)/Tenvantius King of the Silures. 
1 CONT c. AD 1 - 41 Cymbeline (Cunobelinus) King of the Trinovantes. Acknowledged by Rome. 
1 CONT 43 - 51 Guiderius ?Caratacus, King of the Catuvellauni (seized & taken to Rome). 
1 CONT  Arviragus  
1 CONT  Marius  
1 CONT  Coilus  
1 CONT - 156 Lucius  
1 CONT  Geta   
1 CONT  Bassianus  
1 CONT 198 - 217 Sons of Severus Emperors of Rome. 
1 CONT  Asclepiodotus  
1 CONT c.250 Coel (Godhebog) Lord of Colchester (Early Roman capital of Britain), so High King. 
1 CONT ar.283 Eudaf Hen King of Ewyas.  
1 CONT 287 - 293 Carausius Usurper Emperor of Rome. 
1 CONT 293 - 296 Allectus Usurper Emperor of Rome. 
1 CONT 305 - 306 Constantius I Chlorus Emperor of Rome. Married Helena. 
1 CONT 306 - 337 Constantine I the Great Emperor of Rome. 
1 CONT  Octavius  
1 CONT  Trahern  
1 CONT  Maximianus  
1 CONT 383 - 388 Magnus Maximus (Macsen Wledig) Western Roman Emperor 383-388. Selected Coel Hen as his replacement in most of Northern Britain.  
1 CONT ?388 - ?406 Coel Hen, Dux Brittanorum King of Northern Britain, based at Ebrauc.  
1 CONT 400 The reorganisations of Magnus Maximus and his subsequent withdrawal of troops from Britain virtually signals the end of Roman rule over the island. From this point on, all of Britain's High Kings originate from within the coun
1 CONC try. 
1 CONT 406
1 CONT  Marcus Elevated Roman Soldier. Ruled for a few months. 
1 CONT 406 Gracianus (Gratian) Urban magistratus or councillor. Ruled 4 months. 
1 CONT 406 - 411
1 CONT  Constantine III (Custennin ap Selyf) King of Armorica. Usurper Western Roman Emperor. 
1 CONT 411 - ?425 Constans ap Custennin Son. 
1 CONT 425 - c.455 Wortigernos / Vitalinus (Vortigern) King of Powys. Possible 1st Emperor of Britain. Opposed by Ambrosius the Elder. Died in a fire. 
1 CONT 450 The former Celtic tribal associations re-emerge as independent kingdoms developing over the course of the fifth and sixth centuries. 
1 CONT ?c.455 Vortimer (Britu) King of Gwent. Son of Vortigern. Likely held little national power. 
1 CONT c.455 - c.480 Ambrosius Aurelianus King of Caer Gloui. Possible 2nd Emperor of Britain.  
1 CONT c.480 - 511 Arthur Pendragon Son of Uthyr. Poss 3rd (Roman-style)
1 CONT Emperor of Britain. 
1 CONT c.496 Arthur commands the defence of Mons Badonicus against a confederation of invading Saxon tribes led by Aelle of the Suth Saxe. The British victory grants them a generation of relative peace and consigns the South Saxons to su
1 CONC bsequent obscurity. 
1 CONT c.530 - c.540 Constantine III (IV) King of Dumnonia. 
1 CONT c.540 - 549 By this time, the Saxons have recovered from their massive Mons Badonicus defeat and begin a westwards advance. 
1 CONT ar.540 Aurelius Conanus King of Caer Gloui. 
1 CONT - 540 Vortiporus King of Demetia. 
1 CONT c.540 - 549 Malgo (Maelgwyn Gwynedd) King of Gwynedd. 
1 CONT 549 - c.600 Following the death of the powerful Maelgwyn, and given the dearth of information about the Northern British Kings at this time, it is entirely plausible to place the named 'Keretic' and the 'Three unnamed tyrants' bel
1 CONC ow as Kings in the North. It is odd to have such a gap so late in the table but not if those rulers were from the poorly documented North. The Saxon advance in the South also lends weight to this hypothesis (Mick Baker). Their wes
1 CONC twards advance becomes much more rapid, swallowing much of Somerset. The Angles also advance, taking large swathes of Central and Northern Britain, and ending any realistic claim of the High Kings of Britain. After 613, the High K
1 CONC ings are dominant only in Wales and surviving British west coast territories. 
1 CONT  Three unnamed tyrants:  
1 CONT 549 - 560?  Morgan Bulc King of Bernaccia (to 547), and Goutodin (c.560 onwards). 
1 CONT 560? - 579  Rhydderch Hen King of Strathclyde. 
1 CONT 579 - 590  Urien King of Rheged. 
1 CONT 590 - 613 Keretic / Keredic / Ceredig Probably the same Ceredig as the King of Elmet. 
1 CONT 613 - 625 Cadvan King of Gwynedd. 
1 CONT 625 - 634 Cadwallo (Cadwallon ap Cadfan) King of Gwynedd. Also claimed the crown of Deira which now included Elmet. 
1 CONT 634 - 664 Cadwalader King of Gwynedd. Last High King of Britai
1 CONT
