1 CONT
1 CONT Details of the Ancestry of Coel Hen
1 CONT by David Nash Ford
1 CONT
1 CONT COEL HEN'S
1 CONT ANCESTRY
1 CONT Coels Confused 
1 CONT
1 CONT There appear to have been a number of people in Celtic tradition with the name Coel and they have been hopelessly confused over the years:
1 CONT
1 CONT The most well-known was Coel Hen (the Old) - the Old King Cole of nursery rhyme fame - who held sway in Northern Britain in the early 5th century and was probably the last of the Duces Britanniorum. His descendants continued to ru
1 CONC le various kingdoms in the region for several generations.  
1 CONT
1 CONT Then there was Coel Godhebog (the Magnificent), traditionally a 'dux' of Camelodunum (Colchester); probably a decurion in fact. He supposedly lived in the 3rd century and his daughter, St. Helena of Colchester, married the Roman E
1 CONC mperor, Constantius Chlorus. They may have been the ancestors of Magnus Maximus. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Less well known was an earlier Coel ap Mor from the more indefinite realms of mythology. If he existed, he would have lived in the 2nd century. Traditionally, he was the maternal great grandfather of Coel Godhebog's wife, Strada. 
1 CONT
1 CONT All are numbered amongst the supposed 'High-Kings' of Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT The ancestry of Coel Hen (the Old) is given in the pedigree of the Kings of Bryneich in the Harleian MS.3859 as:
1 CONT
1 CONT Beli Beli Mawr (the Great) 
1 CONT Aballac Afallach 
1 CONT Eudelen Euddolen/Eudos/Eifudd, possible duplicate generations 
1 CONT Eudos 
1 CONT Ebiud 
1 CONT Outigern Eudeyrn/Euddigan, duplicate generations 
1 CONT Oudecant 
1 CONT Ritigirn Rhydeyrn 
1 CONT Iumetel Rhifedel 
1 CONT Grat Gratian 
1 CONT Urban Urban 
1 CONT Telpuil Telpwyll 
1 CONT Teuhant Deheuwaint/Tegfan or Tasciovanus, duplicate generations 
1 CONT Tecmant 
1 CONT Coyl Hen Guotepauc Coel Hen (the Old) 
1 CONT
1 CONT It is similarly recorded in the pedigree of the Kings of South Rheged in the Achau Brenhinoedd a Thywysogion Cymru as:
1 CONT
1 CONT Beli Mawr Beli Mawr (the Great) 
1 CONT Aflech Afallach, duplicate generations 
1 CONT Avallach 
1 CONT Enddolev Euddolen/Eudos/Eifudd, possible duplicate generations 
1 CONT Endos 
1 CONT Eneid 
1 CONT Endeyrn Eudeyrn/Euddigan, duplicate generations 
1 CONT Endigant 
1 CONT Rydeyrn Rhydeyrn 
1 CONT Rriuedel Rhifedel 
1 CONT Gradd Gratian 
1 CONT Vrban Urban 
1 CONT Tyddbwyll Telpwyll 
1 CONT Deheuwaint Deheuwaint/Tegfan or Tasciovanus, duplicate generations 
1 CONT Tegvan 
1 CONT Koel Godebawg Coel Hen (the Old) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Coel's father is thus shown to have born the native Celtic name of Tegfan. In Latin, this becomes Tasciovanus, a name known to have been in use in Britain during pre-Roman times, at least amongst the Catuvellauni tribe. Telpwyll i
1 CONC s an otherwise unknown name, probably Celtic, but the extended ancestry have distinctly Roman names (Urban and Gratian), before descending into corrupt mythology. Coel Hen evidently had a mixed racial background. He spawned a wide
1 CONC spread Northern dynasty.
1 CONT ---
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_King_Cole
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] King Coel of Northern Britain
1 CONT An altogether more historical character was Coel Hen, a Welsh name which actually means 'Coel the Old'. He appears in a number of Old Welsh pedigrees – notably the Bonedd Gwyr y Gogledd (or 'The Descent of the Men of the North') –
1 CONC  at the head of several post-Roman Brythonic Royal families of modern Northern England and Southern Scotland, including those ruling Rheged, Elmet and possibly York, Bryneich and the other kingdoms in the Pennines. He is also cons
1 CONC idered to be the father-in-law of Cunedda, founder of the Kingdom of Gwynedd in North Wales. The so-called Arthur stone names him as an ancestor of Artognou, a post-Roman ruler at Tintagel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Projections back from dated individuals suggest that Coel Hen lived around AD 350–420, during the time when the Romans withdrew their forces from Britain. This has led historians, such as John Morris, to suggest that he may have b
1 CONC een the last of the Roman Duces Brittanniarum (Dukes of the Britons) who commanded the Roman army in Northern Britain. He may have taken over the northern capital at Eburacum (York) to rule over what had been the northern province
1 CONC  of Roman Britain. Upon Coel Hen's death, his lands would have been split between his sons and later his grandsons, thus creating the many old northern kingdoms of Britain. WF Skene recorded traditions of his death whilst campaign
1 CONC ing in the Kyle district of what was later called Ayrshire (Scotland), which was subsequently named after him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Welsh sources also give this man the alternative name of Coel Godhebog, meaning 'Coel the Magnificent' or 'Coel the Defender', but David Nash Ford considers this name may have been transferred from Cole of Colchester. From the 15t
1 CONC h century to the 18th century, the two were certainly much confused. There are a number of other lesser known Coels mentioned in various Old Welsh sources too.
1 CONT
1 CONT ====
1 CONT from http://www.britannia.com/history/ebk/gene/coelanc.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Details of the Ancestry of Coel Hen
1 CONT by David Nash Ford
