1 CONC le various kingdoms in the region for several generations.  
1 CONT
1 CONT Then there was Coel Godhebog (the Magnificent), traditionally a 'dux' of Camelodunum (Colchester); probably a decurion in fact. He supposedly lived in the 3rd century and his daughter, St. Helena of Colchester, married the Roman E
1 CONC mperor, Constantius Chlorus. They may have been the ancestors of Magnus Maximus. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Less well known was an earlier Coel ap Mor from the more indefinite realms of mythology. If he existed, he would have lived in the 2nd century. Traditionally, he was the maternal great grandfather of Coel Godhebog's wife, Strada. 
1 CONT
1 CONT All are numbered amongst the supposed 'High-Kings' of Britain.
1 CONT
