0 @N6000000001745124959@ NOTE RHUN (son of No. 4). He succeeded his father as King of Gwynedd about A.D. 550. He is
1 CONT           described as "a great, tall man with red-brown curly hair." (Lloyd)
1 CONT
1 CONT           In retaliation for an attack from his northern Brythonic brethren he made war against them and made
1 CONT           the territory as far north as the Firth of Forth subject to Gwynedd.
0 @N6000000002187905575@ NOTE Rhun Hir ap Maelgwn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhun ap Maelgwn (c.492-c.586, reigned from c.549) (Latin: Rugenus, English: Run), also known as Rhun Hir ('the Tall') was a king of Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ascending to the throne of what was then the most powerful kingdom of Britain in his twenties upon the death of his father, Maelgwn Hir ap Cadwallon, from plague, Rhun soon found himself embroiled in a dynastic dispute with Elidyr
1 CONC  Llydanwyn, king of Rheged. Elidyr had married Rhun's sister, and as a result believed himself to be the proper heir to the throne of Gwynedd. Elidyr's attempted invasion of Gwynedd, proved unsuccessful, and Elidyr himself was kil
1 CONC led in the attempt. Elidyr's cousins, however, Rhydderch Hael of Strathclyde, and Clydno Eiten of Lothian, remained bitter rivals of Rhun, and of his half-brother, Brude, leader of the Picts to the south. Rhydderch and Clydno, aid
1 CONC ed by Elidyr's brother Cinmarc, raided and sacked the town of Arfon (now Caernarfon) in Anglesey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Raising an army from all over northern Wales, Rhun retaliated, marching unopposed through Rheged and across the Pennines to York, effectively seizing control of all of north Wales and northern England and, through alliance with Br
1 CONC ude, even stretching south in the lands held by the Picts. Though his authority was recognized by all, Rhun lacked the resources to maintain administrative control over such a large area, and eventually made peace with Peredur of 
1 CONC York and returned to Gwynedd, probably towards the mid-560s. Rhun reigned for another two decades, but little else is heard of him.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Maelgwn Hir Kings of Gwynedd Followed by:
1 CONT Beli ap Rhun
