0 @N6000000003051149162@ NOTE He was the king who was upbraided by Saint Patrick for his evil ways: his subjects were Christians yet he caused a massacre and sold captured Christians to the Picts and the Scots.
0 @N6000000003949959697@ NOTE (Welsh-Caradog, Latin-Caratacus, English-Caractacus)    Ceretic Guletic (the Land-Holder or Imperator) was King of Strathclyde in the early 5th century. His ancestry does not descent from any of the typical British "founder-gods" 
1 CONC and may therfore be considered as more reliable. His grandfather & great grandfather appear to have born Roman names, Quintillius & Clemens respectively, so he may hail from a tribe of confederated Roman allies. He ruled his Kingd
1 CONC om from the citadel on Dumbarton Rock, or Alcluith as it was then known, and it was here that he received a letter from St. Patrick accusing him of making off with several of his Irish converts
0 @N6000000002188039219@ NOTE Ceretic of Alt Clut
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ceretic Guletic of Alt Clut was a king of Alt Clut (modern Dumbarton) in the fifth century. He appears in the writings of St. Patrick with the Latin name Corocticus, and this appearance in a contemporary historical source makes hi
1 CONC m the first historical king. Of Patrick's two surviving letters, one is addressed to this Corocticus, and in it Patrick lambasts the milites Coroctici ("warband of Ceretic") for his involvement with the Picts and the Scots, and fo
1 CONC r selling newly-christianized Gaels into the hands of the Picts. Ceretic's dates therefore depend on the conclusions of the vast scholarship devoted to discovering the floruit of St Patrick, but the sometime in the fifth century i
1 CONC s probably safe. Ceretic appears also in the Harleian genealogies of the rulers of Alt Clut, from which we know his father (Cynloyp), grand-father (Cinhil) and great-grandfather (Cluim).[1] It is from the latter source that we get
1 CONC  his nickname, Guletic ("Land-holder"). In the Book of Armagh, he is called Coirthech rex Aloo, "Ceretic, King of the Height [of the Clyde]" [2]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT ^ Harleian genealogy 5; see also, Williams, Smyth, and Kirby (eds.), A Biographical Dictionary of Dark Age Britain, (London, 1991), s.v. "Ceretic", pp. 78-8 
1 CONT ^ Alan MacQuarrie, "The Kings of Strathclyde", in A. Grant & K.Stringer (eds.) Medieval Scotland: Crown, Lordship and Community, Essays Presented to G.W.S. Barrow, (Edinburgh, 1993), p. 3. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Smyth, Alfred, Warlords and Holy Men, (Edinburgh, 1984) 
1 CONT MacQuarrie, Alan, "The Kings of Strathclyde", in A. Grant & K.Stringer (eds.) Medieval Scotland: Crown, Lordship and Community, Essays Presented to G.W.S. Barrow, (Edinburgh, 1993), pp. 1-19. 
1 CONT Williams, Anne, Smyth, Alfred P., and Kirby, D.P., (eds.), A Biographical Dictionary of Dark Age Britain, (London, 1991), s.v. "Ceretic", pp. 78-8 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Harleian genealogy 5 
1 CONT David Nash Ford's "Early British Kingdoms" 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Cynloyp? King of Alt Clut
1 CONT mid-400s Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cinuit? 
0 @N6000000002188619513@ NOTE Beli I of Alt Clut
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Beli I of Alt Clut was the ruler of Alt Clut (modern Dumbarton Rock in the West of Scotland), probably sometime in the early-to-mid seventh century. According to the Harleian genealogies, he was the son of Neithon, his predecessor
1 CONC  as king. We know from this genealogy that he was the father of Eugein (Owain),[1] and we know from our extensive knowledge of King Bridei III of the Picts, that he was the father of that illustrious Pictish monarch. In the Elegy 
1 CONC for Bruide mac Bili attributed to Adomnán of Iona, Bridei is called mac rígh Ala Cluaithe[2] ("son of the King of Alt Clut").[3] If the Belin moritur ("death of Beli") in the Annales Cambriae can be taken as a reference to our Bel
1 CONC i,[4] then we have Beli's death date perhaps at 627. He was certainly dead by 642.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Harleian genealogy of the Kings of Alt Clut, here.
1 CONT    2. ^ Alan MacQuarrie, "The Kings of Strathclyde", in A. Grant & K.Stringer (eds.) Medieval Scotland: Crown, Lordship and Community, Essays Presented to G.W.S. Barrow, (Edinburgh, 1993), p. 9.
1 CONT    3. ^ Thomas Owen Clancy (ed.), The Triumph Tree: Scotland's Earliest Poetry, 550–1350, (Edinburgh, 1998), p. 115
1 CONT    4. ^ AC627?, here
1 CONT    5. ^ Alan MacQuarrie, loc. cit.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Clancy, Thomas Owen (ed.), The Triumph Tree: Scotland's Earliest Poetry, 550–1350, (Edinburgh, 1998)
1 CONT     * MacQuarrie, Alan, "The Kings of Strathclyde", in A. Grant & K.Stringer (eds.) Medieval Scotland: Crown, Lordship and Community, Essays Presented to G.W.S. Barrow, (Edinburgh, 1993), pp. 1-19
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
