0 @N6000000002187905522@ NOTE Cadfan ap Iago
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cadfan ap Iago (c. 580–625; reigned from c. 615) (Latin: Catamanus; English: Gideon) was a King of Gwynedd. The son of King Iago, he assumed the crown of Gwynedd probably around 615, shortly after the Battle of Caerllion (today's 
1 CONC Chester), during which the forces of Powys were defeated by Æthelfrith of Bernicia.
1 CONT King Cadfan's gravestone in Llangadwaladr church
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT King Cadfan's gravestone in Llangadwaladr church
1 CONT
1 CONT Cadfan was generally considered to have been a wise and just ruler, noted for his ability to maintain the rule of law and peace during an increasingly hostile period in British history. His memorial stone at Llangadwaladr Church i
1 CONC n Anglesey refers to him as "Catamanus rex sapientisimus opinatisimus omnium regum" (="King Cadfan the wisest and most renowned of all kings"). He was succeeded by his son Cadwallon.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was one of the last of the legendary kings of the Britons as accounted by Geoffrey of Monmouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Iago ap Beli Kings of Gwynedd Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cadwallon ap Cadfan
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Saxon Interregnum Mythical British Kings Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cadwallon
0 @N6000000003051115998@ NOTE He was the "wisest and most splendid of all kings" according to his memorial stone which can still be seen today in the church of Llangadwaladr on Ynys Mon, Anglesey.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Bill Cooper, "After the Flood"]
1 CONT Then, shortly after the year AD 600, came Cadvan. Known in the Welsh chronicles as Cadfan ab lago, king of Gwynedd, he was of north Welsh descent. He began as king of the Venedoti (men of Gwynedd) and succeeded to the kingship of 
1 CONC all the Britons by engaging in battle Ethelfrith, the Saxon king of Northumbria, who, with Ethelbert of Kent, carried out the massacre of the British monks at Bangor in AD 604. Ethelfrith and Cadvan divided the country between the
1 CONC m, Cadvan ruling over the southern half. His reign lasted until ca AD 625. He married a Saxon noblewoman of the Gewissae.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Wikipedia, "Cadfan ap Iago", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT Cadfan ap Iago (c. 580-625; reigned from c. 615) (Latin: Catamanus; English: Gideon) was a King of Gwynedd. The son of King Iago, he assumed the crown of Gwynedd probably around 615, shortly after the Battle of Caerllion (today's 
1 CONC Chester), during which the forces of Powys were defeated by Æthelfrith of Bernicia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cadfan was generally considered to have been a wise and just ruler, noted for his ability to maintain the rule of law and peace during an increasingly hostile period in British history. His memorial stone at Llangadwaladr Church i
1 CONC n Anglesey refers to him as "Catamanus rex sapientisimus opinatisimus omnium regum" (King Cadfan the wisest and most renowned of all kings). He was succeeded by his son Cadwallon.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is one of the last of the legendary kings of Britain as accounted by Geoffrey of Monmouth, who calls him Cadvan and makes him king of the North Welsh, later king of all the Britons."
0 @N6000000003319708363@ NOTE
1 CONT AFN: HPGD-4Q
0 @N6000000003949916882@ NOTE Cadfan succeeded to the throne on his father's death around the year 615.
1 CONT Cadfan is notable if only because his tombstone survives at Llangadwaladr on the island of Môn or Anglesey in north Wales. Or to be more exact his tombstone was recycled and used as building material for the church at Llangadwalad
1 CONC r from whence it was rescued. The inscription on the memorial stone (technically known as ECMW.13) reads;
1 CONT Catamanus rex sapientisimus opinatisimus omnium regum
1 CONT or in English:    Cadfan, wisest and most renowned of all kings
1 CONT The location of the memorial on Anglesey leads many to speculate that it was during the reign of Cadfan that the kings of Gwynedd established their royal capital at Aberffraw. His predecessors seemed to have preferred to base them
1 CONC selves on the mainland at locations such as Degannwy, but Aberffraw became the traditional royal seat of power that persisted right down to the thirteenth century.
1 CONT Son of King Iago of Gwynedd for whom he fought at the Battle of Caer-Legion (Chester), against King Aethelfrith of Bernicia, in AD 613. When it was over, he found that his father was dead and he was King. The Battle of Bangor-is-C
1 CONC oed (Bangor-upon-Dee) followed in quick succession, and the young monarch showed such leadership that he was, afterwards, declared High-King of the Britons.
1 CONT This border campaign was centred on Aethelfrith's attempts to recapture his enemy, the exiled King Edwin of Deira. According to Geoffrey of Monmouth, this young lad had been taken in by Cadfan and raised as his foster-son; but the
1 CONC  two were actually of similar age and so it seems that Edwin was really Cadfan's foster-brother. So many Welshmen fell in the two battles that Edwin felt it best to move on and left Gwynedd for the court of King Redwald of East An
1 CONC glia.
1 CONT Cadfan was a patron of St. Beuno and married Afandreg Ddu (the Black) daughter of King Cynan Garwyn of Powys. He was the "wisest and most splendid of all kings" according to his memorial stone which can still be seen today in the 
1 CONC church of Eglwys Ael (Llangadwaladr) on Ynys Mon (Anglesey). He was buried there after dying, probably at nearby Aberffraw, around AD 625. 
