0 @N6000000003949636964@ NOTE Also known as Octavius.  A shadowy semi-legendary ruler of the Gewisse.  According to Geoffrey of Monmouth, he usurped the the throne after Constantine leaves Britain.  He was dethroned by the General Trahern, fled to Norway but r
1 CONC eturned with an army, defeated Trahern and then ruled for many years.  His home territory was Gwent in southern Wales.  Tradition has it that he married a daughter of Carausius, the Roman commander of the Saxon shore.  He had at l
1 CONC east 4 children.  Helen or Elen who married Magnus Maximus; His eldest son Erbin seems to have ruled in Gwent;, Another son, Cynan, became ruler of Dumnonia. (#P87, Kings and Queens of England, Mike Ashley, p718) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudaf Hen (the Old) first appears in the old Welsh mythological tale, the "Dream of Macsen". The future Roman Emperor, Magnus Maximus, dreamt of Eudaf's beautiful daughter, Elen Lluyddog (of the Host), and sent emissaries across t
1 CONC he Empire to find her. She was discovered in her father's palace at Caer-Segeint (Caernarfon) where the old man sat, carving 'gwyddbwyll' pieces (like chess-men). Maximus came to Britain, married the girl and eventually inherited 
1 CONC her father's kingdom, much to the disgust of his male heir, Conan Meriadoc.
1 CONT
1 CONT If he existed at all, Eudaf lived in the mid-4th century. He would, therefore, have been a Romano-Briton, living an extremely Romanized lifestyle. The Latin Octavius the Old is therefore a much more appropriate form of his name. H
1 CONC is daughter was Helena.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Dream story clearly indicates that Octavius was the monarch around Caernarfon in North Wales, but later writers - chiefly the mistrusted Geoffrey of Monmouth - made him "Duke of the Giwissei" or "Iarl Ergyng ac Ewias": evident
1 CONC ly ruling in Ergyng and Gwent. This may have arisen from his supposed descent from so-called pre-Roman Kings of Siluria (named after the Celtic tribe who lived in that area). Though the connection is persistent and it is equally p
1 CONC ossible that the Caernarfon association is due to Maximus and Helena's later residence there. Octavius would not have been a king at this date, but perhaps a decurion of one of these civitates (Roman towns). However, he is also ca
1 CONC lled one of the High-Kings of Britain. Such a title would, clearly, not have existed either but it may indicate that he held a position of considerable importance in the Roman administration. The official with control of both the 
1 CONC Caernarfon and Gwent areas was the Praeses of Britannia Prima.
1 CONT Geoffrey's mythology has Octavius taking up the British High-Kingship after defeating King Coel Godhebog (the Magnificent)'s brother, Trahearn, in battle near Winchester. So perhaps he took office by force.
1 CONT Early records are confused about Eudaf's descendants. Some stories claim that he had various sons, Conan, Adeon/Gadeon and Eudaf II. Others, that Helena was his sole direct heiress and that Conan, his male heir, was only his nephe
1 CONC w. This appears to fit best. Magnus Maximus and his wife probably inherited Eudaf's position in society, helping the former to put himself forward as Emperor of the West. Conan made excellent marriages and was placated with vast e
1 CONC states given by his cousin's husband. Adeon/Gadeon alias Cadfan was actually his son. Eudaf II appears very late and is probably mythological.
0 @N6000000002188327475@ NOTE Elen
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Elen (known in Welsh tradition as Elen Luyddog; also known as Saint Helen of Caernarfon) was a late 4th century founder of churches in Wales who is remembered as a saint. Traditionally she is said to have been a Romano-British pri
1 CONC ncess and the wife of Macsen or Magnus Clemens Maximus, Emperor in Britain, Gaul and Spain, where he died seeking imperial recognition in 388.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Church tradition
1 CONT
1 CONT Elen was mother of five, including a boy named Constantine, but she lived about sixty years later than Helena of Constantinople, the mother of Constantine the Great with whom she has, in times past, been confused. She is patron of
1 CONC  Llanelan in West Gower and of the church at Penisa'r-waun near Caernarfon, where her feast day is May 22. Together with her sons, Cystennin (Constantine) and Peblig (Publicius, named in the calendar of the Church in Wales), she i
1 CONC s said to have introduced into Wales the Celtic form of monasticism from Gaul. Saint Gregory of Tours and Sulpicius Severus records that Maximus and Elen met Saint Martin of Tours while they were in Gaul.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Literary tradition
1 CONT
1 CONT Elen's story is told in The Dream of Macsen Wledig, one of the tales associated with the Mabinogion. Welsh mythology remembers her as the daughter of a chieftain of north Wales named Eudaf or Eudwy, who probably lived somewhere ne
1 CONC ar the Roman base of Segontium, now Caernarfon. She is remembered for having Macsen build roads across her country so that the soldiers could more easily defend it from attackers, thus earning her the name Elen Luyddog (Elen of th
1 CONC e Hosts). She is said to have ordered the making of Sarn Helen, the great Roman road running from Caernarfon to south Wales via Dolgellau, Pennal and Bremia (Llanddewi Brefi). Though this road bears her name it is considerably old
1 CONC er than Elen's accepted time period. Many other Roman roads in Wales bear her name (e.g. Llwybr Elen) and she is thus acknowledged as the patron saint of British roadbuilders[citation needed] and the protectoress of travellers.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Farmer, David (1997). Oxford Dictionary of Saints, 4th edition. Oxford. (Listed as Helen of Caernarvon).
1 CONT     * Pennick, Thorsons (1997). The Celtic Saints. Thorsons.
