0 @N6000000002187905541@ NOTE Iago ap Beli
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Iago ap Beli (c. 560-613; reigned from c. 599) (Latin: Iacobus; English: James) was King of Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most notable feature of his reign (of which little information exists) is the growing strength of the nearby kingdoms of Mercia, under the rule of Pybba and his son Penda, and of Bernicia, under Æthelfrith. Some evidence exist
1 CONC s to suggest that Iago may have entered into some sort of alliance with Pybba, trading the latter's promise not to attack Gwynedd in exchange for Gwynedd's support against Northumbria, should that support be needed. In 604, after 
1 CONC being driven from his throne by Æthelfrith, Edwin, king of Deira, may have sought refuge at the Iago's court. Perhaps as a result, Æthelfrith turned his wrath against Gwynedd, slaughtering the monks at a monastery at Bangor, and f
1 CONC inally facing the forces of Gwynedd and Powys at the Battle of Chester in 613.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not known whether or not Iago took part in this battle: he may have done so, perishing amidst the slaughter, or he may have already abdicated his throne in favor of his son, Cadfan, and died later than year in a monastery.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Beli ap Rhun Kings of Gwynedd Followed by:
1 CONT Cadfan ap Iago
0 @N6000000001745124981@ NOTE IAGO (son of No. 6). Early in his reign he abdicated in favor of his son Cadfan and became a
1 CONT           monk. He seems to have chosen the quiet life of the monastery rather than the cares and
1 CONT           responsibilities of the kingdom. He is supposed to have died about A.D. 616.
