0 @N6000000006599196866@ NOTE Æthelfrith (died c. 616 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/616>) was King <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_monarchs_of_Northumbria> of Bernicia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernicia> from c. 593 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/593>
1 CONC  until c. 616 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/616>; he was also, beginning c. 604 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/604>, the first Bernician king to also rule Deira <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deira_%28kingdom%29>, to the south of Be
1 CONC rnicia. Since Deira and Bernicia were the two basic components of what would later be defined as Northumbria <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northumbria>, Æthelfrith can be considered, in historical terms, the first Northumbrian kin
1 CONC g. He was especially notable for his successes against the British <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brython> and his victory over the Irish <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_people> of Dál Riata <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A
1 CONC 1l_Riata>. Although he was defeated and killed in battle and replaced by a dynastic rival, his line was eventually restored to power in the 630s <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/630s>.
1 CONT
1 CONT Oswiu (c. 612 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/612>-15 February <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/February_15> 670 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/670>), also known as Oswy, was King of Bernicia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Bernic
1 CONC ia>. His father, Æthelfrith of Bernicia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86thelfrith_of_Bernicia>, was killed in battle, fighting against Rædwald <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%A6dwald>, King of the East Angles <http://en.wik
1 CONC ipedia.org/wiki/King_of_the_East_Angles> and Edwin of Deira <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwin_of_Deira> at the River Idle <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_Idle> in 616. Along with his brothers and their supporters, Oswiu was 
1 CONC then exiled until Edwin's death in 633.
1 CONT Following the death of his brother Oswald <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswald_of_Northumbria>, defeated by Penda <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penda> at the Battle of Maserfield <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Maserfield>
1 CONC  on 5 August <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/August_5> 642 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/642>, Oswiu became King of the Bernicians <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernicia>. He passed the next decade in obscurity as one of many kings 
1 CONC subject to Penda. In 655 Penda invaded Bernicia, driving Oswiu before him. The details of the campaign are unclear, but at the Battle of the Winwæd <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Winw%C3%A6d> Oswiu unexpectedly defeat
1 CONC ed and killed Penda. This victory was followed by Oswiu's short-lived imperium <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperium>-he is traditionally counted as a Bretwalda <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bretwalda>- over much of Great Britain
1 CONC  <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Britain>. He established himself as King of Mercia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Mercia>, setting up his son-in-law, Penda's son Peada <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peada> as a subject 
1 CONC king.
1 CONT Oswiu's unchallenged domination of Britain lasted only a short time, ending when a revolt among the Mercians <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercia> established Penda's son Wulfhere <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wulfhere_of_Mercia> 
1 CONC as their king. A negotiated settlement appears to have been preferred on both sides to prevent war. Divisions within the Northumbrian church led to the Synod of Whitby <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synod_of_Whitby> in 664, where O
1 CONC swiu agreed to settle the Easter controversy <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Easter_controversy> by adopting the Roman <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Catholic_Church> dating. His later years were marred by conflict with his son
1 CONC  Ealhfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ealhfrith_of_Deira>. Oswiu died in 670 and was succeeded by his son Ecgfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecgfrith_of_Northumbria>.
1 CONT
1 CONT Background and early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Anglo-Saxon kingdoms of the early 7th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Oswiu was fifty-eight years old at his death according to Bede <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bede>, placing his birth c. 612.[1] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> At this time, his father was at the height of his
1 CONC  power. Oswiu's mother may have been Æthelfrith's only recorded wife, Acha of Deira, Edwin's sister, but the apparent unwillingness of the Deirans to have him as their king may argue against this. Oswald, who is known to have been
1 CONC  Acha's son, was accepted as king in Deira, while Oswiu appears never to have ruled the kingdom directly.[2] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Æthelfrith ruled over both Bernicia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernicia> and Deira <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deira_%28kingdom%29>. His authority ran from the lands of the Picts <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picts> and the D
1 CONC ál Riata <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A1l_Riata> in modern Scotland <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotland> to Wales <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wales> and the Midlands <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midlands> in the south.
1 CONC [3] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Æthelfrith's power rested on his military success, and this success came to an end in 616, when the exiled Edwin of Deira, with the support of King Rædwald, defeated and kill
1 CONC ed him in battle by the River Idle <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_Idle>.[4] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT On Æthelfrith's death, his sons and their supporters fled Northumbria <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northumbria>, finding sanctuary among the Gaels <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaels> and Picts <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picts
1 CONC > of northern Britain and Ireland <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ireland>. Here they would remain until Edwin's death at the Battle of Hatfield Chase <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Hatfield_Chase> in 633.[5] <http://en.wik
1 CONC ipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>[6] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT In exile, the sons of Æthelfrith were converted to Christianity, or raised as Christians.[5] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> In Oswiu's case, he became an exile at the age of four, and cannot have returned to N
1 CONC orthumbria until aged twenty-one, spending childhood and adolescence in a Gaelic milieu. Bede writes that Oswiu was fluent in the Old Irish language <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Irish_language> and Irish in his faith.[7] <htt
1 CONC p://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT As well as learning the Irish language and being thoroughly christianised, Oswiu may have fought for his Gaelic hosts, perhaps receiving his arms-a significant event-from a King of Dál Riata <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_D
1 CONC %C3%A1l_Riata>, such as Eochaid Buide <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eochaid_Buide>, son of that Áedán mac Gabráin <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%81ed%C3%A1n_mac_Gabr%C3%A1in> whom his father had defeated at the Battle of Degsas
1 CONC tan <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Degsastan>.[8] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> The Irish annals <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_annals> name one Oisiric mac Albruit, rigdomna Saxan-ætheling <htt
1 CONC p://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86theling> Osric-among the dead, alongside Connad Cerr <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Connad_Cerr>, King of Dál Riata, and others of the Cenél nGabráin <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cen%C3%A9l_nGabr%C3
1 CONC %A1in>, at the Battle of Fid Eóin.[9] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Whether Oswiu's marriage with the Uí Néill <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U%C3%AD_N%C3%A9ill> princess Fín of the Cenél nEógain <http://en.wi
1 CONC kipedia.org/wiki/Cen%C3%A9l_nE%C3%B3gain>, and the birth of Aldfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldfrith>, should be placed in the context of his exile, or took place at a later date is uncertain.[10] <http://en.wikipedia.org/w
1 CONC iki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Equally uncertain is the date of Oswiu's return to Northumbria. He may have returned with Eanfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eanfrith_of_Bernicia> on Edwin's death in 633, as Bede appears to write.[5] <http://en.wikipedia.org/
1 CONC wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Eanfrith apostasised <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apostasy> and was killed by Cadwallon <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadwallon>, who was defeated and killed in turn by another brother, Oswald <http://e
1 CONC n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswald_of_Northumbria>, who became king of Bernicia and probably succeeded to his father's old dominance of northern and central Britain.[11] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Eanflæd and Oswine
1 CONT Oswald died in battle against Penda of Mercia at the Battle of Maserfield <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Maserfield>, dated by Bede to 5 August <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/August_5> 642 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/642
1 CONC >.[12] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Oswald's son Œthelwald may have been his preferred successor, but Œthelwald cannot have been an adult in 642. So, the kingship came to Oswiu. Unlike Eanfrith and Osric, Os
1 CONC wiu held to the Christian faith in spite of his brother's defeat by the pagan Penda. This may have been due to his more thoroughly Christian upbringing, but the influence of Bishop Aidan of Lindisfarne <http://en.wikipedia.org/wik
1 CONC i/Aidan_of_Lindisfarne>, by then a major figure in Bernicia, could also have been significant.[13] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Bede summarises Oswiu's reign in this way:
1 CONT Oswald being translated to the heavenly kingdom, his brother Oswy, a young man of about thirty years of age, succeeded him on the throne of his earthly kingdom, and held it twenty-eight years with much trouble, being harassed by t
1 CONC he pagan king, Penda, and by the pagan nation of the Mercians, that had slain his brother, as also by his son Alfred [i.e. Ealhfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ealhfrith_of_Deira>], and by his cousin-german Ethelwald [i.e. Œthe
1 CONC lwald of Deira <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%92thelwald_of_Deira>], the son of his brother who reigned before him.[14] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Oswiu's first recorded action as king of Bernicia was to strengthen his position, and perhaps his claims to Deira, by marrying Edwin's daughter Eanflæd <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eanfl%C3%A6d_of_Deira>, then in exile in the Kin
1 CONC gdom of Kent <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Kent>.[15] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> This marriage took place between 642 and 644.[16] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Oswiu's is known to have been married three times. Eanflæd, his Queen, bore him two sons and two daughters. The sons were Ecgfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecgfrith_of_Northumbria> (644x645-685) and Ælfwine <http://en.wikiped
1 CONC ia.org/wiki/%C3%86lfwine_of_Deira> (c. 660-679), the daughters Osthryth <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osthryth> (died 697) and Ælfflæd <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86lffl%C3%A6d_of_Whitby> (c. 654-714). The Irish princess Fín
1 CONC  was the mother of Aldfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldfrith> (died 705). Finally, the British <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brythons> princess Rieinmellt, of Rheged <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rheged>, is named as a wife 
1 CONC of Oswiu in the Historia Brittonum <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historia_Brittonum>.[17] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> It is thought that Eahlfrith was her son,[18] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Nor
1 CONC thumbria> and Eahlflæd may have been her daughter.[19] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT The first half of Oswiu's reign was spent in the shadow of Penda, who dominated much of Britain from 642 until 655, seemingly making and breaking kings as it suited him.[20] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> The 
1 CONC future kingdom of Northumbria was still composed of two distinct kingdoms in Oswiu's lifetime. The northerly kingdom of Bernicia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernicia>, which extended from the River Tees <http://en.wikipedia.org/
1 CONC wiki/River_Tees> to the Firth of Forth <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firth_of_Forth>, was ruled by Oswiu. The kingdom of Deira <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deira_%28kingdom%29>, lying between the North York Moors <http://en.wikip
1 CONC edia.org/wiki/North_York_Moors> and the Humber <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humber>, was ruled by a series of Oswiu's kinsmen, initially as a separate kingdom, later as a form of appanage <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appanage> f
1 CONC or Oswiu's sons.[21] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT For the first decade of Oswiu's reign, Deira was ruled by an independent king, Oswine <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswine_of_Deira>, son of the apostate Osric <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osric_of_Deira>, who belonged to the riv
1 CONC al Deiran royal family.[22] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Oswine and Oswiu came into conflict circa 651, Bede blames Oswiu for the troubles and writes:
1 CONT For when they had raised armies against one another, Oswin perceived that he could not maintain a war against one who had more auxiliaries than himself, and he thought it better at that time to lay aside all thoughts of engaging, 
1 CONC and to preserve himself for better times. He therefore dismissed the army which he had assembled, and ordered all his men to return to their own homes, from the place that is called Wilfaresdun, that is, Wilfar's Hill, which is al
1 CONC most ten miles distant from the village called Cataract [i.e. Catterick <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catterick>], towards the north-west. He himself, with only one trusty soldier, whose name was Tonhere, withdrew and lay conceale
1 CONC d in the house of Earl [comes] Hunwald, whom he imagined to be his most assured friend. But, alas! it was otherwise; for the earl betrayed him, and Oswy, in a detestable manner, by the hands of his commander [praefectus], Ethilwin
1 CONC , slew him...[14] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT In order to expiate the killing of Oswine, who was later reckoned a saint, Oswiu established a monastery at Gilling <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilling%2C_North_Yorkshire>, where prayers were said for Oswine and for Oswiu.[14] <
1 CONC http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Oswine was followed as king of the Deirans by Oswald's son Œthelwald.
1 CONT Penda
1 CONT Oswiu's relations with Penda were not entirely peaceful between 642 and 655. Bede appears to place a major assault on Bernicia by Penda, which reached the gates of Bamburgh <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamburgh>, at some time bef
1 CONC ore 651 and the death of Bishop Aidan of Lindisfarne <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aidan_of_Lindisfarne>.[23] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> An entry in the Irish annals recording "[t]he battle of Oswy against
1 CONC  Penda" circa 650 may refer to this campaign.[24] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT D.P. Kirby suggests that the killing of Oswine may have led to an improvement in relations between Penda and Oswiu in the early 650s. Oswiu's son Ealhfrith married Penda's daughter Cyneburh, while his daughter Ealhflæd married Pen
1 CONC da's son Peada. Peada was baptised <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baptised> at Ad Murum-in the region of Hadrian's Wall <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadrian%27s_Wall>-by Aidan's successor Finan <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finan>
1 CONC . Peada and Ealhflæd took a missionary group, including Cedd <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedd> and Diuma <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diuma>, to establish a church in their lands.[25] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Nort
1 CONC humbria>
1 CONT In 655 Bede reports that Penda invaded Bernicia at the head of a large army. Bede states that Oswiu offered "an incalculable quantity of regalia and presents as the price of peace", but that Penda refused. Oswiu vowed to give his 
1 CONC daughter Ælfflæd to the church, and to found a dozen monasteries if he was granted the victory, and assisted by Ealhfrith he engaged Penda with a small army in the Battle of the Winwæd <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_W
1 CONC inw%C3%A6d>, which took place in the region of Loidis, which is to say Leeds <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leeds>. He was successful, and Penda was killed, along with many of his allies, including King Æthelhere <http://en.wikiped
1 CONC ia.org/wiki/%C3%86thelhere_of_East_Anglia> of the East Angles. Œthelwald had assisted Penda, but stood aside from the fighting.[26] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT The Historia Brittonum gives a somewhat different account. Here, Oswiu's offer of treasure is accepted, and is associated with the siege of a place named Iudeu. It is assumed that Ecgfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecgfrith_of
1 CONC _Northumbria> was given over as a hostage, into the keeping of Penda's queen Cynewise, at this time.[27] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> The Historia suggests that many of Penda's allies were British kings, and
1 CONC  notes that Cadafael ap Cynfeddw <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadafael_ap_Cynfeddw> joined Œthelwald in avoiding the battle, so gaining the epithet <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epithet> Cadomedd (the Battle-Shirker). The decisiv
1 CONC e battle is located at "Gaius's field".[28] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Overlord of Britain
1 CONT The surprising defeat of the hitherto dominant Penda, and the death of the East Anglian king Æthelhere left Oswiu as the dominant figure in Britain. Œthelwald's ambivalent stance during the campaign which led to the Winwæd appears
1 CONC  to have led to his removal as he disappears from the record at this time. Oswiu installed his adult son Eahlfrith as king of Deirans in Œthelwald's place. Penda's son Peada was installed as king of southern Mercia, while Oswiu to
1 CONC ok the north of the kingdom. Other subject rulers seem to have been established elsewhere in Mercia.
1 CONT Further south, Æthelhere's brother Æthelwold <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86thelwold_of_East_Anglia> may have been established with Oswiu's assistance, as well as that of his kinsman by marriage King Eorcenberht of Kent <http
1 CONC ://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eorcenberht_of_Kent>. Cenwalh of Wessex <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cenwalh_of_Wessex>, who had been driven out of his lands by Penda for putting aside his marriage to Penda's sister, may also have retur
1 CONC ned to power in this period, again with Oswiu's assistance. King Sigeberht the Good <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigeberht_the_Good> of the East Saxcons was Oswiu's ally.[29] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Os
1 CONC wiu's nephew, Eanfrith's son Talorcan <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talorcan_of_the_Picts>, may have also been established as a leading king among the Picts at this time.[30] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Oswiu's total domination lasted only a short time, around three years. The proximate cause was the death of Peada, supposedly poisoned by his wife, Oswiu's daughter Eahlflæd.[31] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONC  This probably occurred at Easter <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Easter> 656, and Oswiu proceeded to install governors or subject kings in Mercia. Probably in late 659, but perhaps in 657, a revolt led by three Mercian noblemen-Imm
1 CONC in, Eata, and Eadberht-installed Penda's son Wulfhere <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wulfhere_of_Mercia> as ruler of the Mercians and drove out Oswiu's supporters.[32] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Oswiu remai
1 CONC ned a force to be reckoned with, and political settlement rather than open warfare appears to have resolved the crisis. Oswiu's kinsman Trumhere <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumhere> was named to be Wulfhere's bishop.[33] <http:
1 CONC //en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> While Wulfhere extended Mercian influence and authority in southern Britain, he apparently continued to recognise Oswiu's primacy.[34] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbri
1 CONC a>
1 CONT Welsh sources suggest that Oswiu campaigned in Wales in the late 650s, imposing tribute on the Welsh kings who had previously been Penda's allies such as Cadafael, the battle-dodging King of Gwynedd <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K
1 CONC ing_of_Gwynedd>.[35] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Elsewhere in the south, Oswiu's ally Sigeberht of the East Saxons was murdered and replaced by his brother Swithhelm <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swithelm_o
1 CONC f_Essex>, who remained a Christian, but distanced himself from Oswiu and the Irish-Northumbrian church. Switthelm was probably subject to the East Angles.[36] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT [edit <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Oswiu_of_Northumbria&action=edit&section=5>] Ecclesiastical politics
1 CONT Eahlfrith and the Synod of Whitby
1 CONT Main article: Synod of Whitby <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synod_of_Whitby>
1 CONT In 664 at the synod of Whitby <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synod_of_Whitby>,[37] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Oswiu accepted the usages of the Roman Church, which led to the departure of Bishop Colman of Li
1 CONC ndisfarne <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colman_of_Lindisfarne>. The reasons of the gathering, and its significance, have been closely studied, and the simplistic explanations offered by Bede, and by Eddius <http://en.wikipedia.org
1 CONC /wiki/Eddius>, the biographer of Wilfrid <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilfrid>, are no longer accepted.
1 CONT Bede writes that the dispute was brought to a head by Oswiu's son Eahlfrith, who had adopted Roman usages at the urging of Wilfrid.[38] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Eahlfrith had been brought up with Irish-N
1 CONC orthumbrian usages, and his rejection of these, along with the expulsion of the future saints Cuthbert of Lindisfarne <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuthbert_of_Lindisfarne> and Eata of Hexham <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eata_of_
1 CONC Hexham> from Ripon <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ripon>, is considered to have had a strong political component.[39] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> Equally, 665 would be a year when, as Bede writes, "that East
1 CONC er was kept twice in one year, so that when the King had ended Lent <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lent> and was keeping Easter, the Queen and her attendants were still fasting and keeping Palm Sunday <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/
1 CONC Palm_Sunday>".[38] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Ecgfrith
1 CONT In 660 Oswiu married his son Ecgfrith to Æthelthryth <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86thelthryth>, daughter of the former East Anglian king Anna <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_of_East_Anglia>.
1 CONT Death
1 CONT Even in his final years, Oswiu remained a major figure in Britain. The newly appointed Archbishop of Canterbury <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archbishop_of_Canterbury>, Theodore of Tarsus <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_of_
1 CONC Tarsus>, came north to meet with him in 669. Bede writes that Oswiu had intended to undertake a pilgrimage <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pilgrimage> to Rome <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rome> in the company of Bishop Wilfrid. How
1 CONC ever, he fell ill and died, aged fifty eight, on 15 February <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/February_15> 670 <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/670>.[1] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria> His elder son by Queen Eanflæd,
1 CONC  Ecgfrith <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecgfrith_of_Northumbria>, succeeded him as King of Bernicia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Bernicia>, while their younger son, Ælfwine <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86lfwine_of_De
1 CONC ira>, succeeded Ecgfrith as King of Deira <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Deira>. He was buried at Whitby Abbey <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitby_Abbey>, alongside Edwin of Deira <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwin_of_Dei
1 CONC ra>. His widow and their daughter Ælflæd were later Abbess of Whitby and were also buried there.[26] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
1 CONT Alcuin <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcuin>, writing about a century after Oswiu's death, describes him as "very just, with equitable laws, unconquered in battle but trustworthy in peace, generous in gifts to the wretched, pious,
1 CONC  equitable to all".[40] <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oswiu_of_Northumbria>
