0 @N6000000002188311769@ NOTE Anna of East Anglia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna was a mid-7th century King of East Anglia. He was the nephew of Raedwald of East Anglia, and probably the second of the sons of Eni, Raedwald's brother, to hold the kingdom, ruling (c. 636–653/654).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Family
1 CONT     * 2 Earlier life and faith
1 CONT     * 3 Emergence of Anna's rule
1 CONT     * 4 Patronage of Cenwalh of Wessex
1 CONT     * 5 Saint Hilda's visit to Anna's kingdom
1 CONT     * 6 Dynastic incorporation of Ely into East Anglia
1 CONT     * 7 Mercian assault on Cnobheresburg
1 CONT     * 8 King Anna's exile
1 CONT     * 9 Battle of Bulcamp, c 653
1 CONT     * 10 Burial place
1 CONT     * 11 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna is always referred to by this name, though it may be an abbreviated or familiar form of a diathematic name. He married before becoming king, some time before 630. His wife, whose name may have been Saewara, brought to the mar
1 CONC riage a daughter from a previous union named Saethryth. The S-alliteration of these names suggests a link with the East Saxon dynasty, a connection which had probably been established earlier through the association of Sigeberht o
1 CONC f East Anglia with the Wuffinga family. Anna had four known daughters, all canonised as saints, a process in which the family took an active part: Seaxburh of Ely (the eldest), Aethelthryth (also called Etheldreda or Audrey), Aeth
1 CONC elburga and Wihtburga, and a son whose name is preserved as Jurmin, possibly a modification of Eormen. Jurmin was of warrior age in 653. Anna himself and all of his daughters became renowned for their saintly Christian virtues.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Earlier life and faith
1 CONT
1 CONT Etheldreda's birth, in 631, is located at Exning, Suffolk, by tradition preserved at Ely (Liber Eliensis). Exning was an important place strategically, as it stood just on the East Anglian side of the Devil's Dyke, a major earthwo
1 CONC rk stretching between the Fen edge and the headwaters of the river Stour, built at an earlier date to defend the East Anglian region from attack from the direction of Cambridge or via the Icknield Way. An Anglo-Saxon cemetery at E
1 CONC xning reveals that it had distinguished occupants during the sixth century. Anna may therefore have been resident there in 631 in a defensive capacity, watching the border in case of assault from Mercia which was hostile to the ne
1 CONC wly-Christian rule of Sigeberht.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna was an extremely devout Christian. Liber Eliensis attributes the establishment of a church at Cratendune, Ely, to Saint Augustine of Canterbury (before 604). In 631 Saint Felix was just beginning his work in East Anglia, and 
1 CONC he is associated with a foundation at Soham (Cambridgeshire), then a Fen Isle lying between Exning and Ely. Anna may therefore have experienced direct Christian teaching in this locality. M.R. James also mentions an oral legend th
1 CONC at Etheldreda was baptised at Exning in a pool known as St Mindred's Well.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Emergence of Anna's rule
1 CONT
1 CONT In 632-3 Edwin of Northumbria and his centre of Christian power in Northumbria was overthrown. Edwin was slain and Northumbria ravaged by Cadwallon ap Cadfan supported by the Mercian armies, and Edwin's family and bishop narrowly 
1 CONC escaped to Kent. However King Oswald of Northumbria emerged to restore Northumbrian authority, and Saint Aidan was sent to Lindisfarne to bring the Irish mission to his court. This gave him independence both from the heathen cause
1 CONC  of Mercia and the Roman ecclesiastical authority of Canterbury in Kent. At about the same time Saint Fursey came to East Anglia from Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Mercians, led by Penda, then turned on East Anglia and slew Sigeberht and Ecgric, and routed the East Anglian army. Anna recovered East Anglian rule and must have relied upon the support of Oswald to sustain it. Felix remained
1 CONC  his bishop at Dommoc until his death in c647. Anna arranged a very important diplomatic marriage between his daughter Seaxburh and King Eorcenberht of Kent (r. 640-664), cementing an alliance between the kingdoms. During the 640s
1 CONC  Anna's daughter Aethelburga and stepdaughter Saethryth were sent to Faremoutiers Abbey in Gaul to live religious lives under abbess Fara. Probably in consequence of this a holy man named Botolph (Saint Botolph), reputedly a chapl
1 CONC ain at Faremoutiers, was granted lands in c 647 for monastic use in East Anglia, but his work was delayed by conflicts in the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Patronage of Cenwalh of Wessex
1 CONT
1 CONT In 641 Oswald was slain by Penda in battle (probably at Oswestry, Shropshire), and Oswine of Northumbria succeeded him as king. Soon afterwards King Cenwalh of Wessex, whose sister was Oswald's widow, but was himself married to th
1 CONC e sister of Penda, renounced his wife. In c644 Penda drove Cenwalh out of Wessex, and he took refuge with King Anna for three years. During that time he was converted to Christianity. This was probably through the teaching of Sain
1 CONC t Felix, who according to William of Malmesbury baptised him, presumably with King Anna as his sponsor. Then with Anna's help he returned to rule Wessex as a Christian king in 647.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Saint Hilda's visit to Anna's kingdom
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 647 Hild (Saint Hilda), a grand-niece of King Edwin's who was baptised with him in 626 and had been encouraged by Saint Aidan, came to the East Anglian court intending to join her sister Hereswith. Hereswith had married AE
1 CONC thilric, brother of Anna (possibly Ecgric of East Anglia), but now a widow she had already left for a religious life in Gaul. Hild remained in East Anglia for a year, until recalled by Aidan to Northumbria to run the monastery at 
1 CONC Hartlepool.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Dynastic incorporation of Ely into East Anglia
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna strengthened the western extent of his kingdom through the marriage of his daughter Etheldreda to Tondberht, Prince of Ely or the South Gyrwae in the southern Fen, possibly in 651 or perhaps slightly later. At Ely Etheldreda 
1 CONC had a minister named Owini, who later accompanied her to Northumbria during her second marriage. Tondberht died, and after this, Ely was always considered part of East Anglia. Thomas, a fenman, became Anna's second bishop at Dommo
1 CONC c c. 647-652. His religious education, like Anna's, may have been rooted in early foundations of Augustine or Felix in the Ely area.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Mercian assault on Cnobheresburg
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna endowed Fursey's monastery at Cnobheresburg (possibly Burgh Castle) with rich buildings and objects. In time St Fursey, growing weary of attacks on the kingdom, followed one of his brothers into a hermitage, leaving the monas
1 CONC tery at Cnobheresburg to his brother Foillan, and a year later went into Gaul. In 651 Penda struck again, attacking Foillan's monastery. Anna arrived on the scene with his force in time to hold them off while the monks escaped to 
1 CONC Nivelles in Gaul by ship, but was himself defeated and driven into exile.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] King Anna's exile
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna possibly took refuge in the area of western Shropshire, the kingdom of Merewalh of the Magonsaetan. His friendship with Merewalh's family may account for the attachment of land-revenues from sites in Shropshire to the foundat
1 CONC ion of Iken, which commenced in 654. It may also explain the early conversion of Merewalh to Christianity, at or before the time of the Northumbrian mission to Mercia. Anna returned to East Anglia in c 653. By then Bishop Thomas h
1 CONC ad died, and Berhtgisl Boniface came from Kent as his successor. Anna's daughter Wihtburga was probably born in his last years.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Battle of Bulcamp, c 653
1 CONT
1 CONT In c.653 Penda had set his son Peada as ruler of the Middle Angles, the province lying between north Oxfordshire, the river Trent to the north-west, and the Fen. Peada became Christian through his marriage to Alhflaed, daughter of
1 CONC  Oswiu of Northumbria, and a mission was sent to the Middle Angles. Penda himself did not convert, but permitted Christian teaching in his kingdom. Soon afterwards the assault of 651 was repeated: Penda with his armies met Anna at
1 CONC  Bulcamp near Blythburgh in Suffolk, and in a set battle defeated the East Anglian army and slew many of them, including Anna and his son Jurmin.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Burial place
1 CONT
1 CONT Blythburgh a mile from Bulcamp was afterwards believed to be the place of that name where the tomb of Anna and Jurmin was pointed out and venerated. The identification is likely, because Blythburgh occupies a defensible position n
1 CONC ear the fordable headwaters of the Blyth estuary flowing towards the sea at Southwold, comparable to Rendlesham as a typical site for a royal dwelling of that period. Part of an 8th century whalebone diptych used for liturgical pu
1 CONC rposes has been found near the site.
1 CONT
1 CONT Botolph began to build his monastery at Icanho (Iken, Suffolk) in the year that Anna was killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Anglo-Saxon Chronicle.
1 CONT     * Bede, Historia Ecclesiastica Gentis Anglorum, Ed. B. Colgrave and R.A.B. Mynors (Oxford 1969), iii.7,8,18,19; iv, 19.
1 CONT     * E.O. Blake (ed.), 1962, Liber Eliensis (Camden 3s, 92).
1 CONT     * M.R. James, Suffolk and Norfolk (London 1930).
1 CONT     * S. Plunkett, Suffolk in Anglo-Saxon Times (Tempus 2005).
1 CONT     * L. Webster and J. Backhouse, The Making of England. Anglo-Saxon Art and Culture AD 600-900 (London 1991)
1 CONT     * S.E. West, N.Scarfe and R.J. Cramp, 1984, 'Iken, St Botolph, and the Coming of East Anglian Christianity', Proc. Suffolk Inst of Archaeol 16.
1 CONT     * D. Whitelock, 1972, 'The Pre-Viking Age Church in East Anglia,'Anglo-Saxon England I.
1 CONT     * B. Yorke, Kings and Kingdoms of Early Anglo-Saxon England (London 1990).
1 CONT
1 CONT English royalty
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ecgric King of East Anglia
1 CONT c. 635 – c. 653 Succeeded by
1 CONT Aethelhere
0 @N6000000002188118960@ NOTE Pybba of Mercia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Pybba (570?–606/15) (also Pibba, Wibba, Wybba) was an early King of Mercia. He was the son of Creoda and father of Penda and Eowa.
1 CONT
1 CONT His dates are sometimes given in genealogies as birth in 570, the beginning of his reign in 593, and death in either 606 or 615, but with no apparent evidence; the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle just mentions him as father of Penda, with n
1 CONC o further detail.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pybba, who is said by the Historia Brittonum to have had 12 sons, is also said to have been succeeded by Cearl, of unknown relationship. His son Penda eventually became king; the Chronicle gives the date of this as 626, although B
1 CONC ede suggests it was not until after the battle of Hatfield Chase in 633.
1 CONT
1 CONT Besides Penda and Eowa (who the author of the Historia Brittonum said were the sons of Pybba who were the best known to him), Pybba also apparently had a son named Coenwalh. Every king from Penda until Ceolwulf, who was deposed in
1 CONC  823, was said to be a descendant of Pybba, either through Penda, Eowa, or Coenwalh (perhaps excluding the short-lived Beornrad, whose background is unknown).
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Creoda King of Mercia
1 CONT c. 593 – 606 or 615 Succeeded by
1 CONT Cearl
0 @N6000000003635729253@ NOTE [3456] AUREJAC.GED file, de 0593 0596 Roi de Mercie
0 @N6000000009304919458@ NOTE #Générale##Générale#Naissance : vers 545 ou plutôt vers 560
1 CONT Profession : Roi de Mercie de 593 à 596.
0 @N6000000002188118996@ NOTE Penda of Mercia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Penda (died November 15, 655[1]) was a 7th-century King of Mercia, a kingdom in what is today the English Midlands. A pagan at a time when Christianity was taking hold in many of the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms, Penda participated in the
1 CONC  defeat of the powerful Northumbrian king Edwin at the Battle of Hatfield Chase in 633.[2] Nine years later, he defeated and killed Edwin's eventual successor, Oswald, at the Battle of Maserfield; from this point he was probably t
1 CONC he most powerful of the Anglo-Saxon rulers of the time. He defeated the East Angles, drove the king of Wessex into exile for three years, and continued to wage war against the Bernicians of Northumbria. Thirteen years after Maserf
1 CONC ield, he suffered a crushing defeat and was killed at the Battle of the Winwaed in the course of a final campaign against the Bernicians.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Descent, beginning of reign, and battle with the West Saxons
1 CONT     * 2 Alliance with Cadwallon and the Battle of Hatfield Chase
1 CONT     * 3 During the reign of Oswald
1 CONT     * 4 Maserfield
1 CONT     * 5 Campaigns between Maserfield and the Winwaed
1 CONT     * 6 Relations with Bernicia; Christianity and Middle Anglia
1 CONT     * 7 Final campaign and the battle of the Winwaed
1 CONT     * 8 Aftermath and historical appraisal
1 CONT     * 9 Notes
1 CONT     * 10 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Descent, beginning of reign, and battle with the West Saxons
1 CONT
1 CONT Penda was a son of Pybba and said to be a descendant of Icel, with a lineage purportedly extending back to Woden. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle gives his descent as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Penda was Pybba's offspring, Pybba was Cryda's offspring, Cryda Cynewald's offspring, Cynewald Cnebba's offspring, Cnebba Icel's offspring, Icel Eomer's offspring, Eomer Angeltheow's offspring, Angeltheow Offa's offspring, Off
1 CONC a Wermund's offspring, Wermund Wihtlaeg's offspring, Wihtlaeg Woden's offspring.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Historia Brittonum says that Pybba had twelve sons, including Penda, but that Penda and Eowa were those best known to its author.[4] (Many of these twelve sons of Pybba may in fact merely represent later attempts to claim desc
1 CONC ent from him.[5]) Besides Eowa, apparently Penda also had a brother named Coenwalh, from whom two later kings were descended.
1 CONT
1 CONT The time at which Penda became king is uncertain, as are the circumstances. Another Mercian king, Cearl, is mentioned by Bede as ruling at the same time as the Northumbrian king Æthelfrith, in the early part of the 7th century. Wh
1 CONC ether Penda immediately succeeded Cearl is unknown, and it is also unclear whether they were related, and if so how closely; Henry of Huntingdon, writing in the 12th century, claimed that Cearl was a kinsman of Pybba.[6] It is als
1 CONC o possible that Cearl and Penda were dynastic rivals.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Penda became king in 626, ruled for thirty years, and was fifty years old at the time of his accession.[3] That he ruled for thirty years should perhaps not be taken as an exact figure,[8] s
1 CONC ince the same source says he died in 655, which would not exactly correspond to the year it gives for the beginning of his reign unless it is considering him to have died in the thirtieth year of his reign.[9] Furthermore, that Pe
1 CONC nda was truly fifty years old at the beginning of his reign has generally been considered doubtful by historians, mainly because of the ages of his children—the idea that Penda, at about eighty years of age, would have left behind
1 CONC  children who were still young (his son Wulfhere was still young three years after Penda's death, according to Bede) has been widely considered implausible.[10] The possibility has been suggested that the Chronicle actually meant 
1 CONC to say that Penda was fifty years old at the time of his death, and therefore about twenty in 626.[11]
1 CONT A map showing the general locations of the Anglo-Saxon peoples around the year 600
1 CONT A map showing the general locations of the Anglo-Saxon peoples around the year 600
1 CONT
1 CONT Bede, in his Historia ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum, says of Penda that he was "a most warlike man of the royal race of the Mercians" and that, following Edwin of Northumbria's defeat in 633 (see below), he ruled the Mercians for 
1 CONC twenty-two years with varying fortune.[12] The noted 20th century historian Frank Stenton was of the opinion that the language used by Bede "leaves no doubt that … Penda, though descended from the royal family of the Mercians, onl
1 CONC y became their king after Edwin's defeat".[13] The Historia Brittonum accords Penda a reign of only ten years,[14] perhaps dating it from the time of the Battle of Maserfield (see below) around 642, although according to the gener
1 CONC ally accepted chronology this would still be more than ten years.[9] Given the apparent problems with the dates given by the Chronicle and the Historia, Bede's account of the length of Penda's reign is generally considered the mos
1 CONC t plausible by historians. Nicholas Brooks noted that, since these three accounts of the length of Penda's reign come from three different sources, and none of them are Mercian (they are West Saxon, Northumbrian, and Welsh), they 
1 CONC may merely reflect the times at which their respective peoples first had military involvement with Penda.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT The question of whether or not Penda was already king during the late 620s assumes greater significance in light of the Chronicle's record of a battle between Penda and the West Saxons under their kings Cynegils and Cwichelm takin
1 CONC g place at Cirencester in 628.[15] If he was not yet king, then his involvement in this conflict might indicate that he was fighting as an independent warlord during this period—as Stenton put it, "a landless noble of the Mercian 
1 CONC royal house fighting for his own hand."[16] On the other hand, he might have been one of multiple rulers among the Mercians at the time, ruling only a part of their territory. The Chronicle says that after the battle, Penda and th
1 CONC e West Saxons "came to an agreement."[17] It has been speculated that this agreement marked a victory for Penda, ceding to him Cirencester and the areas along the lower River Severn.[16] These lands, to the southwest of Mercia, ha
1 CONC d apparently been taken by the West Saxons from the British in 577,[18] and the territory eventually became part of the subkingdom of the Hwicce. Given Penda's role in the area at this time and his apparent success there, it has b
1 CONC een argued that the subkingdom of the Hwicce was established by him; evidence to support this is lacking, although the subkingdom is known to have existed later in the century.[19]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Alliance with Cadwallon and the Battle of Hatfield Chase
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Battle of Hatfield Chase
1 CONT
1 CONT At some point in the late 620s or early 630s, Cadwallon ap Cadfan, the British (Welsh) king of Gwynedd, became involved in a war with Edwin of Northumbria, the most powerful king in Britain at the time. Cadwallon apparently was in
1 CONC itially unsuccessful, but he joined with Penda, who is thought to have been the lesser partner in their alliance,[20] to defeat the Northumbrians in October 633[2] at the Battle of Hatfield Chase. Penda was probably not yet king o
1 CONC f the Mercians at this time, but he is thought to have become king soon afterwards, based on Bede's characterisation of his position. Edwin was killed in the battle, and one of his sons, Eadfrith, fell into Penda's hands.[11]
1 CONT
1 CONT One manuscript of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle says that, following the victory at Hatfield Chase, Cadwallon and Penda went on to ravage "the whole land" of the Northumbrians.[21] Certainly Cadwallon continued the war, but the extent
1 CONC  of Penda's further participation is uncertain. Bede says that the pagans who had slain Edwin—presumably a reference to the Mercians under Penda, although conceivably it could be a derisive misnomer meant to refer to the Christian
1 CONC  British—burned a church and town at Campodonum,[22] although the time at which this occurred is uncertain. Penda may have withdrawn from the war at some point before the defeat and death of Cadwallon at the Battle of Heavenfield,
1 CONC  about a year after Hatfield Chase, since he was not present at this battle; furthermore, Bede makes no mention of Penda's presence in the preceding siege and battle in which Osric of Deira was defeated and killed. Penda's success
1 CONC ful participation in the battle of Hatfield Chase may have elevated his status among the Mercians and enabled him to become king, and he may have withdrawn from the war prior to Heavenfield in order to secure or consolidate his po
1 CONC sition in Mercia. Referring to Penda's successes against the West Saxons and the Northumbrians, D. P. Kirby writes of Penda's emergence in these years as "a Mercian leader whose military exploits far transcended those of his obscu
1 CONC re predecessors."[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] During the reign of Oswald
1 CONT
1 CONT Oswald of Bernicia became king of Northumbria after his victory over Cadwallon at Heavenfield.[11] Penda's status and activities during the years of Oswald's reign are obscure, and various interpretations of Penda's position durin
1 CONC g this period have been suggested. It has been presumed that Penda acknowledged Oswald's authority in some sense after Heavenfield, although Penda was probably an obstacle to Northumbrian supremacy south of the Humber.[23] It has 
1 CONC been suggested that Penda's strength during Oswald's reign could be exaggerated by the historical awareness of his later successes.[24] Kirby says that, while Oswald was as powerful as Edwin had been, "he faced a more entrenched c
1 CONC hallenge in midland and eastern England from Penda".[25] Oswald's moves toward alliance with the West Saxons, who occupied territory to the south of the Mercians, could be seen as an attempt to counter Mercian power.
1 CONT
1 CONT At some point during Oswald's reign, Penda had Edwin's son Eadfrith killed, "contrary to his oath".[11] The possibility that his killing was the result of pressure from Oswald—Eadfrith being a dynastic rival of Oswald—has been sug
1 CONC gested;[23] since the potential existed for Eadfrith to be put to use in Mercia's favour in Northumbrian power struggles while he was alive, it may not have been to Penda's advantage to have him killed.[26] On the other hand, Pend
1 CONC a may have killed Eadfrith for his own reasons. It has been suggested that Penda may have been concerned that Eadfrith could be a threat to him because Eadfrith might seek vengeance for the deaths of his father and brother;[27] it
1 CONC  is also possible that Mercian dynastic rivalry played a part in the killing, since Eadfrith was a grandson of Penda's predecessor Cearl.[7],[27]
1 CONT
1 CONT It was probably at some point during Oswald's reign that Penda fought with the East Angles and defeated them, killing their king Egric and the former king Sigebert, who had been brought out of retirement in a monastery against his
1 CONC  will in the belief that his presence would motivate the soldiers.[28] The time at which the battle occurred is uncertain; it may have been as early as 635, but there is also evidence to suggest it could not have been before 640 o
1 CONC r 641.[29] Presuming that this battle took place before the Battle of Maserfield, it may have been that such an expression of Penda's ambition and emerging power made Oswald feel that Penda had to be defeated in order for Northumb
1 CONC rian dominance of southern England to be secured or consolidated.[25]
1 CONT
1 CONT Penda's brother Eowa was also said by the Historia Brittonum[13] and the Annales Cambriae to have been a king of the Mercians at the time of Maserfield. The question of what sort of relationship of power existed between the brothe
1 CONC rs prior to the battle is a matter of speculation. Eowa may have simply been a sub-king under Penda and it is also possible that Penda and Eowa ruled jointly during the 630s and early 640s, and joint kingships were not uncommon am
1 CONC ong Anglo-Saxon kingdoms of the period. They may have ruled the southern and northern Mercians respectively.[26] That Penda ruled the southern part is a possibility suggested by his early involvement in the area of the Hwicce, to 
1 CONC the south of Mercia, as well as by the fact that, after Penda's death, his son Peada was allowed to rule southern Mercia while the northern part was placed under direct Northumbrian control—this may indicate a special hereditary c
1 CONC laim over southern Mercia by Penda's line that it did not have over the north.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another possibility was suggested by Brooks: Penda might have lost power at some point after Heavenfield, and Eowa may have actually been ruling the Mercians for at least some of the period as a subject ally or puppet of Oswald. B
1 CONC rooks cited Bede's statement implying that Penda's fortunes were mixed during his twenty-two years in power and noted the possibility that Penda's fortunes were low at this time.[30] Thus it may be that Penda was not consistently 
1 CONC the dominant figure in Mercia during the years between Hatfield and Maserfield.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Maserfield
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Battle of Maserfield
1 CONT
1 CONT On August 5, 642,[31] Penda defeated the Northumbrians at the Battle of Maserfield, which was fought near the lands of the Welsh, and Oswald was killed. Surviving Welsh poetry suggests that Penda fought in alliance with the men of
1 CONC  Powys—apparently he was consistently allied with some of the Welsh—perhaps including Cynddylan ap Cyndrwyn, of whom it was said that "when the son of Pyb desired, how ready he was", presumably meaning that he was an ally of Penda
1 CONC , the son of Pybba.[32] If the traditional identification of the battle's location with Oswestry is correct, then this would indicate that it was Oswald who had taken the offensive against Penda; it has been suggested that he was 
1 CONC acting against "a threat posed to his domination of Mercia by a hostile alliance of Penda and Powys."[33] According to Reginald of Durham's 12th century Life of Saint Oswald, Penda fled into Wales prior to the battle, at which poi
1 CONC nt Oswald felt secure and sent his army away; this explanation of events has been regarded as "plausible" but is not found in any other source, and may therefore have been Reginald's invention.[34]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Bede, Penda had Oswald's body dismembered, with his head, hands and arms being placed onto stakes [35] (this may have had a pagan religious significance[36]); Oswald thereafter came to be revered as a saint, with his 
1 CONC death in battle as a Christian king against pagans leading him to be regarded as a martyr.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eowa was killed at Maserfield along with Oswald,[13] although on which side he fought is unknown. It may well be that he fought as a dependent ally of Oswald against Penda. If Eowa was in fact dominant among the Mercians during th
1 CONC e period leading up to the battle, then his death could have marked what the author of the Historia Brittonum regarded as the beginning of Penda's ten-year reign.[10] Thus it may be that Penda prevailed not only over the Northumbr
1 CONC ians but also over his rivals among the Mercians.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Historia Brittonum may also be referring to this battle when it says that Penda first freed (separavit) the Mercians from the Northumbrians. This may be an important clue to the relationship between the Mercians and the Northu
1 CONC mbrians prior to and during Penda's time. There may have existed a "Humbrian confederacy" that included the Mercians until Penda broke free of it.[37] On the other hand, it has been considered unlikely that this was truly the firs
1 CONC t instance of their separation: it is significant that Cearl had married his daughter to Edwin during Edwin's exile, when Edwin was an enemy of the Northumbrian king Æthelfrith. It would seem that if Cearl was able to do this, he 
1 CONC was not subject to Æthelfrith;[11] thus it may be that any subject relationship only developed after the time of this marriage.[37]
1 CONT
1 CONT The battle left Penda with a degree of power unprecedented for a Mercian king—Kirby called him "without question the most powerful Mercian ruler so far to have emerged in the midlands" after Maserfield[25]—and the prestige and sta
1 CONC tus associated with defeating the powerful Oswald must have been very significant. Northumbria was greatly weakened as a consequence of the battle; the kingdom became fractured to some degree between Deira in its southern part and
1 CONC  Bernicia in the north, with the Deirans acquiring a king of their own, Oswine, while in Bernicia, Oswald was succeeded by his brother, Oswiu. Mercia thus enjoyed a greatly enhanced position of strength relative to the surrounding
1 CONC  kingdoms, and Stenton wrote that the battle left Penda as "the most formidable king in England", and observed that although "there is no evidence that he ever became, or even tried to become, the lord of all the other kings of so
1 CONC uthern England … none of them can have been his equal in reputation".[38]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Campaigns between Maserfield and the Winwaed
1 CONT
1 CONT Defeat at Maserfield must have weakened Northumbrian influence over the West Saxons, and the new West Saxon king Cenwealh—who was still pagan at this time—was married to Penda's sister. It may be surmised that this meant he was to
1 CONC  some extent within what Kirby called a "Mercian orbit".[39] However, when Cenwealh (according to Bede) "repudiated" Penda's sister in favour of another wife, Penda drove Cenwealh into exile in East Anglia in 645, where he remaine
1 CONC d for three years before regaining power.[40] Who governed the West Saxons during the years of Cenwealh's exile is unknown; Kirby considered it reasonable to conclude that whoever ruled was subject to Penda. He also suggested that
1 CONC  Cenwealh may not have been able to return to his kingdom until after Penda's death.[39]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 654,[3] the East Anglian king Anna, who had harboured the exiled Cenwealh, was killed by Penda. He was succeeded by a brother, Aethelhere; since Aethelhere was subsequently a participant in Penda's doomed invasion of Bernicia i
1 CONC n 655 (see below), it may be that Penda installed Aethelhere in power.[5] It has been suggested that Penda's wars against the East Angles "should be seen in the light of interfactional struggles within East Anglia."[41] It may als
1 CONC o be that Penda made war against the East Angles with the intention of securing Mercian dominance over the area of Middle Anglia, where Penda established his son Peada as ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the years after Maserfield, Penda also destructively waged war against Oswiu of Bernicia on his own territory. At one point prior to the death of Bishop Aidan (August 31, 651), Bede says that Penda "cruelly ravaged the country 
1 CONC of the Northumbrians far and near" and besieged the royal Bernician stronghold of Bamburgh. When the Mercians were unable to capture it—"not being able to enter it by force, or by a long siege"—Bede reports that they attempted to 
1 CONC set the city ablaze, but that it was saved by a sacred wind supposedly sent in response to a plea from the saintly Aidan: "Behold, Lord, how great mischief Penda does!" The wind is said to have blown the fire back towards the Merc
1 CONC ians, deterring them from further attempts to capture the city.[42] At another point, some years after Aidan's death, Bede records another attack: he says that Penda led an army in devastating the area where Aidan died—he "destroy
1 CONC ed all he could with fire and sword"—but that when the Mercians burned down the church where Aidan died, the post against which he was leaning at the time of his death was undamaged; this was taken to be a miracle.[43] No open bat
1 CONC tles are recorded as being fought between the two sides prior to the Winwaed in 655 (see below), however, and this may mean that Oswiu deliberately avoided battle due to a feeling of weakness relative to Penda. This feeling may ha
1 CONC ve been in religious as well as military terms: N. J. Higham wrote of Penda acquiring "a pre-eminent reputation as a god-protected, warrior–king", whose victories may have led to a belief that his pagan gods were more effective fo
1 CONC r protection in war than the Christian God.[23]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Relations with Bernicia; Christianity and Middle Anglia
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite these apparent instances of warfare, relations between Penda and Oswiu were probably not entirely hostile during this period, since Penda's daughter Cyneburh married Alhfrith, Oswiu's son, and Penda's son Peada married Alh
1 CONC flaed, Oswiu's daughter. According to Bede, who dates the events to 653, the latter marriage was made contingent upon the baptism and conversion to Christianity of Peada; Peada accepted this, and the preaching of Christianity bega
1 CONC n among the Middle Angles, whom he ruled. Bede wrote that Penda tolerated the preaching of Christianity in Mercia itself, despite his own beliefs:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Nor did King Penda obstruct the preaching of the word among his people, the Mercians, if any were willing to hear it; but, on the contrary, he hated and despised those whom he perceived not to perform the works of faith, when 
1 CONC they had once received the faith, saying, "They were contemptible and wretched who did not obey their God, in whom they believed." This was begun two years before the death of King Penda.[44]
1 CONT
1 CONT Peada's conversion and the introduction of priests into Middle Anglia could be seen as evidence of Penda's tolerance of Christianity, given the absence of evidence that he sought to interfere.[45] On the other hand, an interpretat
1 CONC ion is also possible whereby the marriage and conversion could be seen as corresponding to a successful attempt on Oswiu's part to expand Bernician influence at Penda's expense; Higham saw Peada's conversion more in terms of polit
1 CONC ical manoeuvring on both sides than religious zeal.[46]
1 CONT
1 CONT Middle Anglia as a political entity may have been created by Penda as an expression of Mercian power in the area following his victories over the East Angles. Previously there seem to have been a number of small peoples inhabiting
1 CONC  the region, and Penda's establishment of Peada as a subking there may have marked their initial union under one ruler. The districts corresponding to Shropshire and Herefordshire, along Mercia's western frontier near Wales, proba
1 CONC bly also fell under Mercian domination at this time. Here a king called Merewalh ruled over the Magonsaete; in later centuries it was said that Merewalh was a son of Penda, but this is considered uncertain. Stenton, for example, c
1 CONC onsidered it likely that Merewalh was a representative of a local dynasty that continued to rule under Mercian domination.[47]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Final campaign and the battle of the Winwaed
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Battle of the Winwaed
1 CONT
1 CONT In 655,[1] Penda invaded Bernicia with a large army, reported to have been thirty legions strong, with thirty royal or noble commanders (duces regii, as Bede called them), including rulers such as Cadafael ap Cynfeddw of Gwynedd a
1 CONC nd Aethelhere of East Anglia. Penda also enjoyed the support of Aethelwald, the king of Deira and the successor of Oswine, who had been murdered on Oswiu's orders in 651; Bede says Aethelwald acted as Penda's guide during his inva
1 CONC sion.
1 CONT
1 CONT The cause of this war is uncertain. There is a passage in Bede's Ecclesiastical History that suggests Aethelhere of East Anglia was the cause of the war, but it has been argued that an issue of punctuation in later manuscripts con
1 CONC fused Bede's meaning on this point, and that he in fact meant to refer to Penda as being responsible for the war.[48] Although, according to Bede, Penda tolerated some Christian preaching in Mercia, it has been suggested that he p
1 CONC erceived Bernician sponsorship of Christianity in Mercia and Middle Anglia as a form of "religious colonialism" that undermined his power, and that this may have provoked the war.[49] Elsewhere the possibility has been suggested t
1 CONC hat Penda sought to prevent Oswiu from reunifying Northumbria,[32] not wanting Oswiu to restore the kingdom to the power it had enjoyed under Edwin and Oswald; a perception of the conflict in terms of the political situation betwe
1 CONC en Bernicia and Deira could help to explain the role of Aethelwald of Deira in the war, since Aethelwald was the son of Oswald and might not ordinarily be expected to ally with those who had killed his father. Perhaps, as the son 
1 CONC of Oswald, he sought to obtain the Bernician kingship for himself.[49]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Historia Brittonum, Penda besieged Oswiu at Iudeu;[13] this site has been identified with Stirling, in the north of Oswiu's kingdom.[50] Oswiu tried to buy peace: in the Historia Brittonum, it is said that Oswiu o
1 CONC ffered treasure, which Penda distributed among his British allies;[13] Bede states that the offer was simply rejected by Penda, who "resolved to extirpate all of [Oswiu's] nation, from the highest to the lowest". Additionally, acc
1 CONC ording to Bede, Oswiu's son Ecgfrith was being held hostage "at the court of Queen Cynwise, in the province of the Mercians"[51]—perhaps surrendered by Oswiu as part of some negotiations or arrangement. It would seem that Penda's 
1 CONC army then moved back south, perhaps returning home,[52] but a great battle was fought near the river Winwaed (the identification of the Winwaed with a modern river is uncertain, although the River Went is a possibility) on a date 
1 CONC given by Bede as November 15. It may be that Penda's army was attacked by Oswiu at a point of strategic vulnerability, which would help explain Oswiu's victory over forces that were, according to Bede, much larger than his own.[53
1 CONC ]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Mercian force was also weakened by desertions: according to the Historia Brittonum, Cadafael of Gwynedd, "rising up in the night, escaped together with his army" (thus earning him the name Cadomedd, or "battle-shirker"),[13] a
1 CONC nd Bede says that at the time of the battle, Aethelwald of Deira withdrew and "awaited the outcome from a place of safety".[51] If Penda's army was marching home, it may have been for this reason that some of his allies were unwil
1 CONC ling to fight, according to Kirby; it may also be that the allies had different purposes in the war, and Kirby suggested that Penda's deserting allies may have been dissatisfied "with what had been achieved at Iudeu".[52] At a tim
1 CONC e when the Winwaed was swollen with heavy rains, the Mercians were badly defeated and Penda was killed, along with the East Anglian king Aethelhere. Bede says that Penda's "thirty commanders, and those who had come to his assistan
1 CONC ce were put to flight, and almost all of them slain," and that more drowned while fleeing than were killed in the actual battle. He also says that Penda's head was cut off; a connection between this and the treatment of Oswald's b
1 CONC ody at Maserfield is possible.[52] Writing in the 12th century, Henry of Huntingdon emphasised the idea that Penda was suffering the same fate as he had inflicted on others.[53]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Aftermath and historical appraisal
1 CONT
1 CONT With the defeat at the Winwaed, Oswiu came to briefly dominate Mercia, permitting Penda's son Peada to rule its southern portion. Two of Penda's other sons, Wulfhere and Æthelred, later ruled Mercia in succession after the overthr
1 CONC ow of Northumbrian control in the late 650s. The period of rule by Penda's descendants came to an end with his grandson Ceolred's death in 716, after which power passed to descendants of Eowa for most of the remainder of the 8th c
1 CONC entury.
1 CONT
1 CONT Penda's reign is significant in that it marks an emergence from the obscurity of Mercia during the time of his predecessors, both in terms of the power of the Mercians relative to the surrounding peoples and in terms of our histor
1 CONC ical awareness of them. While our understanding of Penda's reign is quite unclear, and even the very notable and decisive battles he fought are surrounded by historical confusion, for the first time a general outline of important 
1 CONC events regarding the Mercians becomes realistically possible. Furthermore, Penda was certainly of great importance to the development of the Mercian kingdom; it has been said that his reign was "crucial to the consolidation and ex
1 CONC pansion of Mercia".[24]
1 CONT
1 CONT Penda was the last great pagan warrior-king among the Anglo-Saxons. Higham wrote that "his destruction sounded the death-knell of English paganism as a political ideology and public religion."[23] After Penda's death, the Mercians
1 CONC  were converted to Christianity, and all three of Penda's reigning sons ruled as Christians. His daughters Cyneburh and Cyneswith became Christian and were saintly figures who according to some accounts retained their virginity th
1 CONC rough their marriages. There was purportedly even an infant grandson of Penda named Rumwold who lived a saintly three-day life of fervent preaching. What is known about Penda is primarily derived from the history written by the No
1 CONC rthumbrian Bede, a priest not inclined to objectively portray a pagan Mercian who engaged in fierce conflict with Christian kings, and in particular with Northumbrian rulers; indeed, Penda has been described as "the villain of Bed
1 CONC e's third book" (of the Historia Ecclesiastica).[54] From the perspective of the Christians who later wrote about Penda, the important theme that dominates their descriptions is the religious context of his wars—for instance, the 
1 CONC Historia Brittonum says that Penda prevailed at Maserfield through "diabolical agency"[14]—but Penda's greatest importance was perhaps in his opposition to the supremacy of the Northumbrians. According to Stenton, had it not been 
1 CONC for Penda's resistance, "a loosely compacted kingdom of England under Northumbrian rule would probably have been established by the middle of the seventh century."[55] In summarising Penda, he wrote the following:
1 CONT
1 CONT     He was himself a great fighting king of the kind most honoured in Germanic saga; the lord of many princes, and the leader of a vast retinue attracted to his service by his success and generosity. Many stories must have been to
1 CONC ld about his dealings with other kings, but none of them have survived; his wars can only be described from the standpoint of his enemies…[56]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
