0 @N6000000002188311710@ NOTE Sigeberht of East Anglia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigebert of East Anglia (also known as Saint Sigebert) was King of East Anglia from c 629 to c634. He was the first English king to receive a Christian baptism and education before coming into his regnal power, and the first to ab
1 CONC dicate in order to enter the monastic life. He played a most important part in the establishment of Christianity in his kingdom.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Family, Exile, Conversion and Education
1 CONT     * 2 Sigeberht's Accession
1 CONT     * 3 Foundation of the East Anglian bishopric - Saint Felix
1 CONT     * 4 Foundation of the East Anglian School
1 CONT     * 5 The Irish Mission to East Anglia - Saint Fursey
1 CONT     * 6 Sigeberht's monastery and his religious abdication
1 CONT     * 7 A Mercian attack: Sigeberht's martyrdom in battle
1 CONT     * 8 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family, Exile, Conversion and Education
1 CONT
1 CONT The most reliable source for him is The Venerable Bede's Ecclesiastical History of the English People (produced 731). Bede states that he was the son of Raedwald (r.c 599-624), but William of Malmesbury describes him as the stepso
1 CONC n. The latter is indicated because the name Sigebert is unlike any in the East Anglian Wuffinga dynasty, but closely resembles the name fashions of the East Saxon royal house. If that identification is correct, Raedwald's wife had
1 CONC  previously been the wife of an East Saxon prince or ruler. Raedwald's own principal heir was Raegenhere (a youth of warrior age in 616, when he was slain in battle) and his second heir was Eorpwald, slain by the heathen Ricberht 
1 CONC in c 627. (There is no ancient record that Ricbert was a son of Raedwald's, nor that he was a king.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Eorpwald, despite his father Raedwald's personal conversion and baptism before 616, and the existence of a Christan altar in Raedwald's temple, was not himself a convert at the time of his accession in c 624. Since it is known tha
1 CONC t Raedwald's Queen (also Sigeberht's own mother) did not become Christian, Sigeberht received limited encouragement in the Faith. He was exiled in Gaul for a long time during the lifetime of Eorpwald, 'while fleeing from the enmit
1 CONC y of Raedwald' (says Bede, iii.18). This supports the stepson theory, if Raedwald was protecting Eorpwald's succession against a possible claim by a son not of the Wuffing line. While in Gaul Sigeberht was converted and baptized, 
1 CONC and became a very Christian and a very learned man. He was strongly impressed by the religious institutions and schools for the study of reading and writing which he found in Gaul.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sigeberht's Accession
1 CONT
1 CONT After the interregnum prompted by Eorpwald's assassination, Sigebert was recalled from Gaul to become ruler of the East Angles. It is likely that he gained the kingdom by military means, because his prowess as a military commander
1 CONC  was later remembered. During his reign part of the Kingdom was governed by his kinsman Ecgric, the relationship described by the Latin term cognatus. This may mean that Ecgric was a son of Raedwald. However some authorities consi
1 CONC der Ecgric to be the same person as AEthilric, named in the East Anglian tally (in the Anglian Collection) as a son of Eni, Raedwald's brother. Whoever Ecgric was, Sigeberht had equal or senior power while he ruled, because the in
1 CONC fluence of his religious patronage was felt both in eastern and western parts of the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigebert's Christian conversion may have been a decisive factor in his achieving royal power, since at that time Edwin of Northumbria (616-632/3) was the senior English king, and only he and Eadbald of Kent were Christian rulers. 
1 CONC Eadbald certainly had contacts with the Frankish rulers. After Dagobert succeeded Clothar II in Francia in 628, Sigeberht's emergence helped to strengthen the English conversion upon which Edwin's power rested. Sigeberht is likely
1 CONC  to have encouraged the conversion of Ecgric, if he was not already Christian. Edwin's encouragement took shape in the marriage of his grand-niece Hereswith, sister of Saint Hilda, to Aethilric, Raedwald's nephew. Hereswith and Hi
1 CONC ld were under Edwin's protection and were baptised with him in 626. This marriage held the presumption that Aethilric was, or would become, Christian, and probably also that he should at some time become King of East Anglia.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Foundation of the East Anglian bishopric - Saint Felix
1 CONT
1 CONT Bede relates that the East Anglian apostle Saint Felix came to England from Burgundy as a missionary bishop, and was sent by Archbishop Honorius of Canterbury to assist Sigeberht. William of Malmesbury has the later story that Fel
1 CONC ix accompanied Sigeberht to East Anglia. In either case, this dates Sigeberht's accession to c629-630, because Felix was Bishop for 17 years, his successor Thomas for 5, and his successor Berhtgisl Boniface for 17 - and Berhtgisl 
1 CONC died in around 669. Sigebert established the bishop's seat of his kingdom for Felix at Dommoc, claimed variously for Dunwich or Walton, Felixstowe (both coastal sites in Suffolk). If at Walton (as Rochester claimed during the thir
1 CONC teenth century), the site of Dommoc may have been within the precinct of a Roman fort which formerly stood there.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Foundation of the East Anglian School
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigeberht also established a school in his kingdom for boys to be taught reading and writing in Latin, on the model that he had witnessed in Gaul. Felix assisted him by obtaining teachers of the kind who taught in Kent. Paulinus o
1 CONC f York was from 633 to 644 bishop of Rochester on the Medway, then the nearest bishopric in Kent to East Anglia. Paulinus had (according to the Whitby Life of Gregory the Great) been connected with the court of Raedwald during the
1 CONC  exile of Edwin.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Irish Mission to East Anglia - Saint Fursey
1 CONT
1 CONT The allegiance of Felix to Canterbury determined the Roman basis of the East Anglian Church, though his training in Burgundy may have been coloured by the teaching of the Irish missionary Saint Columbanus in Luxeuil. In around 633
1 CONC , perhaps shortly before Saint Aidan was sent to Lindisfarne from Iona, the Irish royal hermit and missionary Saint Fursey came from the Athlone area with his priests and brethren to East Anglia. Sigeberht granted him a monastery 
1 CONC site in an old Roman fort called Cnobheresburg, usually identified as Burgh Castle near Yarmouth. Felix and Fursey both effected many conversions and established churches in Sigebert's kingdom. Bede records that Archbishop Honoriu
1 CONC s and Bishop Felix much admired the work of Saint Aidan of Lindisfarne. Therefore it is likely that they also appreciated of Saint Fursey, whose community also lived according to the ascetic principles of Irish Christianity.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sigeberht's monastery and his religious abdication
1 CONT
1 CONT Not long afterwards Sigebert, who had built a monastery for his own use, abdicated his power to Ecgric and retired there to conduct a religious life. Bede does not say where, but later sources name it as Beodricesworth, afterwards
1 CONC  called Bury St Edmunds. If that identification is accepted, the likely site was in the original precinct of the mediaeval Abbey Bury St Edmunds, probably the 'worth' or curtilage of Beodric after whom it was originally named. The
1 CONC  site occupied a strong position on the upper reaches of the Lark valley, which drains north-west into the Great Fen through important early settlements at Icklingham, Culford, West Stow and others. This was a line of access towar
1 CONC ds Ely, where a foundation of Saint Augustine may already have existed, and towards Soham (Cambridgeshire), where Saint Felix is thought to have founded a monastery.
1 CONT
1 CONT The much later formation of West Suffolk and East Suffolk (distinct counties until 1974) reflected the topographical distinction between the eastern watershed draining towards the sea, with its power centre at Rendlesham and trade
1 CONC  capital at Ipswich, and the western rivers draining to the Fen, of which Bury became the county town.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] A Mercian attack: Sigeberht's martyrdom in battle
1 CONT
1 CONT In 636 East Anglia was attacked by the armies of Mercia, and Ecgric was obliged to defend it with a much smaller (though not negligible) force. The East Angles appealed to Sigebert to leave his monastery and lead them in battle, h
1 CONC oping that his presence and the memory of his former military exploits would encourage the army and make them less likely to flee. Sigebert refused, saying that he had renounced his worldly kingdom and now lived only for the heave
1 CONC nly kingdom. However he was dragged from the monastery to the battlefield where, unwilling to bear arms, he went into battle carrying only a staff. Sigebert, Ecgric, and many of the East Angles were slain and their army was routed
1 CONC . In this way King Sigebert of the East Angles won the palm of martyrdom. Bede (HE iii,18) indicates that the leader of the Mercian assault was Penda, and Sigebert is among the names of the kings who, according to an ancient prove
1 CONC rb, were avenged by the slaying of Penda in 654.
1 CONT
1 CONT Penda never accepted Christian teaching, and made war against the powers associated with it, seeing himself as the rightful representative of Anglian custom, rule and identity. However, the Christianity which Sigebert had done so 
1 CONC much to establish in East Anglia was strongly maintained and developed in the reign of his successor, King Anna. As a royal martyr Sigebert's feast day was observed variously on 16 January or 27 September.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bede, Ecclesiastical History, iii.18.
1 CONT     * D.H. Farmer, The Oxford Dictionary of Saints (Oxford 1978). ISBN 0-19-282038-9.
1 CONT     * D. P. Kirby, The Earliest English Kings (Unwin Hyman 1991). ISBN 0-04-445691-3.
1 CONT     * Steven Plunkett, Suffolk in Anglo-Saxon Times, 2005 (Tempus, Stroud). ISBN 0-7524-3139-0.
1 CONT     * A. Williams, A.P. Smyth and D.P. Kirkby (1991), A Biographical Dictionary of Dark Age Britain (Seaby 1991). ISBN 1-85264-047-2.
1 CONT
1 CONT English royalty
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ricberht King of East Anglia with Ecgric
1 CONT 629 – 634 Succeeded by
1 CONT Ecgric (alone)
0 @N6000000002188311693@ NOTE Eorpwald of East Anglia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eorpwald was the son of Raedwald and ruled as King of East Anglia from c 624 to c627.
1 CONT
1 CONT He received Christian teaching and sacraments (i.e. baptism and communion at least) for himself and on behalf of his kingdom or nation, and became the first English king to suffer death as a consequence of his Christian faith, tho
1 CONC ugh the motive for his assassination was probably political as well as religious. The primary source for Eorpwald is Bede's Ecclesiastical History, ii.15.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Eorpwald's inheritance
1 CONT     * 2 Inheritance of senior kingship
1 CONT     * 3 Conversion of Eorpwald
1 CONT     * 4 Death
1 CONT     * 5 Further reading
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Eorpwald's inheritance
1 CONT
1 CONT Raedwald himself was the senior brother of Eni, and his use of the letters R and E in the name-fastening of the next generation reproduced that of his own. On this principle Eorpwald would be junior to his brother Raegenhere, who 
1 CONC was slain at the Battle of the River Idle in 616, and therefore it is inferred that he was Raedwald's younger son, and became heir to Raedwald's kingdom after Raegenhere's death. In the later part of his rule, 616-624, Raedwald wa
1 CONC s not only King of the East Angles within the Wuffinga dynastic succession, but also the most powerful king amongst the rulers of the various English kingdoms, occupying the role which was later described by the term Bretwalda. At
1 CONC  his death, for which a late series of annals supplies the date 624, the regnal power of East Anglia reverted to the kingdom only, while the authority of supreme ruler lay open to whichever king might be powerful enough to seize i
1 CONC t.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Inheritance of senior kingship
1 CONT
1 CONT Raedwald had achieved his position by bringing Northumbria firmly under fealty when, following the battle of 616, he returned Edwin to his rightful position as ruler of Deira (centred at York) and made possible his dominion of Ber
1 CONC nicia, the northern Northumbrian province. While Edwin was in exile at Raedwald's court, shortly before the battle of 616, he was offered certain encouragements that he should some day become a greater king than any Englishman bef
1 CONC ore him, if he would consider accepting Christian teaching. If the account of that conversation contains truth, it signifies that Raedwald foresaw Northumbria's future power and intended that Edwin should succeed to the highest au
1 CONC thority after him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after Raedwald's death Edwin married the sister of the Christian king Eadbald of Kent. An attempt on his life was made by the West Saxons, whose ruling house had ancient links with that of Bernicia. Edwin suppressed the West 
1 CONC Saxons in a military expedition. He then accepted Christian teaching for himself and was baptised with other members of his family and court by Paulinus of York, a member of the Gregorian mission. The first conversion of Anglo-Sax
1 CONC on Northumbria was therefore within the Roman Church. York had formerly been an important bishopric of the Church in Roman Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Conversion of Eorpwald
1 CONT
1 CONT Edwin and Paulinus undertook the conversion of the Northumbrian people, and also those of Lindsey (Lincolnshire) and East Anglia. (Lindsey had been within Raedwald's secure dominion because its western frontier was the scene of th
1 CONC e Battle of the Idle just over the Lincolnshire-Nottinghamshire border, near Gainsborough). This Christian patronage helped to affirm Edwin's position as senior ruler of the English, and until his final confrontation with Cadwallo
1 CONC n ap Cadfan of Gwynedd in 632-3 he also held the British or Welsh powers under his dominion.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was at Edwin's prompting that Eorpwald, together with his kingdom, received the Christian faith and sacraments. Eorpwald was therefore not yet a Christian during his father's lifetime nor at his own accession. It is not known w
1 CONC hether his baptism took place in East Anglia, Northumbria or Kent, but it is very likely that Edwin, now a senior ruler, was his sponsor at baptism. The conversion had the political benefit of bringing the entire eastern seaboard 
1 CONC from Northumbria to Kent under the dominion of Christian rulers in alliance with Edwin, with the single exception of the East Saxons.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Not long after his conversion Eorpwald was slain (occisus) by a heathen (viro gentili) named Ricberht. The circumstances are not recorded, so that it is not known whether Ricberht represented an internal East Anglian opposition to
1 CONC  Christian rule, or if he was an emissary from an external power wishing to diminish Edwin's influence. Eorpwald shares his feast as royal saint and martyr with Edwin of Northumbria, 4 October.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bede states that after the slaying of Eorpwald the kingdom reverted to heathen rule (in errore versata est) for three years. This does not necessarily mean an overt struggle between the worship of the Anglo-Saxon gods and the wors
1 CONC hip of Christ, but could equally express a conflict in the political allegiances which Edwin's rise to power had prompted. The attribution of these three years to a supposed rule of Ricberht is a banner of convenience, though the 
1 CONC fact that his name was remembered at all (when East Anglian history of this period is dependent upon very fragmentary records) indicates that he was a person of some importance.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Further reading
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Colgrave, B. and Mynors, R.A.B. (eds) (1969) Bede's Ecclesiastical History of the English People, Oxford : Clarendon Press, ISBN 0-19-822202-5
1 CONT     * Plunkett,S. (2005) Suffolk in Anglo-Saxon Times, Stroud : Tempus Publishing, ISBN 0-7524-3139-0
1 CONT
1 CONT English royalty
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Raedwald King of East Anglia
1 CONT 625 – 627 Succeeded by
1 CONT Ricberht
0 @N6000000002188311498@ NOTE Ermenilda of Ely
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Saint Ermenilda (or Eormenhild, Ermenildis, Ermengild) (d.700 or 703) was the daughter of king Eorcenberht of Kent and saint Seaxburh of Ely. She married Wulfhere of Mercia, with whom she had a daughter, saint Werburgh, and a son,
1 CONC  Cenred.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ermenilda became a Benedictine nun after her husband died in 675, and eventually became abbess of Minster and Ely consecutively.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her feast day is 13 February.
