0 @N6000000002187891806@ NOTE Óengus II of the Picts
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Óengus (Scottish Gaelic: Óengus mac Fergusa), alternative translations: Onuist, Hungus or Angus, was king of Dál Riata and Fortriu, in modern Scotland, from about 820 until 834. Cennrígmonaid, later St Andrews, may have been found
1 CONC ed in the reign of Óengus
1 CONT
1 CONT According to a legend for which there is no historical evidence, Óengus led the Picts and Scots in battle against the Angles under King Aethelstan of East Anglia. Óengus and his men were surrounded and he prayed for deliverance. D
1 CONC uring the night, St Andrew, who was martyred on a saltire cross, appeared to Óengus and assured him of victory. The next morning, a white saltire against the background of a blue sky appeared to both sides. The Picts and Scots wer
1 CONC e heartened by this, and the Angles were overcome by fear and were defeated. The Saltire has been the national flag of Scotland ever since.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Caustantín King of Dál Riada
1 CONT and the Picts
1 CONT 820-834 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Drust 
0 @N6000000002187891779@ NOTE Óengus I of the Picts
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Óengus I of the Picts (Hypothetical Pictish form: Onuist map Uurguist[1]; O.Ir.: Óengus mac Fergusa; Anglicisation: Angus mac Fergus), was king of the Picts until 761. The reign of Óengus can be reconstructed in some considerable 
1 CONC detail from a variety of sources.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Rise to Power 
1 CONT 2 Dál Riata and Ireland 
1 CONT 3 St Andrews 
1 CONT 4 Al Clut and Northumbria 
1 CONT 5 Death 
1 CONT 6 Legacy 
1 CONT 7 Notes 
1 CONT 8 References 
1 CONT 8.1 Primary sources 
1 CONT 8.2 Secondary sources 
1 CONT 8.3 Further reading (works not consulted) 
1 CONT 9 External links 
1 CONT 10 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Early genealogies make Óengus a member of the Eóganachta of Munster.[2] The branch of the kindred from which he came were located in an area known as Circinn, associated with modern Angus and the Mearns.[3] To conform to the evide
1 CONC nce in the Irish annals, it can be assumed that Óengus was born before 700, and was thus middle-aged by the time he entered into history. His nearer kin are known to have included a son, Bridei (d. 736), and two brothers, Talorgan
1 CONC  (d. 750) and Bridei (d. 763).[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Rise to Power
1 CONT Óengus appears in 728, in the period following the deposition of king Nechtan mac Derilei by Drest son of Talorgan in 724 or 726.[5] It is certain that there were multiple kings of the Picts in this period, although whether kings 
1 CONC were as numerous as in the related Celtic cultures of Wales and Ireland is unknown. In 728-729, four kings, at least, competed for power in Pictland: Drest, who had deposed Nechtan; Nechtan, unwilling to remain in a monastery; Alp
1 CONC ín, of whom little is known; and lastly Óengus, who may originally have been a partisan of Nechtan, and perhaps his acknowledged heir.[6] Alpín's patronym is not given in the Irish Annals, nor in the earlier king lists of the Pict
1 CONC ish Chronicle. In the later and most unreliable king lists it is given as Engus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the sequence of events in the annals, four battles large enough to be reported in Ireland were fought in 728-729.[7] The first, in 728, was fought at Mónidchroibh (location uncertain, Moncrieffe near Perth is sometimes s
1 CONC uggested) between Óengus and Alpín, where Alpín was defeated. A second battle in 728, at the unidentified Castellum Credi, saw Alpín defeated again, although whether by Óengus, or by Nechtan, or his supporters, is unclear. At this
1 CONC  point it is said that Nechtan was restored to his kingship.[8] In 729 a battle between armies supporting Óengus and Nechtan was fought at Monith Carno (perhaps Cairn o' Mount, near Fettercairn) at which the supporters of Óengus w
1 CONC ere victorious. Nechtan's exactores, perhaps tax collectors, are said to have been killed. This battle was reported by Welsh chroniclers. Nechtan himself may not have been present, and lived until 732.[9] It is not at all certain 
1 CONC that Nechtan was again deposed; being loyal to the king, but the enemy of the king's bad servants, is Medieval cliché. A final battle, at Dromo Dergg Blathuug (possibly Drumderg, near Blairgowrie), between Óengus and Drest, ended 
1 CONC with Óengus triumphant and Drest dead. Following these battles, it seems likely that Óengus was the chief king of the Picts, but his claims would continue to be disputed.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 731, a battle between Talorgan son of Congus and Óengus's son Bridei is reported, which Bridei won.[10] Talorgan fled, but three years later he was handed over to Óengus by his brother, and drowned.[11] Also in 734, Talorgan so
1 CONC n of Drest was captured near Dun Ollaigh.[12] It is likely that this is the same Talorgan son of Drest, king of Atholl, who was drowned in 739 as the other Talorgan had been.[13] Whether he is the Talorgan son of Drest who was hel
1 CONC d captive by his brother (half-brother, foster-brother) Nechtan in 713 is unknown.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Dál Riata and Ireland
1 CONT In the 730s, Óengus fought with the Dál Riata. It may be that they were the weakest of his neighbours, but the annals may provide another reason.[15] In 733, it is written that Dúngal mac Selbach profaned the holy site on Tory Isl
1 CONC and and forcibly removed Bruide from it and then attacked Inis Cuirenrigi (location unknown).[16] The following year it is said that Dun Leithfinn was destroyed and Dúngal fled to Ireland to escape the power of Óengus.[17]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 736, Óengus again sent armies to Dál Riata. Dunadd was captured, Creic burned, the lands wasted and two sons of Selbach, Dúngal, who had evidently returned from Ireland, and Feredach, were bound in chains. Shortly afterwards, i
1 CONC t is said, Bridei (or Bruide) son of Óengus son of Fergus died.[18] Following this, a battle between the Dál Riata led by Muiredach, king of the Cenél Loairn, and Talorgan brother of Óengus with the men of Fortriu was a defeat for
1 CONC  Muiredach, who was killed in the pursuit.[19] If this defeat of the Dál Riata were not enough, Óengus is reported to have attacked Dál Riata once again in 741.[20]
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the earlier civil war and these attacks by Óengus, Irish annals record nothing of Dál Riata in Scotland until 768. Woolf concludes that Óengus seems to have effectively destroyed the kingdom.[21] Aed Find, who may have b
1 CONC een king in Dál Riata after this time, did little worthy of the annalists' attention.
1 CONT
1 CONT It would be in this period that Pictish armies campaigned on the coasts of Ireland and Óengus may have been allied with the upstart king Cathal mac Finguine of Cashel (d. 742), a fellow Eóganacht warlord.[22]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT St Andrews
1 CONT The story of the foundation of St Andrews, originally Cennrígmonaid is not entirely certain but the annals report the death of Tuathalán, abbot of Cinrigh Móna, in 747, making it certain that St Andrews had been founded before tha
1 CONC t date and most probably by Óengus.[23] The St Andrews Sarcophagus was executed at the command of Óengus.[24] Later generations conflated this king Óengus mac Fergusa with his 9th century namesake, who may have founded the shrine 
1 CONC to Saint Andrew, but not the site itself.[25]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Al Clut and Northumbria
1 CONT With Dál Riata no longer a threat, if it had been before, the attention of the king may have turned south, to the Kingdom of Al Clut (i.e. Dumbarton; later known as the Kingdom of Strathclyde) and Northumbria. In 740 the king of M
1 CONC ercia and Bretwalda, Aethelbald, ravaged Northumbria while the Northumbrian king Eadberht was campaigning against the Picts.[26] While it may be that this was simply a case of the aggressive Aethelbald taking advantage of Northumb
1 CONC rian weakness, there is evidence that Óengus and Aethelbald were later associated, so they may have been allied at this time.[27]
1 CONT
1 CONT A battle between the Picts and the Britons of Al Clut is recorded in 744.[28] and in 750, at about the time when Kyle was taken from Al Clut by Eadberht of Northumbria, a battle between the Britons and the Picts is reported at a p
1 CONC lace named Mocetauc, Ocky or Catonic (Mugdock, near Milngavie), where Talorgan brother of Óengus died.[29] This was a victory for the Britons, but the victory was a pyrrhic one with the loss of Kyle. Their king Teudebur map Beli i
1 CONC s said to have died that same year.[30]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 750, it is said that the sovereignty of Óengus was ebbing. This is usually taken to refer to the defeat at the hands of the Britons.[31] This may be to take a narrow view, as important events took place in England in 750–752. T
1 CONC he continuator of Bede tells that Cuthred king of the West Saxons rose up against king Aethelbald and Óengus (emphasis added).[32]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 756, by which time Cuthred of Wessex had been brought to heel by Aethelbald of Mercia, Óengus is found again campaigning with Eadberht of Northumbria against the Britons. The campaign is reported thus:[33]
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year of the Lord's incarnation 756, king Eadberht in the eighteenth year of his reign, and Unust, king of the Picts led armies to the town of Dumbarton. And hence the Britons accepted terms there, on the first day of the mo
1 CONC nth of August. But on the tenth day of the same month perished almost the whole army which he led from Govan to Newburgh. [Newburgh is near Hexham. The original Latin is clearer that it was Eadberht's army which was destroyed.][34
1 CONC ]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Death
1 CONT The Annals of Tigernach contain an uncertain entry for 752. It states that there was a battle between Picts, at Asreith in Circinn, where Bridei map Mailcon died.[35] Bridei's death is reported in 581 in the same source.[36] It is
1 CONC  unclear whether the entry is entirely corrupt, whether a different Bridei is meant, or whether the entry appears in the wrong year. This may be a sign of the waning of Óengus's authority mentioned in 750.
1 CONT
1 CONT Óengus died in 761.[37] His death is reported in the usual laconic manner by the annalists, except for the Continuation of Bede, where it is written:[38]
1 CONT
1 CONT Óengus, king of the Picts, died; who, from the beginning to the end of his reign, continued to be a blood-stained and tyrannical butcher.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Pictish Chronicle king lists have it that he was succeeded by his brother, Bridei.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT If Óengus did not meet with the approval of the Northumbrian monkish continuator of the Ecclesiastical History, it appears that he was seen in a better light by Irish poets. Clancy suggests that the following poem refers to this Ó
1 CONC engus:[39]
1 CONT
1 CONT Fó sén dgaib Oengus Albain   Good the day when Óengus took Alba,   
1 CONT Albu thulchach terthiantriathach;   hilly Alba with its strong chiefs;   
1 CONT ruc do chathrachaib costud clárach,   he brought battle to towns with boards,   
1 CONT cossach, lámach, lethansciathach   with feet and hands and with broad shields   
1 CONT
1 CONT An assessment of Óengus is problematic, not least because annalistic sources provide very little information on Scotland in the succeeding generations. His Irish links argue strongly against the idea that the Gaelicisation of east
1 CONC ern Scotland was an artefact only of the period after Cináed mac Ailpín; indeed there are good reasons for believing that this process began long before the time of Óengus.[40] It is argued that many of Pictish kings until the dea
1 CONC th of Eógan mac Fergusa in 839 were the kin of Óengus, in particular the 9th century sons of Fergus, Caustantín and Óengus.[41] At the very least, Óengus should be seen as one of the more important Pictish kings, and one whose inf
1 CONC luence was felt far beyond the borders of Pictland.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT ? Forsyth, "Evidence of a lost Pictish source", passim discusses the various forms of Óengus's name, also providing Ungus(t) as an alternative hypothetical Pictish form. The use of map in Pictish is presumed rather than attested. 
1 CONT ? Forsyth, "Evidence of a lost Pictish source", pp. 27-28. See also genealogies from Rawlinson B 502 under primary sources. A translation of this latter material is wanting. 
1 CONT ? The location of Circinn is far from certain but it is generally located as described, see Watson, Celtic Place Names, pp. 217-231. The case of Fortriu points up the dangers of relying over much on such identifications. Broun, "T
1 CONC he Seven Kingdoms", gives an alternative view. 
1 CONT ? Broun, "Pictish Kings 761-839", pp. 80-82, discusses the descendants of Óengus and his brothers. 
1 CONT ? Annals of Tigernach (AT), sub anno 726; Annals of Ulster (AU), s.a. 726. Smyth, Warlords and Holy Men, pp. 73-75, discusses the reigns of Nechtan and Óengus. He supposes Drest may have been the nephew of Nechtan. Clancy's "Philo
1 CONC sopher King" may shed more light on Nechtan and his rivals. 
1 CONT ? AT, s.a. 724; Annals of Clonmacnoise (AClon) s.a. 722 (for 725); Smyth, Warlords and Holy Men, pp. 73-75, where it is proposed that Alpín may have been a brother of Drest; Henderson, "Primus inter Pares", pp. 155-156. 
1 CONT ? The battles are reported in AT s.a. 728; AU s.a. 728; AT s.a. 729; AU s.a. 729; and Annales Cambriae (AC) s.a. 728. 
1 CONT ? Nechtan's restoration is detailed in AClon s.a. 725 (for 728). 
1 CONT ? AT s.a. 732. 
1 CONT ? AT s.a. 731; AU s.a. 731. 
1 CONT ? AT s.a. 734; AU s.a. 734; also AClon s.a. 731 (for 734). 
1 CONT ? AU s.a. 734. 
1 CONT ? AT s.a. 739; AU s.a. 739; see Bruford, "What happened to the Caledonians ?", for a discussion of Atholl. 
1 CONT ? AT s.a. 713; AU s.a. 713. Cummins, "The Age of the Picts", pp. 96-103 argues that the Talorgan of 713, 734 and 739 is the same person. 
1 CONT ? AU s.a. 730. 
1 CONT ? AT s.a. 733; AU s.a. 733; Smyth, Warlords and Holy Men, pp. 80–81. 
1 CONT ? AU s.a. 734. 
1 CONT ? AT s.a. 736; AU s.a. 736; also AClon s.a. 733 (for 736) where a quite different Bridei is reported to have died; see also Bannerman, "The Scottish Takeover of Pictland", where it is proposed that this Feradach was the father of 
1 CONC the later Pictish king Ciniod map Uuredech. 
1 CONT ? AU s.a. 736; Muiredach is said to have reigned three years and came to the throne in 733 (AU s.a. 733) putting his death at about this time. 
