0 @N6000000002188123104@ NOTE Angeltheow
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Angeltheow, also spelled Angletheow, Engengenthe, or Angenwit (? - c. 477) was the great-great-great-grandfather of Creoda of Mercia and the son of Offa of Angel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Angeltheow's son was Eomer. According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Angeltheow is a fourth-generation descendant of Woden, the same generation as Hengest and Horsa.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is claimed by some[1] that Angeltheow may also be the Swedish king Ongentheow (as mentioned in Beowulf) killed at the Battle of Ravenswood[2], although the battle is said to have taken place some time after the former is report
1 CONC ed to have died.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ 'After the Flood' by Bill Cooper - a Christian Right explanation of post-Genesis historical genealogies
1 CONT    2. ^ Danish site - Beowulf in Cyberspace
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External link
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Angeltheow's immediate family
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Offa of Angel Legendary king of the Angles Succeeded by
0 @N6000000002188118808@ NOTE Beornwulf of Mercia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Beornwulf (killed in 825) was King of Mercia from 823 to 825. His short reign saw the collapse of Mercia's dominant position among the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms of the Heptarchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beornwulf's ancestry is practically unknown. His father was a Mercian nobleman, ealdorman Beorhtweald (died 796). A man named Beornwulf is mentioned as having witnessed a charter of King Coenwulf in 812 and another of King Ceolwul
1 CONC f in 823, but his position on each of these charters suggests he was not of an exceptionally high rank.
1 CONT
1 CONT Beornwulf overthrew Ceolwulf I in 823. Soon after he rebuilt the Abbey of St. Peter (later Gloucester Cathedral). He presided over two synods at Clofesho (an unknown location believed to be near London) with archbishop Wulfred of 
1 CONC Canterbury, in 824 and 825. In 825, he was badly defeated by the King of Wessex, Ecgbert, in battle at Ellandun, fought at Wroughton near Surrey. Ecgbert's son Æðelwulf subsequently captured Kent and drove out its pro-Mercian king
1 CONC , Baldred.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the wake of these events, Mercia's dominance of southern England rapidly unravelled. Essex and Sussex switched their loyalty to Egbert. The East Angles, led by Athelstan and backed by the West Saxons, exploited the moment of we
1 CONC akness by rebelling against Mercian rule, and Beornwulf was killed attempting to crush the revolt. One extant charter of Beornwulf refers to his third regnal year, 825. Two of his sons ruled Mercia after his death, Wiglaf from 827
1 CONC  to 829 and from 830 to 840 and Beorhtwulf from 840 to 852.
1 CONT
1 CONT A silver penny coinage of Beornwulf was struck during his reign. These coins are very rare indeed today, with only around twenty five examples known to exist.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ceolwulf I King of East Anglia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Aethelstan
1 CONT King of Mercia Ludeca
0 @N6000000002188118841@ NOTE Coenwulf of Mercia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Coenwulf (or Cenwulf) (died 821) was King of Mercia from 796 to 821.
1 CONT
1 CONT Coenwulf became king after the death of Ecgfrith, in December 796. Coenwulf was a descendant of Cenwalh of Wessex (d. 674), who had married and repudiated a sister of the kings Penda and Eowa 150 years earlier; thus Coenwulf was a
1 CONC  member of a the Mercian royal line through female descent, which may demonstrate the thoroughness of Offa's purge of the Mercian royal house.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Kingdom of Kent, which had been ruled as a Mercian territory since 785, rose in rebellion in 796. Coenwulf won the Church's backing for a reconquest of Kent, in large part due to its dissatisfaction with the exile of the pro-M
1 CONC ercian Archbishop of Canterbury, Aethelheard, who fled in the face of the rebellion. In 798, Coenwulf invaded Kent, deposed and captured the rebel king Eadberht III Præn, and made his own brother Cuðred king of Kent. He ruled unti
1 CONC l the time of his death in 807. Coenwulf subsequently took control of Kent in name as well as fact and is styled King of the Mercians and the Province of Kent (rex Merciorum atque provincie Cancie) in a charter dated 809 [1].
1 CONT
1 CONT Eadbert Praen was imprisoned at Winchcombe, a religious house closely affiliated with Cenwulf's family which housed the royal archives and was presided over by Cenwulf's daughter Cwoenthryth. Royal control over religious houses in
1 CONC  Kent - specifically Reculver and Minster - resulted in a long dispute with Wulfred, Archbishop of Canterbury from 805 until his death in 832. Both parties sought the support of foreign powers including the emperor Louis the Pious
1 CONC  and the Pope: papal privileges confirming Cenwulf's monastic possessions were obtained, whilst a number of forgeries in favour of episcopal control over monasteries were produced at Canterbury. The issue of lay control over monas
1 CONC teries had been festering for some years, but firm statements against the practice came at synods held at Clofesho in 803 and particularly that at Chelsea in 816. Following the latter synod Wulfred was suspended from office for se
1 CONC veral years. The document written up in 825 recording the history of the debate from the - then victorious - Canterbury viewpoint claims six years, though other evidence contradicts this; four is perhaps more likely. Under Cenwulf
1 CONC  at least, the debate was concluded in the king's favour around 820 when Wulfred was summoned to a meeting in London and agreed to cede a very large and valuable estate to Cenwulf and pay him a considerable sum in gold in exchange
1 CONC  for gaining lordship over the two contested monasteries. Charters from subsequent years show Wulfred had some difficulty enforcing this control, and the king's power over these monasteries remained strong.
1 CONT
1 CONT East Anglia under Cenwulf is considerably murkier. As in Kent, there was an uprising on the death of Offa in 796, who had probably ruled East Anglia directly since 794. Eadwald became king as a result of this rebellion. However, C
1 CONC oenwulf regained control of East Anglia by the early 800s, as can be discerned from the coinage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Throughout his reign, Coenwulf waged war against the Welsh of Powys and Gwynedd; in 798, the ruler of Gwynedd, Caradog ap Meirion was killed, probably in battle against the Mercians. Further campaigns against the Welsh are recorde
1 CONC d in 816 and 818, and Geoffrey Gaimar records that Cenwulf died at Clent near the Welsh border, quite probably making preparations for a campaign that took place under his brother Ceolwulf the following year.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 799, Coenwulf entered into a peace treaty with the West Saxons under Beorhtric, who had been installed as King of Wessex by Offa in 786; Beorhtric remained friendly to Mercian interests until his death in 802, when the less sub
1 CONC missive Egbert became king. Coenwulf may have instigated the failed raid into Wessex of Ælthelmund, earl of the Hwicce, upon the accession of Egbert. However, the major conflict that established the supremacy of Wessex at the expe
1 CONC nse of Mercia would not occur until the 820s, after Coenwulf's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 801, following Cenwulf's harbouring of King Eardwulf of Northumbria's enemies (such as Saint Alcmund of Derby), Mercia was invaded by Eardwulf, but peace was soon made between the two sides.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was during Coenwulf's reign that the archbishopric of Lichfield was abolished, probably before 803, as the Hygeberht who signed as an abbot at the council of Cloveshoe in that year was the former archbishop.
1 CONT
1 CONT Coenwulf died in 821, and was succeeded by his brother Ceolwulf.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Coinage
1 CONT Cross-and-wedges penny of Coenwulf, Canterbury, Beornferth
1 CONT Cross-and-wedges penny of Coenwulf, Canterbury, Beornferth
1 CONT
1 CONT The coinage of Cenwulf follows the broad silver penny format established under Offa and his contemporaries. His very first coins are very similar to the heavy coinage of Offa's last three years, and since the mints at Canterbury a
1 CONC nd in East Anglia were under the control of Eadbert Praen and Eadwald respectively, these earliest pennies must be the product of the London mint. Before 798 the new tribrach type was introduced, initially at London alone but soon
1 CONC  spreading to Canterbury after it was reconquered from the rebels. It was not struck in East Anglia, but there are tribrach pennies in the name of Cuthred, sub-king of Kent. Around 805 a new portrait coinage was introduced to Cant
1 CONC erbury and London. At London a wide variety of styles and reverse designs were used, many of relatively poor artistic standard. The issues of Canterbury, however, were numerous and well-struck, especially in the initial cross and 
1 CONC wedges coinage of Cuthred and Cenwulf and in the earliest portrait pennies of Archbishop Wulfred (the first archiepiscopal pennies to carry a portrait). After around 810 the quality of the portrait declined slightly, and a more di
1 CONC verse range of reverse designs was introduced, though several were common to many or all of the moneyers.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most dramatic development in the coinage of Cenwulf came with the discovery in 2001 of a gold coin bearing the name Coenwulf at Biggleswade in Bedfordshire, England, on a footpath beside the River Ivel. The 4.25g mancus (worth
1 CONC  about 30 days' pay for a skilled worker) is only the eighth known Anglo-Saxon gold coin with a meaningful legend from after about 650, and is also the only one to bear any close relation to the contemporary silver coinage or to e
1 CONC xplicitly name its mint; in this case London, though the exact significance of the reverse legend DE VICO LVNDONIAE is debatable. Initially sold to American collector Allan Davisson for £230,000 at an auction held by Spink auction
1 CONC  house in October of that year, the British Government subsequently put in place an export ban in the hope of saving it for the British public. In February 2006 the coin was bought by the British Museum for £357,832 with the help 
1 CONC of funding from the National Heritage Memorial Fund making it the most expensive British coin purchased until then (though exceeded the following July by the third-known example of a Double Leopard).[2] It is now on display in the
1 CONC  museum's money gallery.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * C. E. Blunt, C. S. S. Lyon and B. H. Stewart, 'The coinage of southern England, 796-840', British Numismatic Journal 32 (1963), 1-74
1 CONT     * N. Brooks, The Early History of the Church of Canterbury (Leicester, 1984), chs. 6-9
1 CONT     * J. J. Earle and Charles Plummer's edition of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, 796, 819 (Oxford, 1892)
1 CONT     * W. de G. Birch, Cartularium Saxonicum, 378 (London, 1885—1893)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Eadwald King of East Anglia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ceolwulf I
1 CONT Cuðred King of Kent
1 CONT Ecgfrith King of Mercia
