0 @N6000000003949663050@ NOTE Sought help from King Alfred the Great and united with him against the Viking threat.   Ruled 885-893.  #EC20 (Ashley's Kings & Queens of England)
1 CONT Hyfaidd was the son of Princess Tangwystl of Dyfed by a lowborn Welshman named Bledrig. He was known as something of a tyrant who often plundered the Cathedral of Mynyw (St.Davids) and expelled the monks. However, Hyfaidd had his 
1 CONC own oppressor in the person of King Anarawd of Gwynedd and his brothers. In order to stop Anarawd's conquests at the Dyfed border, Hyfaidd submitted to the overlordship of King Alfred the Great of England in the 880s, as described
1 CONC  by Asser, the latter's biographer. He died in 893
0 @N6000000002577022236@ NOTE This person has a birthdate that cannot be procesed by TempleReady. "To submit this name for temple work please send your information, includiing your relationship, to the Family History Department, ATTENTION: Medieval Families Un
1 CONC it, 50 East North Temple, Salt Lake City, Utah, 84150."
0 @N6000000003949916744@ NOTE king of Demetia (Dyfed) (a good king, according to Gildas) [HG.2:  "Aircol map Triphun"; ED] [Note:  His reign as king of Dyfed is confirmed by the contemporary testimony of Gildas, who, although he does not provide his name, call
1 CONC s his son Uortiporius the bad son of a good king.  His name comes from the later sources, of which HG is the earliest.  His chronology is very uncertain, due to the uncertain timeframe of Gildas, but the late fifth century would b
1 CONC e a reasonable estimate.  Agricola was of Irish descent, a member of the tribe known as the Déisi, a segment of which moved from Ireland to Wales at an uncertain date, and eventually became rulers of Dyfed.  His claimed father Try
1 CONC ffin (Triphun), if accurately remembered, is nothing more than a name, and there are significant disagreements in the genealogy prior to Tryffin.  If it can be accepted that they hide a grain of truth, it is at least arguable that
1 CONC  Agricola's grandfather was a man who was nicknamed "Briscus" (Irish "Brosc", Welsh "Vreisc").  See DGD and DD for detailed discussions.  It should be noted that the larger generation number for Agricola and his immediate descenda
1 CONC nts is due to the fact that the dynasty of Dyfed appears to have had a somewhat smaller average number of years per generation that the other lines followed (but not enough smaller to cause suspicion), and that Agricola was probab
1 CONC ly a rough contemporary of the Irish men who appear in generations 22 or 23.] (See Mike Ashley, "KIngs & Queens" p136)
1 CONT The son and heir of  King Triffyn Farfog, Aergol Lawhir or Long-Handed held court at Lis Castell (Lydstep) near Din Bych (Tenby). He may possibly have had a second residence at Castell Argoel in Dyfed. Aergol was supposedly a grea
1 CONC t benefactor of the church (later the Cathedral) at Llandaff in the late 5th century; certainly, he was patron of the great Bishops of Glywysing, SS. Teilo and Oudoceus. The latter's father,  King Budic II of Brittany, had found r
1 CONC efuge at Aergol's Court when expelled from his own country. While the former brought order to Aergol's boisterous Royal hall by sending his followers, Llywel and Fidelis, as a calming influence. At some point, Aergol was in confli
1 CONC ct with King Cynan Garwyn of Powys. Their two armies clashed at Crug Dyfed, but the outcome is not recorded. Aergol is remembered by the critical Gildas  as a "good king".
0 @N6000000007329594049@ NOTE [Kopi av ROYALS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign:  878-889
