0 @N6000000002188314052@ NOTE Ecgbert, Archbishop of York
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Ecgbert
1 CONT Title Archbishop of York
1 CONT Consecrated about 734
1 CONT Ended November 19, 766
1 CONT Predecessor Wilfrid II
1 CONT Successor Ethelbert
1 CONT Born unknown
1 CONT Died November 19, 766
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgbert or Ecgberht or Ecgbeorht (died 766) was an eighth century Archbishop of York and correspondent of Bede and Saint Boniface.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Life
1 CONT     * 2 Notes
1 CONT     * 3 References
1 CONT     * 4 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Eata, who was descended from the founder of the kingdom of Bernicia. His brother Eadberht was king of Northumbria from 737 to 758. Ecgbert went to Rome with another brother, and was ordained deacon while still in
1 CONC  Rome.[1] Ecgbert was made bishop of York in 734[2] by his cousin and fellow-Leodwalding Ceolwulf of Northumbria, succeeding Wilfrid II on the latter's resignation. The pallium was sent him in 735 by Pope Gregory III[1] and he bec
1 CONC ame the first northern archbishop after Paulinus of York.[3] Alcuin as a child was given to Ecgbert, and was educated at the school at York that Ecgbert founded.[4][1] Liudger, later the first bishop of Munster, and Aluberht, anot
1 CONC her bishop in Germany, both studied at the school in York.[5] He was the recipient of a famous letter of Bede, dealing with the evils arising from spurious monasteries as well as the problems of large dioceses. Bede urged Ecgbert 
1 CONC to study Gregory the Great's Pastoral Care.[1] Bede's admonition to divide up dioceses, however, fell on deaf ears, as Egbert did not break up his large diocese.[6] The problem of the spurious monasteries came from the secular pra
1 CONC ctice of families setting up monasteries that were totally under their control as a way of making the family lands book-land or land free from secular service. Book-land was exclusively a right of ecclesiastical property, and by t
1 CONC ransfering land to a family controlled monastery, the family would retain the use of the land without having to perform any services to the king for the land.[7] Ecgbert himself wrote a Dialogus ecclesiasticae institutionis, a Pen
1 CONC itentiale and a Pontificale, although the Penitentiale may have had many later additions. The Dialogus was basically a legal law code for the clergy, setting forth the proper procedures for many clerical and eccleisastical issues 
1 CONC including weregild for clerics, entrance to clerical orders, deposition from the clergy, criminal monks, clerics in court, and other matters.[1] It details a code of conduct for the clergy and how the clergy was to behave in socie
1 CONC ty.[8] He was a correspondent of Saint Boniface, who asked him to support his censure of Ethelbald of Mercia. Boniface also asked the archbishop for some of Bede's books, and in return sent wine to be drunk "in a merry day with th
1 CONC e brethern."[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgbert died on November 19, 766.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ a b c d e Mayr-Harting "Ecgberht (d. 766)" Oxford Dictionary of National Biography Online Edition accessed November 9, 2007
1 CONT    2. ^ a b Fryde Handbook of British Chronology p. 224
1 CONT    3. ^ Ashley Mammoth Book of British Kings & Queens p. 288-290
1 CONT    4. ^ Hindley A Brief History of the Anglo-Saxons p. 85
1 CONT    5. ^ Stenton Anglo Saxon England 3rd ed. p. 175
1 CONT    6. ^ Mayr-Harting Coming of Christianity p. 241-243
1 CONT    7. ^ Mayr-Harting Coming of Christianity p. 252-253
1 CONT    8. ^ Mayr-Harting Coming of Christianity p. 251-252
1 CONT    9. ^ quoted in Hindley A Brief History of the Anglo-Saxons p. 143
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
