0 @N6000000003949767835@ NOTE Prince Judicael survived the persecutions of the reign of his brother, Prince Haeloc of Domnonée, and was brought out of hiding in a monastery on the latter’s death. He set up his palace in the Forest of Paimpont. It is recorded t
1 CONC hat:
1 CONT "Terror of his name alone was sufficient to keep evil men from violence, for God, who watched over him without ceasing, had made him brave amd mighty in battle; it happened more than once that with the aid of the Almighty he was a
1 CONC ble to put whole troops of the enemy to flight by the strength of his sword-arm alone"
1 CONT He defeated the Franks twice in battle and, despite the Breton Prince's reputation, in AD 635 King Dagobert of the Franks, sent St. Eligius to demand his submission if he wanted to avoid full scale invasion. Judicael was a practic
1 CONC al man, and quickly travelled to Dagobert's Court ion Paris to recognise him as his overlord. Thus a lasting peace was concluded between the two. He was a pious King who founded the Abbey of Paimpont and helped endow the monastery
1 CONC  of St. Meven at Gaël to where, upon his abdication, he retired.  The principality was left in the hands of his brother, Judoc.
