0 @N6000000003949767948@ NOTE He was presumably the son of Aed, altho it is with him that the names of the rulers shift from Irish to a Romanized Celtic.  Tradition states that he married the daughter of Clotri, a British Lord of Dyfed.   descended from Magnus
1 CONC  Maximus.  Altho the Roman Empire had abandoned Britain by the 470's, attempts to maintain the Roman culture continued, particularly in Gloucester and woutheast Wales.  It is possible that this influence reached into Demetia and t
1 CONC hat one branch of the ruling party became strongly Romanized and found themselves as senior officials in the army of Ambrosius Aurelianus, and subsequentially Arthur.  It is recorded that Cunedda drove the Irish out of north Wales
1 CONC  .  It is likely that at this time Demetia was split and Dyfed was formed on the southwest peninsula.  (Mike Ashley "Kings & Queens", p 135)
1 CONT Triffyn mac Aed Brosc was apparently the first King of Dyfed from the House of Eochaid Allmuir. This leading family from the tribe of the Déssi had crossed the Irish Sea three generations earlier to settle in West Wales (See Aed B
1 CONC rosc's Ancestry). Triffyn the Bearded appears to have married Gwledyr, the heiress of the British Kings of Dyfed, in the mid-5th century, though she was probably somewhat younger than him. Together, they inherited the kingdom. His
1 CONC  name, "Tribune", may indicate that his family was brought over by the Roman administration to help protect the area against invading fellow Hibernians.
0 @N6000000003949905888@ NOTE Probabley the son of Rhys ap Arthfael.  Early in his reign there was a dispute over accession with Gwallwn ap Ceidrich, whom Hywel later slew.  For this act he was excommunicated and forced to do penance by the Bishop of Llandaff.
1 CONC   He was one of the kings that sought help from Alfred the Great due to oppression from Aethelred of Mercia.  His date of death is uncertain but there is a record of a death in Rome in 884 or 885 by this name. (per British Kings &
1 CONC  Queens, Mike Ashley, pg 128)
