0 @N6000000002187551358@ NOTE Osberht of Northumbria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Osberht (died 21 March 867) was king of Northumbria in the middle of the 9th century. Sources on Northumbrian history in this period are limited. Osberht's descent is not known and the dating of his reign is problematic.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Chronicles
1 CONT     * 2 Sagas
1 CONT     * 3 Notes
1 CONT     * 4 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Chronicles
1 CONT
1 CONT Osberht became king after Æthelred son of Eanred was murdered. The date of Æthelred's death is not certain, but is generally placed in 848.[1] However, Symeon of Durham writes that "Ethelred the son of Eanred reigned nine years. W
1 CONC hen he was slain Osbryht held the kingdom for thirteen years" and states that 854 was "the fifth year of the rule of Osbert, the successor of Ethelred, who had been put to death".[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Little is known of Osberht's reign. Symeon states that "Osbert had dared with sacrilegious hand to wrest from that church Wercewurde and Tillemuthe"[3] The Historia de Sancto Cuthberto dates the seizure of these lands to the year 
1 CONC before Osberht's death.[4] Osberht was replaced as king by Ælla. While Ælla is described in most sources as a tyrant, and not a rightful king, one source states that he was Osberht's brother.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Great Heathen Army marched on Northumbria in the late summer of 866, seizing York on 21 November 866.[6] Symeon of Durham, the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Asser, and Æthelweard all recount substantially the same version of events i
1 CONC n varying detail. Symeon's Historia Regum Anglorum gives this account of the battle on 21 March 867 where Osberht and Ælla met their deaths at the hands of the Vikings:[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT     In those days, the nation of the Northumbrians had violently expelled from the kingdom the rightful king of their nation, Osbryht by name, and had placed at the head of the kingdom a certain tyrant, named Alla. When the pagans
1 CONC  came upon the kingdom, the dissension was allayed by divine counsel and the aid of the nobles. King Osbryht and Alla, having united their forces and formed an army, came to the city of York; on their approach the multitude of the
1 CONC  shipmen immediately took flight. The Christians, perceiving their flight and terror, found that they themselves were the stronger party. They fought upon each side with much ferocity, and both kings fell. The rest who escaped mad
1 CONC e peace with the Danes.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT After this, the Vikings appointed one Ecgberht to rule Northumbria.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sagas
1 CONT
1 CONT Ragnarssona þáttr (The Tale of Ragnar's sons) adds a great deal of colour to accounts of the Viking conquest of York. This associates the semi-legendary king of Sweden Ragnar Lodbrok and his sons, Hvitserk, Björn Ironside, Sigurd 
1 CONC Snake-in-the-Eye, Ivar the Boneless, and Ubbe. According to the stories, Ragnar was killed by Ælla, and the army which seized York in 866 was led by Ragnar's sons who avenged his death by subjecting Ælla to the blood eagle.[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT Earlier English sources record that Ælla and Osberht died in battle, the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle stating that "both the kings were slain on the spot"[11] The main figure in the revenge tales in Ivar, who is sometimes associated with
1 CONC  the Viking leader Ímar, brother of Amlaíb Conung, found in the Irish annals. Dorothy Whitelock notes that "it is by no means certain that he should be identified with the son of Ragnar, for the name is not uncommon".[12] The Angl
1 CONC o-Saxon Chronicle does not name the leaders in Northumbria, but it does state that "Hingwar and Hubba" slew King Edmund of East Anglia (Saint Edmund) some years later.[13] Hubba is named as a leader of the army in Northumbria by A
1 CONC bbo of Fleury, and by the Historia Historia de Sancto Cuthberto. Symeon of Durham lists the leaders of the Viking army as "Halfdene, Inguar, Hubba, Beicsecg, Guthrun, Oscytell, Amund, Sidroc and another duke of the same name, Osbe
1 CONC rn, Frana, and Harold."[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT Norman historian Geoffrey Gaimar and Geoffrey of Wells both associate an Englishman named "Bern" or "Buern" with bringing the Danes to England, in Gaimar's case to Northumbria, in Geoffrey of Wells' mid-twelfth-century hagiography
1 CONC  of Saint Edmund, to East Anglia. Gaimar's account has "Buern" seeking revenge for Osberht's rape of his wife.[15]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ However, D.P. Kirby suggests that it may have been as late as 853; Kirby, p. 196.
1 CONT    2. ^ Symeon of Durham, pp. 653 & 761. Kirby, pp. 196–197, notes the dating problems: if Osberht reigned thirteen years, he was either deposed in around 862, or did not become king until c. 853. Kirby suggests that the discrepan
1 CONC cies are due to confusion caused by the omission of Eardwulf's second reign from regnal lists.
1 CONT    3. ^ Symeon of Durham, p. 654.
1 CONT    4. ^ Kirby, p. 196.
1 CONT    5. ^ Kirby, p. 197.
1 CONT    6. ^ Higham, pp. 178–179; ASC s.a. 867.
1 CONT    7. ^ Dated by Symeon of Durham, p. 654.
1 CONT    8. ^ Symeon of Durham, p. 470.
1 CONT    9. ^ Higham, p.179.
1 CONT   10. ^ Whitelock, p. 225ff.
1 CONT   11. ^ ASC, s.a. 867.
1 CONT   12. ^ Whitelock, p. 227.
1 CONT   13. ^ ASC, s.a. 870.
1 CONT   14. ^ Symeon of Durham, p. 654. Whitelock, p. 227, discusses the leaders of the Great Army in various sources.
1 CONT   15. ^ Whitelock, pp. 228–230; Gaimar, pp. 760–763.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Osberht at the Prosopography of Anglo-Saxon England.
1 CONT     * Geoffrey Gaimar; J. Stevenson translator (1854). The History of the English according to the translation of Master Geoffrey Gaimar. Church Historians of England, volume II, part II. Seeley's. Retrieved on 2007-01-27.
1 CONT     * Kirby, D.P., The Earliest English Kings. London: Unwin, 1991. ISBN 0-04-445692-1
1 CONT     * Higham, N.J., The Kingdom of Northumbria AD 350-1100. Stroud: Sutton, 1993. ISBN 0-86299-730-5
1 CONT     * Symeon of Durham; J. Stevenson translator (1855). The Historical Works of Simeon of Durham. Church Historians of England, volume III, part II. Seeley's. Retrieved on 2007-01-27.
