0 @N6000000002188311424@ NOTE Oswulf of Northumbria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Oswulf was king of Northumbria from 758 to 759. He succeeded his father Eadberht, who had abdicated and joined the monastery at York. Oswulf's uncle was Ecgbert, Archbishop of York.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spite of his father's long reign, and his powerful uncle, Oswulf did not hold the throne for long. He was murdered within a year of coming to power, by members of his familia, that is by his servants or bodyguards, at Market We
1 CONC ighton, on 24th July 759.
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of Oswulf's brother, Oswine, is recorded at "Eldunum near Mailros" in August 761, in battle against Æthelwald Moll, who had seized the throne on Oswulf's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Further reading
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Higham, N.J., The Kingdom of Northumbria AD 350-1100. Stroud: Sutton, 1993. ISBN 0-86299-730-5
1 CONT     * Marsden, J., Northanhymbre Saga: The History of the Anglo-Saxon Kings of Northumbria. London: Cathie, 1992. ISBN 1-85626-055-0
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * List of monarchs of Northumbria
1 CONT
