1 CONC  the new king, Edmund having been murdered in 946 and succeeded by his brother Edred. Eric Bloodaxe took York in 948, before being driven out by Edred, and when Amlaíb Cuaran again took York in 949–950, Máel Coluim raided Northumb
1 CONC ria as far south as the Tees taking "a multitude of people and many herds of cattle" according to the Chronicle.[4] The Annals of Ulster for 952 report a battle between "the men of Alba and the Britons [of Strathclyde] and the Eng
1 CONC lish" against the foreigners, i.e. the Northmen or the Norse-Gaels. This battle is not reported by the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, and it is unclear whether it should be related to the expulsion of Amlaíb Cuaran from York or the return
