0 @N6000000003219704772@ NOTE (Research):Æthelflæd (daughter of Alfred 'the Great') apparently wielded considerable power in Mercia even before the death of her husband, Ealdorman Æthelred, who was in poor health towards the end of his life. According to an Ir
1 CONC ish source, the 'Three Fragments', it was her who had to defend Chester against a Hiberno-Norse Viking, Ingimund, who had settled in the Wirral peninsula. The so called 'Mercian Register' (found inserted into Manuscripts B and C o
1 CONC f the 'Anglo-Saxon Chronicle') places the refurbishment of Chester in 907, although it makes no mention of Æthelflæd. It does, however, report that she built a stronghold at a site called Bremesbyrig (possibly Bromesberrow, near L
1 CONC edbury, Herefordshire) in 910, the year before Æthelred's death. Sir Frank Stenton ('Anglo-Saxon England') Writes: "The record of the fortresses which she built for the protection of Mercia shows that she had an eye for country, a
1 CONC nd the ability to forecast the movements of her enemies. It was through reliance on her guardianship of Mercia that her brother [Edward] was enabled to begin the forward movement against the southern Danes which is the outstanding
1 CONC  feature of his reign."  The 'Mercian Register' records:912 "... came Ethelfleda [Æthelflæd], lady of the Mercians, on the holy eve called the invention of the holy cross [2nd May], to Scergeat [unidentified], and built the fortre
1 CONC ss there, and the same year that at Bridgnorth."913 "This year by the permission of God went Ethelfleda, lady of Mercia, with all the Mercians to Tamworth; and built the fort there in the fore-part of the summer; and before Lammas
1 CONC  that at Stafford."914 "... that at Eddisbury, in the beginning of the summer; and the same year, late in the autumn, that at Warwick."915 "... was built, after mid-winter, that at Chirbury and that at Weardbyrig [perhaps Warburto
1 CONC n]; and the same year before mid-winter that at Runcorn."On 16th June 916, it appears that "innocent Abbot Ecgberht" was killed by the Welsh. The redoubtable Æthelflæd took prompt action. Three days later an army was despatched in
1 CONC to Wales. The wife of the king of Brycheiniog, and thirty-three others, were taken captive.In 917, the English offensive against Danish held territory began in earnest. Æthelflæd played her part: "This year Ethelfleda, lady of the
1 CONC  Mercians, with the help of God, before Lammas [1st August], conquered the town called Derby, with all that thereto belonged; and there were also slain four of her thegns, that were most dear to her, within the gates."  The follow
1 CONC ing year (918): "This year Ethelfleda got into her power, with God's assistance, in the early part of the year, without loss, the town of Leicester; and the greater part of the army that belonged thereto submitted to her. And the 
1 CONC Yorkists had also promised and confirmed, some by agreement and some with oaths, that they would be in her interest....Sir Frank Stenton asserts that: "There is little doubt that this offer was intended to obtain her support again
1 CONC st the Norse raiders from Ireland, who are known to have been at large in the north at the time... So far as is known, no proposal of the kind was ever made to King Edward, her brother, and no English army intervened to prevent th
1 CONC e establishment of the Norse kingdom of York."The 'Three Fragments' contain an account of a battle fought against Viking forces, which may equate with a battle fought at Corbridge, Northumbria, in 918. Though the story is obviousl
1 CONC y embroidered, it does not seem an unreasonable claim (though it is recorded nowhere else) that Æthelflæd entered into an alliance with the "men of Alba" (i.e. the Picts and Scots) and the Strathclyde Britons to counter the threat
1 CONC  posed by Hiberno-Norse invaders..... But very soon after they had done this, she died at Tamworth, twelve days before midsummer, the eighth year of her having rule and right lordship over the Mercians; and her body lies at Glouce
1 CONC ster, within the east porch of St.Peter's church."The 'Three Fragments' suggest that Æthelflæd achieved great renown as a result of her military success at the battle of 918. Indeed, her death is recorded in the 'Annales Cambriae'
1 CONC  (Annals of Wales), and the 'Annals of Ulster' comment: "Ethelfled, a very famous queen of the Saxons, dies."William of Malmesbury (d.c.1143), in his 'Gesta Regum Anglorum' (Deeds of the English Kings) writes: "... Ethelfleda, sis
1 CONC ter of the king and widow of Ethered [Æthelred], ought not to be forgotten, as she was a powerful accession to his [i.e. Edward's] party, the delight of his subjects, the dread of his enemies; a woman of an enlarged soul, who, fro
1 CONC m the difficulty experienced in her first (or rather only) labour, ever after refused the embraces of her husband, protesting that it was unbecoming the daughter of a king to give way to a delight, which after a time produced such
1 CONC  painful consequences. This spirited heroine assisted her brother greatly with her advice; she was of equal service in building cities, nor could you easily discern whether it were more owing to fortune or her own exertions, that 
1 CONC a woman should be able to protect men at home, and to intimidate them abroad."
