0 @N6000000002248115116@ NOTE Edward was Alfred's son. Edward together with his sister Ethelfleda of Mercia, fought stoutly against the Danes. Ethelfleda built many forts notably at Chester,Hereford, Bridgenorth, Shrewsbury, Warwick, Gloucester and Tamworth. K
1 CONC nown as The Lady of the Mercians, she died in 918 and Mercia was then united with Wessex. In 914, Edward secured the release of the Bishop of Llandaff (Cardiff) who had been captured by the Norsemen and following this, the princes
1 CONC  of both North and South Wales pledged their perpetual allegiance to him. Edward doubled the size of the kingdom during his reign. It is now generally acknowledged that Edward died on the 7th July 924 but some historians give the 
1 CONC date as 925. His death occurred at Farndon in Cheshire and he was buried at Winchester. Abroad, the African state of Ghana was at the peak of it's power. And, an event that was to affect England a century and a half later, the Vik
1 CONC ing Rollo (Rolf) was acknowledged as the Lord of Normandy in France.
