1 CONT ACADEMIC AMERICAN ENCYCLOPEDIA
1 CONT EDWARD THE ELDER, KING OF WESSEX
1 CONT The Anglo-Saxon king EDWARD THE ELDER, ru led 899-924, unified most of
1 CONT England south of the Humber River. He succeeded his father, ALFRED, as
1 CONT king of Wessex and continued the wars of resistance ag ainst the Danes.
1 CONT Conducting a series of joint campaigns with his sister, AETH ELFLAED of
1 CONT Mercia, he destroyed a Danish army in 910 and by 918 had virtually
1 CONT extinguished Danish power in
1 CONT England. On Aethelflaed's death in the same y ear, English Mercia was
1 CONT incorporated into Wessex. In 920, Edward received the formal submissions
1 CONT of Raegnald, the Scandinavian king of York; Ealdred, rule r of English
1 CONT Northumbria; the Welsh king of Strathclyde; and possibly Constan tine II
1 CONT of Scotland. He died on July 17, 924, and was succeeded by his son
1 CONT ATHELSTAN.
1 CONT EAST ANGLIA
1 CONT East Anglia, one of the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms of Engl and, borders on the
1 CONT North Sea and includes the modern counties of Norfolk and Suffolk. Low,
1 CONT forested hills separate the Fens (now drained and cultivated) in the west
1 CONT from the shallow river valleys with flooded peat hollows ("Broads ") and
1 CONT estuaries in the east.
1 CONT East Anglia was settled in the 5th century by Saxons, Angles, Swabians,
1 CONT and Frisians. Little is known of early East Anglia n kings prior to
1 CONT Raedwald (r. c.600-627). Raedwald was converted to Christian ity, and his
1 CONT son founded (c.630) a see at Dunwich for the Burgundian bishop F elix.
1 CONT Rivalry with Mericia led to the subjugation of East Anglia by the mid-7 th
1 CONT century. Danish invasions from the mid-9th century resulted in the
1 CONT
1 CONT inclu sion (886) of East Anglia under Danelaw. In 917 the Danes were
1 CONT defeated by ED WARD THE ELDER, and East Anglia became an earldom of
1 CONT England.
1 CONT Encyclopedia Britannica Online at britannica.com:
