0 @N6000000001354425525@ NOTE AETHELFLAED or ETHELFLEDA, the "Lady of the Mercians," the eldest child of Alfred the Great, was married about 866 to Aethelred, earl of Mercia, to whom Alfred entrusted the control of Mercia. On the accessionof her brother, Edwar
1 CONC d, Aethelflaed and her husband continued to holdMercia. In 907 they fortified Chester, and in 909 and 910 either Aethelflaed or her huaband must have led the Mercian host as the battles of Tettenhall and Wednesfield (or Tettenhall
1 CONC -Wednesfield, if these battles are one and the same]. It was probably about this time that Aethelred fell ill, and the Norwegians and Danes from Ireland successfully besieged Chester. Aethelflaed won the support of the Danes again
1 CONC st theNorwegians, and seems also to have entered into an alliance with the Scots and the Welsh against the pagans.
1 CONT In 911 Aethered died, and Edward took over Middlesex and Oxfordshire.Except for this, Aethelflaed's authority remained unimpaired. In 912 she fortified "Scergeat" and Bridgnorth, Tamworth and Stafford in 913,Eddisbury and Warwick 
1 CONC in 914, Cherbury, "Weardbyrig" and Runcorn in 915. In 916 she sent an expedition against the Welsh, which advanced asfar as Brecknock. In 917 Derby was captured from the Danes, and the yext year Leicester and York both submitted t
1 CONC o her. She died in 918 at Tamworth (June 12), and was buried in St. Peter's Church at Gloucester. This noble queen, whose career was as distringuished as that of herfather and brother, left one daughter, Aelfwyn. For some months A
1 CONC elfwyn seems to have wielded her mother's authority, and then, just beforeChristmas of 919, Edward took Mercia into his own hands, and Aelfwyn was "led away" into Wessex. Aethelflaed and her huisband wielded almost kingly authorit
1 CONC y; the royal title is often given them by chroniclers. [Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 1, p. 274, AETHELFLAED orETHELFLEDA]
1 CONT -------------------------
1 CONT Aethelflaed, also spelled ETHELFLEDA, or AELFLED, also called LADY OFTHE MERCIANS (d. June 12, 918, Tamworth, Eng.), Anglo-Saxon ruler of Mercia in England.
1 CONT The daughter of King Alfred the Great, she helped her brother Edward the Elder, king of the West Saxons (reigned 899-924), in conquering the Danish armies occupying eastern England. Aethelflaed became the effective ruler of Mercia
1 CONC  some years before the death (911) of her husband, Aethelred, ealdorman of the Mercians. While Edward fortified (910-916) the southeast Midlands, Aethelflaed was building fortresses aroundMercia. By 917 she and Edward were ready t
1 CONC o launch a massive joint assault on the Danish positions. Aethelflaed quickly captured Derby, andin 918 she occupied Leicester, but she died before the campaign was successfully completed. Edward then claimed his sister's kingdom 
1 CONC and completed the subjugation of the Danes. Because Aethelflaed had extendedher influence into Wales and Northumbria, Edward was able to assert his authority over these regions as well. Thus, almost all of England came under his c
1 CONC ontrol.
