0 @N6000000002187592523@ NOTE Eadgyth
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT For other persons named Eadgyth, see Eadgyth (disambiguation).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ædgyth or Edith of England (910 - 26 January 946) was the daughter of Edward the Elder, King of England and Ælfflæd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Alfred the Great, King of Wessex and his wife Ealhswith.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Athelstan of England sent two of his sisters to Germany, instructing Otto I, Holy Roman Emperor to choose whichever one pleased him best. Otto chose Edith and married her in 929. The remaining sister Algiva or Adiva was marri
1 CONC ed to a "king near the Jupiter mountains" (the Alps). The precise identity of this sister is debated. She may have been Eadgifu of England who married King Charles III of France, or another sister otherwise unknown to history. (Ac
1 CONC cording to the entry for Boleslaus II of Bohemia, Adiva was his wife.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Like her brother, Athelstan, Edith was devoted to the cult of Saint Oswald, and was instrumental in introducing this cult into Germany after her marriage to the emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edith and Otto's children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Liutgarde, married Conrad the Red 
1 CONT Liudolf, Duke of Swabia (930-September 6, 957) 
1 CONT Her tomb is located at the Cathedral of Magdeburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Matilda of Ringelheim German Queen
1 CONT 936 – 946 Succeeded by
1 CONT Adelaide of Italy 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Freytag von Loringhoven, Baron. Stammtafeln zur Geschichte der Europäischen Staaten, 1965. 
1 CONT Klaniczay, Gábor. Holy Rulers and Blessed Princesses, 2002. 
0 @N6000000002322325010@ NOTE Edith, married Otto, King of the Germans, afterwards Emperor. Her father gave
1 CONT as dowry the flourishing town of Magdeburg.
