0 @N6000000001563192146@ NOTE Æthelstan (924-40 AD)
1 CONT
1 CONT The grandson of Alfred the Great, Æthelstan succeeded his father, Edward the Elder, to the throne of Wessex. He was the first English sovereign ever to be crowned on the King's Stone at Kingston-upon-Thames in 925. Incorrectly cla
1 CONC imed by some to be the first King of All England, Æthelstan was a great warrior, nonetheless, whose fame stemmed from his conquests in Cornwall and Wales, and his defeat of a combined force of Scots, Welsh and Vikings at the battl
1 CONC e of Brunanburh in 938. Æthelstan was a patron of monastic communities and especially supported the monastery at Malmesbury </magical/magic4.html>, where his tomb can be found, today.
0 @N6000000001578844387@ NOTE Æthelstan (924-40 AD)
1 CONT
1 CONT The grandson of Alfred the Great, Æthelstan succeeded his father, Edward the Elder, to the throne of Wessex. He was the first English sovereign ever to be crowned on the King's Stone at Kingston-upon-Thames in 925. Incorrectly cla
1 CONC imed by some to be the first King of All England, Æthelstan was a great warrior, nonetheless, whose fame stemmed from his conquests in Cornwall and Wales, and his defeat of a combined force of Scots, Welsh and Vikings at the battl
1 CONC e of Brunanburh in 938. Æthelstan was a patron of monastic communities and especially supported the monastery at Malmesbury </magical/magic4.html>, where his tomb can be found, today.
0 @N6000000002322323928@ NOTE King from 924-939, he was considered the most brillant among the old English
1 CONT kings. He was the first to hold the title of King of England. In 937, the King
1 CONT of Dublin, Guthfrithson and the Scots invaded far into England, but were
1 CONT soundly defeated at an old ancient place called Brunnanburh. Northumbrian
1 CONT Danes were not present at the battle, whereas a few years earlier they had
1 CONT been the greatest threat to his father. Athelstan was succeeded by 1/2 brother
1 CONT Edmund, son of Edward the Elder.
