0 @N6000000000337389853@ NOTE Source #1:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 2
1 CONT
1 CONT She was his third wife
0 @N6000000000437150101@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Queen Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of England
0 @N6000000003796257044@ NOTE From: Chris Bennett <cbennett@TIERNAN.COM> 
1 CONT Subject: Family of Edward the Elder 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Tracey Robinson asked for information concerning the wives and children of
1 CONT Edward the Elder. Here is what I have. Children are listed male first. This
1 CONT is not necessarily the order of birth which is unknown, by and large.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eadward I the Elder, king of Wessex 899-924 married
1 CONT
1 CONT 1) Ecgwynn (poss. a concubine), origins unknown, by whom he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT (a) Athelstan, b c 894, king of Mercia 924, king of Wessex 924 after the
1 CONT death of Aelfweard, d Oct 26 940.
1 CONT (b) Eadgyth, m 925 Sihtric Coach, king of York
1 CONT
1 CONT 2) before 901 Aelfflead, d. Aethelhelm, ealdorman of Wiltshire (s. Aethelred
1 CONT I), by who he had
1 CONT
1 CONT (a) Aelfweard, king of Wessex 924, d 16 days after his father
1 CONT (b) Eadwine, drowned 933
1 CONT (c) Eadflaed, a nun
1 CONT (d) Eadgifu, m (1) 918, as his third wife, Charles III the Simple, k of the
1 CONT Franks; m (2) 951 Herbert III of Vermandois
1 CONT (e) Aethelhild, a lay sister
1 CONT (f) Eadhild m c926 as his second wife Hugh "the Great" Ct of Paris
1 CONT (g) Eadgyth m c929 Otto I, later king of Germany, Emperor
1 CONT (h) Aelfgifu m after 929 a duke or king "near the Alps" -- see discussion
1 CONT
1 CONT Possibly the unaffiliated Alfred, who opposed the accession of Athelstan,
1 CONT was also a son of this marriage, a younger brother of Aelfweard?
1 CONT
1 CONT 3) after 919 Eadgifu, d Sigehelm, ealdorman of Kent, by whom he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT (a) Eadmund I, b c 920, king 940-May 26 946, m (1) Aelfgifu, m (2) as her
1 CONT first husband Aethelflead, d Aelfgar, ealdorman of Essex, who m (2) Athelstan
1 CONT Rota, ealdorman of Mercia
1 CONT (b) Eadred, b c 924 king 946-Nov 23 955
1 CONT (c) Eadburh, a nun
1 CONT (d) Eadgifu, m Louis, "prince" of Aquitaine, otherwise unidentified. Since
1 CONT her reported name is the same as her mother's name, and the marriage is only
1 CONT reported by William of Malmesbury (12c), one might reasonably argue for a
1 CONT mistake here.
1 CONT
1 CONT The identity of the husband of Aelfgifu (2.h) is a subject of much discussion.
1 CONT She and Eadgyth were sent to Germany as two candidates for the hands of Otto in
1 CONT 928; Eadgyth was selected, and Aelfgifu was married off thereafter. The
1 CONT following candidates have been proposed:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1) Emperor Louis III, the Blind, who however died in 928, so can be
1 CONT conclusively ruled out.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2) Alberic, s Majolus, ct of Narbonne, based on a charter which is, however, a
1 CONT forgery (and Narbonne is nowhere near the Alps).
1 CONT
1 CONT 3) Conrad the Peacable, k of Burgundy, whose first wife Adelana is of unknown
1 CONT origin; this is probably the most widely seen identification -- e.g. [2]
1 CONT accepts it without question. However, Conrad succeeded under regency in 937,
1 CONT i.e. as a child, which makes the chronology very hard to sustain, since
1 CONT Aelfgifu should have married at most a few years after her sister.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4) Boleslav II, b c 920, d of Bohemia 967-999, based on coins naming a woman
1 CONT "Adivea", which is close to the name "Adiva" given to Aelfgifu in a German poem
1 CONT celebrating the marriage of Otto. Bohemian chronicles name his wife as "Emma",
1 CONT a foreigner of noble descent, It is notable that Emma of Normandy, wife of
1 CONT Aethelred II and Canute, was known in England as "Aelfgifu". Emma of Bohemia
1 CONT is known to have died in 1005/6. While Bohemia is not particularly near the
1 CONT Alps, it does border Germany and might reasonably be misreported by Anglo-
1 CONT Saxons with a fuzzy notion of central European geography. Some scholars argue
1 CONT that "Emma" was Bavarian or south German.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5) Eberhard IV, ct of Alsace or Nordgau, based on a statement whose origin
1 CONT could not be traced by Germond that his wife was "Eadiva, daughter of the king
1 CONT of the Angles". Europaische Stammtafeln shows him marrying Liutgard, widow of
1 CONT Adalbert ct of Metz and daughter of a Ct Wigerich.
1 CONT
1 CONT 6) Eblus, son of a D. of Aquitaine (DNB quoting a secondary source); this
1 CONT appears to be related to "Louis, prince of Aquitaine". Aquitaine, of course,
1 CONT is nowhere near the Alps nor Germany. If Eadgifu (3.d) is a mistake, as seems
1 CONT likely to me, she might be a corruption of Aelfgifu and "Louis of Aquitaine"
1 CONT would then support this proposal. [Tapsell shows that the Duke of Aquitaine in
1 CONT these years was called Eblus, 890-893 and 926-935].
1 CONT
1 CONT To my mind, none of these proposals is particularly strong. (4) is the best
1 CONT case, though the chronology is unusual. If the primary source of (5) or
1 CONT especially of (6) turned out to be solid then one of these might be preferable.
1 CONT
1 CONT References:
1 CONT
1 CONT [1] W. G. Searle: Anglo-Saxon Bishops, Kings and Nobles (Cambridge, 1899)
1 CONT [2] D. P. Kirby, A. P Smyth, A. Williams: A Biographical Dictionary of Dark
1 CONT Age Britain (London, 1991)
1 CONT [3] A. Germond: The Daughters of King Edward the Elder, in Journal of Ancient &
1 CONT Medieval Studies I (1982) 91 (Torrance CA, available from the Augustan Society).
1 CONT [4] R. F. Tapsell: Monarchs, Rulers, Dynasties and Kingdoms of the World
1 CONT (London, 1983)
1 CONT
