1 CONT WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 17450296 = 1841416
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists ..." Edmund the Magnificent
1 CONT
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta 1993
1 CONT Edmund I (921-46), Saxon king of the English (939-46), the son of King Edward the Elder. He participated in the Battle of Brunanburh in 937 and succeeded his half brother Athelstan as king in 939. The following year Olaf Godfreyso
1 CONC n, a Viking ruler of Dublin, seized the territory of Northumbria in northern England and extended his rule as far south as Leicester. After Olaf's death in 941, Edmund made war on the Vikings, expelling them from the country three
1 CONC  years later. In 945 Edmund occupied the kingdom of Strathclyde, west of Northumbria, and turned it over to his ally Malcolm I MacDonald, king of Scotland (reigned 943-54). The following year Edmund was stabbed to death by a robbe
1 CONC r and was succeeded by his brother Edred (reigned 945-55). Edmund was known as a legal reformer, especially for his restrictions on the blood feud.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Anglo Saxon Chronicle", Part 1:
1 CONT A.D. 495. 'Then succeeded Edmund, his brother, and reigned six years and a half, wanting two nights.'
1 CONT
1 CONT "Anglo Saxon Chronicle", Part 2:
1 CONT A.D. 946. This year King Edmund died, on St. Augustine's mass day. That was widely known, how he ended his days: -- that Leof stabbed him at Pucklechurch. And Ethelfleda of Damerham, daughter of Alderman Elgar, was then his queen.
1 CONC  And he reigned six years and a half.
0 @N6000000003949656327@ NOTE The first of the six Boy Kings, he reigned from 939 to 946. He had to meet a general uprising of the Danes of Mercia as well as those of the North. In the suppression of this he showed himself to be a great statesman as well as a 
1 CONC great warrior. Little is definitely known about the policy of the Scots at this time but it appears that they joined the English whenever they were afraid of the Danes, and joined the Danes whenever they were afraid of the English
1 CONC . Edmund made it to be the interest of the Scottish King permanently to join the English. The southern part of the kingdom of Strathclyde had for some time been under the English Kings. In 945 Edmund took the remainder, but gave i
1 CONC t to Malcolm on condition that he should be his fellow worker by sea and land. The king of the Scots thus entered into a position of dependent alliance towards Edmund. A great step was thus taken; the dominant powers in the island
1 CONC  were to be English and Scots, not English and Danes. Edmund thought it worth while to conciliate the Scottish Celts rather than to endeavor to conquer them. The result of Edmund's statesmanship was soon seen, but he did not live 
1 CONC to gather its fruits. On May 26, 946 an outlaw named Lief, who had taken his seat at a banquet in his hall, slew him as Edmund was attempting to drag him out by his hair
0 @N6000000001956348260@ NOTE
1 CONT King of England 940-960
1 CONT Edmund The Deed-doer, Latin Edmundus Magnificusking of the English (939-946), who recaptured areas of northern England that had been occupied by the Vikings.
1 CONT He was the son of the West Saxon king Edward the Elder (reigned 899-924) and the half brother of King Athelstan (reigned 924-939), under whom the political unification of England had been accomplished. On Athelstan's death (939),O
1 CONC laf Guthfrithson, the Norse king of Dublin, occupied Northumbria and raided the Midlands.
1 CONT Edmund recovered the Midlands after Olaf died in 942, and in944 he regained Northumbria, driving out the Norse kings Olaf Sihtricson and Raegnald. He captured Strathclyde in 945 and entrusted it to Malcolm I, king of Scots, inretu
1 CONC rn for a promise of military support. Thus, Edmund inaugurated a policy of establishing a secure frontier and peaceful relations with Scotland. In addition, his reignmarks the beginning of the 10th-century monastic revival in Engl
1 CONC and. The king was killed in his palace by an exiled robber and was succeeded by his brother, Eadred (reigned 946-955); Edmund's sons eventually acceded to power as kings Eadwig (reigned 957-959) and Edgar (reigned 959-975).
0 @N6000000004286732375@ NOTE !Name is;  Edmund I, "The Magnificent", King Of /ENGLAND/
0 @N6000000006296745620@ NOTE Edmund I (Old English </wiki/Old_English_language>: Eadmund) (922 - May 26 </wiki/May_26>, 946 </wiki/946>), called the Elder, the Deed-doer, the Just, or the Magnificent, was King of England </wiki/King_of_England> from 939 until
1 CONC  his death. He was a son of Edward the Elder </wiki/Edward_the_Elder> and half-brother of Athelstan </wiki/Athelstan_of_England>. Athelstan died on October 27 </wiki/October_27>, 939 </wiki/939>, and Edmund succeeded him as king.
1 CONT Shortly after his proclamation as king he had to face several military threats. King Olaf III Guthfrithson </wiki/Olaf_III_Guthfrithson> conquered Northumbria </wiki/Northumbria> and invaded the Midlands </wiki/English_Midlands>. 
1 CONC When Olaf died in 942 Edmund reconquered the Midlands. In 943 he became the god-father of King Olaf of York </wiki/Amla%C3%ADb_Cuar%C3%A1n>. In 944, Edmund was successful in reconquering Northumbria. In the same year his ally Olaf
1 CONC  of York lost his throne and left for Dublin </wiki/Dublin> in Ireland </wiki/Ireland>. Olaf became the king of Dublin as Olaf Cuaran </wiki/Olaf_Cuaran> and continued to be allied to his god-father. In 945 Edmund conquered Strath
1 CONC clyde </wiki/Kingdom_of_Strathclyde> but ceded the territory to King Malcolm I of Scotland </wiki/Malcolm_I_of_Scotland> in exchange for a treaty of mutual military support. Edmund thus established a policy of safe borders and pea
1 CONC ceful relationships with Scotland </wiki/Scotland>. During his reign, the revival of monasteries </wiki/Monastery> in England began.
1 CONT One of Edmund's last political movements of which we have some knowledge is his role in the restoration of Louis IV of France </wiki/Louis_IV_of_France> to the throne. Louis, son of Charles the Simple </wiki/Charles_the_Simple> an
1 CONC d his Anglo-Saxon queen Eadgifu </wiki/Eadgifu_of_England>, had resided at the West-Saxon court for some time until 936, when he returned to be crowned king of France. In the summer of 945, he was captured by the Norsemen of Rouen
1 CONC  and subsequently released by Duke Hugh the Great </wiki/Hugh_the_Great>, who however, held him in custody. The chronicler Richerus </wiki/Richerus> claims that Eadgifu wrote letters both to Edmund and to Otto I </wiki/Otto_I> in 
1 CONC which she requested support for her son; Edmund responded to her plea by sending angry threats to Hugh, who however, brushed them aside.[1] Flodoard </wiki/Flodoard>'s Annales, one of Richerus' sources, report:
1 CONT “	Edmund, king of the English, sent messengers to Duke Hugh about the restoration of King Louis, and the duke accordingly made a public agreement with his nephews and other leading men of his kingdom. [...] Hugh, duke of the Frank
1 CONC s, allying himself with Hugh the Black </wiki/Hugh_the_Black>, son of Richard, and the other leading men of the kingdom, restored to the kingdom King Louis.[2][3]
1 CONT
1 CONT On 26 May, 946, Edmund was murdered by Leofa, an exiled thief, while celebrating St Augustine's Mass Day in Pucklechurch </wiki/Pucklechurch> (South Gloucestershire).[4] John of Worcester </wiki/John_of_Worcester> and William of M
1 CONC almesbury </wiki/William_of_Malmesbury> add some lively detail by suggesting that Edmund had been feasting with his nobles, when he spotted Leofa in the crowd. He attacked the intruder in person, but in the event, Edmund and Leofa
1 CONC  were both killed.[5]
1 CONT Edmund's sister Eadgyth </wiki/Eadgyth>, wife to Otto I </wiki/Otto_I,_Holy_Roman_Emperor>, died (earlier) the same year, as Flodoard </wiki/Flodoard>'s Annales for 946 report.[6]
1 CONT Edmund was succeeded as king by his brother Edred </wiki/Edred_of_England>, king from 946 until 955. Edmund's sons later ruled England as:
1 CONT Eadwig of England </wiki/Eadwig_of_England>, King from 955 until 957, king of only Wessex </wiki/Wessex> and Kingdom of Kent </wiki/Kingdom_of_Kent> from 957 until his death on October 1 959 </wiki/959>.
1 CONT Edgar of England </wiki/Edgar_of_England>, king of only Mercia </wiki/Mercia> and Northumbria </wiki/Northumbria> from 957 until his brother's death in 959, then king of England from 959 until 975.
0 @N6000000006348204662@ NOTE Edmund I (940-46 AD)
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Edward the Elder, succeeded his half-brother, Æthelstan, with whom he had fought at Brunanburh. Combated the Norse Vikings in Northumbria and subdued them in Cumbria and Strathclyde. He entrusted these lands to an ally, Mal
1 CONC colm I of Scotland. Edmund met his death when he was killed at Pucklechurch, in Gloucestershire, by a robber.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  Britannia.com
