0 @N6000000003949647913@ NOTE  Baldwin II -the Bald- from his stronghold at Bruges maintained, as did his father before him, a vigorous defence of his lands against the incursions of the Northmen. On his mother's side a descendant of Charlemagne, he strengthen
1 CONC ed the dynastic importance of his family by marrying Aelfthryth, daughter of Alfred the Great. On his death in 918 his possessions were divided between his two sons Arnulf the Elder and Adolphus, but the latter survived only a sho
1 CONC rt time and Arnulf succeeded to the whole inheritance.
1 CONT He annexed lands and rights in the area between the river Schelde and Artois and had his opponents murdered. His final resting place was in Gent, in the Saint Peter's Abbey. 
1 CONT The early years of Baldwin's rule were marked by a series of devastating Viking raids. Little north of the Somme was untouched. Baldwin recovered, building new fortresses and improving city walls, and taking over abandoned propert
1 CONC y, so that in the end he held far more territory, and held it more strongly, than had his father. He also took advantage of the conflicts between Charles the Simple and Odo, Count of Paris to take over the Ternois and the Boulonni
1 CONC as. Baldwin married Elfreda (or Elstrude), a daughter of King Alfred the Great of England. The marriage was motivated by the common Flemish-English opposition to the Vikings, and was the start of an alliance that was a mainstay of
1 CONC  Flemish policy for centuries to come. (Wikipedia)
