0 @N6000000003098208245@ NOTE Founder of Wessex, First King; Reigned over West Saxons, 519-34
0 @N6000000003796158594@ NOTE IMPERIUM
1 CONT URL: http://www.ghgcorp.com/shetler/oldimp/443.html
1 CONT Cerdic, King of Wessex
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Born: ? 
1 CONT Died: 534 
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Father: Elesa 
1 CONT Mother: ? 
1 CONT Married (1): ? 
1 CONT Children: 
1 CONT Cynric, King of Wessex
1 CONT Creoda 
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT King of Wessex 519-534 
1 CONT Cerdic and Cynric, two Saxon princes, came to Britain in 495, and fought with the Britons the very day they landed. In 508, they slew a British king Natanleod. In 514, the subjects of the princes arrived in Britain at what became 
1 CONC Wessex. In 519, they fought with the Britons at Charford, and in that year Cerdic assumed the government of Wessex as its first king. In 527, the two fought the Britons at Cerdic's-ley, and in 530 they conquered the Isle of Wight.
1 CONC  In 534, Cerdic died, and Cyrnic took the government. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Cerdic was given an ancestry that took him back to Woden, the chief Anglo-Saxon god. Cerdic was said to be the son of Elesa, son of Gewis, son of Wye, son of Frewin, son of Frithgar, son of Brand, son of Balday, son of Woden. For 
1 CONC Wodens own ancestors back to Adam, the first man, see Hengist, King of Kent. 
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT e-mail Joe Shetler at: shetler@ghgcorp.com 
1 CONT
1 CONT =========================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick Rose's Genealogy
1 CONT URL: http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=PED&db=:1416850&id=I88739834
1 CONT ID: I98113263 
1 CONT Name: (King) Cerdic OF WESSEX 
1 CONT Given Name: (King) Cerdic 
1 CONT Surname: of Wessex 
1 CONT Sex: M 
1 CONT Birth: 470 in Saxony, Germany 
1 CONT Death: 534 in Wessex, England 
1 CONT Note: 
1 CONT CERDIC (fl. c 490-530?) was remembered in later Anglo-Saxon tradition as the first Germanic king of Wessex. The annals of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle record some very dubious traditions of his military exploits which are assigned da
1 CONC tes between 495 and 534. The most intriguing thing about this shadowy personage is his name, which is not Germanic but Celtic; compare, for example, the king Cerdic of the British kingdom of Elmet in the early seventh century. Wha
1 CONC tever may be the implications the presence of this anomalous name in the genealogies did not embarrass those who claimed tobe his descendants: 'his kin goes back to Cerdic' was a regular boast of the chroniclers who recorded the d
1 CONC oings of later kings of Wessex. If the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle is to be believed, Cerdic's operations occurred in that area of Hampshire and south Wiltshire extending northwards from Southampton towards Winchester and Salisbury. The
1 CONC  rather meagre archaeological record of the early Anglo-Saxon period confirms that this region was one nucleus of the later kingdom of Wessex. Archaeology reveals much thicker Germanic settlement of the upper Thames valley, notabl
1 CONC y around Dorchester-on-Thames which was later, significantly, to be the site of the first West Saxon bishropric under Birinus; but on the early traditions of this region the literary sourcesare silent. It is a reminder that the or
1 CONC igins of the political entity later known as the kingdom of Wessex were morecomplex and diverse than we shall ever know. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Who's Who in Roman Britain and Anglo-Saxon England, Richard Fletcher, Shepheard-Walwyn Publishing Ltd, London, 1984] ---------- Cerdic (d. 534), founder of the West Saxon kingdom, or Wessex. All the sovereigns
1 CONT of England except Canute, Hardecanute, the two Harolds, and William the Conqueror are said to be descended from him. A Continental ealdorman who in 495 landed in Hampshire, Cerdic was attacked at once by the Britons. Nothing more 
1 CONC is heard of him until 508, when he defeated the Britons with great slaughter. Strengthened by fresh arrivals of Saxons,he gained another victory in 519 at Certicesford, a spot which has been identified with the modern Charford, an
1 CONC d in this year took the title of king. Turning westward, Cerdic appears to have been defeated
1 CONT by the Britons in 520 at Badbury or Mount Badon, in Dorset, and in 527 yet another fight with the Britons is recorded. His last work was the conquest of the Isle of Wight, probably in the interest of some Jutishallies.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Father: Elesa b: 439 in Saxony, Germany
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 1 Spouse Unknown
1 CONT Children
1 CONT  (Prince) Creoda OF WESSEX b: 493
1 CONT
1 CONT ==================================================
1 CONT BRITANNIA, THE KINGS of WESSEX
1 CONT URL:  http://www.britannia.com/history/monarchs/wessex.html
