0 @N6000000003635701127@ NOTE [3073] COLVER31.TXT file, 'came as part of the Saxon Invasion', b. abt 475
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonists", King of the West Saxons, 519-534; he became the ancestor of the English royal line
1 CONT
1 CONT "Our Royal Descent from Alfred 'the Great' ..." in Steve Clare papers, landed in England 495, founder of kingdom of Wessex, d 534
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/users/mh10006/FamTree/interesting.html The Romans left Britain in 406 AD. Cerdic, first King of the West Saxons, arrived in Britain 495, possibly fought against native leader Arthur, died 534, is generally 
1 CONC accepted as the historical start of the British Royal line. His descent from Woden and then from Bedwig is regarded as legend. For what it's worth, beyond this point the Anglo Saxon Chronicles attempts to link Bedwig to the Biblic
1 CONC al Noah (who is mythological), while the Icelandic Prose Edda shows further descent from Thor and then attempts to link to Greek mythology.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Anglo Saxon Chronicle", Part 1: A.D. 495. 'This year came two leaders into Britain, Cerdic and Cynric his son, with five ships, at a place that is called Cerdic's-ore. And they fought with the Welsh the same day. Then he died, an
1 CONC d his son Cynric succeeded to the government, and held it six and twenty winters.
1 CONT ' Later in the same paragraph in detailing the lineage from Ethelwulf back to Cerdic, Cynric is listed as Cerdic's grandson with his son being called Creoda.
1 CONT
1 CONT 'A.D. 508. This year Cerdic and Cynric slew a British king, whose name was Natanleod, and five thousand men with him. After this was the land named Netley, from him, as far as Charford.
1 CONT ...
1 CONT A.D. 514. This year came the West-Saxons into Britain, with three ships, at the place that is called Cerdic's-ore. And Stuff and Wihtgar fought with the Britons, and put them to flight.
1 CONT
1 CONT A.D. 519. This year Cerdic and Cynric undertook the government of the West-Saxons; the same year they fought with the Britons at a place now called Charford. From that day have reigned the children of the West-Saxon kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT A.D. 527. This year Cerdic and Cynric fought with the Britons in the place that is called Cerdic's-ley.
1 CONT
1 CONT A.D. 530. This year Cerdic and Cynric took the isle of Wight, and slew many men in Carisbrook.
1 CONT
1 CONT A.D. 534. This year died Cerdic, the first king of the West- Saxons....'
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.jedh.com/genealogy/d0006/g0000024.html#I2223 WAS A SAXON EARLDORMAN WHO FOUNDED A SETTLEMENT ON THE COAST OF HAMPSHIRE, ENGLAND IN 495, ASSUMED THE TITLE OF KING OF THE WEST SAXONS IN 519, AND HENCE BECAME THE ANCESTOR 
1 CONC OF THE ENGLISH ROYAL LINE.
0 @N6000000006296787919@ NOTE According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle </wiki/Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle>, Cerdic landed in Hampshire </wiki/Hampshire> in 495 with his son Cynric </wiki/Cynric_of_Wessex> in three keels (ships). He is said to have fought a British kin
1 CONC g named Natanleod </wiki/Natanleod> at Netley Marsh </wiki/Netley_Marsh> in Hampshire </wiki/Hampshire> and killed him in 508, and to have fought at Charford </wiki/Charford> (Cerdic's Ford) in 519, after which he became first kin
1 CONC g of Wessex </wiki/Wessex>. The conquest of the Isle of Wight </wiki/Isle_of_Wight> is also mentioned among his campaigns, and it was later given to his kinsmen, Stuf and Wihtgar (who had supposedly arrived with the West Saxons </
1 CONC wiki/Wessex> in 514). Cerdic is said to have died in 534 and was succeeded by his son Cynric </wiki/Cynric_of_Wessex>.
1 CONT The early history of Wessex in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle </wiki/Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle> is clearly muddled. David Dumville </wiki/David_Dumville> has suggested that Cerdic's true regnal dates are 538-554. Some scholars suggest that
1 CONC  Cerdic was the Saxon leader defeated by the British at the Battle of Mount Badon </wiki/Battle_of_Mount_Badon>, which was probably fought sometime between 490 and 518. This cannot be the case if Dumville is correct, and others as
1 CONC sign this battle to Ælle </wiki/Aelle_of_Sussex> or another Saxon leader.
1 CONT It should also be noted that while Cerdic's area of operation was, according to the Chronicle, in the area north of Southampton, there is also stronger archaeological evidence of early Anglo-Saxon activity in the area around Dorch
1 CONC ester-on-Thames </wiki/Dorchester-on-Thames>. This is the later location of the first West Saxon bishopric, in the first half of the seventh century, so it appears likely that the origins of the kingdom of Wessex are more complex 
1 CONC than the version provided by the surviving traditions.[1]
1 CONT Some scholars have gone so far as to suggest that Cerdic is purely a legendary figure, and had no actual existence, but this is a minority view. However, the earliest source for Cerdic, the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, was put together 
1 CONC in the late ninth century; though it probably does record the extant tradition of the founding of Wessex, the intervening four hundred years mean that the account cannot be assumed to be accurate.[2][3]
1 CONT Descent from Cerdic became a necessary criterion for later kings of Wessex, and Egbert of Wessex </wiki/Egbert_of_Wessex>, progenitor of the English royal house and subsequent rulers of England </wiki/List_of_English_monarchs> and
1 CONC  Britain </wiki/Monarchy_of_the_United_Kingdom>, claimed him as an ancestor.
1 CONT The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle </wiki/Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle> provides a pedigree tracing Cerdic's ancestry back to Woden </wiki/W%C5%8Dden> and the antediluvian patriarchs </wiki/Patriarchs_(Bible)>. However, this pedigree has been sho
1 CONC wn by Kenneth Sisam to have resulted from a process of elaboration upon a root pedigree borrowed from the kings of Bernica </wiki/List_of_monarchs_of_Northumbria>, and hence prior to Cerdic himself it has no historical basis.[4]
1 CONT Curiously, the name Cerdic is thought to be British - a form of the name Ceretic </wiki/Ceretic> or Caradog </wiki/Caradog> (in Latin </wiki/Latin> Caratacus </wiki/Caratacus>) - rather than Germanic in origin. One explanation for
1 CONC  this is the possibility that Cerdic's mother was British and that he was given a name used by his mother's people; if so, this would provide evidence for a degree of mixing, both cultural and biological, between the invaders and 
1 CONC the native British. Alternatively, the use of a British name may indicate that Cerdic was a native Briton, and that his dynasty became Anglicised over time. This view is supported by the non-Germanic names of some of his successor
1 CONC s including Ceawlin </wiki/Ceawlin>, Cedda and Caedwalla </wiki/Caedwalla>. If this were the case then the records of Cerdic landing in Britain, which were written down many generations after the events they purport to portray, mu
1 CONC st be looked on as being in the realms of legend.[5]
1 CONT J.N.L. Myres noted that when Cerdic and Cynric first appear in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle in 495 they are described as ealdormen </wiki/Ealdormen>, which at that point in time was a fairly junior rank. Myres remarks that,
1 CONT It is thus odd to find it used here to describe the leaders of what purports to be an independent band of invaders, who origins and authority are not otherwise specified. It looks very much as if a hint is being conveyed that Cerd
1 CONC ic and his people owed their standing to having been already concerned with administrative affairs under Roman authority on this part of the Saxon Shore.
1 CONT Furthermore, it is not until 519 that Cerdic and Cynric are recorded as "beginning to reign", suggesting that they ceased being dependent vassals or ealdormen and became independent Kings in their own right.
1 CONT Summing up, Myres believed that,
1 CONT It is thus possible ... to think of Cerdic as the head of a partly British noble family with extensive territorial interests at the western end of the Litus Saxonicum </wiki/Saxon_Shore>. As such he may well have been entrusted in
1 CONC  the last days of Roman, or sub-Roman authority with its defence. He would then be what in later Anglo-Saxon terminology could be described as an ealdorman. ... If such a dominant native family as that of Cerdic had already develo
1 CONC ped blood-relationships with existing Saxon and Jutish settlers at this end of the Saxon Shore, it could very well be tempted, once effective Roman authority had faded, to go further. It might have taken matters into its own hands
1 CONC  and after eliminating any surviving pockets of resistance by competing British chieftains, such as the mysterious Natanleod of annal 508, it could 'begin to reign' without recognizing in future any superior authority.
1 CONT Some would disagree with Myres, as Cerdic is reported to have landed in Hampshire. Some also would say that the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle proves that Cerdic was indeed a Saxon, however it does not prove that he had no Celtic blood. So
1 CONC me scholars believe that it is likely that his mother was a British Celt who left for the Continent or perhaps was a Continental Celt. Geoffrey Ashe </wiki/Geoffrey_Ashe> postulates he may be a son of Riothamus </wiki/Riothamus>.
0 @N6000000007283504493@ NOTE
1 CONT Cerdic of Wessex
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Cerdic of Wessex (c. 467–534), was the King of Wessex (519–534), and was regarded as the ancestors to all subsequent Kings of Wessex.
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT • 1 Official life and career
1 CONT • 2 Origins
1 CONT • 3 Modern times
1 CONT • 4 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Official life and career
1 CONT He is first mentioned in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle when he landed in Hampshire in 495 and in 519 gained a great victory at Charford, "but Hengest and Adelle's men had touched hardly more than the coast, and the true conquest of So
1 CONC uthern Britain was reserved for a fresh band of Saxons, a tribe known as the Gewissas, who landed under Cerdic and his son Cynric on the shores of the Southampton Water, and pushed in 495 to the great Downs of Gwent where Winchest
1 CONC er offered so rich a prize. Nowhere was the strife fiercer than here; and it was not till 519 that a decisive victory at Charford ended the struggle for the "Gwent" and set the crown of the West-Saxons on the head of Cerdic." The 
1 CONC West Saxons also fought a British king named Natanleod in Wiltshire and slew him. Under his leadership the West Saxons also advanced into Dorset and Somerset. The conquest of the Isle of Wight is also mentioned among his campaigns
1 CONC , and it was later given to his nephews, Stuf and Wihtgar (who brought many other Saxons with them).
1 CONT In 530 he and his son gradually conquered the country from Sussex to the River Avon in Hampshire; they also passed the Thames and subdued the country as far as Bedford. They were called the West Saxons and the Kingdom of Cerdic wa
1 CONC s named Wessex. Cerdic died in 534 and was succeeded by his son Cynric.
1 CONT Some scholars believe that Cerdic was the Saxon leader defeated by the British at the battle of Mount Badon, which was fought sometime between 490 and 516. However, others assign this battle to Ælle or another Saxon leader.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Origins
1 CONT Curiously, the name Cerdic is thought to be British – a form of the name Ceretic – rather than Germanic in origin. One explanation for this is the possibility that Cerdic's mother was British and that he was given a name used by h
1 CONC is mother's people; if so, this would provide evidence for a degree of mixing, both cultural and biological, between the invaders and the native British.
1 CONT J.N.L. Myres noted that when Cerdic and Cynric first appear in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle in 495 they are described as ealdormen, which at that point in time was fairly junior rank. Myres remarks that "It is thus odd to find it use
1 CONC d here to describe the leaders of what purports to be an independent band of invaders, who origins and authority are not otherwise specified. It looks very much as if a hint is being conveyed that Cerdic and his people owed their 
1 CONC standing to having been already concerned with administrative affairs under Roman authority on this part of the Saxon Shore." Furthermore, it is not until 519 that Cerdic and Cynric are recorded as "beginning to reign", suggesting
1 CONC  that they ceased being dependant vassals or ealdormen and became independent Kings in their own right.
1 CONT Summing up, Myres believed that It is thus possible ... to think of Cerdic as the head of a partly British noble family with extensive territorial interests at the western end of the "Litus Saxonicum. As such he may well have been
1 CONC  entrusted in the last days of Roman, or sub-Roman authority with its defense. He would then be what in later Anglo-Saxon terminology could be described as an ealdorman. ... If such a dominant native family as that of Cerdic had a
1 CONC lready developed blood-relationships with existing Saxon and Jutish settlers at this end of the Saxon Shore, it could very well be tempted, once effective Roman authority had faded, to go further. It might have taken matters into 
1 CONC its own hands and after eliminating any surviving pockets of resistance by competing British chieftains, such as the mysterious Natanleod of annal 508, it could 'begin to reign' without recognizing in future any superior authority
1 CONC ."
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Modern times
1 CONT The name Cedric (as opposed to Cerdic) arose from a misspelling in the novel Ivanhoe.
1 CONT In the 2004 film King Arthur, Cerdic and Cynric were depicted as Saxon invaders, and were killed, respectively, by Arthur and Lancelot at the Battle of Badon Hill (Mons Badonicus).
0 @N6000000007329673381@ NOTE Cerdic, the Saxon Chieftain who founded the Kingdom of Wesses is also
1 CONT considered the virtual founder of the British Monarchy.  Earlier genealogy
1 CONT is considered legendary.  The only qualification for kingship in early
1 CONT days was that they should be descended from Cerdic.  Otherwise, fitness to
1 CONT rule was the only other factor taken into consideration.  The genealogy
1 CONT prior to Cerdic should be considered a reignal one and not one of blood;
1 CONT as this always borders on legend.
1 CONT [Gordon Fisher[:  Alan B Wilson says in reply to Scott McGee (cf also
1 CONT Todd  Farmerie in reply to Tom Camfield), in connection with early
1 CONT medieval  genealogy of West Saxon kings in Britain:  'Beyond Cerdic is
1 CONT not historical.'
1 CONT Actually, there's doubt about the historicity of Cerdic.  For example,
1 CONT Lucien  Musset says in *The Germanic Invasions* (1975, translation of
1 CONT *Les Invasions:  Les Vagues Germaniques*, 1965), p 231-2:
1 CONT 'A good deal of our information about the origins of England, whether it
1 CONT comes from Britain itself or from elsewhere, has a markedly epic or
1 CONT mythological flavour.  .....  the attitude of historians at the beginning
1 CONT of  this century was categorical:  it was all a hopeless jumble in which
1 CONT crass  ignorance and the most extravagant flights of fancy vied for
1 CONT supremacy.  The  great French medievalist  Ferdinand Lot was the supreme
1 CONT exponent of this  critical attitude. .....   Since then historians have
1 CONT been cautiously reacting  against Lot's  hypercritical attitude.  .....
1 CONT Some scholars, like T. C.  Lethbridge and  C. F. C. Hawkes, would ...
1 CONT restore to these heroic figures  [viz,  Vortigern, Hengist and Horsa] the
1 CONT historical reality of which others  have  deprived them. Cerdic has been
1 CONT less fortunate:  his Anglian genealogy,  his  Celtic name, and his band
1 CONT of Jutes supposedly participating in the  foundation of a Saxon kingdom,
1 CONT have earned him universal distrust, especially  as archseology has shown
1 CONT that the alleged area of his landing,  near  Southampton, remained in
1 CONT British hands for at least fifty years after the  date given for his
1 CONT arrival.'
1 CONT Earlier in this century (the 20th of the Christian era, that is,  in case
1 CONT someone reads this in the 21st century of that era), Sir Charles Oman
1 CONT wrote  in *A History of England before the Norman Conquest* (5th edn 1924
1 CONT 1st was  1910), p 225:  'It is safer to regard the existence of any
1 CONT Cerdic as founder of the West  Saxon realm with deep suspicion.'  And on
1 CONT p 227, Oman refers to 'Ceawlin,  the  first historical king of the West
1 CONT Saxons'.//
1 CONT [Gordon Fisher, soc.med Sept '95]: With regard to my recent postings,
1 CONT one  concerning CREODA, listed as son of CERDIC and father of CYNRIC in
1 CONT Asser's  *Life of Alfred the Great*, and another concerning the
1 CONT historicity of CENRIC,  it has been argued that the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle
1 CONT (which omits CREODA) is not  reliable on CERDIC as father of CYNRIC.
1 CONT Here, for example,  is part of what  Sir  Charles Oman had to say on the
1 CONT subject:
1 CONT 'The first strange fact that we notice in this narrative [from the
1 CONT Anglo-Saxon  Chronicle] is that there is apparently a duplication of the
1 CONT incidents.  Cerdic and his son arrive in 495 at Cerdicsora, but in 514
1 CONT 'the West Saxons'  arrive at the same Cerdicsora under Stuf and Wihtgar.
1 CONT In 509 Cerdic and  Cynric slay Natan-leod in the district near
1 CONT Cerdicsford:  in 519 they fight  again at Cerdicsford and 'obtain the
1 CONT kingdom'.  When we  reflect that Cerdic  lands in 495 with a son who is
1 CONT full grown and able to  act as his colleague, i.  e., at an age that
1 CONT cannot be less than forty, and  probably is more, and that  he then
1 CONT proceeds to fight and conquer for a  period of thirty-five years
1 CONT (495-530) we cannot but be confirmed in our  suspicion that there is
1 CONT something  wrong with the chronology.  ..... Many  modern philologists
1 CONT tell us that  Cerdic (Ceretic, Certic) is not a  Teutonic name at all,
1 CONT but Celtic. .....  It is safer to regard the  existence of any Cerdic as
1 CONT founder of the West  Saxon realm with deep  suspicion.  There remains the
1 CONT most fatal objection of  all to the early  history of the kingdom of
1 CONT Wessex, as related by the  Anglo-Saxon Chronicle.   The lands where
1 CONT Cerdic, Port, Stuf and Wihtgar are found as conquerors in  this narrative
1 CONT are described by Bede, an authority far older and better  than  the
1 CONT Chronicle, as not Saxon at all but Jutish.  We are distinctly told by
1 CONT him, as has been already pointed out, that the Isle of Wight and  the
1 CONT lands  opposite it on the mainland had been settled by Jutes, and that
1 CONT even in his  own day the people on the mainland north of the Solent were
1 CONT called the  Jute-folk (*Jutorum natio*).  If this was so, we have no room
1 CONT for West  Saxon  conquerors, such as the alleged Cerdic and Cynric, in
1 CONT this direction. ....
1 CONT Ceawlin, as has been already said, is the first king  of the West Saxons
1 CONT of  whose existence we can be sure.  Bede mentions him  as next after
1 CONT Aella among  those monarchs who held an 'imperium' over all  Britain
1 CONT south of Humber.  The  Wessex genealogy which makes him son of  Cynric
1 CONT and grandson of Cerdic may  have been compiled at a very much later
1 CONT date.  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle  fixes his accession at 560; whether
1 CONT this  be correct or no, he was certainly  the leader who renewed the
1 CONT attack on  the Britons which had ceased since the  disaster at the Mons
1 CONT Badonicus in  516.'   - Sir Charles Oman, *A History of  England Before
1 CONT the Norman  Conquest*, 5th edn 1924, p 225-227 passim. //[Gordon Fisher[:  Alan B Wilson says in reply to Scott McGee (cf also
1 CONT Todd  Farmerie in reply to Tom Camfield), in connection with early
1 CONT medieval  genealogy of West Saxon kings in Britain:  'Beyond Cerdic is
1 CONT not historical.'
1 CONT Actually, there's doubt about the historicity of Cerdic.  For example,
1 CONT Lucien  Musset says in *The Germanic Invasions* (1975, translation of
1 CONT *Les Invasions:  Les Vagues Germaniques*, 1965), p 231-2:
1 CONT 'A good deal of our information about the origins of England, whether it
1 CONT comes from Britain itself or from elsewhere, has a markedly epic or
1 CONT mythological flavour.  .....  the attitude of historians at the beginning
1 CONT of  this century was categorical:  it was all a hopeless jumble in which
1 CONT crass  ignorance and the most extravagant flights of fancy vied for
1 CONT supremacy.  The  great French medievalist  Ferdinand Lot was the supreme
1 CONT exponent of this  critical attitude. .....   Since then historians have
1 CONT been cautiously reacting  against Lot's  hypercritical attitude.  .....
1 CONT Some scholars, like T. C.  Lethbridge and  C. F. C. Hawkes, would ...
1 CONT restore to these heroic figures  [viz,  Vortigern, Hengist and Horsa] the
1 CONT historical reality of which others  have  deprived them. Cerdic has been
1 CONT less fortunate:  his Anglian genealogy,  his  Celtic name, and his band
1 CONT of Jutes supposedly participating in the  foundation of a Saxon kingdom,
1 CONT have earned him universal distrust, especially  as archseology has shown
1 CONT that the alleged area of his landing,  near  Southampton, remained in
1 CONT British hands for at least fifty years after the  date given for his
1 CONT arrival.'
1 CONT Earlier in this century (the 20th of the Christian era, that is,  in case
1 CONT someone reads this in the 21st century of that era), Sir Charles Oman
1 CONT wrote  in *A History of England before the Norman Conquest* (5th edn 1924
1 CONT 1st was  1910), p 225:  'It is safer to regard the existence of any
1 CONT Cerdic as founder of the West  Saxon realm with deep suspicion.'  And on
1 CONT p 227, Oman refers to 'Ceawlin,  the  first historical king of the West
1 CONT Saxons'.//
1 CONT [Gordon Fisher, soc.med Sept '95]: With regard to my recent postings,
1 CONT one  concerning CREODA, listed as son of CERDIC and father of CYNRIC in
1 CONT Asser's  *Life of Alfred the Great*, and another concerning the
1 CONT historicity of CENRIC,  it has been argued that the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle
1 CONT (which omits CREODA) is not  reliable on CERDIC as father of CYNRIC.
1 CONT Here, for example,  is part of what  Sir  Charles Oman had to say on the
1 CONT subject:
1 CONT 'The first strange fact that we notice in this narrative [from the
1 CONT Anglo-Saxon  Chronicle] is that there is apparently a duplication of the
1 CONT incidents.  Cerdic and his son arrive in 495 at Cerdicsora, but in 514
1 CONT 'the West Saxons'  arrive at the same Cerdicsora under Stuf and Wihtgar.
1 CONT In 509 Cerdic and  Cynric slay Natan-leod in the district near
1 CONT Cerdicsford:  in 519 they fight  again at Cerdicsford and 'obtain the
1 CONT kingdom'.  When we  reflect that Cerdic  lands in 495 with a son who is
1 CONT full grown and able to  act as his colleague, i.  e., at an age that
1 CONT cannot be less than forty, and  probably is more, and that  he then
1 CONT proceeds to fight and conquer for a  period of thirty-five years
1 CONT (495-530) we cannot but be confirmed in our  suspicion that there is
1 CONT something  wrong with the chronology.  ..... Many  modern philologists
1 CONT tell us that  Cerdic (Ceretic, Certic) is not a  Teutonic name at all,
1 CONT but Celtic. .....  It is safer to regard the  existence of any Cerdic as
1 CONT founder of the West  Saxon realm with deep  suspicion.  There remains the
1 CONT most fatal objection of  all to the early  history of the kingdom of
1 CONT Wessex, as related by the  Anglo-Saxon Chronicle.   The lands where
1 CONT Cerdic, Port, Stuf and Wihtgar are found as conquerors in  this narrative
1 CONT are described by Bede, an authority far older and better  than  the
1 CONT Chronicle, as not Saxon at all but Jutish.  We are distinctly told by
1 CONT him, as has been already pointed out, that the Isle of Wight and  the
1 CONT lands  opposite it on the mainland had been settled by Jutes, and that
1 CONT even in his  own day the people on the mainland north of the Solent were
1 CONT called the  Jute-folk (*Jutorum natio*).  If this was so, we have no room
1 CONT for West  Saxon  conquerors, such as the alleged Cerdic and Cynric, in
1 CONT this direction. ....
1 CONT Ceawlin, as has been already said, is the first king  of the West Saxons
1 CONT of  whose existence we can be sure.  Bede mentions him  as next after
1 CONT Aella among  those monarchs who held an 'imperium' over all  Britain
1 CONT south of Humber.  The  Wessex genealogy which makes him son of  Cynric
1 CONT and grandson of Cerdic may  have been compiled at a very much later
1 CONT date.  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle  fixes his accession at 560; whether
1 CONT this  be correct or no, he was certainly  the leader who renewed the
1 CONT attack on  the Britons which had ceased since the  disaster at the Mons
1 CONT Badonicus in  516.'   - Sir Charles Oman, *A History of  England Before
1 CONT the Norman  Conquest*, 5th edn 1924, p 225-227 passim. //[Gordon Fisher[:  Alan B Wilson says in reply to Scott McGee (cf also
1 CONT Todd  Farmerie in reply to Tom Camfield), in connection with early
1 CONT medieval  genealogy of West Saxon kings in Britain:  'Beyond Cerdic is
1 CONT not historical.'
1 CONT Actually, there's doubt about the historicity of Cerdic.  For example,
1 CONT Lucien  Musset says in *The Germanic Invasions* (1975, translation of
1 CONT *Les Invasions:  Les Vagues Germaniques*, 1965), p 231-2:
1 CONT 'A good deal of our information about the origins of England, whether it
1 CONT comes from Britain itself or from elsewhere, has a markedly epic or
1 CONT mythological flavour.  .....  the attitude of historians at the beginning
1 CONT of  this century was categorical:  it was all a hopeless jumble in which
1 CONT crass  ignorance and the most extravagant flights of fancy vied for
1 CONT supremacy.  The  great French medievalist  Ferdinand Lot was the supreme
1 CONT exponent of this  critical attitude. .....   Since then historians have
1 CONT been cautiously reacting  against Lot's  hypercritical attitude.  .....
1 CONT Some scholars, like T. C.  Lethbridge and  C. F. C. Hawkes, would ...
1 CONT restore to these heroic figures  [viz,  Vortigern, Hengist and Horsa] the
1 CONT historical reality of which others  have  deprived them. Cerdic has been
1 CONT less fortunate:  his Anglian genealogy,  his  Celtic name, and his band
1 CONT of Jutes supposedly participating in the  foundation of a Saxon kingdom,
1 CONT have earned him universal distrust, especially  as archseology has shown
1 CONT that the alleged area of his landing,  near  Southampton, remained in
1 CONT British hands for at least fifty years after the  date given for his
1 CONT arrival.'
1 CONT Earlier in this century (the 20th of the Christian era, that is,  in case
1 CONT someone reads this in the 21st century of that era), Sir Charles Oman
1 CONT wrote  in *A History of England before the Norman Conquest* (5th edn 1924
1 CONT 1st was  1910), p 225:  'It is safer to regard the existence of any
1 CONT Cerdic as founder of the West  Saxon realm with deep suspicion.'  And on
1 CONT p 227, Oman refers to 'Ceawlin,  the  first historical king of the West
1 CONT Saxons'.//
1 CONT [Gordon Fisher, soc.med Sept '95]: With regard to my recent postings,
1 CONT one  concerning CREODA, listed as son of CERDIC and father of CYNRIC in
1 CONT Asser's  *Life of Alfred the Great*, and another concerning the
1 CONT historicity of CENRIC,  it has been argued that the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle
1 CONT (which omits CREODA) is not  reliable on CERDIC as father of CYNRIC.
1 CONT Here, for example,  is part of what  Sir  Charles Oman had to say on the
1 CONT subject:
1 CONT 'The first strange fact that we notice in this narrative [from the
1 CONT Anglo-Saxon  Chronicle] is that there is apparently a duplication of the
1 CONT incidents.  Cerdic and his son arrive in 495 at Cerdicsora, but in 514
1 CONT 'the West Saxons'  arrive at the same Cerdicsora under Stuf and Wihtgar.
1 CONT In 509 Cerdic and  Cynric slay Natan-leod in the district near
1 CONT Cerdicsford:  in 519 they fight  again at Cerdicsford and 'obtain the
1 CONT kingdom'.  When we  reflect that Cerdic  lands in 495 with a son who is
1 CONT full grown and able to  act as his colleague, i.  e., at an age that
1 CONT cannot be less than forty, and  probably is more, and that  he then
1 CONT proceeds to fight and conquer for a  period of thirty-five years
1 CONT (495-530) we cannot but be confirmed in our  suspicion that there is
1 CONT something  wrong with the chronology.  ..... Many  modern philologists
1 CONT tell us that  Cerdic (Ceretic, Certic) is not a  Teutonic name at all,
1 CONT but Celtic. .....  It is safer to regard the  existence of any Cerdic as
1 CONT founder of the West  Saxon realm with deep  suspicion.  There remains the
1 CONT most fatal objection of  all to the early  history of the kingdom of
1 CONT Wessex, as related by the  Anglo-Saxon Chronicle.   The lands where
1 CONT Cerdic, Port, Stuf and Wihtgar are found as conquerors in  this narrative
1 CONT are described by Bede, an authority far older and better  than  the
1 CONT Chronicle, as not Saxon at all but Jutish.  We are distinctly told by
1 CONT him, as has been already pointed out, that the Isle of Wight and  the
1 CONT lands  opposite it on the mainland had been settled by Jutes, and that
1 CONT even in his  own day the people on the mainland north of the Solent were
1 CONT called the  Jute-folk (*Jutorum natio*).  If this was so, we have no room
1 CONT for West  Saxon  conquerors, such as the alleged Cerdic and Cynric, in
1 CONT this direction. ....
1 CONT Ceawlin, as has been already said, is the first king  of the West Saxons
1 CONT of  whose existence we can be sure.  Bede mentions him  as next after
1 CONT Aella among  those monarchs who held an 'imperium' over all  Britain
1 CONT south of Humber.  The  Wessex genealogy which makes him son of  Cynric
1 CONT and grandson of Cerdic may  have been compiled at a very much later
1 CONT date.  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle  fixes his accession at 560; whether
1 CONT this  be correct or no, he was certainly  the leader who renewed the
1 CONT attack on  the Britons which had ceased since the  disaster at the Mons
1 CONT Badonicus in  516.'   - Sir Charles Oman, *A History of  England Before
1 CONT the Norman  Conquest*, 5th edn 1924, p 225-227 passim. //
0 @N6000000000167705109@ NOTE
1 CONT Cerdic, King of the West Saxons, 519-534, was a Saxon earldorman who founded a settlement on the coast of Hampshire, England, in 495, assumed the title of King of the West Saxons in 519, and became the ancestor of the English roya
1 CONC l line.  A.D. 495.  This year came two leaders into Britain, Cerdic and Cynric, his son, with five ships...519.  This year Cerdic and Cynric undertook the government of the West Saxons; the same year they fought with the Britons a
1 CONC t a place called Charford.  From that day have reigned the children of the West Saxon kings.  530.  They conquered the isle of Wight...534.  This year died Cerdic, the first king of the West Saxons.  Cynric his son succeeded to th
1 CONC e government, and reigned afterwards twenty-six winters.  (AngloSaxonChronicles, text by Ingram, Everyman's edition.  Trelawney Dayrell Reed, The Rise of Wessex, chart p. 31, gens. 1-8.)
