1 CONT CHARLES II I, FRANKISH EMPEROR (CHARLES THE FAT)
1 CONT Charles III, or Charles the Fat, b. 839 , d. Jan. 13, 888, Frankish
1 CONT Emperor, reunited for the last time the empire of CHARLEMAGNE (except
1 CONT Burgundy). He was the youngest son of Louis The German, from whom he
1 CONT inherited (876) the kingdom of Swabia. Three years later, on the
1 CONT resignation of his sick
1 CONT brother Carloman, he became king of Italy, and he was crowned emperor by
1 CONT Pope John VIII in 881. His succession to Saxony on the death (882) of his
1 CONT other brother Louis The Younger, made Charles King of all the East Franks
1 CONT (Germany). The deaths of the West Frankish kings Louis III ( 882) and
1 CONT Carolman (884) gave him France. Charles was deposed in 887 by Arnulf .
1 CONT Encyclopedia Britannica Online at britannica.com:
1 CONT Charles III
1 CONT born Sept. 17, 879
1 CONT died Oct. 7, 929, Péronne, Fr.
1 CONT by name Charles The Simple, French C harles Le Simple king of France
1 CONT (893-922), whose authority came to be accepte d by Lorraine and who
1 CONT settled the Northmen in Normandy but who became the fir st Carolingian
1 CONT ruler of the western kingdom to lose his crown.
1 CONT The posthumou s son of Louis II the Stammerer, Charles was too young to
1 CONT assume the throne o n the death of his half-brother, Carloman, in 884 or
1 CONT that of his cousin, Char les the Fat, in 888. On Jan. 28, 893, however, he
1 CONT was crowned king by Fulk, a rchbishop of Reims; and, although he renounced
1 CONT his rights after civil war in 897, the death of King Eudes in the
1 CONT following year brought him general recogn ition as king.
1 CONT Charles was strongly under the influence of Robert, brother of the dead
1 CONT Eudes. It was Robert's victory against the Northmen at Chartres in 9 11
1 CONT which paved the way for the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte later that
1 CONT ye ar, by which Charles ceded territory, in the area later known as
1 CONT Normandy, to the Viking leader Rollo and his men; in return, Rollo became
1 CONT a Christian and Charles's vassal. The Normans who had such an impact on
1 CONT Europe in the 11th a nd 12th centuries were the final product of this
1 CONT settlement.
1 CONT In 911 also, th e magnates of Lorraine (Lotharingia) accepted the
1 CONT authority of Charles on the death of the last Carolingian king of the
1 CONT East Franks, Louis the Child. Char les's preoccupation with Lotharingian
1 CONT affairs and councillors alienated the n obles of Neustria, however, and in
1 CONT 922 they elected Robert king. Charles kill ed Robert in battle in 923 but
1 CONT was soon taken prisoner by Herbert, count of V ermandois, who used him for
1 CONT his own gain against Rudolf, Robert's son-in-law and the new king.
1 CONT
