0 @N6000000002187580278@ NOTE Louis IV of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis IV (920 – September 10, 954), called d'Outremer or Transmarinus, reigned as king of France from 936 to 954, a member of the Carolingian dynasty, the son of Charles III and Eadgifu of England, a daughter of King Edward the El
1 CONC der. He was a fifth generation descendant of Charlemagne.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was only two years old when his father was deposed by the nobles, who set up Robert I in his place. When he was only three years old, Robert died and was replaced by Rudolph, duke of Burgundy. Rudolph's ally, a Carolingian hims
1 CONC elf, Count Herbert II of Vermandois, took Charles captive by treachery and the young Louis's mother took the boy "over the sea" to the safety of England, hence his nickname.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles died in 929, but Rudolph ruled on until 936, when Louis was summoned back to France unanimously by the nobles, especially Hugh the Great, who had probably organised his return to prevent Herbert II, or Rudolph's brother Hu
1 CONC gh the Black, taking the throne. He was crowned king at Laon by Artald, archbishop of Rheims, on Sunday 19 June 936. Effectively, his sovereignty was limited to the town of Laon and to some places in the north of France, Louis dis
1 CONC played a keenness beyond his years in obtaining the recognition of his authority by his feuding nobles. Nonetheless, his reign was filled with conflict; in particular with Hugh the Great, count of Paris.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 939, Louis became involved in a struggle with the Emperor Otto the Great on the question of Lorraine, but then married Otto's sister Gerberga of Saxony (914 – May 5, 984). They had two sons and a daughter (and 5 more children w
1 CONC ithout issue): Lothair, his successor; Charles, later duke of Lower Lorraine; and Matilda.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis IV fell from his horse and died September 10, 954, at Rheims, in the Marne, and is interred there at Saint-Remi Cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Raoul King of Western Francia
1 CONT 936–954 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Lothair 
0 @N6000000002322288545@ NOTE He spent most of his rein struggling against his powerful vassal, Hugh the
1 CONT Great, as Louis proved not to be the puppet monarch Hugh had anticipated.
1 CONT In 945, while intervening in Norman politics, Louis was captured and handed
1 CONT over to Hugh, who imprisoned him for a year. On his release, Louis closely
1 CONT allied himself with Otto I to retake Reims in 946. In 949 Louis again
1 CONT received control of Laon, and Hugh, excommunicated by French and German
1 CONT synods and by the Pope, made peace in 951 that lasted until Louis's death.
