0 @N6000000002187651194@ NOTE Liudolf, Duke of Swabia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Liudolf (930–6 September 957) was the duke of Swabia from 950 until 954. He was the only son of Otto I, king of Germany, from his wife Eadgyth, daughter of Edward the Elder, king of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Liudolf married Ida, daughter of Duke Herman I. When Herman died, Otto appointed his eldest son and heir apparent duke. Liudolf was a popular ruler with the tribe. After the usurpation of Berengar II of Italy, he invaded Lombardy 
1 CONC in 951. His father foiled his plans and invaded as well, leaving Liudolf without much gains. When Otto married Adelaide, the heiress to Italy, Liudolf felt his position threatened and raised the flag of revolt in 953. Though he ha
1 CONC d the support of his Swabians, his ally, Conrad the Red, was opposed by his own subjects in Lorraine. The Bavarians of Henry I, Duke of Bavaria, Liudolf's uncle, support Liudolf, but Henry and Otto together put down the rebellion.
1 CONC  In 954, he was deprived of his duchy and, though reconciled with his father, he did not regain it. He invaded Italy for a second time in 957 and many cities capitulated before him and Berengar fled. He died unexpectedly of fever 
1 CONC amidst his victorious campaign at Pombia, near Novara, on September 6.
1 CONT
1 CONT His son by Ida, Otto, was later duke of Bavaria and Swabia, his daughter Mathilde abess of a canoness monastery in Essen. He also founded the city of Stuttgart in southern Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Herman I Duke of Swabia
1 CONT 950–954 Succeeded by
1 CONT Burchard III 
0 @N6000000002176646270@ NOTE Liuolf, too, had risen against his father with the aid of several magnates,
1 CONT compelling Otto to withdraw from the Italian campaign to Saxony; but the
1 CONT position of the rebels began to deteriorate when the Magyars invaded Germany
1 CONT in 955, for the rebels could not be accused of complicity with the enemy of
1 CONT the Reich. After prolonged fighting, Liudolf had to submith in 955, enabling
1 CONT Otto to decisively defeat the Magyors in the Battle of the Lechfeld, near
1 CONT Augsburg, in August 955; they never invaded Germany again.
