0 @N6000000003300400669@ NOTE EDGAR (r. 959-975)
1 CONT Edgar, king in Mercia and the Danelaw from 957, succeeded his brother a s king of the English on Edwy's death in 959 - a death which probably pre vented civil war breaking out between the two brothers. Edgar was a fir m and capabl
1 CONC e ruler whose power was acknowledged by other rulers in Brita in, as well as by Welsh and Scottish kings. Edgar's late coronation in 97 3 at Bath was the first to be recorded in some detail; his queen Aelfthry th was the first con
1 CONC sort to be crowned queen of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edgar was the patron of a great monastic revival which owed much to his a ssociation with Archbishop Dunstan. New bishoprics were created, Benedict ine monasteries were reformed and old monastic sites were re-endowed wit h royal g
1 CONC rants, some of which were of land recovered from the Vikings.
1 CONT In the 970s and in the absence of Viking attacks, Edgar - a stern judg e - issued laws which for the first time dealt with Northumbria (parts o f which were in the Danelaw) as well as Wessex and Mercia. Edgar's coinag e was unifor
1 CONC m throughout the kingdom. A more united kingdom based on roya l justice and order was emerging; the Monastic Agreement (c.970) praise d Edgar as 'the glorious, by the grace of Christ illustrious king of th e English and of the oth
1 CONC er peoples dwelling within the bounds of the isla nd of Britain'. After his death on 8 July 975, Edgar was buried at Glasto nbury Abbey, Somerset.
