0 @N6000000002187592318@ NOTE Edgar the Peaceful
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Edgar the Peaceful 
1 CONT King of England 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign October 1, 959 – July 8, 975 
1 CONT Born 943/944 
1 CONT  Wessex, England 
1 CONT Died July 8, 975 
1 CONT  Winchester, Wessex, England 
1 CONT Buried Glastonbury Abbey 
1 CONT Predecessor Edwy 
1 CONT Successor Edward the Martyr 
1 CONT Consort Æthelflæd, Wulfthryth and Ælfthryth 
1 CONT Issue Edward the Martyr
1 CONT Ethelred the Unready 
1 CONT Father Edmund I 
1 CONT Mother Elgiva 
1 CONT
1 CONT King Edgar, also known as Edgar the Peaceful (c. 943 or 944 – July 8, 975) was the younger son of Edmund I of England. His cognomen, "the Peaceable", was not necessarily a comment on the deeds of his life, for he was a strong lead
1 CONC er, shown by the seizure of the Northumbrian and Mercian kingdoms from his older brother, Edwy, in 958. Edgar was held to be king north of the Thames by a conclave of his nobles, and the aspirational ruler set himself to succeed t
1 CONC o the English throne. With Edwy's death in October 959, Edgar immediately recalled Dunstan (eventually canonised as St. Dunstan) from exile to have him made Bishop of Worcester (and the Bishop of London after, and finally the Arch
1 CONC bishop of Canterbury). The allegation Dunstan at first refused to crown Edgar because of disapproval for his way of life is a discreet reference in popular histories to Edgar's mistress[citation needed], Wulfthryth (later a nun at
1 CONC  Wilton), who bore him a daughter Eadgyth. Dunstan remained Edgar's advisor throughout his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edgar's reign was a peaceful one, and it is probably fair to say that it saw the Anglo-Saxon kingdom of England at its height. Although the political unity of England was the achievement of his predecessors, it was Edgar who saw t
1 CONC o its consolidation. By the end of Edgar's reign there was practically no likelihood of any recession back to its state of rival kingships, and the division of its domains.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Monastic Reform Movement that restored the Benedictine Rule to England's undisciplined monastic communities saw its height during the time of Dunstan, Aethelwold and Oswald. However, the extent and importance of the movement i
1 CONC s still debated amongst academics.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Obverse of a penny minted for Edgar.Edgar was crowned at Bath, but not until 973, in an imperial ceremony planned not as the initiation, but as the culmination of his reign (a move that must have taken a great deal of preliminary 
1 CONC diplomacy). This service, devised by Dunstan himself and celebrated with a poem in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle forms the basis of the present-day British coronation ceremony. The symbolic coronation was an important step; other king
1 CONC s of Britain came and gave their allegiance to Edgar shortly afterwards at Chester. Six kings in Britain, including the kings of Scotland and of Strathclyde, pledged their faith that they would be the king's liege-men on sea and l
1 CONC and. Later chroniclers made the kings into eight, all plying the oars of Edgar's state barge on the River Dee. Such embellishments may not be factual, but the main outlines of the "submission at Chester" appear true.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edgar had several children. He died on July 8, 975 at Winchester, and was buried at Glastonbury Abbey. He left two sons, the eldest named Edward, the son of his first wife Ethelfleda (not to be confused with Ethelfleda, Lady of th
1 CONC e Mercians), and Ethelred, the youngest, the child of his second wife Ælfthryth. He was succeeded by his eldest son, Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Edgar’s death to the Norman Conquest there was not a single succession to the throne that was not contested. Although perhaps a simplification, Edgar’s death did seem to be the beginning of the end for Anglo-Saxon England tha
1 CONC t resulted in three 11th century successful conquests, two Danish and one Norman.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Genealogy
1 CONT For a more complete genealogy including ancestors and descendants, see House of Wessex family tree.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Diagram based on the information found on Wikipedia
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Medieval Sourcebook: Anglo-Saxon Dooms: laws of King Edgar, a fragment 
1 CONT Edgar of England At Find A Grave 
1 CONT Oxford Dictionary of National Biography entry 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Edwy King of England
1 CONT 959–975 Succeeded by
1 CONT Edward the Martyr 
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT King of Mercia 957 - 959. King of Northumbria 957 - 959. King of the English 959 - 975. Edgar was crowned by Dunstan, Archbishop of Canterbury, and Oswald, Archbishop of York. Edgar is considered to have been the first king of a u
1 CONC nited England. Affair with Wulfthryth (his mistress). Child: Edith (illegitimate). Events: Coronation May 11, 973. Bath Abbey, Avon, England. Source: RoyaList, Leo van de Pas. 
0 @N6000000002322323997@ NOTE One of the first acts was to recall monastic reformer St. Dunstan, exiled by
1 CONT his brother. Ultimately, St. Dunstan was made Archbishop of Canterbury. In
1 CONT 959, Edgar succeeded to the entire English kingdom. His Reign was notable for
1 CONT establishment of national consolidation, reformation of the clergy,
1 CONT improvement of the judiciary system and formation of a new fleet to defend
1 CONT the coast from Scandinavian Vikings.
