1 CONT From http://www.camelotintl.com/royal/cgi/person?p=164:
1 CONT
1 CONT Title(s): King of Mercia ( 957 - 959); King of Northumbria ( 957 - 959); King of the English ( 959 - 975)
1 CONT Cause of death: Natural Causes
1 CONT Buried at: Glastonbury Abbey, Somerset, England
1 CONT Marriages: 959: At age 15 married Ethelflaed; 965: At age 21 married Elfthryth, age 20; Unknown date: Wulfthryth
1 CONT
1 CONT May 11, 973:  Coronation, Bath Abbey, Avon, England
1 CONT
1 CONT  Children:
1 CONT              With Ethelflaed:  Edward II, King of the English
1 CONT              With Elfthryth: Edmund;  Ethelred II, King of the English
1 CONT              With Wulfthryth: Edith
1 CONT
1 CONT  Notes: 1. Edgar was crowned by Dunstan, Archbishop of Canterbury, and Oswald, Archbishop of York.
1 CONT              2. Edgar is considered to have been the first king of a united England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edgar ruled 959-975. Edgar was born in 943. He was married to Æthelflaed, who gave him his heir Edward, Wulfryth whose child was Eadgyth, and finally his third wife, Ælfthryth gave him Edmund and Æthelred.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edgar had challenged his predecessor to the throne, and had taken over Mercia at the age of fourteen. He gained the English throne when he was sixteen.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the King who made the public recognition of English majesty, by a delayed coronation, fourteen years after his accession, and was paid homage by all the Scottish and Welsh kings, who were said to have rowed him on the Dee a
1 CONC t Chester.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is claimed that he stole his second wife from a nunnery, but before marrying her, he made her be a mistress for some years. Knowing that doing this would have put him in rather an unfavourable light in the the eyes of the Churc
1 CONC h, Edgar helped a monastic revival within England. He founded forty religious houses, and aided architecture at this time.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was lucky to have the sagious advice of three saints who were alive in this era, and the fact that they were canonised in this time, gives great praise to the reign of Edgar. His reign eventually gave great power to the Church.
