0 @N6000000002134196377@ NOTE king of England (known as the Peaceful or Peaceable) was born in 944 and was the younger of the two sons of Edmund I. During the reign of his brother Edwy, he was chosen king of Mercia and Northumbria, and succeeded Edwy in 959. H
1 CONC e recalled Dunstan (previously exiled by his brother Edwy), made him bishop of Worcester, of London, and, on the death of Odo, archbishop of Canterbury, and gave himself up to his direction. His reign was notable for the establish
1 CONC ment of national consolidation, reformation of the clergy, improvement of the judiciary system. The reign of Edgar was peaceful, the Northmen making no descents on England, perhaps in consequence of the large fleet kept up by the 
1 CONC king. Monasteries were restored, and many new ones built; the married clergy expelled, and church power raised to a higher point than before, which made Edgar a favourite and got him a good name with monkish historians. Edgar was 
1 CONC not crowned till 973, and the same year took place the stately ceremonial on the Dee, when six or eight subject kings attended him. Edgar is said to have imposed on the Welsh an annual tribute of 300 wolves' heads, instead of a mo
1 CONC ney tax. Died, 975. He left two sons, Edward(the Martyr) and Ethelred, who both succeeded to the crown
0 @N6000000002176666876@ NOTE One of the first acts was to recall monastic reformer St. Dunstan, exiled by
1 CONT his brother. Ultimately, St. Dunstan was made Archbishop of Canterbury. In
1 CONT 959, Edgar succeeded to the entire English kingdom. His Reign was notable for
1 CONT establishment of national consolidation, reformation of the clergy,
1 CONT improvement of the judiciary system and formation of a new fleet to defend
1 CONT the coast from Scandinavian Vikings.
0 @N6000000001956352457@ NOTE
1 CONT King of the Mercians and Northumbrians from 957 who became king of the West Saxons, or Wessex, in 959 and is reckoned as king of all England from that year. He was efficient and tolerant of local customs, and his reign was peacefu
1 CONC l. He was most important as a patron of the English monastic revival.
1 CONT The younger son of Edmund I, king of the English, Edgar was made king of the Mercians and Northumbrians in place of Eadwig, his brother, who was deposed. On Eadwig's death (Oct. 1, 959), Edgar succeeded to the West Saxon throne. H
1 CONC is ecclesiastical policy was also that of the archbishop of Canterbury, St. Dunstan, who insisted on strict observance of theBenedictine Rule. The king supported Archbishop Oswald of York and Bishop Aethelwold of Winchester in fou
1 CONC nding abbeys. Edgar's laws were the first in England to prescribe penalties for nonpayment of tithes and Peter's pence, the annual contribution made by Roman Catholics for support of the Holy See.
