0 @N6000000003615683277@ NOTE The Earl Leofric's successor in 1055 was their son Aelfgar. When Aelfgar himself died in 1062, the King appointed Aelfgar's son Eadwine to succeed him in the Mercian dynasty. Lady Godiva's grandson Eadwine is supposed to have been
1 CONC  only fourteen years old when he became Earl of Mercia. Eadwine had a younger brother, Morkere, who succeeded, by a rebellion of the Northumbrian nobility, to the ill-fated Tostig's Earldom of Northumbria in 1065. Young Morkere wa
1 CONC s deprived of it by the Conqueror, who gave Northumbria to Gospatrick of the royal Scottish line.
0 @N6000000003949631257@ NOTE Aelfgar, of age 1051, d. shortly after 1062, Earl of East Anglia 1053, Earl of Mercia 1057, banished 1058; m. Aelfgifu, by whom 3 known sons: Eadwine, Morkere, and Burchard, whose issue are unknown, and a daughter Aldgyth [as well
1 CONC  as Edith or Aldgyth]. [Ancestral Roots, Line 176a-3]
1 CONT     Last year I bought a wonderful book "The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle" translated and edited by Michael Swanton, there are a few family trees and in this is shown that Aelfgar, Earl of East Anglia 1051-1057, Earl of Mercia 1057 was m
1 CONC arried to Aelgifu, daughter of Siferth thegn of the Seven Boroughs (killed in 1015) and Ealdgyth (daughter of Aelfthryth) who secondly married Edmund Ironside, King of England. Aelfthryth (son of Wulfrun) is a brother of Aelfhelm,
1 CONC  Ealdorman of Northumbria (killed 1006) who in turn was father of Aelgifu married to Cnut King of England. These genealogical pages are the last of the book before the bibliography. I hope you have a copy otherwise would you like 
1 CONC me to photocopy and post?  Leo van de Pas
0 @N6000000004533059441@ NOTE Aelfgar, of age 1051, d. shortly after 1062, Earl of East Anglia1053, Earl of Mercia 1057, banished 1058; m. Aelfgifu, by whom 3 knownsons: Eadwine, Morkere, and Burchard, whose issue are unknown, and adaughter Aldgyth [as well as
1 CONC  Edith or Aldgyth].  [Ancestral Roots,Line 176a-3]
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is excerpted from a post to SGM, 22 Jun 1999, by Leo vande Pas:
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Leofric and Godiva :
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfgar "the Saxon", Earl of Mercia, died circa 1059/1062 was marriedto Elfgifu, daughter of Sigeferth and Ealdgyth. In 1055, he was forcedto seek the protection of Gruffyd in Wales, in that year Gruffyd andAlfgar burned down St.Ae
1 CONC thelbert's minster and all the town ofHereford.
1 CONT
1 CONT    In 1058 Alfgar, without having given reason, was outlawed, and wentto Ireland and Wales where he got himself a great band and thentravelled to Hereford. After a violent battle Alfgar was reinstatedand given back all that had ea
1 CONC rlier been taken from him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfgar and Elfgifu are the parents of :
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.Edwin
1 CONT
1 CONT 2.Morcar
1 CONT
1 CONT 3.Ealdgyth of Mercia  married (1) circa 1056 Gruffyd ap Llywelyn, Kingof Deheubarth 1044-1063, son of Llywelyn ap Seiswill, King ofDeheubarth and Gwynedd, and Angharad of Wales
