0 @N6000000007329725201@ NOTE [Alan Wilson <abwilson -at- uclink2.berkeley>, qoting Weis 7th ed.,
1 CONT 1992,  and others] ..not included in Weis.
1 CONT [Lawrence Crowl, 503-737-2554    Computer Science Department <crowl
1 CONT -at-  cs.  orst.edu>, Oregon State University]: 'Alan B. Wilson' wrote:
1 CONT > 1.  Cerdic of Wessex King (Circa 467 - 534).  > [2. Creoda of Wessex
1 CONT Prince (Circa 493 - )not included in Weis].  > 3.  Cynric of Wessex King
1 CONT (Circa 500 - 560)  (son of Cerdic in   Anglo  Saxon  Chronicles).
1 CONT > 4.  Ceawlin of Wessex King (Circa 525 - 591/593).  and ...Tom Camfield
1 CONT <camfield -at- olympus.net> wrote:  >Where does CREODA come from? I have
1 CONT what I believe to be a quote   from the  >Saxon Chronicle: 'A.D. 495 -
1 CONT This year came two leaders into   Britain,  Cerdic  >and Cynric, his son,
1 CONT with five shiops, at a place that is called  Cerdic's-ore. And >they
1 CONT fought with the Welsh the same day.' Cynric   then  should be numbered 2
1 CONT (above). Was Creoda his brother? If so, was he a   later  arrival?
1 CONT The entry from Dorothy Whitelock, 'The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, A   Revised
1 CONT Translation' reads:
1 CONT 495  In this year two chieftains, Cerdic and his son Cynric, came with
1 CONT five  ships to Britain at the place which is called   Cerdicesora , and
1 CONT they fought  against the Britons on the same day.
1 CONT Creoda comes from a much later entry in  some  copies of the   Chronicle.
1 CONT (The copies have  many  inconsistencies, additions, and omissions.)
1 CONT 855-858  ... the son of Ceawlin, the son of Cynric, the son of Creoda,
1 CONT the  son of Cerdic.
1 CONT My interpretation is that Creoda should not be present in the
1 CONT genealogy.  However, I suspect people tend to leave it in so as not to
1 CONT lose   information.  //
1 CONT [unknown gedcom from D. Pettit to R. Demaray May '96] Creeda Ruler   of
1 CONT West  Saxons.
