0 @N6000000006296745571@ NOTE Ælfthryth (c. 945-1000, also Alfrida, Elfrida or Elfthryth) was the second or third wife of King Edgar of England </wiki/Edgar_of_England>. Ælfthryth was the first king's wife known to have been crowned </wiki/Coronation> and anoi
1 CONC nted </wiki/Anointing> as Queen of the Kingdom of England </wiki/Kingdom_of_England>. Mother of King Ethelred the Unready </wiki/Ethelred_the_Unready>, she was a powerful political figure. She was linked to the murder of her steps
1 CONC on King Edward the Martyr </wiki/Edward_the_Martyr> and appeared as a stereotypical bad Queen and evil stepmother in many medieval histories.
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=%C3%86lfthryth,_wife_of_Edgar&action=edit&section=1>] Early life
1 CONT Ælfthryth was the daughter of Ealdorman </wiki/Ealdorman> Orgar </wiki/Orgar>. Her mother was a member of the royal family of Wessex </wiki/Wessex>. The family's power lay in the west of Wessex. Ordgar was buried in Exeter </wiki/
1 CONC Exeter> and his son Ordwulf founded, or refounded, Tavistock Abbey </wiki/Tavistock_Abbey>.[1]
1 CONT Ælfthryth was first married to Æthelwald </wiki/%C3%86thelwald,_Ealdorman_of_East_Anglia>, son of Æthelstan Half-King </wiki/%C3%86thelstan_Half-King> as recorded by Byrhtferth of Ramsey </wiki/Byrhtferth_of_Ramsey> in his Life of
1 CONC  Saint Oswald of Worcester </wiki/Oswald_of_Worcester>.[2] Later accounts, such as that preserved by William of Malmesbury </wiki/William_of_Malmesbury>, add vivid detail of unknown reliability.
1 CONT According to William, the beauty of Ordgar's daughter Ælfthryth was reported to King Edgar. Edgar, looking for a Queen, sent Æthewald to see Ælfthryth, ordering him "to offer her marriage [to Edgar] if her beauty were really equal
1 CONC  to report." When she turned out to be just as beautiful as was said, Æthelwald married her himself and reported back to Edgar that she was quite unsuitable. Edgar was eventually told of this, and decided to repay Æthelwald's betr
1 CONC ayal in like manner. He said that he would visit the poor woman, which alarmed Æthelwald. He asked Ælfthryth to make herself as unattractive as possible for the king's visit, but she did the opposite. Edgar, quite besotted with he
1 CONC r, killed Æthelwald during a hunt.[3]
1 CONT The historical record does not record the year of Æthelwald's death, let alone its manner. No children of Æthelwald and Ælfthryth are known.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=%C3%86lfthryth,_wife_of_Edgar&action=edit&section=2>] Edgar's queen
1 CONT Edgar had previously been married to Æthelflæd, by whom he had a son named Edward, and perhaps to Wulfthryth, with whom he had a daughter named Eadgifu-later known as Saint Edith of Wilton </wiki/Edith_of_Wilton>. Sound political 
1 CONC reasons encouraged the match between Edgar, whose power base was centred in Mercia </wiki/Mercia>, and Ælfthryth, whose family were powerful in Wessex. In addition to this, and her link with the family of Æthelstan Half-King, Ælft
1 CONC hryth also appears to have been connected to the family of Ælfhere, Ealdorman of Mercia </wiki/%C3%86lfhere,_Ealdorman_of_Mercia>.[4]
1 CONT Edgar married Ælfthryth in either 964 or 965. In 966 Ælfthryth gave birth to a son who was named Edmund. In King Edgar's charter (S 745) regranting privileges to New Minster, Winchester </wiki/New_Minster,_Winchester> that same ye
1 CONC ar, the infant Edmund is called "clito legitimus" (legitimate ætheling </wiki/%C3%86theling>), and appears before Edward in the list of witnesses. Edmund died young, circa </wiki/Circa> 970, but in 968 Ælfthryth had given birth to
1 CONC  a second son who was called Æthelred.[5]
1 CONT King Edgar organised a second coronation, perhaps to bolster his claims to be ruler of all of Britain </wiki/Great_Britain> at Bath </wiki/Bath,_Somerset> on 11 May </wiki/May_11> 973 </wiki/973>. Here Ælfthryth was also crowned a
1 CONC nd anointed, granting her a status higher than any recent queen.[6]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=%C3%86lfthryth,_wife_of_Edgar&action=edit&section=3>] Queen dowager
1 CONT Edgar died in 975 leaving two sons, Edward and Æthelred. Edward was almost an adult, and was supported by many key figures including Archbishops Dunstan </wiki/Dunstan> and Oswald </wiki/Oswald_of_Worcester> and the brother of Ælf
1 CONC thryth's first husband, Æthelwine, Ealdorman of East Anglia </wiki/%C3%86thelwine,_Ealdorman_of_East_Anglia>. Supporting the claims of the child Æthelred were the Queen dowager, Bishop Æthelwold of Winchester </wiki/%C3%86thelwold
1 CONC _of_Winchester>, and Ælfhere, Ealdorman of Mercia.[7]
1 CONT On 18 March </wiki/March_18> 978 </wiki/978>, while visiting Ælfthryth at Corfe </wiki/Corfe_Castle>, King Edward was killed by servants of the Queen, leaving the way clear for Æthelred to be installed as king. Edward was soon con
1 CONC sidered a martyr, and Ælfthryth blamed for his murder. Due to Æthelred's youth, Ælfthryth served as regent for her son until his coming of age in 984. By then her earlier allies Æthelwold and Ælfhere had died, and she withdrew fro
1 CONC m the court at this time. However, she remained an important figure, being responsible for the care of Æthelred's children by his first wife, Ælfgifu </wiki/%C3%86lfgifu_of_York>.[8]
1 CONT Although her reputation was marked by the murder of her stepson, Ælfthryth was a religious woman, taking an especial interest in monastic reform when Queen. Late in life she retired to Wherwell </wiki/Wherwell> where she died on 1
1 CONC 7 November </wiki/November_17>, between 999 and 1001.[9]
0 @N6000000007283504582@ NOTE
1 CONT Early Life and First Marriage
1 CONT She was born at Lydford, the daughter of Earl Ordgar, Alderman of Devon and Wulfrith Redburch. Elfrida was married c. 962-963 to Ethelwold, Alderman of the East Angles.
1 CONT Ethelwold was later killed after being struck by a spear while hunting with King Edgar. Rumors at the time alleged that Edgar had deliberately slain Ethelwold in order to marry Elfrida.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT As Queen Consort
1 CONT Following her marriage to Edgar in 965, Elfrida bore a son, Ethelred (Ethelred the Unready), in 968. She was crowned queen alongside her husband at Bath Abbey on May 11, 973, making her the second English queen to actually be crow
1 CONC ned with the title (the first having been Ethelwulf's second wife Judith). King Edgar died in 975.
1 CONT Elfrida was alleged to have been a party to the murder of her step-son Edward the Martyr at her residence in Corfe Castle on March 18, 978. This was to place her son Ethelred on the throne as King. Elfrida believed her son's claim
1 CONC  to the throne was superior to her step-son's because both Ethelred's parents had been crowned and consecrated. Henry of Huntingdon relates:
1 CONT "[Edward] was treasonably slain by his own family... it is reported that his stepmother, that is the mother of King Ethelred, stabbed him with a dagger while she was in the act of offering him a cup to drink."
1 CONT That Elfrida had her henchmen commit the murder for her is a more likely scenario. In 1970 an examination was performed on remains believed to be Edward's, which had been unearthed in 1931 in the ruins of Shaftesbury Abbey. The ex
1 CONC amination suggested that the young man had been stabbed in the back while riding his horse and had been dragged along the ground by the animal with his foot caught in a stirrup.
1 CONT Following the murder, in 979 Elfrida founded the Benedictine Abbey of St. Mary and St. Melor (near Amesbury, England). In 986 she became a nun and founded the Benedictine Monastery at Wherwell, Hampshire. She died on November 17, 
1 CONC 1000.
