0 @N6000000002187651184@ NOTE Conrad, Duke of Lorraine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Conrad the Red (German: Konrad der Rote; died 10 August 955) was the Duke of Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Werner, Count of the Nahegau, Speyergau, and Wormsgau. His mother was a sister of Conrad I of Germany. In 941, he succeeded his father in his counties and obtained an additional territory, the Niddagau. In 944 or
1 CONC  945, he was also invested with Lorraine by King Otto I.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 947, he married Liutgard, daughter of Otto and Edith, daughter of Edward the Elder, King of England. He and Liutgard had one son, Otto of Worms, later duke of Swabia and Carinthia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 953, Conrad joined his brother-in-law, Liudolf, Duke of Swabia, in rebellion against Otto, who bitterly complained about Conrad's ingratitude. The rebellion was quashed and Conrad was deprived of Lorraine, which was instead gra
1 CONC nted to Otto's brother, Bruno I, Archbishop of Cologne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eventually Conrad and Otto were reconciled, and, in 955, Conrad was killed in the Battle of Lechfeld while fighting alongside Otto against the Magyars. According to the chronicler Widukind of Corvey:
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Conrad, the foremost of all in combat, suffering from battle fatigue caused by an unusually hot sun, loosened the straps of his armor to catch his breath when an arrow pierced his throat and killed him instantly.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conrad's body was carried in state to Worms, where he was given a lavish funeral and buried at the cathedral by his son Otto. Conrad was the great-grandfather of Conrad II, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Weinfurter, Stefan. The Salian Century: Main Currents in an Age of Transition, 1999 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT vacant Duke of Lotharingia
1 CONT 944-953 Succeeded by
1 CONT Bruno the Great 
0 @N6000000002322312113@ NOTE Lotharingia, later known as Lorraine. Conrad was an ancestor of the Salian
1 CONT dynasty of the German kings. He belonged to a family of Franconian counts
1 CONT with rich lands around Speyer and Worms. After helping Otto, later Otto I
1 CONT emperor, suppress a rebellion, he was given the duchy of Lotharingia and the
1 CONT hand in marriage to Otto's daughter, Liudgard. He was killed in the Battle of
1 CONT Lechfeld, when the largest Hungarian army to invade German lands was
1 CONT destroyed. His services there seem to have been decisive for Otto's victory.
