0 @N6000000001578886448@ NOTE He was married 3 times and had 16 children including Edmund II Ironside, King of England, (born 989) and Edward (St.) the Confessor, King of England, (born c1002).
1 CONT In the face of Danish raids, he was forced to pay huge tributes (Danegeld) to the enemy.  His reprisal was the St. Brides massacre in which all Danes living in England were killed.  He chose the 13th November 1002 for this co-ordi
1 CONC nated massacre, St. Brice's day. The deed was done. Thousands were killed including the sister of the king of Denmark, Swegn ( Sweyn ) Forkbeard. This so incensed Swegn, that he planned a near full scale invasion of England. Massa
1 CONC cres by Danes were catalogued all over the country. England was more or less bankrupt and starving. Even the Danes returned to Denmark in 1005. They returned in 1006 and demanded even more danegeld. Aethelred agreed to 36,000 poun
1 CONC ds of silver in a final attempt to buy peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Danish fleet was often based in Greenwich as a base to attack London.  The Archbishop of Canterbury (Alfege) was murdered in Greenwich by a Danish invading force on Easter Day 1012.  He was driven into exile in Normandy by Swe
1 CONC yn but returned after his death. Died during Cnut's invasion of England.
1 CONT Æthelred II, the Unready (978-1016 AD)
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded to the throne after the murder of his half-brother, Edward II, the Martyr, at the age of ten. His reign was plagued by poor advice from his personal favorites and suspicions of his complicity in Edward's murder. His w
1 CONC as a rather long and ineffective reign, which was notable for little other than the payment of the Danegeld, an attempt to buy off the Viking invaders with money. The relentless invasions by the Danish Vikings, coupled with their 
1 CONC ever-escalating demands for more money, forced him to abandon his throne in 1013. He fled to Normandy for safety, but was later recalled to his old throne at the death of Svein Forkbeard in 1014. He died in London in 1016.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred II became king following the murder of Edward the Martyr. What followed was a disastrous time for England where all the good work was to go to waste. England, as affluent as it had ever been since the time of the Romans w
1 CONC as about to descend back into the dark pit it had spent all those years climbing out of. To blame Aethelred for all that was about to happen would be unfair, but one must ask the question of how Alfred, Edward I or Aethelstan woul
1 CONC d have dealt with the particular set of circumstances that now confronted Aethelred. His weak leadership and favouritism for selected henchmen. with nepotism abound, probably because of his perpetual fear of insurrection and plot,
1 CONC  England declined rapidly. The countries strength had kept the Danes away. In 980 the Danes returned in force. England was slowly returning to the dark ages again. Aethelred did virtually nothing. It was left to valiant individual
1 CONC s to resist their advance. The most famous was the Battle of Maldon in 991, where Byrhtnoth, the reeve of Essex defended the causeway from the onslaught before being killed
1 CONT
1 CONT Time Line
1 CONT 1002-13 November (St. Brice's Day): Ethelred ordered the massacre of Danes living in England
1 CONT 1002/3: Birth of Edward (the Confessor) - Sweyn Forkbeard invaded England (again)
1 CONT 1007: Danes bought off from England by Ethelred - Ship money first levied in England
1 CONT 1009: Danish invasion of England
1 CONT 1012: 19 April - St. Alphege (954 - 1012) martyred on Easter Sunday, on spot of St. Alfege Church in Greenwich.
1 CONT 1013: Sweyn Forkbeard invaded England (again) - Ethelred fled to Norway
0 @N6000000002319320872@ NOTE Suspected by many of complicity in Edward's murder, he seemed an inefficient
1 CONT ruler with successions of Danish raids. While the kingdom of England was
1 CONT conquered, it escaped disintegration under him. 1013: King of Denmark was
1 CONT recognized as "king everywhere" and Ethelred exiled at his brother-in-law's
1 CONT court, Richard, Duke of Normandy. Sweg Forkbeard, the Danish King, died in
1 CONT 1014, however, and the English nobility recalled Ethelred. He was succeeded
1 CONT by his son, Edmund II, when he died in the spring of 1016.
0 @N6000000002329577547@ NOTE Ethelred II of England
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred II the Unready [EthelUn.jpg]
1 CONT
1 CONT Rank: 15th
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled: March 18, 978-December 25, 1013
1 CONT
1 CONT and February 2, 1014-April 23, 1016
1 CONT
1 CONT Predecessor: Edward the Martyr
1 CONT
1 CONT Date of Birth: 968
1 CONT
1 CONT Place of Birth: Wessex
1 CONT
1 CONT Wives: Elgiva and Emma
1 CONT
1 CONT Buried: Old Saint Paul's Cathedral
1 CONT
1 CONT Date of Death: April 23, 1016
1 CONT
1 CONT Parents: Edgar and Elfrida
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred II, known as the Unready (968-1013 and 1014-1016), was a King of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded to the throne aged 10 following the death of his father King Edgar and subsequent murder of his half-brother Edward the Martyr. His nickname "The Unready" does not mean that he was ill-prepared, but derives from the A
1 CONC nglo-Saxon 
1 CONT unraede meaning without counsel. Ethelred the Clueless sums up the meaning well.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred had at least sixteen children from two marriages, the second of these, in 1002, being to Emma of Normandy, whose great-nephew, William I of England, would later use this relationship as the basis of his claim on the thron
1 CONC e.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He attempted to buy off the Vikings by payment of what was to become known as Danegeld; he had little choice in the matter since he was unable to place any trust in his generals.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1013, Ethelred fled to Normandy, seeking protection by his brother-in-law, Robert of Normandy, when England was over-run by Svein Haraldsson of Denmark and his forces. He returned in February, 1014, following the death of Svein
1 CONC  Haraldsson. 
1 CONT Ethelred died on April 23, 1016, in London, where he was buried. He was succeeded by his son, Edmund II of England.
0 @N6000000002322325081@ NOTE Suspected by many of complicity in Edward's murder, he seemed an inefficient
1 CONT ruler with successions of Danish raids. While the kingdom of England was
1 CONT conquered, it escaped disintegration under him. 1013: King of Denmark was
1 CONT recognized as "king everywhere" and Ethelred exiled at his brother-in-law's
1 CONT court, Richard, Duke of Normandy. Sweg Forkbeard, the Danish King, died in
1 CONT 1014, however, and the English nobility recalled Ethelred. He was succeeded
1 CONT by his son, Edmund II, when he died in the spring of 1016.
0 @N6000000001744822884@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred II, King of the English (The Unready)
1 CONT
1 CONT Title(s): King of the English ( 978 - 1013); King of the English (1014 - 1016)
1 CONT Born about 967, died April 23, 1016 at age 49 in London of natural causes.
1 CONT Buried at: St. Paul's Cathedral, London, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages:   985: At age 18 married Elfgifu; about July 1002: At age 36 married Emma of Normandy, age 18.
1 CONT
1 CONT Events:
1 CONT  About October 1013: Deposition
1 CONT       February 1014: Resumption
1 CONT       April 14, 979: Coronation, Kingston upon Thames, Surrey, England
1 CONT
1 CONT  Children:
1 CONT               With Emma of Normandy:
1 CONT                    Alfred
1 CONT                    Edward III, King of the English
1 CONT                    Godgifu
1 CONT               With Elfgifu:
1 CONT                    Edgar
1 CONT                    Edith
1 CONT                    Edmund II, King of the English
1 CONT                    Edred
1 CONT                    Edwy
1 CONT                    Egbert
1 CONT                    Elfgifu
1 CONT                    Ethelstan
1 CONT                    Unknown 4th daughter of King Ethelred the Unready
1 CONT                    Unknown 5th daughter of King Ethelred the Unready
1 CONT                    Wulfhild
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes:
1 CONT 1. Ethelred's mother, Elfthryth, beat him with candles so hard and so often that he had a life-long phobia of them.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the assassination of his brother, Edward, Ethelred II was propelled upon the English throne at the age of 10 years. The circumstance of his rise to power, as a pawn in the ambitions of others, did not alter throughout hi
1 CONC s 48 years of life.
1 CONT
1 CONT The nickname of Unready is though to derive from the Saxon word meaning 'with no reputable policy' which can be taken as both accurate and ironic in that Ethelred is itself said to have meant 'noble policy'.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred was married twice. His first wife, Elfigfu of Mercia bore him no less than 11 children: Athelstan, Ecgbert, Edmund, Edred, Edwig, Edgar, Wulfhild, Edgyrth, Elfgifu and a further two other daughters whose names are not com
1 CONC monly recorded.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second marriage, to Emma of Normandy, produced three children, Edward, Alfred and Goda.
1 CONT
1 CONT All through his reign he was shackled by the fact that he could not fully trust the support of his generals, both military and political, at a time when the fearsome Danish invaders were a constant threat to the English. In an act
1 CONC  of, what proved to be, futile appeasement Ethelred attempted to stem the Danish ambitions by paying what was known as Danegeld.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1009 the King of the Danes, Sweyn decided that as well as keeping the territory, and monies, he had taken from the English he would now pursue the whole country. Four years later, in 1013, Sweyn had overrun the country and Ethe
1 CONC lred had fled to Normandy to seek protection from Emma's brother, Robert the Good.
1 CONT
1 CONT This flight brings into the picture the grandson of Robert the Good, William, later to be known as William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sweyn died in 1014 and Ethelred reclaimed the English crown for a further 2 years before his death at the age of 48 in 1016.Ethelred II, King of the English (The Unready)
1 CONT
