1 CONT Microsoft Encarta 1993 "The Unready," is a corruption of the Old English 'unraed', "bad counsel," which is a reference to his misfortunes.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists ..." Aethelred II the Redeless
1 CONT
1 CONT "Anglo Saxon Chronicle", Part 2:
1 CONT A.D. 978. ... This year was King Edward slain ... In this same year succeeded Ethelred Etheling, his brother, to the government; and he was afterwards very readily, and with great joy to the counsellors of England, consecrated kin
1 CONC g at Kingston.
1 CONT
1 CONT A.D. 979. In this year was Ethelred consecrated king, on the Sunday fortnight after Easter, at Kingston. And there were at his consecration two archbishops, and ten diocesan bishops.
0 @N6000000003949711481@ NOTE Aethelred, one of the most hated kings of England, inherited the throne when he was ten years old. His name comes from the Anglo Saxon name for him, "Unraed," which means "evil-counseled," or "uncounseled," and was a pun on his na
1 CONC me. Later generations, after the language had changed, produced the pun we have here. He was supposed to be licentious and mean.
1 CONT He was not ready for the Danes to renew their attack. They had been pretty docile since Alfred, the Great. He lost all of England. His son Edmund II Ironside, briefly recovered some of it, but then King Canute (and 2 other Danish 
1 CONC kings) took over until Aethelred's other son, Edward the Confessor, regained the throne--only to lose it (for the Saxons anyway) in the next generation to William the Conqueror.Reigned from 979-1013 (deposed) and 1014-1016. In the
1 CONC  face of Danishraids, he was forced to pay huge tributes (Dangeld) to the enemy. He was driven into exile by Sweyn, but returned after the latter's death.  Aethelred died during the invasion of England by Canute. Burke says he die
1 CONC d in 1010. His tomb was lost when the old St Paul's was destroyed in the great fire of London 1666. 
1 CONT According to St. Dunstan his life began with an ill omen, for at his baptism he made water in the font. From this 'the man of God' predicted the slaughter of the English people that would take place in his time. He became king aft
1 CONC er the murder of his elder brother Edward, but his kingdom was eventually dismembered by his son, Edmund Ironside, and Cnut the Dane. Though not as capable as his predecessors, he reigned longer than anyone before him. 
1 CONT His derogatory nickname, coined in the twelfth century, is merely a pun on his name, meaning 'noble counsel'. The early years (973-983) were dominated by his mother; his personal rule over the period from 983 till 993 was oppressi
1 CONC ve. Unfortunately, the Viking assaults starting in 988 were of a ferocity unmatched since Alfred's day, and he could do little but negotiate temporary respites and massive tributes, which encouraged further attacks. 
1 CONT He tried to combat the Vikings by diplomacy, notably by marrying Emma, daughter of the Duke of Normandy, and raising a fleet and large armies. In 1000 he led an expedition to Strathclyde to disrupt the Viking settlements around th
1 CONC e Irish Sea. 
1 CONT On 13 November 1002, St. Brice's day, he ordered all the Danish men who were in England to be slain, because the king had been informed that they would treacherously deprive him, and then all his councillers, of life, and possess 
1 CONC this kingdom afterwards. Aethelred's reward for this was the wrath of King Sweyn Forkbeard of Denmark whose sister had been a victim of the massacre. 
1 CONT Sweyn, together with his son, Cnut, began conquering territory. His unexpected attacks in southern and midland England both destroyed the morale of king and country and so disillusioned the nobility that Aethelred could no longer 
1 CONC trust them. In 1013 when Sweyn was chosen king, Aethelred fled, then returned in 1014, after Sweyn had died, promising better rule. 
1 CONT However, when he failed to reassert control, his son Edmund made himself independent ruler in the Danelaw, which in turn was taken by Cnut. This assisted the collapse of the kingdom of Aethelred, who died in 1016. 
0 @N6000000001563244760@ NOTE He was married 3 times and had 16 children including Edmund II Ironside, King of England, (born 989) and Edward (St.) the Confessor, King of England, (born c1002).
1 CONT In the face of Danish raids, he was forced to pay huge tributes (Danegeld) to the enemy.  His reprisal was the St. Brides massacre in which all Danes living in England were killed.  He chose the 13th November 1002 for this co-ordi
1 CONC nated massacre, St. Brice's day. The deed was done. Thousands were killed including the sister of the king of Denmark, Swegn ( Sweyn ) Forkbeard. This so incensed Swegn, that he planned a near full scale invasion of England. Massa
1 CONC cres by Danes were catalogued all over the country. England was more or less bankrupt and starving. Even the Danes returned to Denmark in 1005. They returned in 1006 and demanded even more danegeld. Aethelred agreed to 36,000 poun
1 CONC ds of silver in a final attempt to buy peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Danish fleet was often based in Greenwich as a base to attack London.  The Archbishop of Canterbury (Alfege) was murdered in Greenwich by a Danish invading force on Easter Day 1012.  He was driven into exile in Normandy by Swe
1 CONC yn but returned after his death. Died during Cnut's invasion of England.
1 CONT Æthelred II, the Unready (978-1016 AD)
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded to the throne after the murder of his half-brother, Edward II, the Martyr, at the age of ten. His reign was plagued by poor advice from his personal favorites and suspicions of his complicity in Edward's murder. His w
1 CONC as a rather long and ineffective reign, which was notable for little other than the payment of the Danegeld, an attempt to buy off the Viking invaders with money. The relentless invasions by the Danish Vikings, coupled with their 
1 CONC ever-escalating demands for more money, forced him to abandon his throne in 1013. He fled to Normandy for safety, but was later recalled to his old throne at the death of Svein Forkbeard in 1014. He died in London in 1016.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred II became king following the murder of Edward the Martyr. What followed was a disastrous time for England where all the good work was to go to waste. England, as affluent as it had ever been since the time of the Romans w
1 CONC as about to descend back into the dark pit it had spent all those years climbing out of. To blame Aethelred for all that was about to happen would be unfair, but one must ask the question of how Alfred, Edward I or Aethelstan woul
1 CONC d have dealt with the particular set of circumstances that now confronted Aethelred. His weak leadership and favouritism for selected henchmen. with nepotism abound, probably because of his perpetual fear of insurrection and plot,
1 CONC  England declined rapidly. The countries strength had kept the Danes away. In 980 the Danes returned in force. England was slowly returning to the dark ages again. Aethelred did virtually nothing. It was left to valiant individual
1 CONC s to resist their advance. The most famous was the Battle of Maldon in 991, where Byrhtnoth, the reeve of Essex defended the causeway from the onslaught before being killed
1 CONT
1 CONT Time Line
1 CONT 1002-13 November (St. Brice's Day): Ethelred ordered the massacre of Danes living in England
1 CONT 1002/3: Birth of Edward (the Confessor) - Sweyn Forkbeard invaded England (again)
1 CONT 1007: Danes bought off from England by Ethelred - Ship money first levied in England
1 CONT 1009: Danish invasion of England
1 CONT 1012: 19 April - St. Alphege (954 - 1012) martyred on Easter Sunday, on spot of St. Alfege Church in Greenwich.
1 CONT 1013: Sweyn Forkbeard invaded England (again) - Ethelred fled to Norway
0 @N6000000005076750973@ NOTE Æthelred II 'the Unready', King of England was born between 966 and 969.1 He was the son of Eadgar 'the Peaceful', King of England and Ælfthryth (?).3 He married, firstly, Ælgifu (?), daughter of Thored of Northumbria, Ealdorman o
1 CONC f York and Hilda (?), between 980 and 985.1 He married, secondly, Emma de Normandie, daughter of Richard I, 3rd Duc de Normandie and Gunnor de Crêpon, on 5 April 1002 at Winchester Cathedral, Winchester, Hampshire, England.4 He di
1 CONC ed on 23 April 1016 at London, England, murdered.5 He died in 1016. He was buried at St. Paul's Cathedral, The City, London, England.5
1 CONT      Æthelred II 'the Unready', King of England succeeded to the title of King Æthelred II of England on 18 March 978.1 He was crowned King of England on 4 April 978 at Kingston-upon-Thames, London, England.1 He abdicated as King 
1 CONC of England in 1013.1 He succeeded to the title of King Æthelred II of England on 3 February 1014.1
1 CONT      Ethelred was the son of King Edgar and began to reign when only 11 years old. He was a weakling, totally unable to withstand the Danish onslaught that re-started on his accession. He continually attempted to buy off the Danes
1 CONC  - Danegeld - as when he lost the Battle of Maldon in 991. In a state of near panic he ordered the slaughter of all Danes whether peaceful settlers or not and this foul deed was put in hand on St. Brices Day 13 Nov 1002. Among the
1 CONC  victims was the sister of Sweyn, King of Denmark. The Norsemen were furious and ravaged the country from Cornwall to Kent and from South Wales to East Anglia. By 1013, Sweyn, who was accompanied by his son Canute, was proclaimed 
1 CONC King but he died soon afterwards. Ethelred fled to Normandy when Sweyn's rule prevailed and then on Sweyn's death he returned but the English lords placed severe restrictions on him. The Danes led by Canute returned in 1015 and la
1 CONC nding at Poole they crossed the Thames at Cricklade.
1 CONT Children of Æthelred II 'the Unready', King of England and Ælgifu (?)
1 CONT Æthelstan (?) b. c 986, d. bt 1012 - 10153
1 CONT Eadred (?) b. bt 987 - 1002, d. bt 1012 - 10156
1 CONT Edgar (?) b. bt 987 - 1002, d. bt 1012 - 10157
1 CONT Edmund II 'Ironside', King of England+ b. bt 988 - 993, d. 30 Nov 10166
1 CONT Edward (?) b. bt 988 - 1002, d. b 1004
1 CONT Eadwig (?) b. bt 989 - 1002, d. 10176
1 CONT Ecgberht (?) b. bt 990 - 1002, d. c 10056
1 CONT Eadgyth (?)+ b. bt 991 - 10027
1 CONT Ælfgifu (?)+ b. bt 991 - 10028
1 CONT Wulfhild (?) b. bt 992 - 10027
1 CONT unknown daughter (?) b. bt 993 - 10027
1 CONT unknown daughter2 (?) b. bt 994 - 1002, d. a 10514
1 CONT Children of Æthelred II 'the Unready', King of England and Emma de Normandie
