1 CONT King of the English 979 - 1013. King of the English 1014 - 1016. Events: Coronation April 14, 979. Kingston upon Thames, Surrey, England. Deposition about October 1013. Resumption February 1014. Ethelreds mother, Elfthryth beat hi
1 CONC m with candles so hard and so often that he had a life-long phobia of them. In the face of the Danish raids, he was forced to pay huge tributes (Danengeld) the enemy. He was driven in exile by Sweyn but returned after his death. E
1 CONC thelred did during Canutes invasion of England. Source: RoyaList, Leo van de Pas. *Burke* says he died 1010. Faced with an upsurge of Viking activity, he beggared the kingdom to buy the invaders off. By the time of his death, the 
1 CONC Vikings were powerful enough to take over the kingdom and install one of their own as king, which was Canute. Source: Plantagenet Somerset Fry. 
0 @N6000000006348209432@ NOTE
1 CONT Æthelred II, the Unready (978-1016 AD)
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded to the throne after the murder of his half-brother, Edward II, the Martyr, at the age of ten. His reign was plagued by poor advice from his personal favorites and suspicions of his complicity in Edward's murder. His w
1 CONC as a rather long and ineffective reign, which was notable for little other than the payment of the Danegeld, an attempt to buy off the Viking invaders with money. The relentless invasions by the Danish Vikings, coupled with their 
1 CONC ever-escalating demands for more money, forced him to abandon his throne in 1013. He fled to Normandy for safety, but was later recalled to his old throne at the death of Svein Forkbeard in 1014. He died in London in 1016.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  Britannia.com
