0 @N6000000007329689026@ NOTE [Frank Mitchell, <fmitchel -at-amug.org> soc.gen.med 05-03-96]
0 @N6000000008799906647@ NOTE [Jeremiah Brown.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Ancestry.com 81120.GED, references Larousse Bibliographical Dictionary, pp598-599]
1 CONT
1 CONT Lady Godiva (d. 1080), English noblewoman and religious benefactor, wife of Leofric, Earl of Chester (d. 1057). According to the 13th century chronicler Roger of Wendover, she rode naked through the market-place of Coventry in ord
1 CONC er to persuade her husband to reduce the taxes he had imposed. A later embellishment of the legend suggests that she requested the townspeople to remain indoors, which they all did, except for "peeping Tom", who was struck blind. 
1 CONC Godiva built and endowed monasteries at Coventry and Stow.
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Ancestry.com 139798.GED]
1 CONT She is reported in history as having ridden nude through the city streets of Coventry, in order to protest the unfair taxation imposed by her husband. The Godiva procession was instituted May 31, 1678 as part of the Coventry Fair,
1 CONC  was celebrated at intervals until 1826.
0 @N6000000001353883679@ NOTE About the year 1028, a wealthy widow named Godgifu, "gift of God", orGodiva (there are 17 different spellings of her name), believing herself to be on her deathbed, bequeathed her considerable property to themonastery at Ely, Engl
1 CONC and. But she recovered and a decade or so laterremarried, soon interesting her new husband, Leofric, the earl of Mercia, in her charitable donations. Leofric and Godiva moved to Coventryin Warwickshire from Shrewsbury, Shropshire 
1 CONC where Leofric had earned his fortune in the mutton trade.
1 CONT In 1043 the earl and his lady founded a Benedictine monastery at Coventry on the site of the present-day bombed-out ruin of Coventry Cathedral and nearby new Coventry Cathedral which was built by German students after World War II
1 CONC . On October 4, the monastery (or abbey) which was named in honor of St. Eunice of Saxmundham and was dedicated to St.Peter, St. Osburg, All Saints, and the Virgin Mary, to whom Godiva was particularly devoted. Her later gifts of 
1 CONC gold and gems made the monastery chapel one of the richest in England. After the earl's death, Lady Godiva continued her patronage of at least half a dozen more monasteries.
1 CONT Yet, it is not for such good works that Lady Godiva is remembered, but rather for her legendary ride through Coventry in the nude. The people of Coventry, as most subjects of Edward the Confessor in England, were suffering from th
1 CONC e burden of high taxes. But their pleas for relief went unheeded by Leofric, who had his own obligations to meet and who was not above passing them on to the citizenry. Instead of mercy, Coventry's inhabitants received notice of a
1 CONC  tax increase. To meet it they would have to impoverish themselves.
1 CONT Filled with compassion for the desperate people, Lady Godiva approached her husband - as she had on earlier occasions - to ask that he suspend the onerous levies. Perhaps angered at her persistence, and wishing to put an end to he
1 CONC r tiresome requests, the earl made an outlandishproposal. "Mount your horse naked," he said to his wife, "and pass through the market of the town, from one end to the other, when the people are assembled." On her return from the r
1 CONC ide, he promised, Godiva would be granted her wish and the townspeople would be spared the burdensome new taxes.
1 CONT Leofric expected his wife to withdraw in shock and embarrassment. Instead, to his astonishment, she agreed. The following day, completely unclothed, Godiva mounted a charger and rode through the marketplace. She was accompanied by
1 CONC  two female servants also on horseback, though fully dressed.
1 CONT The story of her ride first appeared in the Flores Historiarum of Roger of Wendover, an historian who lived some two centuries after Lady Godiva's time. He seems to have based his account on the work of an earlier but now lost chr
1 CONC onicle. Subsequent writers steadily embellished the tale. In one version, Lady Godiva's body was concealed by her flowing hair and another has her enveloped in a God-granted cloak of invisibility. In yet another, she orders Covent
1 CONC ry's inhabitants to remain indoors behind shuttered windows on the morning of the ride, thus sparing herself the rude stares of the common folk. Early in the 18th century, another character was added to the narrative. A tailor nam
1 CONC ed Tom, it was claimed, disobeyed her command. When he peeked through a crack in the shutter of his window, Tom was miraculously struck blind. The justly punished voyeur was the original "peeping Tom."
1 CONT As for Leofric's taxes, the humbled earl rewarded his wife's act of compassion by lifting all tolls on Coventry except those for keeping horses. As late at the 17th century, the town was still boasting of sucha tax-exempt status.
0 @N6000000002932667030@ NOTE [My Ancestors.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Godiva, Lady (died 1067), English heroine; subject of legend and poetry; supported and built monasteries  
1 CONT
1 CONT Coventry probably dates from a convent in Saxon times, but a Benedictine priory was founded in 1043 by Leofric, earl of Mercia, and his wife Godiva. Lady Godiva's legendary ride through the town while naked on a white horse is com
1 CONC memorated by a statue in the city center. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Lady Godiva, or Godgifu, (abt. 1010 - bef. 1085) may be one of the most 
1 CONT famous, or infamous, noblewomen in English history. She was married to 
1 CONT Leofric, Earl of Mercia, in the central English city of Coventry. They 
1 CONT had moved to Coventry from Shrewsbury where Leofric had gained his title 
1 CONT and a fortune from success in the mutton trade.
1 CONT Godiva is famous for the dramatic, even scandalous way she stood up to 
1 CONT her husband. After he imposed particularly onerous taxes on the people 
1 CONT of Mercia, she protested -- by riding naked on a white horse through 
1 CONT Coventry. One version of the historical story relates that Leofric, 
1 CONT tired of his wife's protests, proclaimed that he would eliminate almost 
1 CONT all taxes in Coventry if she would ride her horse through Coventry's 
1 CONT marketplace in the middle of the day, clothed only in what God had given 
1 CONT her.
1 CONT This one deed has obscured much of the rest of Godiva and Leofric's 
1 CONT lives. They were very devout Christians. In 1043, they founded an abbey 
1 CONT or monastery (historians disagree which) in Coventry, which came to 
1 CONT serve not only as a center for educating and housing those who were 
1 CONT "called" by God but also as a social center for the town's festivals. As 
1 CONT Leofric and Godiva's philanthropy grew, so did Leofric's power in 
1 CONT governing Coventry. He became responsible for much of the community's 
1 CONT financial affairs, including building and maintaining public works. It 
1 CONT was largely for these community purposes that Leofric's tendency to 
1 CONT impose more and more taxes developed.
1 CONT Not only has Godiva's daring act lived on in legend, it has contributed 
1 CONT one of the common sayings of the English language. In the 17th century, 
1 CONT a new "detail" was added to the story of Godiva's ride: All but one 
1 CONT townsperson were said to have looked away, rather than commit the crime 
1 CONT of seeing nobility naked. A tailer named Thomas, however, looked, and 
1 CONT was supposedly struck blind. He became the original Peeping Tom.
1 CONT Godiva's legendary ride has been commemorated by the people of Coventry 
1 CONT by a statue in the city's center. [My Ancestors2.FTW]
1 CONT
