1 CONT Godiva is famous for the dramatic, even scandalous way she stood up to 
1 CONT her husband. After he imposed particularly onerous taxes on the people 
1 CONT of Mercia, she protested -- by riding naked on a white horse through 
1 CONT Coventry. One version of the historical story relates that Leofric, 
1 CONT tired of his wife's protests, proclaimed that he would eliminate almost 
1 CONT all taxes in Coventry if she would ride her horse through Coventry's 
1 CONT marketplace in the middle of the day, clothed only in what God had given 
1 CONT her.
1 CONT This one deed has obscured much of the rest of Godiva and Leofric's 
1 CONT lives. They were very devout Christians. In 1043, they founded an abbey 
1 CONT or monastery (historians disagree which) in Coventry, which came to 
1 CONT serve not only as a center for educating and housing those who were 
1 CONT "called" by God but also as a social center for the town's festivals. As 
1 CONT Leofric and Godiva's philanthropy grew, so did Leofric's power in 
1 CONT governing Coventry. He became responsible for much of the community's 
1 CONT financial affairs, including building and maintaining public works. It 
1 CONT was largely for these community purposes that Leofric's tendency to 
1 CONT impose more and more taxes developed.
1 CONT Not only has Godiva's daring act lived on in legend, it has contributed 
1 CONT one of the common sayings of the English language. In the 17th century, 
1 CONT a new "detail" was added to the story of Godiva's ride: All but one 
1 CONT townsperson were said to have looked away, rather than commit the crime 
1 CONT of seeing nobility naked. A tailer named Thomas, however, looked, and 
1 CONT was supposedly struck blind. He became the original Peeping Tom.
1 CONT Godiva's legendary ride has been commemorated by the people of Coventry 
1 CONT by a statue in the city's center. 
0 @N6000000003319709245@ NOTE
1 CONT AFN: 8XQ9-4D
1 CONT http://www.familysearch.org/Eng/Search/AF/individual_record.asp?recid=7256074&lds=0
0 @N6000000003949658998@ NOTE This is the famous Lady Godiva. She complained constantly to her husband that the taxes were too high on the townspeople of Coventry. He finally said that he would reduce the taxes if she would ride nude through the marketplace on
1 CONC  market day. She arranged for all of the men to remain inside and covered her entire body, except her legs, with her hair. Supposedly one person named Tom did not remain inside during her ride and became known as "Peeping Tom". Le
1 CONC ofric eliminated all taxes, except for one on horses. During Edward I's reign, a check was made and the only tax in Coventry was one on horses. Since 1678 the town of Coventry still celebrates the ride during its annual fair.
1 CONT Concerning her dates of birth and death.  Originally had 980-1067, but this was not in agreement with Ancestral Roots line 176a-2, which has her birth at c1010 and her marriage at c1030. Nor was it consistant with the Encyclopedia
1 CONC  Britannica which had Godiva fl. 1040-1080. However the 980 birth date was consistant with her gr grandaughter Nesta b. c1055/7, dau of Edith, dau of Aelfgar, son of Godiva. With some serious scrunching of dates I could conceive o
1 CONC f:
1 CONT Godiva b. 1010 (originally 980)
1 CONT Aelfgar b. 1025 (originally 1002)
1 CONT Edith b. 1041 (originally 1030)
1 CONT Nesta b. 1057
1 CONT with two other daughters of Leofric born to a "first wife or mistress". It is physically possible for 3 generations in a row born at the 15/16th year of their parents life (however unlikely), so that is what I have done.
1 CONT Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yaoo.com, e-mailed:   Hi Jim, 
1 CONT You may want to adjust the date of death [of Godiva, which at the time was 10 Sep 1067]] 
1 CONT Warwickshire - the Domesday Book in 1086 
1 CONT Perhaps one of the most famous early personalities of the period was Lady Godiva (Godwa or Godgifu) who allegedly rode naked the streets of Coventry in Warwickshire as a protest against her husband's high taxes on the people of th
1 CONC e city. This husband, Earl Leofric, a Saxon Earl of Mercia, died an old man in 1057, nine years before the Norman Conquest. They seemingly had issue, at least one daughter, who married into the Malet family. 29 years after her hus
1 CONC band's death, Lady (Countess) Godiva held many estates in Warwickshire, including Coventry, as revealed by the Domesday Book in 1086. Chronologically, either Leofric had married a child bride, or Lady Godivawas a very old woman at
1 CONC  the time of the Domesday Survey in 1086. The former is the most likely. The lordships bestowed on Lady Godiva in Warwickshire by William the Conqueror in 1066 were considerable, probably the result of an alliance struck either wi
1 CONC th Leofric or Lady Godiva before the Conquest. Since, Lady Godiva was a wealthy woman in 1057, and still wealthy after the Conquest, it is not likely she displayed herself in protest after that date, since she would have been prot
1 CONC esting her own taxation. She apparently inherited her lands and titles in 1057. Therefore, the event in question probably took place, if at all, several years before 1057, when, young and innocent, the impatient and passionate Lad
1 CONC y Godiva, appalled by her aging husband's despotic ways, leapt on her nag and took to the streets of Coventry in all her naked glory, perhaps too young to realize that within a few short years she would be in full control of all t
1 CONC he taxation of her husband's considerable holdings at the time of his death, holdings which she carried through to at least 1086. [Ref: ]
1 CONT Other refs:    aka: Gogdifu [Dict of Brit History p153-4, Ency Britannica] 
1 CONT Godiva, on her own account, was a great benefactress to the city, and bestowed much wealth on the monastery there, which had been founded by Leofric and Godwin jointly. She showered on the monks jewels and ornaments, "having sent 
1 CONC for skilful goldsmiths, who, with all the gold and silver she had, made crosses, images of saints, and other decorations." The value of the jewels bestowed on Coventry Abbey are said to be inestimable; and on her death-bed the Cou
1 CONC ntess bequeathed a precious circlet of gems, which she wore round her neck, valued at one hundred marks of silver (about 2000l. of our money), to the image of the Virgin in Coventry Abbey, praying that all who come thither would s
1 CONC ay as many prayers as there were gems in it! [Saxon Chronicle, Fl. Wigorn, R. Wendover.]
1 CONT Godiva's ancestry is uncertain, but she was evidently of an old, noble family. [Ref: Weis AR7 176A]   sister of Thorold of Buckingham, sheriff of Lincs [Ref: Weis AR7 176A, Watney 673] Turold the Sheriff [Ref: KeatsRohan Lucy)  ww
1 CONC w.historiccoventry.co.uk/main/history.php
0 @N6000000000337405708@ NOTE Source: Complete Peerage, Vol I, p. 22; Vol VI, pp. 452-453 - founder of the church of Coventry;  Earl of Mercia by 1032
0 @N6000000000437047597@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Iii, Earl Of Mercia
0 @N6000000001783482746@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofric, Earl of Mercia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofric (born 968, died 31 August or 30 September 1057) was the Earl of Mercia and founded monasteries at Coventry and Much Wenlock. Leofric is remembered as the husband of Lady Godiva.
1 CONT
1 CONT Life and Political Influence
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofric was the son of Ealdorman Leofwine of the Hwicce, who died c. 1023. Leofric's elder brother Northman was killed in 1017, in the losing battles against Cnut.
1 CONT
1 CONT The victorious Cnut divided England into four great provinces: Wessex, East Anglia, Mercia and Northumbria each of which he eventually placed under the control of an earl (a title new to the English, replacing the Anglo-Saxon "eal
1 CONC dorman"). Mercia he initially left in the hands of Eadric Streona, who had been Ealdorman of Mercia since 1007, but Eadric was killed later in the same year of 1017.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mercia may have been given to Leofric immediately after that. He had certainly become Earl of Mercia by the 1030s. This made him one of the most powerful men in the land, second only to Earl Godwin of Wessex among the mighty earls
1 CONC . He may have had some connection by marriage with Ælfgifu of Northampton, the first wife of Cnut. That might help to explain why he supported her son Harold Harefoot against Harthacanute, Cnut's son by Emma, when Cnut died in 103
1 CONC 5.
1 CONT
1 CONT However Harold died in 1040 and was succeeded by Harthacanute, who made himself unpopular with heavy taxation in his short reign. Two of his tax-collectors were killed at Worcester by angry locals. The king was so enraged by this 
1 CONC that in 1041 he ordered Leofric and his other earls to plunder and burn the city, and lay waste the whole area.This command must have sorely tested Leofric. Worcester was the cathedral city of the Hwicce, his people.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Harthacanute died suddenly in 1042, he was succeeded by his half-brother Edward the Confessor. Leofric loyally supported Edward when he came under threat at Gloucester from Earl Godwin in 1051. Leofric and Earl Siward of Nort
1 CONC humbria gathered a great army to meet that of Godwin. Wise heads counselled that battle would be folly, with the flower of England on both sides. Their loss would leave England open to its enemies. So the issue was resolved by les
1 CONC s bloody means. Earl Godwin and his family were outlawed for a time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Leofric's power was then at its height. But in 1055 his son Ælfgar was outlawed, "without any fault", says the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle. He raised an army in Ireland and Wales and brought it to Hereford, where he clashed with th
1 CONC e army of Earl Ralph of Herefordshire and severely damaged the town. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle wryly comments "And then when they had done most harm, it was decided to reinstate Earl Ælfgar".
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofric died "at a good old age" in 1057 at his estate at Kings Bromley in Staffordshire. According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, he died on 30 September, but the chronicler of Worcester gives the date as 31 August. Both agree tha
1 CONC t he was buried at Coventry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofric used a double-headed eagle as his personal device, and this has been adopted by various units of the British Army as a symbol for Mercia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Religious Works
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Leofric and Godiva were noted for great generosity to religious houses. In 1043 he founded and endowed a Benedictine monastery at Coventry. John of Worcester tells us that "He and his wife, the noble Countess Godgifu, a worsh
1 CONC ipper of God and devout lover of St Mary ever-virgin, built the monastery there from the foundations out of their own patrimony, and endowed it adequately with lands and made it so rich in various ornaments that in no monastery in
1 CONC  England might be found the abundance of gold, silver, gems and precious stones that was at that time in its possession."
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 1050s Leofric and Godiva appear jointly in the grant of land to the monastery of St Mary, Worcester, and the endowment of the minister at Stow St Mary, Lincolnshire. She and her husband are commemorated as benefactors of ot
1 CONC her monasteries at Leominster, Chester, Much Wenlock, and Evesham.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Apart from Northman, killed in 1017, Leofric had at least two other brothers. Edwin was killed in battle by Gruffydd ap Llywelyn in 1039. Godwine died some time before 1057.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Child
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofric may have married more than once. His famous wife Godiva survived him and may have been a second or later wife. Since there is some question about the date of marriage for Leofric and Godgifu, it is not clear that she was t
1 CONC he mother of Ælfgar, Leofric's only known child. If Godiva was married to Earl Leofric only in 1040, she could not have been the mother of Ælfgar (whose own children were born in that decade or earlier). If she was married earlier
1 CONC  (as early as 1017, as some sources claim), she could have been Ælfgar's mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ælfgar succeeded Leofric as Earl of Mercia.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leofric,_Earl_of_Mercia>
