0 @N6000000002187763828@ NOTE Leofric, Earl of Mercia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofric (b. abt May 14, 968 - died August 31, 1057) was the Earl of Mercia, who, in 1043, founded monasterys at Coventry and Much Wenlock. Leofric is best remembered as the husband of Godiva, who is said to have ridden through the
1 CONC  streets of Coventry naked, in order to persuade her husband to reduce the burden of taxes placed on their subjects by order of King Harthacanute.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married in 1030 and is said to have died in Bromley, Staffordshire, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the novel by Charles Kingsley, taken directly from a twelfth century copy of Hereward's biography by Leofrc's former chaplain, they were the parents of Hereward the Wake (or strictly, Leofric was his father). Though some see th
1 CONC is as merely a literary device, there is no real reason for thinking so.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had at least three sons, Ælfgar, Hereward and an un-named younger one. The eldest, Alfgar, Earl Of Mercia, succeeded him. Hereward fought William I then came to terms with him and the third was killed in his father's house at B
1 CONC ourne, by Normans attempting to evict him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT
1 CONT Leofwine, Earl of Mercia
1 CONT  Leofric, Earl of Mercia Followed by:
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfgar, Earl Of Mercia
1 CONT
