0 @N6000000005740755845@ NOTE Held Titles 6th Earl of Leicester, Earl of Northampton , Duke of Mercia , He was also Grandmaster of the free Masons and Supertendent to the Construction of Westminister Abbey
1 CONT       Succeeded his father as one of the three great Earls of the realm of England( between 1024 and 1032), played part as Mediator between Edward the Confessor and Earl Godwin10561, Benefactor of the Church, Like his wife Godgifu
1 CONC , Famed in legend as Lady Godiva, Countess of Coventry, Daughter of Thoroid the Earl, Her sister Elgiva was First wife of King Ethelred ll ( The Unruly) The parents of King Edmund Ironside. Leofric's niece Edelfleda, Daughter of h
1 CONC is brother Eadwine, Earl of Northampton , Married Edmund Ironside. Leofric's Brother in law King Ethelred, Was father of Edward the Confessor .  Algiva, The daughter of Leofric's brother Elfhealm, Earl of northampton, Married King
1 CONC  Canute  and by him had Sweyn, King of norway, and Harold l ( harefoot), King of England. Leofric Was Captain-General of Canute's Army. The kingmaker , Leofric put upon the throne first his nephew , Harold l and then Hardicanute a
1 CONC nd Edward the Confessor  Leofric founded the Monastery of Conventry, Endowing it with 24 Lordships and vast riches. He was (says sir william Dugdale) " A person of singular piety".and Ailredus Rievallensis is authority for the sta
1 CONC tement " That this Devout Leofric , upon a time , attending the Pious King Edward the confessor near the Altar in the Abbey, Church of St. Peter at westminster, Which he had founded , At the elevation of the Host, beheld the Real 
1 CONC Body of Christ, standing on the Floor , and with his right hand depicting the sign of the Cross upon King Edward.and that stepping towards the King to let him know what he had seen. The King said 'Stand still Leofric, stand still,
1 CONC  I see that which thou seest'  Also that after the mass was ended , he further said ' My Leofric , I do conjure thee that so long as we live thou dost not reveal this , lest the credit thereof should suffer through the emulation o
1 CONC f unbelievers' Whereunto he obeyed. But to the that the truth of this miracle might not be concealed from posterity, wrote the thereof in a schedule , and delivered it to a monk of Worcester, with Command that it should be put int
1 CONC o a shine and not opened until after the death of  himself and the King "
