1 CONT The monastery of Saint Columba was founded on the north bank of the River Tay in the 6th century or early 7th century following the expedition of Columba into the land of the Picts. Probably originally constructed as a simple grou
1 CONC p of wattle huts, the monastery - or at least its church - was rebuilt in the 9th century by Kenneth I of Scotland (reigned 843-858). Kenneth I brought relics of Columba from Iona to Dunkeld at the same time others were taken to K
1 CONC ells in Ireland, to protect them from Viking raids. Dunkeld became the prime bishopric in eastern Scotland until supplanted in importance by St Andrews since the 10th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT While the title of Hereditary Lay Abbot was a feudal position that was often exercised in name only, Crinán does seem to have acted as Abbot in charge of the monastery in his time. He was thus a man of high position in both cleric
1 CONC al and secular society.
1 CONT
1 CONT The magnificent semi-ruined Dunkeld Cathedral, built in stages between 1260 and 1501, stands today on the grounds once occupied by the monastery. The Cathedral contains the only surviving remains of the previous monastic society: 
1 CONC a course of red stone visible in the east choir wall that may be re-used from an earlier building, and two stone 9th century-10th century cross-slabs in the Cathedral Museum.
0 @N6000000003319702442@ NOTE
1 CONT AFN: 8XJK-GL
0 @N6000000003177759331@ NOTE Hereditary Lay Abbot of Dunkeld
0 @N6000000003232674243@ NOTE 2  PLAC Abbot Of Dunkeld
1 CONT
1 CONT [Sargent.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Alias: Seneschal of the /Isles/
1 CONT Hereditary lay abbot of Dunkeld and Governor of the Scots Islands.  He was a warrior as well as an abbot.  Died in battle. [91502.ftw]
0 @N6000000003484295899@ NOTE Hereditary Lay Abbot of Dunkeld
0 @N6000000003949656882@ NOTE Crinan; Lay Abbot of Dunkeld; married c1000 Bethoc, elder daughter of Malcolm II, and was killed 1045 in battle against Macbeth (the historic figure who murdered Crinan's elder son Duncan I, and subsequently is portrayed as having
1 CONC  usurped the Scottish throne, most famously by Shakespeare), leaving (Maldred), with an elder son (Duncan I "The Gracious" King of Scots 1034-40; ancestor of the Sovereigns of England and later England, Ireland, Scotland, Great Br
1 CONC itain, etc.). [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT Crinan the Thane (Albanock, Grimus), of the kin of St. Columba, Lord of the Isles, Governor of Scots Island, Earl of Strathclye, hereditary Abbot of Dunkeld.
1 CONT The following additional information was given in a post-em by Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yahoo.com:
1 CONT Lay Abbot of Dunkeld, Governor of the Scots Islands [Ref: Weis AR7 170:19] 
1 CONT Heriditary Abbott of Dunkeld, or the Kindred of St. Columba [Ref: Royal Highness: Ancestors of the Royal Child by Sir Ian Moncreiffe, 1982, Hamish Hamilton, London Pg 20] note: "or the kindred of" = ? Either was or wasn't... Curt
1 CONT In what was probably a shrewd political move, Malcolm II married his daughter Bethoc to a representative of the other major center of political power in Scotland, the church. Considering the close ties between ruling dynasties and
1 CONC  the offices of major abbacies throughout Ireland and Scotland, it is also possible that Crinan was descended from a king of the Scots, which would completely legitimize his son's rule. [Ref: The Genealogy of the Early Medieval Sc
1 CONC ottish Kings, Edinburgh by Michael R. Davidson 1995, Holland House, Edinburgh, Scotland]
1 CONT Malcolm's elder daughter Bethoc married Crinan 'the Thane', lay abbot of Dunkeld. At this period, when Celtic Monasticism was in decline, lay abbots appear to have been as accepted a part of the ecclesiastical structure as they be
1 CONC came centuries later on the eve of the Reformation. Crinan was a great nobleman, as his title implies, and he possessed the added prestige of belonging to the kindred of St. Columba. It was from his abbacy of Dunkeld that the new 
1 CONC royal House took its name, for Crinan and Bethoc were the parents of King Duncan I...Meanwhile, Macbeth consolidated his triumph by defeating and slaying Duncan's father, Crinan, in a battle at Dunkeld in 1045. [Ref: The Kings and
1 CONC  Queens of Scotland]    Macbeth...probably a grandson of Malcolm II...asserted his claim to the throne against Duncan I, whom he killed near Elgin. In 1045 he killed Crinan, Duncan's father in battle, but in 1057 he was himself ki
1 CONC lled by Duncan's son, Malcolm Canmore (Malcolm III). [Ref: A Dictionary of British History by J.P. Kenyon, 1983 Stein and Day, Scarborough House, Briarcliff Manor, NY]
1 CONT Research note: Supposed father: Since the abbacy of Dunkeld appears to have been hereditary in Crínán's family (his grandson Æthelred held the title), it has sometimes been suggested that Crínán may have been the son of this earli
1 CONC er abbot of Dunkeld whose death is known form both the Irish and Scottish sources [e.g., AU; ESSH 1: 471, 473, 577; KKES 252]. While the relationship is not impossible (assuming that Crínán's father died when he was an infant), th
1 CONC ere is no known evidence to support it, and it cannot be accepted without further evidence [Ref: Henry Project citing: 
0 @N6000000004533699981@ NOTE Abbot of Dunkeld and Abthane of Dull.
0 @N6000000004851188545@ NOTE aka the Thane, Lord of Mormaer, Abbot of Dunkeld, Earl of Strathclyde
1 CONT
1 CONT son Duncan became Duncan I King of Scots in 1034
1 CONT son Maldred of Allerdale was Earl-Lord Carlisle, from whom the Earls of Dunbar descend in unbroken male line
1 CONT
1 CONT killed in battle at Dunkeld in 1045
0 @N6000000006296745511@ NOTE Crínán of Dunkeld (died 1045) was the lay abbot </wiki/Lay_abbot> of the diocese of Dunkeld </wiki/Dunkeld>, and perhaps the Mormaer of Atholl </wiki/Mormaer_of_Atholl>. Crínán was progenitor of the House of Dunkeld </wiki/House_o
1 CONC f_Dunkeld>, the dynasty who would rule Scotland until the later 13th century.
1 CONT Crinán was married to Bethoc </wiki/Bethoc>, daughter of King Malcolm II of Scotland </wiki/Malcolm_II_of_Scotland> (reigned 1005-1034). As Malcolm II had no son, the strongest hereditary claim to the Scottish throne descended thr
1 CONC ough Bethóc, and Crinán's eldest son Donnchad I </wiki/Donnchad_I_of_Scotland> (reigned 1034-1040), became King of Scots. Some sources indicate that Malcolm II designated Duncan as his successor under the rules of tanistry </wiki/
1 CONC Tanistry> because there were other possible claimants to the throne.
1 CONT Crinán's second son, Maldred of Allerdale, held the title of Lord of Cumbria. It is said that from him, the Earls of Dunbar </wiki/Earl_of_Dunbar>, for example Patrick Dunbar, 9th Earl of Dunbar </wiki/Patrick_Dunbar,_9th_Earl_of_
1 CONC Dunbar>, descend in unbroken male line.
1 CONT Crinán was killed in battle in 1045 at Dunkeld.
1 CONT The monastery </wiki/Monastery> of Saint Columba </wiki/Columba> was founded on the north bank of the River Tay </wiki/River_Tay> in the 6th century or early 7th century following the expedition of Columba into the land of the Pic
1 CONC ts </wiki/Picts>. Probably originally constructed as a simple group of wattle </wiki/Wattle-and-daub> huts, the monastery - or at least its church - was rebuilt in the 9th century by Kenneth I of Scotland </wiki/Kenneth_I_of_Scotl
1 CONC and> (reigned 843-858). Caustantín of the Picts </wiki/Caustant%C3%ADn_of_the_Picts> brought Scotland's share of the relics of Columba from Iona </wiki/Iona> to Dunkeld </wiki/Dunkeld> at the same time others were taken to Kells <
1 CONC /wiki/Kells> in Ireland </wiki/Ireland>, to protect them from Viking </wiki/Viking> raids. Dunkeld </wiki/Dunkeld> became the prime bishopric in eastern Scotland </wiki/Scotland> until supplanted in importance by St Andrews </wiki
1 CONC /St_Andrews> since the 10th century.
1 CONT While the title of Hereditary Lay Abbot was a feudal </wiki/Feudal> position that was often exercised in name only, Crinán does seem to have acted as Abbot in charge of the monastery in his time. He was thus a man of high position
1 CONC  in both clerical and secular society.
1 CONT The magnificent semi-ruined Dunkeld Cathedral </wiki/Dunkeld_Cathedral>, built in stages between 1260 and 1501, stands today on the grounds once occupied by the monastery. The Cathedral contains the only surviving remains of the p
1 CONC revious monastic society: a course of red stone visible in the east choir </wiki/Choir> wall that may be re-used from an earlier building, and two stone 9th century-10th century cross-slabs in the Cathedral Museum.
